export default {"format":"cadcraft-help","version":1,"app":"markupcraft","name":"MarkupCraft","common":"basics","sections":{"start":"Getting started","tasks":"Walkthroughs","switching":"Coming from other software","reference":"Reference","trouble":"Troubleshooting and limits"},"variants":{"os":{"mac":"Mac","windows":"Windows","linux":"Linux"},"browser":{"chromium":"Chrome or Edge","firefox":"Firefox","safari":"Safari"},"shell":{"web":"In the browser","desktop":"Desktop app"},"net":{"online":"Online","offline":"Offline"},"input":{"mouse":"Mouse","touch":"Touch"}},"topics":[{"slug":"whats-new","scope":"markupcraft","title":"Novità della 0.7","summary":"RFI, documenti da approvare e giornali di cantiere in una nuova scheda Cantiere, con scadenze, ritardi in rosso, CSV e BCF, lavoro offline e Unisci; assistenti che impostano stati, rispondono ed esportano l'elenco; elenchi di unità che seguono le Impostazioni; pannelli che si ripiegano e finestre di dialogo che si spostano.","section":"start","order":5,"keywords":["novità","release notes","elenco delle modifiche","modifiche","0.7","versione","nuove funzioni","cantiere","registri di cantiere","rfi","richiesta di informazioni","documento da approvare","giornale di cantiere","diario di cantiere","in ritardo","scadenza","assegnati a me","puntina","unisci","offline","tablet","bcf","csv","assistente","mcp","stato dell'annotazione","risposta","esportare","unità","elenchi di unità","stella","calibrare","compatta","pannelli","finestre di dialogo","portoghese"],"headings":[{"id":"novit","text":"Novità","level":2},{"id":"rfi-documenti-da-approvare-e-giornali-di-cantiere","text":"RFI, documenti da approvare e giornali di cantiere","level":3},{"id":"assistenti-ai","text":"Assistenti AI","level":3},{"id":"in-ogni-app","text":"In ogni app","level":2},{"id":"non-ancora-in-questa-versione","text":"Non ancora in questa versione","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EEcco cosa è cambiato in MarkupCraft 0.7 rispetto alla 0.6. La grande novità sono i registri di cantiere: RFI, documenti da approvare e giornali di cantiere conservati nel PDF accanto alle annotazioni.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"novit\"\u003ENovità\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"rfi-documenti-da-approvare-e-giornali-di-cantiere\"\u003ERFI, documenti da approvare e giornali di cantiere\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELa scheda Cantiere.\u003C/strong\u003E Aprire la scheda \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECantiere\u003C/span\u003E e scegliere \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERFI\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDocumento da approvare\u003C/span\u003E o \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGiornale di cantiere\u003C/span\u003E. Toccare o fare clic sulla tavola dove va un RFI o un documento da approvare e una puntina segna il punto; un giornale di cantiere va sulla tavola aperta senza puntina (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPosiziona puntina\u003C/span\u003E ne aggiunge una in seguito, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESposta puntina\u003C/span\u003E sposta qualsiasi puntina). Ogni registro ha il proprio numero (RFI-0001, SUB-0001, LOG-0001), un titolo, un testo, uno stato, un assegnatario, una scadenza, una discussione di risposte e un elenco di nomi di allegati. Vedere \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/markupcraft/field-records\" data-topic=\"markupcraft/field-records\"\u003ETracciare RFI, documenti da approvare e giornali di cantiere\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EVedere cosa è in ritardo.\u003C/strong\u003E Filtrare l&#039;elenco per tipo, stato, scadenza (in ritardo, in scadenza entro 7 giorni, con o senza scadenza), questa tavola o tutte le tavole, una ricerca nel testo oppure \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAssegnati a me\u003C/span\u003E. Un registro oltre la scadenza e ancora in attesa è contrassegnato \u003Cstrong\u003EIn ritardo\u003C/strong\u003E, in rosso, nell&#039;elenco e sulla sua puntina. Vedere \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/markupcraft/field-records#find-what-matters\" data-topic=\"markupcraft/field-records\" data-hash=\"find-what-matters\"\u003ETrovare ciò che conta\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECSV e BCF.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECSV\u003C/span\u003E scrive i registri elencati come foglio di calcolo; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBCF\u003C/span\u003E li scrive come argomenti BCF, ciascuno con un&#039;istantanea del suo punto sulla tavola. Entrambi seguono i filtri. Vedere \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/markupcraft/field-records#export\" data-topic=\"markupcraft/field-records\" data-hash=\"export\"\u003EEsportare\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOffline, e due copie rese una.\u003C/strong\u003E I registri sono salvati nel PDF e non richiedono rete, quindi un tablet in cantiere può aggiungerli, rispondere e salvarli. Tornati in ufficio, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUnisci\u003C/span\u003E unisce i registri di un&#039;altra copia dello stesso PDF in base ai loro id, come un unico passo di annullamento. Vedere \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/markupcraft/field-records#save-offline-and-merging-copies\" data-topic=\"markupcraft/field-records\" data-hash=\"save-offline-and-merging-copies\"\u003ESalvare, lavorare offline e unire copie\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"assistenti-ai\"\u003EAssistenti AI\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPiù cose che un assistente può fare.\u003C/strong\u003E Il proprio assistente, collegato tramite CadCraft Studio, può ora impostare lo stato di un&#039;annotazione (\u003Ccode\u003Emarkup_status\u003C/code\u003E, registrato nella sua discussione), rispondere in una discussione (\u003Ccode\u003Emarkup_reply\u003C/code\u003E) ed esportare l&#039;elenco \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAnnotazioni\u003C/span\u003E come CSV o BCF (\u003Ccode\u003Emarkups_export\u003C/code\u003E, ciò che l&#039;elenco mostra con i suoi filtri). Ogni modifica è un passo di annullamento, firmato con il nome dell&#039;assistente. Vedere \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/markupcraft/use-with-ai-assistants#what-an-assistant-can-ask-for\" data-topic=\"markupcraft/use-with-ai-assistants\" data-hash=\"what-an-assistant-can-ask-for\"\u003ECosa può chiedere un assistente\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"in-ogni-app\"\u003EIn ogni app\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUnità.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EImpostazioni\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGenerali\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUnità\u003C/span\u003E ha un elenco ciascuno per lunghezza, area, volume, angolo e pendenza, con le dimensioni esatte date dalle definizioni delle unità (il piede topografico statunitense è un&#039;unità a sé). Disattivare le unità che non si usano mai ed escono dagli elenchi; contrassegnarne una con la stella e il nuovo lavoro parte con quella. Un file mantiene sempre la propria unità. In MarkupCraft, le unità di misura e di posizione in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EImpostazioni\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMarkupCraft\u003C/span\u003E e l&#039;unità nella finestra di calibrazione seguono gli interruttori, la stella imposta l&#039;unità di misura finché non si salvano le impostazioni proprie di MarkupCraft, e la distanza reale digitata durante la calibrazione può portare il simbolo di qualsiasi unità, ad esempio \u003Ccode\u003E3 yd\u003C/code\u003E o \u003Ccode\u003E1.5 km\u003C/code\u003E. Vedere \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/unit-lists\" data-topic=\"basics/unit-lists\"\u003EUnità, valori digitati ed elenchi delle unità\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUna disposizione più densa e pannelli che si ripiegano.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EImpostazioni\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGenerali\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDensità della disposizione\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECompatta\u003C/span\u003E fa stare più cose sullo schermo (i touch screen e i telefoni mantengono i pulsanti grandi). Ogni pannello laterale ha una sottile barra del titolo: trascinarla per ancorare o rendere mobile il pannello, rilasciarla sulla barra di un altro pannello per condividere un riquadro come schede, fissarla perché scorra via sul bordo, oppure nasconderla e riportarla dal bordo. Vedere \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/arrange-panels#make-the-layout-denser\" data-topic=\"basics/arrange-panels\" data-hash=\"make-the-layout-denser\"\u003EDisporre i pannelli\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELe finestre di dialogo si spostano.\u003C/strong\u003E Trascinare una finestra di dialogo dalla barra del titolo per toglierla di mezzo al disegno; ricompare dove è stata lasciata. Fare doppio clic sulla barra del titolo per centrarla di nuovo. Vedere \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/arrange-panels#move-a-dialog\" data-topic=\"basics/arrange-panels\" data-hash=\"move-a-dialog\"\u003ESpostare una finestra di dialogo\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERicette per gli assistenti AI.\u003C/strong\u003E Dieci richieste svolte per il proprio assistente (riordinare un disegno, stampare un set, verificare i disegni, interrogare un modello, totalizzare un computo, provare una variante di parte…), ciascuna con gli strumenti che chiama e i permessi che richiede, e ciascuna rieseguita dai nostri test. Le app non contengono AI. Vedere \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/assistants-recipes\" data-topic=\"basics/assistants-recipes\"\u003ERicette per gli assistenti AI\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELingue.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cstrong\u003EPortuguês (Portugal)\u003C/strong\u003E è nuovo in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EImpostazioni\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGenerali\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELingua\u003C/span\u003E. Questa, \u003Cstrong\u003EPortuguês (Brasil)\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EEspañol (México)\u003C/strong\u003E e \u003Cstrong\u003EBahasa Indonesia\u003C/strong\u003E hanno ora ogni parola delle app, come bozze non ancora riviste da un madrelingua. Gli argomenti della guida sono in inglese.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECadCraft Studio\u003C/strong\u003E dice perché un controllo degli aggiornamenti è fallito (nessun file per il proprio sistema, nessuna rete, firma non valida) invece di un semplice &quot;Fine&quot;, e smette di riproporre una scheda Novità chiusa finché non c&#039;è una versione più recente.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"non-ancora-in-questa-versione\"\u003ENon ancora in questa versione\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELa 0.7 non è firmata digitalmente.\u003C/strong\u003E La firma e il pacchetto per il Microsoft Store sono passati alla 0.8 perché la convalida del certificato non è ancora arrivata. Verificare lo SHA-256 accanto a ogni file su getcadcraft.com/download. In Windows, se SmartScreen blocca il programma di installazione, scegliere \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUlteriori informazioni\u003C/span\u003E, poi \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEsegui comunque\u003C/span\u003E. Su Mac, la prima volta, fare clic con il tasto destro su CadCraft Studio e scegliere \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EApri\u003C/span\u003E. CadCraft Studio non è ancora in uno store di app e non esiste ancora una versione per iPad o iPhone.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EGli elenchi di angolo e pendenza in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUnità\u003C/span\u003E vengono salvati, ma nessun campo li legge ancora.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EI registri di cantiere non sono condivisi in una sessione Live di CadCraft Studio (le sessioni Live trasportano solo le annotazioni); riunire le copie con \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUnisci\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUnisci prende un registro per intero dalla copia modificata per ultima: se due persone hanno cambiato campi diversi dello stesso registro, prevalgono i campi della copia più recente. Le discussioni e i nomi degli allegati vengono uniti.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EGli allegati sono solo nomi; i file restano dove sono. Non ci sono team, notifiche né progetti nel cloud per i registri, e gli assistenti non hanno ancora strumenti per i registri.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EI registri di cantiere sono stati provati in un browser con dimensioni da telefono e tablet con la rete interrotta, ma non ancora a mano su un vero tablet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAncora dalla 0.6: ruotare gli oggetti della pagina di un angolo qualsiasi, disegnare nuove forme direttamente nella pagina, cambiare il carattere o la dimensione di una riga e il testo che scorre su più righe. Una copia salvata con modifiche alla pagina non è ancora stata aperta in Acrobat.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAncora dalla 0.5: annullare una modifica di pagina (come aggiungere una pagina vuota), leggere il testo dalle tavole scansionate e le sessioni Live oltre la propria rete.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETutto il resto è in \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/markupcraft/not-built-yet\" data-topic=\"markupcraft/not-built-yet\"\u003ECosa non è ancora implementato\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Ecco cosa è cambiato in MarkupCraft 0.7 rispetto alla 0.6. La grande novità sono i registri di cantiere: RFI, documenti da approvare e giornali di cantiere conservati nel PDF accanto alle annotazioni. Novità RFI, documenti da approvare e giornali di cantiere La scheda Cantiere. Aprire la scheda Cantiere e scegliere RFI, Documento da approvare o Giornale di cantiere. Toccare o fare clic sulla tavola dove va un RFI o un documento da approvare e una puntina segna il punto; un giornale di cantiere va sulla tavola aperta senza puntina (Posiziona puntina ne aggiunge una in seguito, Sposta puntina sposta qualsiasi puntina). Ogni registro ha il proprio numero (RFI-0001, SUB-0001, LOG-0001), un titolo, un testo, uno stato, un assegnatario, una scadenza, una discussione di risposte e un elenco di nomi di allegati. Vedere Tracciare RFI, documenti da approvare e giornali di cantiere. Vedere cosa è in ritardo. Filtrare l'elenco per tipo, stato, scadenza (in ritardo, in scadenza entro 7 giorni, con o senza scadenza), questa tavola o tutte le tavole, una ricerca nel testo oppure Assegnati a me. Un registro oltre la scadenza e ancora in attesa è contrassegnato In ritardo, in rosso, nell'elenco e sulla sua puntina. Vedere Trovare ciò che conta. CSV e BCF. CSV scrive i registri elencati come foglio di calcolo; BCF li scrive come argomenti BCF, ciascuno con un'istantanea del suo punto sulla tavola. Entrambi seguono i filtri. Vedere Esportare. Offline, e due copie rese una. I registri sono salvati nel PDF e non richiedono rete, quindi un tablet in cantiere può aggiungerli, rispondere e salvarli. Tornati in ufficio, Unisci unisce i registri di un'altra copia dello stesso PDF in base ai loro id, come un unico passo di annullamento. Vedere Salvare, lavorare offline e unire copie. Assistenti AI Più cose che un assistente può fare. Il proprio assistente, collegato tramite CadCraft Studio, può ora impostare lo stato di un'annotazione (markup_status, registrato nella sua discussione), rispondere in una discussione (markup_reply) ed esportare l'elenco Annotazioni come CSV o BCF (markups_export, ciò che l'elenco mostra con i suoi filtri). Ogni modifica è un passo di annullamento, firmato con il nome dell'assistente. Vedere Cosa può chiedere un assistente. In ogni app Unità. Impostazioni › Generali › Unità ha un elenco ciascuno per lunghezza, area, volume, angolo e pendenza, con le dimensioni esatte date dalle definizioni delle unità (il piede topografico statunitense è un'unità a sé). Disattivare le unità che non si usano mai ed escono dagli elenchi; contrassegnarne una con la stella e il nuovo lavoro parte con quella. Un file mantiene sempre la propria unità. In MarkupCraft, le unità di misura e di posizione in Impostazioni › MarkupCraft e l'unità nella finestra di calibrazione seguono gli interruttori, la stella imposta l'unità di misura finché non si salvano le impostazioni proprie di MarkupCraft, e la distanza reale digitata durante la calibrazione può portare il simbolo di qualsiasi unità, ad esempio 3 yd o 1.5 km. Vedere Unità, valori digitati ed elenchi delle unità. Una disposizione più densa e pannelli che si ripiegano. Impostazioni › Generali › Densità della disposizione › Compatta fa stare più cose sullo schermo (i touch screen e i telefoni mantengono i pulsanti grandi). Ogni pannello laterale ha una sottile barra del titolo: trascinarla per ancorare o rendere mobile il pannello, rilasciarla sulla barra di un altro pannello per condividere un riquadro come schede, fissarla perché scorra via sul bordo, oppure nasconderla e riportarla dal bordo. Vedere Disporre i pannelli. Le finestre di dialogo si spostano. Trascinare una finestra di dialogo dalla barra del titolo per toglierla di mezzo al disegno; ricompare dove è stata lasciata. Fare doppio clic sulla barra del titolo per centrarla di nuovo. Vedere Spostare una finestra di dialogo. Ricette per gli assistenti AI. Dieci richieste svolte per il proprio assistente (riordinare un disegno, stampare un set, verificare i disegni, interrogare un modello, totalizzare un computo, provare una variante di parte…), ciascuna con gli strumenti che chiama e i permessi che richiede, e ciascuna rieseguita dai nostri test. Le app non contengono AI. Vedere Ricette per gli assistenti AI. Lingue. Português (Portugal) è nuovo in Impostazioni › Generali › Lingua. Questa, Português (Brasil), Español (México) e Bahasa Indonesia hanno ora ogni parola delle app, come bozze non ancora riviste da un madrelingua. Gli argomenti della guida sono in inglese. CadCraft Studio dice perché un controllo degli aggiornamenti è fallito (nessun file per il proprio sistema, nessuna rete, firma non valida) invece di un semplice \"Fine\", e smette di riproporre una scheda Novità chiusa finché non c'è una versione più recente. Non ancora in questa versione La 0.7 non è firmata digitalmente. La firma e il pacchetto per il Microsoft Store sono passati alla 0.8 perché la convalida del certificato non è ancora arrivata. Verificare lo SHA-256 accanto a ogni file su getcadcraft.com/download. In Windows, se SmartScreen blocca il programma di installazione, scegliere Ulteriori informazioni, poi Esegui comunque. Su Mac, la prima volta, fare clic con il tasto destro su CadCraft Studio e scegliere Apri. CadCraft Studio non è ancora in uno store di app e non esiste ancora una versione per iPad o iPhone. Gli elenchi di angolo e pendenza in Unità vengono salvati, ma nessun campo li legge ancora. I registri di cantiere non sono condivisi in una sessione Live di CadCraft Studio (le sessioni Live trasportano solo le annotazioni); riunire le copie con Unisci. Unisci prende un registro per intero dalla copia modificata per ultima: se due persone hanno cambiato campi diversi dello stesso registro, prevalgono i campi della copia più recente. Le discussioni e i nomi degli allegati vengono uniti. Gli allegati sono solo nomi; i file restano dove sono. Non ci sono team, notifiche né progetti nel cloud per i registri, e gli assistenti non hanno ancora strumenti per i registri. I registri di cantiere sono stati provati in un browser con dimensioni da telefono e tablet con la rete interrotta, ma non ancora a mano su un vero tablet. Ancora dalla 0.6: ruotare gli oggetti della pagina di un angolo qualsiasi, disegnare nuove forme direttamente nella pagina, cambiare il carattere o la dimensione di una riga e il testo che scorre su più righe. Una copia salvata con modifiche alla pagina non è ancora stata aperta in Acrobat. Ancora dalla 0.5: annullare una modifica di pagina (come aggiungere una pagina vuota), leggere il testo dalle tavole scansionate e le sessioni Live oltre la propria rete. Tutto il resto è in Cosa non è ancora implementato.","lang":"it"},{"slug":"getting-started","scope":"markupcraft","title":"Primi passi con MarkupCraft","summary":"Aprire un set di tavole, mettere la prima nuvola e il primo callout su una tavola e salvare una copia annotata.","section":"start","order":10,"keywords":["inizio","primi passi","aprire pdf","revisione","annotazione","penna rossa","set di tavole","tutorial","guida rapida"],"headings":[{"id":"cosa-serve","text":"Cosa serve","level":2},{"id":"la-prima-revisione-in-dieci-passi","text":"La prima revisione in dieci passi","level":2},{"id":"online-e-offline","text":"Online e offline","level":2},{"id":"cosa-ricorda-l-app-e-cosa-no","text":"Cosa ricorda l'app e cosa no","level":2},{"id":"dove-andare-dopo","text":"Dove andare dopo","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EMarkupCraft serve a rivedere i disegni: si aprono set di tavole in PDF, si annotano con nuvole, callout, testo, timbri e misure, si tiene traccia dello stato di ogni commento e si salva una copia del PDF che qualsiasi visualizzatore PDF può mostrare.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFunziona nel browser. I PDF vengono aperti sul proprio computer e \u003Cstrong\u003Enon vengono mai caricati\u003C/strong\u003E. Salvare scarica una nuova copia; il file originale non viene modificato.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EMarkupCraft è in sviluppo. La guida descrive la versione in uso. Ciò che non fa ancora è elencato in \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/markupcraft/not-built-yet\" data-topic=\"markupcraft/not-built-yet\"\u003ECosa non è ancora implementato\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"cosa-serve\"\u003ECosa serve\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUna versione recente di Chrome, Edge, Firefox o Safari su un computer Mac, Windows o Linux, oppure Safari o Chrome su un iPad. Vedere \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/browsers\" data-topic=\"basics/browsers\"\u003EBrowser e computer\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUno o più file PDF. Un intero set di tavole può essere un solo PDF o molti.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUn mouse con rotellina è la soluzione più comoda. Funziona anche un trackpad, e anche un touch screen (vedere \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/markupcraft/review-on-a-tablet\" data-topic=\"markupcraft/review-on-a-tablet\"\u003ERivedere su iPad o touch screen\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"la-prima-revisione-in-dieci-passi\"\u003ELa prima revisione in dieci passi\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAprire MarkupCraft nel browser. Al centro della schermata compare &quot;Aprire un set di tavole&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EScegliere \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EApri PDF\u003C/span\u003E (oppure \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EApri\u003C/span\u003E all&#039;estremità sinistra della barra degli strumenti, oppure premere \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command O\"\u003E⌘O\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+O\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), selezionare uno o più PDF e confermare. È anche possibile trascinare i PDF da \u003Cspan class=\"hp\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hpv\" data-v=\"os:mac\" title=\"Mac\"\u003EFinder\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hp-sep\"\u003E / \u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hpv\" data-v=\"os:windows\" title=\"Windows\"\u003EEsplora file\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hp-sep\"\u003E / \u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hpv\" data-v=\"os:linux\" title=\"Linux\"\u003Eil file manager\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/span\u003E e rilasciarli sull&#039;area della tavola. La prima volta la barra di stato in basso indica &quot;Caricamento del lettore PDF…&quot;. Poi ogni pagina diventa una tavola nell&#039;elenco \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETavole\u003C/span\u003E a sinistra e la prima tavola riempie il centro.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFare clic su una tavola nell&#039;elenco per mostrarla. Girare la rotellina del mouse sulla tavola per ingrandire dove si trova il puntatore.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EScegliere \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENuvola\u003C/span\u003E nel gruppo \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERivedi\u003C/span\u003E della barra degli strumenti (oppure premere \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"C\"\u003EC\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EC\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E). La prima volta che si sceglie uno strumento di disegno, MarkupCraft chiede il nome. Digitarlo e scegliere \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESalva\u003C/span\u003E. Ogni annotazione lo registra. (Altro in \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/markupcraft/set-your-name\" data-topic=\"markupcraft/set-your-name\"\u003EImpostare il nome dell&#039;autore\u003C/a\u003E.)\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETrascinare un riquadro intorno all&#039;area da segnalare. Compare una nuvola di revisione rossa, già selezionata.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EScegliere \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECallout\u003C/span\u003E nel gruppo \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAnnota\u003C/span\u003E (oppure premere \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"K\"\u003EK\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EK\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E). Premere sull&#039;elemento indicato, trascinare dove deve andare il testo e rilasciare.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDigitare il commento nella casella che compare. Fare clic ovunque fuori dalla casella (oppure premere \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Return\"\u003E⌘ Return\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Enter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) per terminare.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPremere \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E due volte: la prima per annullare la selezione, la seconda per tornare allo strumento \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESeleziona\u003C/span\u003E. Fare clic sulla nuvola. La scheda \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProprietà\u003C/span\u003E a destra mostra chi l&#039;ha creata, quando e il suo \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStato\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EScegliere \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESalva\u003C/span\u003E nella barra degli strumenti (oppure premere \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E). Il browser scarica una copia con un nome del tipo &quot;A-Plans (marked up).pdf&quot;. La barra di stato indica quante annotazioni sono state scritte.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAprire quella copia in qualsiasi visualizzatore PDF per verificare: la nuvola e il callout ci sono, come normali commenti PDF con il proprio nome.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPer continuare a lavorare in seguito, aprire in MarkupCraft la copia &quot;(marked up)&quot;. Le annotazioni tornano modificabili, con stati, risposte e numeri di punch.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"online-e-offline\"\u003EOnline e offline\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"net:online\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EOnline\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESi è online. MarkupCraft carica il lettore PDF la prima volta che si apre un PDF e il programma di scrittura PDF la prima volta che si salva. Per lavorare in seguito senza connessione (in un sopralluogo, in aereo), scegliere ora \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESalva per l&#039;uso offline\u003C/span\u003E nella barra in alto e attendere &quot;Pronto per l&#039;uso offline&quot;. La copia comprende il lettore e il programma di scrittura PDF, quindi aprire e salvare PDF funziona poi offline. Vedere \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/offline\" data-topic=\"basics/offline\"\u003ELavorare offline\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"net:offline\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EOffline\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESi è offline. Se la barra in alto indica &quot;Pronto per l&#039;uso offline&quot;, MarkupCraft è stato salvato per l&#039;uso offline in questo browser e tutto funziona: aprire, annotare, misurare, salvare. In caso contrario, usare ciò che è già stato caricato in questa scheda e non ricaricare la pagina finché non si è di nuovo online. Vedere \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/offline\" data-topic=\"basics/offline\"\u003ELavorare offline\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"cosa-ricorda-l-app-e-cosa-no\"\u003ECosa ricorda l&#039;app e cosa no\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELe annotazioni vivono nel PDF salvato.\u003C/strong\u003E Nulla viene conservato su un server. L&#039;app non salva il PDF da sola, ma conserva una copia di ripristino in questo browser finché non lo si fa. Salvare prima di chiudere la scheda; il browser chiede conferma se si tenta di uscire con annotazioni non salvate (tranne su un iPad, vedere \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/markupcraft/review-on-a-tablet\" data-topic=\"markupcraft/review-on-a-tablet\"\u003ERivedere su iPad o touch screen\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EIl nome, gli stili degli strumenti e la cassetta degli strumenti\u003C/strong\u003E restano in questo browser su questo computer. Un altro browser o computer riparte da zero e la cancellazione dei dati del sito dal browser li rimuove. Vedere \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/markupcraft/browser-storage\" data-topic=\"markupcraft/browser-storage\"\u003ECosa resta in questo browser\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIl salvataggio nel cloud e le sessioni di revisione condivise tra persone non sono ancora disponibili.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"dove-andare-dopo\"\u003EDove andare dopo\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/markupcraft/workspace\" data-topic=\"markupcraft/workspace\"\u003EUn giro della schermata\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/markupcraft/mark-up-a-sheet\" data-topic=\"markupcraft/mark-up-a-sheet\"\u003EAnnotare una tavola\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/markupcraft/calibrate-and-measure\" data-topic=\"markupcraft/calibrate-and-measure\"\u003ECalibrare una tavola e misurare\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/markupcraft/coming-from\" data-topic=\"markupcraft/coming-from\"\u003ESe si proviene da altri software di annotazione\u003C/a\u003E, per chi rivede già i disegni con un altro programma.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"MarkupCraft serve a rivedere i disegni: si aprono set di tavole in PDF, si annotano con nuvole, callout, testo, timbri e misure, si tiene traccia dello stato di ogni commento e si salva una copia del PDF che qualsiasi visualizzatore PDF può mostrare. Funziona nel browser. I PDF vengono aperti sul proprio computer e non vengono mai caricati. Salvare scarica una nuova copia; il file originale non viene modificato. MarkupCraft è in sviluppo. La guida descrive la versione in uso. Ciò che non fa ancora è elencato in Cosa non è ancora implementato. Cosa serve Una versione recente di Chrome, Edge, Firefox o Safari su un computer Mac, Windows o Linux, oppure Safari o Chrome su un iPad. Vedere Browser e computer. Uno o più file PDF. Un intero set di tavole può essere un solo PDF o molti. Un mouse con rotellina è la soluzione più comoda. Funziona anche un trackpad, e anche un touch screen (vedere Rivedere su iPad o touch screen). La prima revisione in dieci passi Aprire MarkupCraft nel browser. Al centro della schermata compare \"Aprire un set di tavole\". Scegliere Apri PDF (oppure Apri all'estremità sinistra della barra degli strumenti, oppure premere Ctrl+O), selezionare uno o più PDF e confermare. È anche possibile trascinare i PDF da Finder / Esplora file / il file manager e rilasciarli sull'area della tavola. La prima volta la barra di stato in basso indica \"Caricamento del lettore PDF…\". Poi ogni pagina diventa una tavola nell'elenco Tavole a sinistra e la prima tavola riempie il centro. Fare clic su una tavola nell'elenco per mostrarla. Girare la rotellina del mouse sulla tavola per ingrandire dove si trova il puntatore. Scegliere Nuvola nel gruppo Rivedi della barra degli strumenti (oppure premere C). La prima volta che si sceglie uno strumento di disegno, MarkupCraft chiede il nome. Digitarlo e scegliere Salva. Ogni annotazione lo registra. (Altro in Impostare il nome dell'autore.) Trascinare un riquadro intorno all'area da segnalare. Compare una nuvola di revisione rossa, già selezionata. Scegliere Callout nel gruppo Annota (oppure premere K). Premere sull'elemento indicato, trascinare dove deve andare il testo e rilasciare. Digitare il commento nella casella che compare. Fare clic ovunque fuori dalla casella (oppure premere Ctrl+Enter) per terminare. Premere Esc due volte: la prima per annullare la selezione, la seconda per tornare allo strumento Seleziona. Fare clic sulla nuvola. La scheda Proprietà a destra mostra chi l'ha creata, quando e il suo Stato. Scegliere Salva nella barra degli strumenti (oppure premere Ctrl+S). Il browser scarica una copia con un nome del tipo \"A-Plans (marked up).pdf\". La barra di stato indica quante annotazioni sono state scritte. Aprire quella copia in qualsiasi visualizzatore PDF per verificare: la nuvola e il callout ci sono, come normali commenti PDF con il proprio nome. Per continuare a lavorare in seguito, aprire in MarkupCraft la copia \"(marked up)\". Le annotazioni tornano modificabili, con stati, risposte e numeri di punch. Online e offline Si è online. MarkupCraft carica il lettore PDF la prima volta che si apre un PDF e il programma di scrittura PDF la prima volta che si salva. Per lavorare in seguito senza connessione (in un sopralluogo, in aereo), scegliere ora Salva per l'uso offline nella barra in alto e attendere \"Pronto per l'uso offline\". La copia comprende il lettore e il programma di scrittura PDF, quindi aprire e salvare PDF funziona poi offline. Vedere Lavorare offline. Si è offline. Se la barra in alto indica \"Pronto per l'uso offline\", MarkupCraft è stato salvato per l'uso offline in questo browser e tutto funziona: aprire, annotare, misurare, salvare. In caso contrario, usare ciò che è già stato caricato in questa scheda e non ricaricare la pagina finché non si è di nuovo online. Vedere Lavorare offline. Cosa ricorda l'app e cosa no Le annotazioni vivono nel PDF salvato. Nulla viene conservato su un server. L'app non salva il PDF da sola, ma conserva una copia di ripristino in questo browser finché non lo si fa. Salvare prima di chiudere la scheda; il browser chiede conferma se si tenta di uscire con annotazioni non salvate (tranne su un iPad, vedere Rivedere su iPad o touch screen). Il nome, gli stili degli strumenti e la cassetta degli strumenti restano in questo browser su questo computer. Un altro browser o computer riparte da zero e la cancellazione dei dati del sito dal browser li rimuove. Vedere Cosa resta in questo browser. Il salvataggio nel cloud e le sessioni di revisione condivise tra persone non sono ancora disponibili. Dove andare dopo Un giro della schermata Annotare una tavola Calibrare una tavola e misurare Se si proviene da altri software di annotazione, per chi rivede già i disegni con un altro programma.","lang":"it"},{"slug":"workspace","scope":"markupcraft","title":"Un giro della schermata","summary":"La barra degli strumenti, l'elenco delle tavole, l'area della tavola, i cinque pannelli a destra e la barra di stato, con i nomi che hanno sullo schermo.","section":"start","order":20,"keywords":["schermata","disposizione","barra degli strumenti","pannelli","barra laterale","miniature","proprietà","barra di stato","interfaccia","schermo intero","tema","modalità scura"],"headings":[{"id":"la-barra-in-cima","text":"La barra in cima","level":2},{"id":"la-barra-degli-strumenti","text":"La barra degli strumenti","level":2},{"id":"l-elenco-delle-tavole-a-sinistra","text":"L'elenco delle tavole (a sinistra)","level":2},{"id":"l-area-della-tavola-al-centro","text":"L'area della tavola (al centro)","level":2},{"id":"i-pannelli-a-destra","text":"I pannelli (a destra)","level":2},{"id":"la-barra-di-stato-in-basso","text":"La barra di stato (in basso)","level":2},{"id":"su-uno-schermo-piccolo","text":"Su uno schermo piccolo","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EMarkupCraft riempie la finestra del browser. Dall&#039;alto in basso e da sinistra a destra:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"la-barra-in-cima\"\u003ELa barra in cima\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ELa barra sottile sopra tutto appartiene alla famiglia CadCraft, non a MarkupCraft stesso. Contiene:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eil nome dell&#039;app e la versione in uso,\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EInformazioni su MarkupCraft\u003C/span\u003E, che apre la pagina dell&#039;app sul sito,\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGuida\u003C/span\u003E, che apre questa guida in un pannello laterale (oppure premere \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F1\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E),\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESalva per l&#039;uso offline\u003C/span\u003E, che conserva MarkupCraft, il suo lettore e programma di scrittura PDF e questa guida in questo browser così si apre senza connessione; poi indica &quot;Pronto per l&#039;uso offline&quot; (vedere \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/offline\" data-topic=\"basics/offline\"\u003ELavorare offline\u003C/a\u003E),\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eil pulsante della modalità schermo (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESchermo intero\u003C/span\u003E), e\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EImpostazioni\u003C/span\u003E (oppure \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command ,\"\u003E⌘,\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+,\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), con una scheda \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMarkupCraft\u003C/span\u003E per le impostazioni proprie dell&#039;app e \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGenerali\u003C/span\u003E per quelle condivise da ogni app CadCraft (vedere \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/markupcraft/settings\" data-topic=\"markupcraft/settings\"\u003EImpostazioni di MarkupCraft\u003C/a\u003E), e\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECarta\u003C/span\u003E e \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModello\u003C/span\u003E, i temi chiaro e scuro. In \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModello\u003C/span\u003E le tavole sono disegnate chiaro su scuro; le annotazioni mantengono i propri colori.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIl pulsante della modalità schermo, o \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control Shift F\"\u003E⌃⇧F\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+F\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, scorre tre modalità: normale, schermo intero e schermo intero con le barre nascoste. Con le barre nascoste, spostare il puntatore sul bordo superiore dello schermo (o toccarlo) e le barre tornano. Questa scorciatoia usa il tasto Control su ogni computer, Mac compreso.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA schermo intero, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E fa anche uscire il browser dallo schermo intero. Se si voleva solo annullare un disegno, premere di nuovo \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control Shift F\"\u003E⌃⇧F\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+F\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E per tornare a schermo intero.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"la-barra-degli-strumenti\"\u003ELa barra degli strumenti\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESotto la barra in cima, gli strumenti sono raggruppati ed etichettati:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EGruppo\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EPulsanti\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFile\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EApri\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESalva\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EChiudi\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESeleziona\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESeleziona\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPan\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDisegna\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPenna\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEvidenzia\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELinea\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFreccia\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERettangolo\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEllisse\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPolilinea\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPoligono\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERivedi\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENuvola\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENuvola libera\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAnnota\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECallout\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETesto\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENota\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETimbro\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMisura\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECalibra\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELunghezza\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArea\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EConteggio\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EModifica\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAnnulla\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERipristina\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECopia\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EIncolla\u003C/span\u003E, e \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAltro\u003C/span\u003E: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETaglia\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECopia\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EIncolla\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EIncolla nella stessa posizione\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDuplica\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EElimina\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESeleziona tutto\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDeseleziona tutto\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ELo strumento in uso appare premuto. Tenere il puntatore su un pulsante per vedere il suo tasto (ad esempio &quot;Rettangolo (R)&quot;); il menu \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAltro\u003C/span\u003E elenca accanto a ogni comando il suo tasto. I pulsanti che al momento non possono agire sono attenuati: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECopia\u003C/span\u003E finché non si seleziona un&#039;annotazione, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EIncolla\u003C/span\u003E finché non si copia qualcosa. Un piccolo punto dopo \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESalva\u003C/span\u003E indica che alcune annotazioni non sono ancora salvate. L&#039;elenco completo dei tasti è in \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/markupcraft/keyboard\" data-topic=\"markupcraft/keyboard\"\u003ETastiera e mouse\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUno strumento resta attivo dopo l&#039;uso, così si possono disegnare più nuvole di seguito. Premere \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E (due volte se la nuova annotazione è ancora selezionata) o \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"V\"\u003EV\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EV\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E per tornare a \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESeleziona\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"l-elenco-delle-tavole-a-sinistra\"\u003EL&#039;elenco delle tavole (a sinistra)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETavole\u003C/span\u003E elenca ogni pagina di ogni PDF aperto. Ogni riga mostra una piccola immagine della tavola, il suo nome, il numero di tavola o il file e la pagina, e quante annotazioni ha. Il conteggio dopo il titolo è il numero di tavole.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFare clic su una riga per mostrare quella tavola.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDigitare in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECerca tavole\u003C/span\u003E per filtrare per nome di tavola, numero di tavola o nome del file. Premere \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E per aprire la prima corrispondenza.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EI nomi delle tavole provengono dal PDF quando li contiene. Vedere \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/markupcraft/open-a-plan-set\" data-topic=\"markupcraft/open-a-plan-set\"\u003EAprire un set di tavole e passare da una tavola all&#039;altra\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"l-area-della-tavola-al-centro\"\u003EL&#039;area della tavola (al centro)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ELa tavola su cui si lavora. Prima che sia aperto qualcosa mostra &quot;Aprire un set di tavole&quot; con un pulsante \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EApri PDF\u003C/span\u003E. Rilasciare qui i PDF in qualsiasi punto per aprirli.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESotto la tavola, una barra stretta contiene:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ele frecce di tavola precedente e successiva e &quot;3 di 12: A-101 — Pianta del piano&quot; (tenere il puntatore sopra per vedere il file e la pagina),\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eriduzione dello zoom, la casella dello zoom (digitare una percentuale e premere \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E; 100% è la dimensione reale su uno schermo tipico), aumento dello zoom,\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdatta alla pagina\u003C/span\u003E e \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdatta alla larghezza\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOgni tavola ricorda il proprio zoom finché il set è aperto.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EQuando si confrontano due tavole, qui compare una legenda: &quot;Solo sulla tavola vecchia&quot;, &quot;Solo sulla tavola nuova&quot; e &quot;Su entrambe&quot;. Vedere \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/markupcraft/compare-revisions\" data-topic=\"markupcraft/compare-revisions\"\u003EConfrontare due revisioni\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"i-pannelli-a-destra\"\u003EI pannelli (a destra)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECinque schede:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EScheda\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ECosa mostra\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProprietà\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EL&#039;annotazione selezionata: autore, date, valori misurati, stato, oggetto, testo, commento, stile, risposte e voce di punch. Con uno strumento di disegno attivo e nulla di selezionato, le impostazioni che useranno le nuove annotazioni. Con nulla di selezionato e \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESeleziona\u003C/span\u003E attivo, la tavola: numero, titolo, da dove è stato letto il nome, formato della carta, scala e \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERinomina tavola\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAnnotazioni\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOgni annotazione del set aperto, con filtri, ed esportazioni \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECSV\u003C/span\u003E e \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBCF\u003C/span\u003E. Vedere \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/markupcraft/markups-list-and-export\" data-topic=\"markupcraft/markups-list-and-export\"\u003EL&#039;elenco delle annotazioni\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPunti da completare\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EVoci di punch numerate con assegnatario, scadenza e stato. Vedere \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/markupcraft/punch-list\" data-topic=\"markupcraft/punch-list\"\u003ECreare un elenco punch\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStrumenti\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELa cassetta degli strumenti: strumenti salvati e con stile. Vedere \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/markupcraft/tool-chest\" data-topic=\"markupcraft/tool-chest\"\u003ELa cassetta degli strumenti\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EConfronta\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESovrapporre due tavole. Vedere \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/markupcraft/compare-revisions\" data-topic=\"markupcraft/compare-revisions\"\u003EConfrontare due revisioni\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"la-barra-di-stato-in-basso\"\u003ELa barra di stato (in basso)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA sinistra, dove si trova il puntatore sulla tavola, in pollici (o millimetri, in \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/markupcraft/settings\" data-topic=\"markupcraft/settings\"\u003EImpostazioni\u003C/a\u003E) sulla carta.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAl centro, i messaggi: cosa si aspetta uno strumento, cosa è stato appena salvato, perché qualcosa è stato rifiutato. \u003Cstrong\u003ELeggerla quando qualcosa non accade.\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIl pulsante della scala della tavola (&quot;Non calibrata&quot;, oppure la scala). Fare clic per impostare la scala. Vedere \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/markupcraft/calibrate-and-measure\" data-topic=\"markupcraft/calibrate-and-measure\"\u003ECalibrare una tavola e misurare\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIl nome (&quot;Impostare il nome&quot; finché non ne viene dato uno). Fare clic per cambiarlo.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"su-uno-schermo-piccolo\"\u003ESu uno schermo piccolo\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESotto circa 1180 pixel di larghezza i pannelli si restringono e le immagini delle tavole si riducono. Su uno schermo delle dimensioni di un telefono le parti si impilano: barra degli strumenti (da far scorrere di lato), la tavola, la barra di stato, i pannelli, poi l&#039;elenco delle tavole in fondo. Un tablet in orizzontale mantiene la disposizione completa.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"MarkupCraft riempie la finestra del browser. Dall'alto in basso e da sinistra a destra: La barra in cima La barra sottile sopra tutto appartiene alla famiglia CadCraft, non a MarkupCraft stesso. Contiene: il nome dell'app e la versione in uso, Informazioni su MarkupCraft, che apre la pagina dell'app sul sito, Guida, che apre questa guida in un pannello laterale (oppure premere F1), Salva per l'uso offline, che conserva MarkupCraft, il suo lettore e programma di scrittura PDF e questa guida in questo browser così si apre senza connessione; poi indica \"Pronto per l'uso offline\" (vedere Lavorare offline), il pulsante della modalità schermo (Schermo intero), e Impostazioni (oppure Ctrl+,), con una scheda MarkupCraft per le impostazioni proprie dell'app e Generali per quelle condivise da ogni app CadCraft (vedere Impostazioni di MarkupCraft), e Carta e Modello, i temi chiaro e scuro. In Modello le tavole sono disegnate chiaro su scuro; le annotazioni mantengono i propri colori. Il pulsante della modalità schermo, o Ctrl+Shift+F, scorre tre modalità: normale, schermo intero e schermo intero con le barre nascoste. Con le barre nascoste, spostare il puntatore sul bordo superiore dello schermo (o toccarlo) e le barre tornano. Questa scorciatoia usa il tasto Control su ogni computer, Mac compreso. A schermo intero, Esc fa anche uscire il browser dallo schermo intero. Se si voleva solo annullare un disegno, premere di nuovo Ctrl+Shift+F per tornare a schermo intero. La barra degli strumenti Sotto la barra in cima, gli strumenti sono raggruppati ed etichettati: Gruppo Pulsanti File Apri, Salva, Chiudi Seleziona Seleziona, Pan Disegna Penna, Evidenzia, Linea, Freccia, Rettangolo, Ellisse, Polilinea, Poligono Rivedi Nuvola, Nuvola libera Annota Callout, Testo, Nota, Timbro Misura Calibra, Lunghezza, Area, Conteggio Modifica Annulla, Ripristina, Copia, Incolla, e Altro: Taglia, Copia, Incolla, Incolla nella stessa posizione, Duplica, Elimina, Seleziona tutto, Deseleziona tutto Lo strumento in uso appare premuto. Tenere il puntatore su un pulsante per vedere il suo tasto (ad esempio \"Rettangolo (R)\"); il menu Altro elenca accanto a ogni comando il suo tasto. I pulsanti che al momento non possono agire sono attenuati: Copia finché non si seleziona un'annotazione, Incolla finché non si copia qualcosa. Un piccolo punto dopo Salva indica che alcune annotazioni non sono ancora salvate. L'elenco completo dei tasti è in Tastiera e mouse. Uno strumento resta attivo dopo l'uso, così si possono disegnare più nuvole di seguito. Premere Esc (due volte se la nuova annotazione è ancora selezionata) o V per tornare a Seleziona. L'elenco delle tavole (a sinistra) Tavole elenca ogni pagina di ogni PDF aperto. Ogni riga mostra una piccola immagine della tavola, il suo nome, il numero di tavola o il file e la pagina, e quante annotazioni ha. Il conteggio dopo il titolo è il numero di tavole. Fare clic su una riga per mostrare quella tavola. Digitare in Cerca tavole per filtrare per nome di tavola, numero di tavola o nome del file. Premere Enter per aprire la prima corrispondenza. I nomi delle tavole provengono dal PDF quando li contiene. Vedere Aprire un set di tavole e passare da una tavola all'altra. L'area della tavola (al centro) La tavola su cui si lavora. Prima che sia aperto qualcosa mostra \"Aprire un set di tavole\" con un pulsante Apri PDF. Rilasciare qui i PDF in qualsiasi punto per aprirli. Sotto la tavola, una barra stretta contiene: le frecce di tavola precedente e successiva e \"3 di 12: A-101 — Pianta del piano\" (tenere il puntatore sopra per vedere il file e la pagina), riduzione dello zoom, la casella dello zoom (digitare una percentuale e premere Enter; 100% è la dimensione reale su uno schermo tipico), aumento dello zoom, Adatta alla pagina e Adatta alla larghezza. Ogni tavola ricorda il proprio zoom finché il set è aperto. Quando si confrontano due tavole, qui compare una legenda: \"Solo sulla tavola vecchia\", \"Solo sulla tavola nuova\" e \"Su entrambe\". Vedere Confrontare due revisioni. I pannelli (a destra) Cinque schede: Scheda Cosa mostra Proprietà L'annotazione selezionata: autore, date, valori misurati, stato, oggetto, testo, commento, stile, risposte e voce di punch. Con uno strumento di disegno attivo e nulla di selezionato, le impostazioni che useranno le nuove annotazioni. Con nulla di selezionato e Seleziona attivo, la tavola: numero, titolo, da dove è stato letto il nome, formato della carta, scala e Rinomina tavola. Annotazioni Ogni annotazione del set aperto, con filtri, ed esportazioni CSV e BCF. Vedere L'elenco delle annotazioni. Punti da completare Voci di punch numerate con assegnatario, scadenza e stato. Vedere Creare un elenco punch. Strumenti La cassetta degli strumenti: strumenti salvati e con stile. Vedere La cassetta degli strumenti. Confronta Sovrapporre due tavole. Vedere Confrontare due revisioni. La barra di stato (in basso) A sinistra, dove si trova il puntatore sulla tavola, in pollici (o millimetri, in Impostazioni) sulla carta. Al centro, i messaggi: cosa si aspetta uno strumento, cosa è stato appena salvato, perché qualcosa è stato rifiutato. Leggerla quando qualcosa non accade. Il pulsante della scala della tavola (\"Non calibrata\", oppure la scala). Fare clic per impostare la scala. Vedere Calibrare una tavola e misurare. Il nome (\"Impostare il nome\" finché non ne viene dato uno). Fare clic per cambiarlo. Su uno schermo piccolo Sotto circa 1180 pixel di larghezza i pannelli si restringono e le immagini delle tavole si riducono. Su uno schermo delle dimensioni di un telefono le parti si impilano: barra degli strumenti (da far scorrere di lato), la tavola, la barra di stato, i pannelli, poi l'elenco delle tavole in fondo. Un tablet in orizzontale mantiene la disposizione completa.","lang":"it"},{"slug":"files","scope":"markupcraft","title":"I file che MarkupCraft apre e salva","summary":"PDF in ingresso, una copia PDF annotata in uscita, più file CSV, BCF e della cassetta degli strumenti; dove va ciascuno, online e offline.","section":"start","order":30,"keywords":["pdf","aprire","salvare","esportare","scaricare","csv","bcf","bcfzip","json","cassetta degli strumenti","tipi di file","formati","caricare","password","cifrato"],"headings":[{"id":"cosa-apre-markupcraft","text":"Cosa apre MarkupCraft","level":2},{"id":"annotazioni-gi-presenti-in-un-pdf","text":"Annotazioni già presenti in un PDF","level":3},{"id":"file-che-non-si-aprono","text":"File che non si aprono","level":3},{"id":"cosa-salva-markupcraft","text":"Cosa salva MarkupCraft","level":2},{"id":"dove-vanno-i-download","text":"Dove vanno i download","level":2},{"id":"online-e-offline","text":"Online e offline","level":2},{"id":"da-sapere","text":"Da sapere","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"cosa-apre-markupcraft\"\u003ECosa apre MarkupCraft\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EFile\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ECome\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ECosa succede\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPDF (\u003Ccode\u003E.pdf\u003C/code\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EApri\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command O\"\u003E⌘O\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+O\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, oppure rilasciare sull&#039;area della tavola. Più file insieme vanno bene.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOgni pagina diventa una tavola. Le annotazioni già presenti nel file vengono rilette (sotto).\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECassetta degli strumenti (\u003Ccode\u003E.json\u003C/code\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EScheda \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStrumenti\u003C/span\u003E, poi \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EImporta\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EGli strumenti vengono aggiunti alla cassetta. Vedere \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/markupcraft/tool-chest\" data-topic=\"markupcraft/tool-chest\"\u003ELa cassetta degli strumenti\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EQualsiasi altro file viene rifiutato con &quot;MarkupCraft apre file PDF.&quot; nella barra di stato. Se si rilascia un insieme misto, i PDF si aprono e la barra di stato indica quanti file non erano PDF.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"annotazioni-gi-presenti-in-un-pdf\"\u003EAnnotazioni già presenti in un PDF\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELe annotazioni salvate da MarkupCraft\u003C/strong\u003E tornano esattamente: tipo, stile, autore, date, stato, risposte, numero di punch, la scala della tavola e gli eventuali nomi di tavola digitati.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EI commenti di altri programmi PDF\u003C/strong\u003E vengono letti a loro volta. Rettangoli, ellissi, linee e frecce, polilinee, poligoni, tratti a mano libera (penna), caselle di testo e note adesive diventano normali annotazioni MarkupCraft modificabili. Anche le loro risposte e i loro stati di revisione vengono importati.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETutto il resto\u003C/strong\u003E (evidenziazioni di testo fatte selezionando il testo, timbri di altri programmi e altri tipi) viene mostrato com&#039;è, contrassegnato come sola lettura. Si può selezionare, vedere nell&#039;elenco delle annotazioni e rispondere, ma non spostare, modificare o eliminare.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EI collegamenti e i campi modulo vengono lasciati stare e non sono elencati.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUna scala salvata da un altro programma (un viewport di misura) viene usata come scala della tavola quando MarkupCraft ne comprende l&#039;unità.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"file-che-non-si-aprono\"\u003EFile che non si aprono\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUn PDF che richiede una password: &#039;&quot;nome&quot; richiede una password per essere aperto. Aprire una copia non protetta.&#039;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUn file danneggiato o che non è davvero un PDF: &#039;Impossibile leggere &quot;nome&quot; come PDF (…)&#039;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUn PDF che si apre ma è protetto da modifiche si apre normalmente. Il salvataggio viene rifiutato (vedere sotto).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EQuando si aprono più file insieme, quelli che funzionano si aprono e una finestra intitolata &quot;Alcuni file non si sono aperti&quot; elenca gli altri.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"cosa-salva-markupcraft\"\u003ECosa salva MarkupCraft\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIl salvataggio è sempre un \u003Cstrong\u003Edownload\u003C/strong\u003E di un nuovo file. Una pagina web non può sovrascrivere un file sul computer e MarkupCraft non modifica mai l&#039;originale.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EScelta\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ERisultato\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ENote\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESalva\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;&lt;nome&gt; (marked up).pdf&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUna copia del PDF originale con ogni annotazione scritta come vero commento PDF. Salvare di nuovo un file &quot;(marked up)&quot; mantiene lo stesso nome; non diventa mai &quot;(marked up) (marked up)&quot;. Vedere \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/markupcraft/save-the-marked-up-pdf\" data-topic=\"markupcraft/save-the-marked-up-pdf\"\u003ESalvare il PDF annotato\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EScheda \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAnnotazioni\u003C/span\u003E, poi \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECSV\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;&lt;nome&gt; markups.csv&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELe annotazioni come elencate (si applicano i filtri).\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EScheda \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAnnotazioni\u003C/span\u003E, poi \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBCF\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;&lt;nome&gt; markups.bcfzip&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBCF 2.1 per gli strumenti di coordinamento BIM, un&#039;istantanea per annotazione. Vedere \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/markupcraft/bcf-basics\" data-topic=\"markupcraft/bcf-basics\"\u003ENozioni di base su BCF\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EScheda \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPunti da completare\u003C/span\u003E, poi \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEsporta CSV\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;&lt;nome&gt; punch list.csv&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELe voci di punch in ordine di numero.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EScheda \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStrumenti\u003C/span\u003E, poi \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEsporta\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;MarkupCraft tool chest.json&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELa cassetta degli strumenti, da condividere o conservare.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E&quot;&lt;nome&gt;&quot; è il nome del primo PDF aperto senza &quot;.pdf&quot;.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EI file CSV si aprono in qualsiasi foglio di calcolo. Sono in UTF-8 con il marcatore che i fogli di calcolo cercano, così i nomi con accenti vengono resi correttamente. Una cella che inizia con =, +, - o @ riceve un apostrofo iniziale, così un foglio di calcolo non la esegue mai come formula.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"dove-vanno-i-download\"\u003EDove vanno i download\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDecide il browser: di solito la cartella Download, oppure chiede ogni volta se è impostato così. Vedere \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/browsers#what-differs-between-browsers\" data-topic=\"basics/browsers\" data-hash=\"what-differs-between-browsers\"\u003EBrowser e computer\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:chromium\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EChrome or Edge\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EQuando si salvano insieme tutti i file modificati di un set, Chrome ed Edge possono chiedere se questo sito può scaricare più file. Scegliere \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EConsenti\u003C/span\u003E, altrimenti si ottiene solo la prima copia.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:safari\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003ESafari\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESu un iPad, Safari chiede prima di ogni download e mette il file nell&#039;app File, in Download. Su un Mac va nella cartella impostata nelle impostazioni di Safari.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"online-e-offline\"\u003EOnline e offline\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EI PDF non lasciano mai questo computer\u003C/strong\u003E, online o offline. Non c&#039;è alcun caricamento da nessuna parte.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIl lettore PDF viene caricato la prima volta che si apre un PDF (&quot;Caricamento del lettore PDF…&quot;) e il programma di scrittura PDF la prima volta che si salva (&quot;Caricamento del programma di scrittura PDF…&quot;) o si apre un file che contiene già commenti.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ECon una connessione, entrambi si caricano dal sito.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPer lavorare senza connessione,\u003C/strong\u003E scegliere \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESalva per l&#039;uso offline\u003C/span\u003E nella barra in alto mentre si è online. Il pulsante conta &quot;Salvataggio di N su M&quot; e poi indica &quot;Pronto per l&#039;uso offline&quot;. Quella copia comprende il lettore PDF e il programma di scrittura PDF (circa 6 MB in tutto) e questa guida, quindi in seguito aprire, annotare e salvare PDF funziona offline in questo browser.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESe il pulsante indica &quot;Salvataggio non riuscito&quot;, alcuni file non sono stati scaricati: controllare la connessione e fare di nuovo clic. Se indica &quot;Offline non disponibile&quot;, questa finestra del browser non può conservare una copia offline (finestre private e alcuni computer gestiti); usare una finestra normale.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPer aggiornare o eliminare la copia, fare clic su &quot;Pronto per l&#039;uso offline&quot; e scegliere &quot;Salva di nuovo (scarica la versione più recente)&quot; o &quot;Rimuovi la copia offline&quot;. Altro in \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/offline\" data-topic=\"basics/offline\"\u003ELavorare offline\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"net:offline\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EOffline\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESi è offline. Se la barra in alto indica &quot;Pronto per l&#039;uso offline&quot;, tutto funziona, salvataggio compreso. In caso contrario e se in questa scheda non si è ancora salvato, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESalva\u003C/span\u003E può fallire con &quot;Impossibile caricare il programma di scrittura PDF&quot;. Le annotazioni sono ancora nella scheda: tenerla aperta, tornare online e scegliere di nuovo \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESalva\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"da-sapere\"\u003EDa sapere\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EI set di tavole grandi richiedono tempo per aprirsi: MarkupCraft legge il testo di ogni pagina per trovare i numeri di tavola. La barra di stato conta le tavole man mano.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAprire due volte lo stesso PDF aggiunge due volte le sue tavole. In tal caso chiudere il set e riaprirlo.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EChiudi\u003C/span\u003E chiude tutti i file aperti insieme. Se alcuni hanno annotazioni non salvate, chiede: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAnnulla\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EChiudi senza salvare\u003C/span\u003E o \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESalva prima le copie\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIl salvataggio nel cloud non è ancora disponibile e non esistono ancora versioni desktop. Conservare i PDF con gli altri file di progetto ed eseguirne il backup come di consueto. Vedere \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/your-files\" data-topic=\"basics/your-files\"\u003EDove vanno i file\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"Cosa apre MarkupCraft File Come Cosa succede PDF (.pdf) Apri, Ctrl+O, oppure rilasciare sull'area della tavola. Più file insieme vanno bene. Ogni pagina diventa una tavola. Le annotazioni già presenti nel file vengono rilette (sotto). Cassetta degli strumenti (.json) Scheda Strumenti, poi Importa Gli strumenti vengono aggiunti alla cassetta. Vedere La cassetta degli strumenti. Qualsiasi altro file viene rifiutato con \"MarkupCraft apre file PDF.\" nella barra di stato. Se si rilascia un insieme misto, i PDF si aprono e la barra di stato indica quanti file non erano PDF. Annotazioni già presenti in un PDF Le annotazioni salvate da MarkupCraft tornano esattamente: tipo, stile, autore, date, stato, risposte, numero di punch, la scala della tavola e gli eventuali nomi di tavola digitati. I commenti di altri programmi PDF vengono letti a loro volta. Rettangoli, ellissi, linee e frecce, polilinee, poligoni, tratti a mano libera (penna), caselle di testo e note adesive diventano normali annotazioni MarkupCraft modificabili. Anche le loro risposte e i loro stati di revisione vengono importati. Tutto il resto (evidenziazioni di testo fatte selezionando il testo, timbri di altri programmi e altri tipi) viene mostrato com'è, contrassegnato come sola lettura. Si può selezionare, vedere nell'elenco delle annotazioni e rispondere, ma non spostare, modificare o eliminare. I collegamenti e i campi modulo vengono lasciati stare e non sono elencati. Una scala salvata da un altro programma (un viewport di misura) viene usata come scala della tavola quando MarkupCraft ne comprende l'unità. File che non si aprono Un PDF che richiede una password: '\"nome\" richiede una password per essere aperto. Aprire una copia non protetta.' Un file danneggiato o che non è davvero un PDF: 'Impossibile leggere \"nome\" come PDF (…)'. Un PDF che si apre ma è protetto da modifiche si apre normalmente. Il salvataggio viene rifiutato (vedere sotto). Quando si aprono più file insieme, quelli che funzionano si aprono e una finestra intitolata \"Alcuni file non si sono aperti\" elenca gli altri. Cosa salva MarkupCraft Il salvataggio è sempre un download di un nuovo file. Una pagina web non può sovrascrivere un file sul computer e MarkupCraft non modifica mai l'originale. Scelta Risultato Note Salva (Ctrl+S) \"\u003Cnome\u003E (marked up).pdf\" Una copia del PDF originale con ogni annotazione scritta come vero commento PDF. Salvare di nuovo un file \"(marked up)\" mantiene lo stesso nome; non diventa mai \"(marked up) (marked up)\". Vedere Salvare il PDF annotato. Scheda Annotazioni, poi CSV \"\u003Cnome\u003E markups.csv\" Le annotazioni come elencate (si applicano i filtri). Scheda Annotazioni, poi BCF \"\u003Cnome\u003E markups.bcfzip\" BCF 2.1 per gli strumenti di coordinamento BIM, un'istantanea per annotazione. Vedere Nozioni di base su BCF. Scheda Punti da completare, poi Esporta CSV \"\u003Cnome\u003E punch list.csv\" Le voci di punch in ordine di numero. Scheda Strumenti, poi Esporta \"MarkupCraft tool chest.json\" La cassetta degli strumenti, da condividere o conservare. \"\u003Cnome\u003E\" è il nome del primo PDF aperto senza \".pdf\". I file CSV si aprono in qualsiasi foglio di calcolo. Sono in UTF-8 con il marcatore che i fogli di calcolo cercano, così i nomi con accenti vengono resi correttamente. Una cella che inizia con =, +, - o @ riceve un apostrofo iniziale, così un foglio di calcolo non la esegue mai come formula. Dove vanno i download Decide il browser: di solito la cartella Download, oppure chiede ogni volta se è impostato così. Vedere Browser e computer. Quando si salvano insieme tutti i file modificati di un set, Chrome ed Edge possono chiedere se questo sito può scaricare più file. Scegliere Consenti, altrimenti si ottiene solo la prima copia. Su un iPad, Safari chiede prima di ogni download e mette il file nell'app File, in Download. Su un Mac va nella cartella impostata nelle impostazioni di Safari. Online e offline I PDF non lasciano mai questo computer, online o offline. Non c'è alcun caricamento da nessuna parte. Il lettore PDF viene caricato la prima volta che si apre un PDF (\"Caricamento del lettore PDF…\") e il programma di scrittura PDF la prima volta che si salva (\"Caricamento del programma di scrittura PDF…\") o si apre un file che contiene già commenti. Con una connessione, entrambi si caricano dal sito. Per lavorare senza connessione, scegliere Salva per l'uso offline nella barra in alto mentre si è online. Il pulsante conta \"Salvataggio di N su M\" e poi indica \"Pronto per l'uso offline\". Quella copia comprende il lettore PDF e il programma di scrittura PDF (circa 6 MB in tutto) e questa guida, quindi in seguito aprire, annotare e salvare PDF funziona offline in questo browser. Se il pulsante indica \"Salvataggio non riuscito\", alcuni file non sono stati scaricati: controllare la connessione e fare di nuovo clic. Se indica \"Offline non disponibile\", questa finestra del browser non può conservare una copia offline (finestre private e alcuni computer gestiti); usare una finestra normale. Per aggiornare o eliminare la copia, fare clic su \"Pronto per l'uso offline\" e scegliere \"Salva di nuovo (scarica la versione più recente)\" o \"Rimuovi la copia offline\". Altro in Lavorare offline. Si è offline. Se la barra in alto indica \"Pronto per l'uso offline\", tutto funziona, salvataggio compreso. In caso contrario e se in questa scheda non si è ancora salvato, Salva può fallire con \"Impossibile caricare il programma di scrittura PDF\". Le annotazioni sono ancora nella scheda: tenerla aperta, tornare online e scegliere di nuovo Salva. Da sapere I set di tavole grandi richiedono tempo per aprirsi: MarkupCraft legge il testo di ogni pagina per trovare i numeri di tavola. La barra di stato conta le tavole man mano. Aprire due volte lo stesso PDF aggiunge due volte le sue tavole. In tal caso chiudere il set e riaprirlo. Chiudi chiude tutti i file aperti insieme. Se alcuni hanno annotazioni non salvate, chiede: Annulla, Chiudi senza salvare o Salva prima le copie. Il salvataggio nel cloud non è ancora disponibile e non esistono ancora versioni desktop. Conservare i PDF con gli altri file di progetto ed eseguirne il backup come di consueto. Vedere Dove vanno i file.","lang":"it"},{"slug":"open-a-plan-set","scope":"markupcraft","title":"Open a plan set and move between sheets","summary":"Open one PDF or many, or start with blank pages, find sheets by number or name, zoom and pan, and rename a sheet when the file gets it wrong.","section":"tasks","order":10,"keywords":["open","new","blank page","blank sheet","sketch","grid","untitled","plan set","sheets","pages","thumbnails","page labels","sheet number","title block","rename","navigate","zoom","pan","fit","search sheets"],"headings":[{"id":"open-the-set","text":"Open the set","level":2},{"id":"start-with-blank-pages","text":"Start with blank pages","level":2},{"id":"how-sheets-get-their-names","text":"How sheets get their names","level":2},{"id":"move-between-sheets","text":"Move between sheets","level":2},{"id":"zoom-and-pan","text":"Zoom and pan","level":2},{"id":"bookmarks","text":"Bookmarks","level":2},{"id":"turn-the-view","text":"Turn the view","level":2},{"id":"rename-a-sheet","text":"Rename a sheet","level":2},{"id":"take-the-pdf-to-takeoffcraft-or-bring-it-here","text":"Take the PDF to TakeoffCraft, or bring it here","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"open-the-set\"\u003EOpen the set\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen\u003C/span\u003E in the toolbar, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command O\"\u003E⌘O\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+O\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn the file picker, select every PDF of the set (\u003Cspan class=\"hp\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hpv\" data-v=\"os:mac\" title=\"Mac\"\u003Ehold ⌘ and click each file\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hp-sep\"\u003E / \u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hpv\" data-v=\"os:windows,linux\" title=\"Windows or Linux\"\u003Ehold Ctrl and click each file\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/span\u003E, or select a range with Shift), then confirm. Or drag the files from \u003Cspan class=\"hp\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hpv\" data-v=\"os:mac\" title=\"Mac\"\u003EFinder\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hp-sep\"\u003E / \u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hpv\" data-v=\"os:windows\" title=\"Windows\"\u003EFile Explorer\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hp-sep\"\u003E / \u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hpv\" data-v=\"os:linux\" title=\"Linux\"\u003Eyour file manager\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/span\u003E onto the sheet area. &quot;Drop PDFs to open them&quot; appears while you drag.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWait while the status bar says &quot;Opening &lt;file&gt;…&quot;. For big files it counts: &quot;(10 of 60 sheets)&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EResult: the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESheets\u003C/span\u003E list shows every page of every file, in the order you opened them, and the status bar says something like &quot;Opened 3 files (42 sheets in the set).&quot; If the files already had comments, it adds how many were read.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo add more files to the set later, open them the same way. They join the end of the list.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"start-with-blank-pages\"\u003EStart with blank pages\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo sketch or take notes without a drawing, make blank pages instead of opening a PDF:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew\u003C/span\u003E in the toolbar, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStart with a blank page\u003C/span\u003E on the start screen, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command N\"\u003E⌘N\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+N\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E where your browser lets the page have it (the desktop app and Studio always do).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPick the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaper size\u003C/span\u003E: Letter, Tabloid or ANSI B, ARCH D, ARCH E, A4, A3 or A1, and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPortrait\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELandscape\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPick a \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGrid\u003C/span\u003E: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENone\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDots\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESquares\u003C/span\u003E. The grid is every 1/4 in with a heavier line each inch on inch paper, and every 5 mm with a heavier line each 10 mm on metric paper. It is printed on the page.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType how many \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPages\u003C/span\u003E you want, from 1 to 20, then choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECreate\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EResult: a new PDF named &quot;Untitled-1.pdf&quot; (then &quot;Untitled-2.pdf&quot;, and so on) opens like any other file, made here on this computer. Every tool works on it, and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave\u003C/span\u003E downloads &quot;Untitled-1 (marked up).pdf&quot;. MarkupCraft remembers your choices for next time.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe pages have no scale: the status bar says &quot;Not calibrated&quot;. The grid is not a scale. To measure, calibrate the sheet or pick a scale first; see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/markupcraft/calibrate-and-measure\" data-topic=\"markupcraft/calibrate-and-measure\"\u003ECalibrate and measure\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo add a page to a blank set, open one of its sheets and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd a blank page\u003C/span\u003E under the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESheets\u003C/span\u003E list. The new page has the same paper and grid and goes at the end. Like every page change, it cannot be undone; see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/markupcraft/pages-and-printing\" data-topic=\"markupcraft/pages-and-printing\"\u003EPrint, and change the pages\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"how-sheets-get-their-names\"\u003EHow sheets get their names\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EMarkupCraft names each sheet &quot;&lt;sheet number&gt; — &lt;title&gt;&quot;, for example &quot;A-101 — Floor plan&quot;, from the first of these it finds:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA name you typed with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERename sheet\u003C/span\u003E (below).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe PDF&#039;s own page labels, when the person who made the PDF set them (for example &quot;A-101&quot;).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe title block: MarkupCraft reads the page&#039;s text and looks for something shaped like a sheet number (A-101, A1.01, M-201A, KA1-1), favoring the lower-right corner, the right-hand strip and the bottom band, and takes the largest wording near it as the title.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOtherwise, the file name and page: &quot;Plans — page 3&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo see which one was used, press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E until nothing is selected and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E is on, then look at \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E: &quot;Read from&quot; says &quot;The page label&quot;, &quot;The title block&quot;, &quot;The file name&quot; or &quot;Typed by hand&quot;.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EScanned sheets, and PDFs whose lettering was exported as lines, have no text to read. They are named by file and page until you rename them. MarkupCraft cannot read text from images yet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"move-between-sheets\"\u003EMove between sheets\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick a sheet in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESheets\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn ↓\"\u003Efn ↓\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EPage Down\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E for the next sheet and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn ↑\"\u003Efn ↑\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EPage Up\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E for the previous one, or use the arrows in the bar under the sheet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESearch sheets\u003C/span\u003E: part of a number (&quot;A-2&quot;), a word of the title (&quot;roof&quot;), or a file name. The list narrows as you type (&quot;4 of 42&quot;). Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to open the first match. Clear the box to see every sheet again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach sheet keeps its own zoom and position until you close the set.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"zoom-and-pan\"\u003EZoom and pan\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"input:mouse\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EMouse\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EZoom:\u003C/strong\u003E turn the wheel over the sheet. It zooms around the pointer. Or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"+\"\u003E+\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E+\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"−\"\u003E−\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E−\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E (also with \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command\"\u003E⌘\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), or use the zoom buttons. Type an exact percentage in the zoom box.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFit:\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFit page\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command 0\"\u003E⌘0\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+0\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) shows the whole sheet; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFit width\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command 9\"\u003E⌘9\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+9\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) fills the width.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPan:\u003C/strong\u003E drag with the middle button (the wheel), or drag with the right button, or hold \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and drag, or choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPan\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"H\"\u003EH\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EH\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) and drag. With nothing selected, the arrow keys move the sheet too.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"os:mac;input:mouse\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EMac, Mouse\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn a Mac trackpad, two-finger scrolling zooms (it does not scroll the sheet). To pan, hold \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and drag with one finger, or switch to \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPan\u003C/span\u003E with \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"H\"\u003EH\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EH\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. A pinch zooms in Chrome, Edge and Firefox.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"input:touch\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003ETouch\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EZoom and pan:\u003C/strong\u003E put two fingers on the sheet. Spread or pinch them to zoom around the point between them, and move them together to pan. Both happen at once.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPan with one finger:\u003C/strong\u003E choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPan\u003C/span\u003E first. With any other tool, one finger draws or selects.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFit page\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFit width\u003C/span\u003E are in the bar under the sheet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou can zoom out well past the whole sheet and in up to 6400%. The sheet is redrawn sharp a moment after you stop moving.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"bookmarks\"\u003EBookmarks\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBookmarks\u003C/span\u003E, at the top of the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESheets\u003C/span\u003E panel, shows the bookmarks (outline) of the PDF the open sheet belongs to. Choose one to go to its page. The pencil renames a bookmark and the cross removes it. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBuild from sheets\u003C/span\u003E makes one bookmark per sheet, named as the sheets list shows them (sheet number and title), and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd for this sheet\u003C/span\u003E adds one for the sheet you are on. Changes are part of the file&#039;s unsaved work: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave\u003C/span\u003E writes them into the copy, where every PDF viewer shows them.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"turn-the-view\"\u003ETurn the view\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe two turn buttons at the right of the bar under the sheet turn the view a quarter turn either way, for a sheet drawn sideways. Only the view turns: the PDF and its markups stay as they are, and the next sheet opens the way its PDF is (each sheet remembers its own turn while the set is open). Text you add on a turned view reads upright as you see it. To turn the page itself in the saved PDF, use \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPages\u003C/span\u003E, then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERotate page clockwise\u003C/span\u003E; see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/markupcraft/pages-and-printing\" data-topic=\"markupcraft/pages-and-printing\"\u003EPrint, and change the pages\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"rename-a-sheet\"\u003ERename a sheet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen a sheet&#039;s name is wrong or missing:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EShow the sheet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E until nothing is selected and the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E tool is on. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E now shows the sheet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERename sheet\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESheet number\u003C/span\u003E and the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETitle\u003C/span\u003E, then choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EResult: the sheets list, the bar under the sheet and the markups list all use the new name, and &quot;Read from&quot; says &quot;Typed by hand&quot;. Renaming can be undone with \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo go back to what the file says, open \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERename sheet\u003C/span\u003E again, clear both boxes, and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen you save, a typed sheet number becomes the page&#039;s label in the PDF, so other PDF viewers show &quot;A-101&quot; in their page box too. If you give any page of a file a number, every page of that file gets a label in the copy (its typed number, else its old label, else its page number). A typed title is kept for MarkupCraft but other viewers do not show it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"take-the-pdf-to-takeoffcraft-or-bring-it-here\"\u003ETake the PDF to TakeoffCraft, or bring it here\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen TakeoffCraft is open in the same place (the lab, Try or the online apps), the bar under the sheet has \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen in TakeoffCraft\u003C/span\u003E. It opens the sheet&#039;s PDF in TakeoffCraft in a new tab, as it was opened here, so you can measure quantities on it. Your markups stay here; save them as usual.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETakeoffCraft&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMarkupCraft\u003C/span\u003E button does the same the other way: the PDF opens here as if you had chosen \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen\u003C/span\u003E. If the browser blocks the new tab, allow pop-ups for this site and choose the button again.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Open the set Choose Open in the toolbar, or press Ctrl+O. In the file picker, select every PDF of the set (hold ⌘ and click each file / hold Ctrl and click each file, or select a range with Shift), then confirm. Or drag the files from Finder / File Explorer / your file manager onto the sheet area. \"Drop PDFs to open them\" appears while you drag. Wait while the status bar says \"Opening \u003Cfile\u003E…\". For big files it counts: \"(10 of 60 sheets)\". Result: the Sheets list shows every page of every file, in the order you opened them, and the status bar says something like \"Opened 3 files (42 sheets in the set).\" If the files already had comments, it adds how many were read. To add more files to the set later, open them the same way. They join the end of the list. Start with blank pages To sketch or take notes without a drawing, make blank pages instead of opening a PDF: Choose New in the toolbar, or Start with a blank page on the start screen, or press Ctrl+N where your browser lets the page have it (the desktop app and Studio always do). Pick the Paper size: Letter, Tabloid or ANSI B, ARCH D, ARCH E, A4, A3 or A1, and Portrait or Landscape. Pick a Grid: None, Dots or Squares. The grid is every 1/4 in with a heavier line each inch on inch paper, and every 5 mm with a heavier line each 10 mm on metric paper. It is printed on the page. Type how many Pages you want, from 1 to 20, then choose Create. Result: a new PDF named \"Untitled-1.pdf\" (then \"Untitled-2.pdf\", and so on) opens like any other file, made here on this computer. Every tool works on it, and Save downloads \"Untitled-1 (marked up).pdf\". MarkupCraft remembers your choices for next time. The pages have no scale: the status bar says \"Not calibrated\". The grid is not a scale. To measure, calibrate the sheet or pick a scale first; see Calibrate and measure. To add a page to a blank set, open one of its sheets and choose Add a blank page under the Sheets list. The new page has the same paper and grid and goes at the end. Like every page change, it cannot be undone; see Print, and change the pages. How sheets get their names MarkupCraft names each sheet \"\u003Csheet number\u003E — \u003Ctitle\u003E\", for example \"A-101 — Floor plan\", from the first of these it finds: A name you typed with Rename sheet (below). The PDF's own page labels, when the person who made the PDF set them (for example \"A-101\"). The title block: MarkupCraft reads the page's text and looks for something shaped like a sheet number (A-101, A1.01, M-201A, KA1-1), favoring the lower-right corner, the right-hand strip and the bottom band, and takes the largest wording near it as the title. Otherwise, the file name and page: \"Plans — page 3\". To see which one was used, press Esc until nothing is selected and Select is on, then look at Properties: \"Read from\" says \"The page label\", \"The title block\", \"The file name\" or \"Typed by hand\". Scanned sheets, and PDFs whose lettering was exported as lines, have no text to read. They are named by file and page until you rename them. MarkupCraft cannot read text from images yet. Move between sheets Click a sheet in Sheets. Press Page Down for the next sheet and Page Up for the previous one, or use the arrows in the bar under the sheet. Type in Search sheets: part of a number (\"A-2\"), a word of the title (\"roof\"), or a file name. The list narrows as you type (\"4 of 42\"). Press Enter to open the first match. Clear the box to see every sheet again. Each sheet keeps its own zoom and position until you close the set. Zoom and pan Zoom: turn the wheel over the sheet. It zooms around the pointer. Or press + and − (also with Ctrl), or use the zoom buttons. Type an exact percentage in the zoom box. Fit: Fit page (Ctrl+0) shows the whole sheet; Fit width (Ctrl+9) fills the width. Pan: drag with the middle button (the wheel), or drag with the right button, or hold Space and drag, or choose Pan (H) and drag. With nothing selected, the arrow keys move the sheet too. On a Mac trackpad, two-finger scrolling zooms (it does not scroll the sheet). To pan, hold Space and drag with one finger, or switch to Pan with H. A pinch zooms in Chrome, Edge and Firefox. Zoom and pan: put two fingers on the sheet. Spread or pinch them to zoom around the point between them, and move them together to pan. Both happen at once. Pan with one finger: choose Pan first. With any other tool, one finger draws or selects. Fit page and Fit width are in the bar under the sheet. You can zoom out well past the whole sheet and in up to 6400%. The sheet is redrawn sharp a moment after you stop moving. Bookmarks Bookmarks, at the top of the Sheets panel, shows the bookmarks (outline) of the PDF the open sheet belongs to. Choose one to go to its page. The pencil renames a bookmark and the cross removes it. Build from sheets makes one bookmark per sheet, named as the sheets list shows them (sheet number and title), and Add for this sheet adds one for the sheet you are on. Changes are part of the file's unsaved work: Save writes them into the copy, where every PDF viewer shows them. Turn the view The two turn buttons at the right of the bar under the sheet turn the view a quarter turn either way, for a sheet drawn sideways. Only the view turns: the PDF and its markups stay as they are, and the next sheet opens the way its PDF is (each sheet remembers its own turn while the set is open). Text you add on a turned view reads upright as you see it. To turn the page itself in the saved PDF, use Pages, then Rotate page clockwise; see Print, and change the pages. Rename a sheet When a sheet's name is wrong or missing: Show the sheet. Press Esc until nothing is selected and the Select tool is on. Properties now shows the sheet. Choose Rename sheet. Type the Sheet number and the Title, then choose Save. Result: the sheets list, the bar under the sheet and the markups list all use the new name, and \"Read from\" says \"Typed by hand\". Renaming can be undone with Ctrl+Z. To go back to what the file says, open Rename sheet again, clear both boxes, and choose Save. When you save, a typed sheet number becomes the page's label in the PDF, so other PDF viewers show \"A-101\" in their page box too. If you give any page of a file a number, every page of that file gets a label in the copy (its typed number, else its old label, else its page number). A typed title is kept for MarkupCraft but other viewers do not show it. Take the PDF to TakeoffCraft, or bring it here When TakeoffCraft is open in the same place (the lab, Try or the online apps), the bar under the sheet has Open in TakeoffCraft. It opens the sheet's PDF in TakeoffCraft in a new tab, as it was opened here, so you can measure quantities on it. Your markups stay here; save them as usual. TakeoffCraft's MarkupCraft button does the same the other way: the PDF opens here as if you had chosen Open. If the browser blocks the new tab, allow pop-ups for this site and choose the button again.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"set-your-name","scope":"markupcraft","title":"Set your author name","summary":"Give the name that goes on every markup, reply and stamp, or use your account's name, and know where it is kept.","section":"tasks","order":20,"keywords":["author","name","account name","signed in","user name","initials","identity","who made it","stamp name","reviewer"],"headings":[{"id":"signed-in-your-account-s-name","text":"Signed in: your account's name","level":2},{"id":"the-first-time-signed-out","text":"The first time (signed out)","level":2},{"id":"change-it","text":"Change it","level":2},{"id":"where-the-name-is-kept","text":"Where the name is kept","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EEvery markup records who made it and when. Stamps print the name (&quot;By Ana Ruiz, Oct 10, 2026&quot;), replies show it, the markups list can filter by it, and other PDF viewers show it as the comment&#039;s author.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"signed-in-your-account-s-name\"\u003ESigned in: your account&#039;s name\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf you are signed in to your CadCraft account, MarkupCraft uses your account&#039;s name and never asks. Markups, replies, stamps and the status bar show it. In \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMarkupCraft\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EYour name\u003C/span\u003E it reads &quot;From your account (your name)&quot;.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETo use another name, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUse another name\u003C/span\u003E and type it. A name you type wins from then on.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETo go back to your account&#039;s name, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUse my account name\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOnly the name is used, never your email address. If you change your name on the site, MarkupCraft shows it the next time it opens.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-first-time-signed-out\"\u003EThe first time (signed out)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen you are not signed in, the first time you pick a drawing tool, a box called &quot;Your name on markups&quot; opens.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType your name the way you want reviewers to see it (up to 80 characters).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EResult: your name appears at the right end of the status bar, and every new markup carries it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf you choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENot now\u003C/span\u003E, markups are made with no author (&quot;Not recorded&quot; in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E) and stamps show only the date. MarkupCraft asks again the next time you open it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"change-it\"\u003EChange it\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick your name (or &quot;Set your name&quot;) at the right end of the status bar.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType the new name and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOr open \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command ,\"\u003E⌘,\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+,\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), stay on the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMarkupCraft\u003C/span\u003E tab and type it under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EYour name\u003C/span\u003E. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/markupcraft/settings\" data-topic=\"markupcraft/settings\"\u003EMarkupCraft settings\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOnly markups you make from now on get the new name. Markups already drawn keep the name they were made with; there is no way to change the author of an existing markup.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"where-the-name-is-kept\"\u003EWhere the name is kept\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYour name is stored \u003Cstrong\u003Ein this browser, on this computer\u003C/strong\u003E (the browser&#039;s local storage for this website). That means:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAnother browser on the same computer, or the same browser on another computer, asks again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA private or incognito window asks every time, and forgets when it closes.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClearing this site&#039;s data (cookies and site data) in the browser&#039;s settings forgets it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ENothing is sent anywhere. The name only leaves this computer inside the PDFs you save.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:safari\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003ESafari\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESafari may clear a website&#039;s stored data if you have not visited it for a while. If MarkupCraft asks for your name again, that is why.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf several people share one computer and one browser login, each should check the name in the status bar before starting, since it is shared.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESee also \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/markupcraft/browser-storage\" data-topic=\"markupcraft/browser-storage\"\u003EWhat stays in this browser\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Every markup records who made it and when. Stamps print the name (\"By Ana Ruiz, Oct 10, 2026\"), replies show it, the markups list can filter by it, and other PDF viewers show it as the comment's author. Signed in: your account's name If you are signed in to your CadCraft account, MarkupCraft uses your account's name and never asks. Markups, replies, stamps and the status bar show it. In Settings › MarkupCraft › Your name it reads \"From your account (your name)\". To use another name, choose Use another name and type it. A name you type wins from then on. To go back to your account's name, choose Use my account name. Only the name is used, never your email address. If you change your name on the site, MarkupCraft shows it the next time it opens. The first time (signed out) When you are not signed in, the first time you pick a drawing tool, a box called \"Your name on markups\" opens. Type your name the way you want reviewers to see it (up to 80 characters). Choose Save. Result: your name appears at the right end of the status bar, and every new markup carries it. If you choose Not now, markups are made with no author (\"Not recorded\" in Properties) and stamps show only the date. MarkupCraft asks again the next time you open it. Change it Click your name (or \"Set your name\") at the right end of the status bar. Type the new name and choose Save. Or open Settings (Ctrl+,), stay on the MarkupCraft tab and type it under Your name. See MarkupCraft settings. Only markups you make from now on get the new name. Markups already drawn keep the name they were made with; there is no way to change the author of an existing markup. Where the name is kept Your name is stored in this browser, on this computer (the browser's local storage for this website). That means: Another browser on the same computer, or the same browser on another computer, asks again. A private or incognito window asks every time, and forgets when it closes. Clearing this site's data (cookies and site data) in the browser's settings forgets it. Nothing is sent anywhere. The name only leaves this computer inside the PDFs you save. Safari may clear a website's stored data if you have not visited it for a while. If MarkupCraft asks for your name again, that is why. If several people share one computer and one browser login, each should check the name in the status bar before starting, since it is shared. See also What stays in this browser.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"mark-up-a-sheet","scope":"markupcraft","title":"Mark up a sheet","summary":"Draw revision clouds, callouts, text boxes, arrows, shapes, pen strokes and highlights, and set their color and line before or after.","section":"tasks","order":30,"keywords":["markup","redline","cloud","revision cloud","callout","leader","text box","arrow","line","rectangle","ellipse","circle","polyline","polygon","pen","freehand","ink","highlighter","color","line width"],"headings":[{"id":"revision-clouds","text":"Revision clouds","level":2},{"id":"callouts","text":"Callouts","level":2},{"id":"text-boxes","text":"Text boxes","level":2},{"id":"arrows-and-lines","text":"Arrows and lines","level":2},{"id":"rectangles-ellipses-polylines-and-polygons","text":"Rectangles, ellipses, polylines and polygons","level":2},{"id":"pen-and-highlighter","text":"Pen and highlighter","level":2},{"id":"color-line-and-fill","text":"Color, line and fill","level":2},{"id":"subject-and-comment","text":"Subject and comment","level":2},{"id":"next","text":"Next","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EEvery tool works the same way: pick it, draw on the sheet, and the new markup is selected so you can see it in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E. The tool stays on for the next one. The status bar always says what the tool expects next.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"input:touch\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003ETouch\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn a touch screen, use one finger to draw. A second finger cancels what the first one started and pinches or pans instead, so you never leave a mark by accident while zooming. More in \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/markupcraft/review-on-a-tablet\" data-topic=\"markupcraft/review-on-a-tablet\"\u003EReview on an iPad or touch screen\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"revision-clouds\"\u003ERevision clouds\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EBox cloud:\u003C/strong\u003E choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECloud\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"C\"\u003EC\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EC\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) and drag a box. Hold \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to make it square.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFree cloud\u003C/strong\u003E around an odd shape:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFree cloud\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift C\"\u003E⇧C\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift+C\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick each corner of the area.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFinish with a double-click, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or a right-click. On touch, double-tap.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhile you click points, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Delete\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EBackspace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E removes the last point and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E cancels the whole shape. Hold \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to keep the next side at a multiple of 45°. A free cloud needs at least three points.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe scallops always bulge outward and their size follows the line width: a thicker line gives bigger arcs.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"callouts\"\u003ECallouts\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA callout is a text box with a leader pointing at something.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECallout\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"K\"\u003EK\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EK\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress on the point you want to call out and drag to where the text should go. Let go. If you just click without dragging, the box is placed up and to the right of the point.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType the text. The box grows downward as the lines wrap.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick anywhere outside the box, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Return\"\u003E⌘ Return\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Enter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, to finish. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E throws the callout away.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf you finish without typing anything, no callout is added (&quot;Nothing typed, so no callout was added.&quot;).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAfter it is drawn, select it to move the box, drag the leader&#039;s tip or its bend (the two diamond handles), or resize the box.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"text-boxes\"\u003EText boxes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EText\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"T\"\u003ET\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ET\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick where the text starts (you get a box of a sensible width), or drag a box of the size you want.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType, then click outside or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Return\"\u003E⌘ Return\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Enter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EText has no border by default. To give it one, raise \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELine width\u003C/span\u003E in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E. To give it a background, tick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFill\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo change the words later: with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E on, double-click the text, or select it and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit on the sheet\u003C/span\u003E in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E. You can also edit it in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EText\u003C/span\u003E box in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"arrows-and-lines\"\u003EArrows and lines\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArrow\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"A\"\u003EA\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EA\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELine\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"L\"\u003EL\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EL\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDrag from the start to the end. Or click the start, let go, and click the end.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EHold \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to keep it at a multiple of 45°.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe arrowhead is at the end where you finished.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"rectangles-ellipses-polylines-and-polygons\"\u003ERectangles, ellipses, polylines and polygons\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERectangle\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"R\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEllipse\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"E\"\u003EE\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EE\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E): drag a box. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E makes a square or a circle. A click without dragging draws nothing and the status bar says to drag.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPolyline\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Y\"\u003EY\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EY\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPolygon\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"G\"\u003EG\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EG\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E): click points; finish with a double-click, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or a right-click (double-tap on touch). \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Delete\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EBackspace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E removes the last point. A polygon closes itself and needs three points; a polyline needs two.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"pen-and-highlighter\"\u003EPen and highlighter\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPen\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"P\"\u003EP\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EP\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E): drag to draw freehand. The stroke is smoothed a little when you let go.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHighlight\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"I\"\u003EI\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EI\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E): the same, with a wide, see-through yellow stroke that tints the drawing under it instead of covering it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EHold \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E while you draw to get one straight stroke from where you started.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe highlighter is a freehand stroke. It does not snap to text in the PDF.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"color-line-and-fill\"\u003EColor, line and fill\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETwo ways:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EBefore drawing:\u003C/strong\u003E pick the tool. With nothing selected, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E shows &quot;&lt;tool&gt; tool&quot; settings: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EColor\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELine width\u003C/span\u003E (in points), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFill\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFill opacity\u003C/span\u003E for closed shapes, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpacity\u003C/span\u003E, and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EText size\u003C/span\u003E for text. Every new markup of that kind uses them, and MarkupCraft remembers them in this browser.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAfter drawing:\u003C/strong\u003E select one or more markups and change the same controls under &quot;Style&quot;. With several selected, the change applies to all of them in one step.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe defaults: red markups, a yellow highlighter, blue measurements, orange counts, and stamps colored by meaning.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESaved looks you reuse every day belong in the tool chest: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/markupcraft/tool-chest\" data-topic=\"markupcraft/tool-chest\"\u003EThe tool chest\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"subject-and-comment\"\u003ESubject and comment\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEvery markup also has a \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESubject\u003C/span\u003E (a short label such as &quot;Cloud&quot; or &quot;RFI&quot;, shown in the markups list and in other PDF viewers) and a \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EComment\u003C/span\u003E (longer notes). Fill them in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E. For a conversation about a markup, use replies: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/markupcraft/statuses-and-replies\" data-topic=\"markupcraft/statuses-and-replies\"\u003EStatuses and replies\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"next\"\u003ENext\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/markupcraft/select-and-edit\" data-topic=\"markupcraft/select-and-edit\"\u003ESelect, move and change markups\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/markupcraft/stamps-and-notes\" data-topic=\"markupcraft/stamps-and-notes\"\u003EStamps and notes\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/markupcraft/markup-types\" data-topic=\"markupcraft/markup-types\"\u003EEvery markup type\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"Every tool works the same way: pick it, draw on the sheet, and the new markup is selected so you can see it in Properties. The tool stays on for the next one. The status bar always says what the tool expects next. On a touch screen, use one finger to draw. A second finger cancels what the first one started and pinches or pans instead, so you never leave a mark by accident while zooming. More in Review on an iPad or touch screen. Revision clouds Box cloud: choose Cloud (C) and drag a box. Hold Shift to make it square. Free cloud around an odd shape: Choose Free cloud (Shift+C). Click each corner of the area. Finish with a double-click, Enter or a right-click. On touch, double-tap. While you click points, Backspace removes the last point and Esc cancels the whole shape. Hold Shift to keep the next side at a multiple of 45°. A free cloud needs at least three points. The scallops always bulge outward and their size follows the line width: a thicker line gives bigger arcs. Callouts A callout is a text box with a leader pointing at something. Choose Callout (K). Press on the point you want to call out and drag to where the text should go. Let go. If you just click without dragging, the box is placed up and to the right of the point. Type the text. The box grows downward as the lines wrap. Click anywhere outside the box, or press Ctrl+Enter, to finish. Esc throws the callout away. If you finish without typing anything, no callout is added (\"Nothing typed, so no callout was added.\"). After it is drawn, select it to move the box, drag the leader's tip or its bend (the two diamond handles), or resize the box. Text boxes Choose Text (T). Click where the text starts (you get a box of a sensible width), or drag a box of the size you want. Type, then click outside or press Ctrl+Enter. Text has no border by default. To give it one, raise Line width in Properties. To give it a background, tick Fill. To change the words later: with Select on, double-click the text, or select it and press Enter, or choose Edit on the sheet in Properties. You can also edit it in the Text box in Properties. Arrows and lines Choose Arrow (A) or Line (L). Drag from the start to the end. Or click the start, let go, and click the end. Hold Shift to keep it at a multiple of 45°. The arrowhead is at the end where you finished. Rectangles, ellipses, polylines and polygons Rectangle (R) and Ellipse (E): drag a box. Shift makes a square or a circle. A click without dragging draws nothing and the status bar says to drag. Polyline (Y) and Polygon (G): click points; finish with a double-click, Enter or a right-click (double-tap on touch). Backspace removes the last point. A polygon closes itself and needs three points; a polyline needs two. Pen and highlighter Pen (P): drag to draw freehand. The stroke is smoothed a little when you let go. Highlight (I): the same, with a wide, see-through yellow stroke that tints the drawing under it instead of covering it. Hold Shift while you draw to get one straight stroke from where you started. The highlighter is a freehand stroke. It does not snap to text in the PDF. Color, line and fill Two ways: Before drawing: pick the tool. With nothing selected, Properties shows \"\u003Ctool\u003E tool\" settings: Color, Line width (in points), Fill and Fill opacity for closed shapes, Opacity, and Text size for text. Every new markup of that kind uses them, and MarkupCraft remembers them in this browser. After drawing: select one or more markups and change the same controls under \"Style\". With several selected, the change applies to all of them in one step. The defaults: red markups, a yellow highlighter, blue measurements, orange counts, and stamps colored by meaning. Saved looks you reuse every day belong in the tool chest: see The tool chest. Subject and comment Every markup also has a Subject (a short label such as \"Cloud\" or \"RFI\", shown in the markups list and in other PDF viewers) and a Comment (longer notes). Fill them in Properties. For a conversation about a markup, use replies: see Statuses and replies. Next Select, move and change markups Stamps and notes Every markup type","lang":"en"},{"slug":"scroll-pages","scope":"markupcraft","title":"Scroll through pages, or one page at a time","summary":"See a PDF's pages one under the other and scroll through them, or keep one page on screen at a time. Switch whenever you like.","section":"tasks","order":31,"keywords":["scroll","scrolling","continuous","all pages","one page","page at a time","tabs","page tabs","single page","multi page","next page","document view","reading"],"headings":[{"id":"scroll-pages","text":"Scroll pages","level":2},{"id":"one-page-at-a-time","text":"One page at a time","level":2},{"id":"good-to-know","text":"Good to know","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EMarkupCraft shows a PDF in two ways. Switch between them with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScroll pages\u003C/span\u003E on the sheet bar, under the sheet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"scroll-pages\"\u003EScroll pages\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe pages of the PDF are one under the other, like a document.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe mouse wheel scrolls through them. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E with the wheel scrolls sideways. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control\"\u003E⌃\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E with the wheel zooms, and so does a pinch on a trackpad.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe page in the middle of the screen is the open sheet: the sheet bar, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E, the sheets list and the markups list follow it as you scroll.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick or start drawing on any page you see, and you mark up that page. Markups on the other pages show as you scroll.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn ↓\"\u003Efn ↓\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EPage Down\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn ↑\"\u003Efn ↑\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EPage Up\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E scroll to the next and previous page.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn a workspace layout with tabs, each PDF gets one tab, and its pages scroll inside it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"one-page-at-a-time\"\u003EOne page at a time\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOnly the open page is on screen. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn ↓\"\u003Efn ↓\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EPage Down\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn ↑\"\u003Efn ↑\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EPage Up\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and the sheets list go from page to page, and each page keeps its own zoom. In a workspace layout with tabs, each page you open gets a tab.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"good-to-know\"\u003EGood to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMarkupCraft remembers your choice in this browser.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWith several PDFs open, Scroll pages shows the pages of one PDF at a time: the one the open sheet belongs to. Pick a sheet of another PDF (or its tab) to scroll through that one.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EComparing two sheets always shows one page at a time; Scroll pages comes back when you stop comparing.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETurning the view (the turn buttons on the sheet bar) turns every page in the column.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"MarkupCraft shows a PDF in two ways. Switch between them with Scroll pages on the sheet bar, under the sheet. Scroll pages The pages of the PDF are one under the other, like a document. The mouse wheel scrolls through them. Shift with the wheel scrolls sideways. Ctrl with the wheel zooms, and so does a pinch on a trackpad. The page in the middle of the screen is the open sheet: the sheet bar, Properties, the sheets list and the markups list follow it as you scroll. Click or start drawing on any page you see, and you mark up that page. Markups on the other pages show as you scroll. Page Down and Page Up scroll to the next and previous page. In a workspace layout with tabs, each PDF gets one tab, and its pages scroll inside it. One page at a time Only the open page is on screen. Page Down, Page Up and the sheets list go from page to page, and each page keeps its own zoom. In a workspace layout with tabs, each page you open gets a tab. Good to know MarkupCraft remembers your choice in this browser. With several PDFs open, Scroll pages shows the pages of one PDF at a time: the one the open sheet belongs to. Pick a sheet of another PDF (or its tab) to scroll through that one. Comparing two sheets always shows one page at a time; Scroll pages comes back when you stop comparing. Turning the view (the turn buttons on the sheet bar) turns every page in the column.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"clean-up-and-bring-in","scope":"markupcraft","title":"Clean up pen strokes, and bring in review comments and site progress","summary":"Turn freehand pen strokes into lines, circles, arcs and rectangles; bring a permit package's review comments and a site's survey progress in as notes.","section":"tasks","order":32,"keywords":["pen","freehand","clean up","straighten","circle","line","rectangle","arc","permit","review comments","progress","drone","scan","survey","notes","csv"],"headings":[{"id":"clean-up-pen-strokes","text":"Clean up pen strokes","level":2},{"id":"bring-in-review-comments","text":"Bring in review comments","level":2},{"id":"bring-in-hazop-node-boxes","text":"Bring in HAZOP node boxes","level":2},{"id":"bring-in-site-progress","text":"Bring in site progress","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"clean-up-pen-strokes\"\u003EClean up pen strokes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDraw with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPen\u003C/span\u003E, then open the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E menu in the Edit group and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EClean up pen strokes\u003C/span\u003E. Each selected pen stroke becomes what it was meant to be: a straight stroke becomes a line (made exactly level or plumb when it was nearly so), a round one an ellipse, part of a circle an arc, a closed four-cornered one a rectangle, anything else a polyline or polygon. Loose ends of strokes drawn together are pulled onto each other. With no pen stroke selected, every pen stroke on the open sheet is cleaned. It is one undo step: Ctrl+Z puts the freehand back. The shape is worked out on your computer by fixed rules; nothing is sent anywhere.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"bring-in-review-comments\"\u003EBring in review comments\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EBimCraft&#039;s permit package holds \u003Ccode\u003E01-review-comments.csv\u003C/code\u003E: the comments a plan reviewer is likely to make, each with the rule, the clause, what is needed, what was found and what to do. In the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETasks\u003C/span\u003E tab, open \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPermit review comments\u003C/span\u003E and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBring in comments (CSV)\u003C/span\u003E. Each comment becomes an Open note in a column down the left of the open sheet; drag each to the place it is about. They are computed from the checks that exist, not from a plan examiner.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"bring-in-hazop-node-boxes\"\u003EBring in HAZOP node boxes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECadCraft&#039;s P&amp;ID add-on draws the nodes of a HAZOP study and can save them as \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHAZOP node boxes\u003C/span\u003E (a JSON file). Open the printed P&amp;ID sheet here, go to the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETasks\u003C/span\u003E tab, open \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHAZOP node boxes\u003C/span\u003E and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBring in node boxes (JSON)\u003C/span\u003E. Each node becomes an Open rectangle on the sheet with its number, name, equipment and lines in the text, and the team writes its findings as replies on it. The sheet is taken as printed at full size from the drawing&#039;s lower left corner; if it was printed at another scale, type that number in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPrinted at\u003C/span\u003E first (0.5 for 1:2). A node that falls outside the page is not drawn, and the panel says how many. The markers are a study aid: they show where the nodes are, they do not judge the study.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"bring-in-site-progress\"\u003EBring in site progress\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECivilCraft keeps a row for each drone flight or scan of the site. In the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETasks\u003C/span\u003E tab, open \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESite progress\u003C/span\u003E and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBring in progress (JSON)\u003C/span\u003E. Each survey becomes a note on the open sheet saying how much is done, what is left to cut or fill, what was over-dug or over-filled and what stands off the plan. A survey with something off the plan stays Open; a clean one is Accepted.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Clean up pen strokes Draw with Pen, then open the More menu in the Edit group and choose Clean up pen strokes. Each selected pen stroke becomes what it was meant to be: a straight stroke becomes a line (made exactly level or plumb when it was nearly so), a round one an ellipse, part of a circle an arc, a closed four-cornered one a rectangle, anything else a polyline or polygon. Loose ends of strokes drawn together are pulled onto each other. With no pen stroke selected, every pen stroke on the open sheet is cleaned. It is one undo step: Ctrl+Z puts the freehand back. The shape is worked out on your computer by fixed rules; nothing is sent anywhere. Bring in review comments BimCraft's permit package holds 01-review-comments.csv: the comments a plan reviewer is likely to make, each with the rule, the clause, what is needed, what was found and what to do. In the Tasks tab, open Permit review comments and choose Bring in comments (CSV). Each comment becomes an Open note in a column down the left of the open sheet; drag each to the place it is about. They are computed from the checks that exist, not from a plan examiner. Bring in HAZOP node boxes CadCraft's P&ID add-on draws the nodes of a HAZOP study and can save them as HAZOP node boxes (a JSON file). Open the printed P&ID sheet here, go to the Tasks tab, open HAZOP node boxes and choose Bring in node boxes (JSON). Each node becomes an Open rectangle on the sheet with its number, name, equipment and lines in the text, and the team writes its findings as replies on it. The sheet is taken as printed at full size from the drawing's lower left corner; if it was printed at another scale, type that number in Printed at first (0.5 for 1:2). A node that falls outside the page is not drawn, and the panel says how many. The markers are a study aid: they show where the nodes are, they do not judge the study. Bring in site progress CivilCraft keeps a row for each drone flight or scan of the site. In the Tasks tab, open Site progress and choose Bring in progress (JSON). Each survey becomes a note on the open sheet saying how much is done, what is left to cut or fill, what was over-dug or over-filled and what stands off the plan. A survey with something off the plan stays Open; a clean one is Accepted.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"fill-a-form","scope":"markupcraft","title":"Fill in a PDF form","summary":"Type in a PDF's form fields, tick its boxes and pick from its lists; the values are saved into the form itself.","section":"tasks","order":33,"keywords":["form","forms","fillable","fill in","fill out","acroform","field","fields","text field","check box","checkbox","tick","radio button","drop-down","dropdown","list","permit","application","inspection form","submittal form","sign off"],"headings":[{"id":"fill-in-the-form","text":"Fill in the form","level":2},{"id":"save-the-filled-in-form","text":"Save the filled-in form","level":2},{"id":"good-to-know","text":"Good to know","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EMany PDFs come with a form: permit applications, inspection checklists, submittal cover sheets. MarkupCraft shows the form&#039;s fields and their values, and lets you fill them in. The values go into the PDF&#039;s own fields, so Acrobat and every other PDF reader show and keep them.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"fill-in-the-form\"\u003EFill in the form\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen the PDF. When it has a form, the status bar says how many fields it has.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFill form\u003C/span\u003E in the toolbar (or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E). Every field on the page gets a box you can type in.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick a field and type. Tick boxes, pick radio buttons, choose from drop-downs and lists. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Tab\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E goes to the next field.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETo move the sheet while you fill, drag anywhere outside the fields.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or pick another tool when you are done. What you typed stays on the sheet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWith \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E, a double click on a field turns \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFill form\u003C/span\u003E on and puts you in that field.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"save-the-filled-in-form\"\u003ESave the filled-in form\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave\u003C/span\u003E. The copy you download has your values in its fields, with your markups beside them. The status bar says how many fields were filled.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo hand in a form nobody can change, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPages\u003C/span\u003E, then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave a flattened copy…\u003C/span\u003E: the values become part of the page. Printing does the same.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"good-to-know\"\u003EGood to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA field the PDF locks (read-only) can&#039;t be changed. Its box is grey.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EText up to the field&#039;s own length limit is kept; extra characters are not typed.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESome forms come without a drawn picture of their boxes and buttons, and other programs show them blank. MarkupCraft draws them, and when you save it adds the picture, so they show everywhere.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUndo in a field is the field&#039;s own: press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control Z\"\u003E⌃Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E while you are typing in it. The markup undo doesn&#039;t undo form values.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIf your browser closes, the values you typed come back with the rest of your unsaved work.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EValues typed in letters the standard PDF font can&#039;t write (for example Chinese) are saved, and the PDF asks each reader to draw them itself.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESignature fields and push buttons are not filled in here. To sign, use \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStamp\u003C/span\u003E and your signature.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EValues typed into a form are not shared in a live session yet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"Many PDFs come with a form: permit applications, inspection checklists, submittal cover sheets. MarkupCraft shows the form's fields and their values, and lets you fill them in. The values go into the PDF's own fields, so Acrobat and every other PDF reader show and keep them. Fill in the form Open the PDF. When it has a form, the status bar says how many fields it has. Choose Fill form in the toolbar (or press F). Every field on the page gets a box you can type in. Click a field and type. Tick boxes, pick radio buttons, choose from drop-downs and lists. Tab goes to the next field. To move the sheet while you fill, drag anywhere outside the fields. Press Esc or pick another tool when you are done. What you typed stays on the sheet. With Select, a double click on a field turns Fill form on and puts you in that field. Save the filled-in form Choose Save. The copy you download has your values in its fields, with your markups beside them. The status bar says how many fields were filled. To hand in a form nobody can change, choose Pages, then Save a flattened copy…: the values become part of the page. Printing does the same. Good to know A field the PDF locks (read-only) can't be changed. Its box is grey. Text up to the field's own length limit is kept; extra characters are not typed. Some forms come without a drawn picture of their boxes and buttons, and other programs show them blank. MarkupCraft draws them, and when you save it adds the picture, so they show everywhere. Undo in a field is the field's own: press Ctrl+Z while you are typing in it. The markup undo doesn't undo form values. If your browser closes, the values you typed come back with the rest of your unsaved work. Values typed in letters the standard PDF font can't write (for example Chinese) are saved, and the PDF asks each reader to draw them itself. Signature fields and push buttons are not filled in here. To sign, use Stamp and your signature. Values typed into a form are not shared in a live session yet.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"select-and-edit","scope":"markupcraft","title":"Select, move and change markups","summary":"Pick markups by clicking or with a window, move, resize, nudge, cut, copy, paste, paste in place, duplicate, delete and undo.","section":"tasks","order":40,"keywords":["select","selection","select all","select none","window","crossing","move","resize","grips","handles","nudge","cut","copy","paste","paste in place","duplicate","clipboard","another tab","delete","undo","redo","edit"],"headings":[{"id":"pick-markups","text":"Pick markups","level":2},{"id":"move-and-resize","text":"Move and resize","level":2},{"id":"change-what-it-says-or-how-it-looks","text":"Change what it says or how it looks","level":2},{"id":"copy-paste-and-duplicate","text":"Copy, paste and duplicate","level":2},{"id":"delete","text":"Delete","level":2},{"id":"undo-and-redo","text":"Undo and redo","level":2},{"id":"copy-markups-to-other-sheets","text":"Copy markups to other sheets","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"V\"\u003EV\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EV\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) first. Pressing \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E also gets you there: the first press clears the selection, the next one switches back to \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"pick-markups\"\u003EPick markups\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"input:mouse\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EMouse\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOne markup:\u003C/strong\u003E click it. A dashed box and handles appear, and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E shows it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAdd or remove one:\u003C/strong\u003E hold \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and click.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFully inside a window:\u003C/strong\u003E drag from left to right on an empty part of the sheet. Only markups entirely inside the box are picked (solid outline).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETouching a window:\u003C/strong\u003E drag from right to left. Anything the box touches is picked (green, dashed outline).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EEverything on this sheet:\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command A\"\u003E⌘A\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+A\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect all\u003C/span\u003E in the toolbar&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit\u003C/span\u003E group.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENothing:\u003C/strong\u003E click an empty spot, press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect none\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"input:touch\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003ETouch\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOne markup:\u003C/strong\u003E tap it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESeveral:\u003C/strong\u003E drag one finger across an empty part of the sheet to draw a window. Left to right picks what is fully inside; right to left picks what it touches.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETapping with \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E held only works with a keyboard attached. Without one, use a window.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOne finger never pans in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E; use two fingers, or the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPan\u003C/span\u003E tool.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ERead-only comments that came with the file show a gray dashed box and no handles. They can be selected and replied to, not changed.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"move-and-resize\"\u003EMove and resize\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMove:\u003C/strong\u003E drag a selected markup. Hold \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to move only along multiples of 45°. Several selected markups move together.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EResize:\u003C/strong\u003E with one markup selected, drag a handle. Square handles resize the box (hold \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to keep its proportions); round handles move one corner point of a line, polyline, polygon, cloud or measurement (hold \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E for 45°); diamond handles move a callout&#039;s tip and bend. Pen strokes scale from their box.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENudge:\u003C/strong\u003E press the arrow keys to move the selection one screen pixel; hold \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E for ten. Each press is one undo step.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"change-what-it-says-or-how-it-looks\"\u003EChange what it says or how it looks\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EText, callouts and notes:\u003C/strong\u003E double-click the markup, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit on the sheet\u003C/span\u003E in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E. Type, then click outside or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Return\"\u003E⌘ Return\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Enter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E cancels the edit.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EStamps:\u003C/strong\u003E pick a different stamp in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E (double-click opens \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E for you).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EStyle:\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EColor\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELine width\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFill\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpacity\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EText size\u003C/span\u003E under &quot;Style&quot; in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E. With several selected, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E shows the kinds you picked and changes them all at once.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"copy-paste-and-duplicate\"\u003ECopy, paste and duplicate\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe toolbar&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit\u003C/span\u003E group has \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECopy\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaste\u003C/span\u003E; its \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E menu has all of these, each with its key.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ECommand\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKey\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it does\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECut\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command X\"\u003E⌘X\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+X\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECopies the selected markups and deletes them\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECopy\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command C\"\u003E⌘C\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+C\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECopies the selected markups\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaste\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command V\"\u003E⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPuts a copy on the sheet you are on (where, below)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaste in place\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift Command V\"\u003E⇧⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPuts the copy at exactly the spot it was copied from: on the same sheet, on top of the original; on another sheet, at the same place on the paper\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDuplicate\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command D\"\u003E⌘D\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+D\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMakes a copy of the selection a little down and to the right, without touching what you copied before\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EWhere Paste puts the copy:\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command V\"\u003E⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E with the pointer on the sheet puts the copy centered where the pointer is. Move the pointer, press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command V\"\u003E⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E again, and you place another.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaste\u003C/span\u003E button (the pointer is on the toolbar then), or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command V\"\u003E⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E with the pointer off the sheet, puts it a little down and to the right of the original. Each paste of the same copy steps a little further, so copies never hide one another. On another sheet the first paste lands where the original was on its sheet. If that spot is out of view, the copy lands in the middle of the view.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETo make \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command V\"\u003E⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E always paste beside the original, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBeside the copy\u003C/span\u003E under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaste\u003C/span\u003E in \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/markupcraft/settings\" data-topic=\"markupcraft/settings\"\u003EMarkupCraft settings\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe pasted markups are selected, so you can drag them straight away. Each cut, paste or duplicate is one undo step: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E takes the whole paste away.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPasted copies are new markups: they get your name, today&#039;s date, the status Open, no punch number and an empty thread. Their kind, shape, style, text and comment come along. Read-only comments that came with the file cannot be copied or cut.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EBetween tabs:\u003C/strong\u003E what you copy is also kept in this browser, so another MarkupCraft tab in the same browser can paste it. Copying markups does not use the computer&#039;s clipboard: you cannot paste them into another program, and text you copied elsewhere does not paste as a markup.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERight-click menu:\u003C/strong\u003E right-click a markup (or empty paper) for \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECut\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECopy\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaste here\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaste in place\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDuplicate\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave selection as a stamp…\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFlatten into the page when saved\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EInsert a picture here…\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect all\u003C/span\u003E. Right-clicking a markup that is not selected selects it first. Dragging with the right button still pans. On a touch screen, a long press on a markup opens the same menu (on text, it starts editing the text). The menu key or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift F10\"\u003E⇧ F10\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift+F10\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E opens it at the selection.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPage content selected with the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EContent\u003C/span\u003E tool copies and pastes too: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/markupcraft/page-content\" data-topic=\"markupcraft/page-content\"\u003EChange the page&#039;s own lines, pictures and text\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo copy part of the page itself as a picture (a rectangle, an ellipse or a freeform shape), or as a see-through overlay for another sheet or app, see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/markupcraft/copy-an-area\" data-topic=\"markupcraft/copy-an-area\"\u003ECopy an area of the page, or an overlay\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"delete\"\u003EDelete\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn Delete\"\u003Efn Delete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Delete\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EBackspace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E in the toolbar, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E. The status bar says how many were deleted. Read-only comments from the file are never deleted (&quot;kept as in the file&quot;).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"undo-and-redo\"\u003EUndo and redo\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E undoes, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Y\"\u003E⌘Y\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Y\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift Command Z\"\u003E⇧⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E redoes. The toolbar has \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUndo\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERedo\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe status bar says what was undone (&quot;Undid move markup.&quot;).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUndo covers every change to markups, statuses, replies, punch items, sheet scales and sheet names, up to 500 steps.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EHistory is cleared when you close the set.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"copy-markups-to-other-sheets\"\u003ECopy markups to other sheets\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo put the same stamp, note or cloud on several sheets, select it (or several markups) and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECopy to other sheets…\u003C/span\u003E in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E. Tick the sheets, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAll sheets\u003C/span\u003E, and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECopy\u003C/span\u003E. Each sheet gets a copy in the same place on the page, signed with your name. One \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E removes them all.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Choose Select (V) first. Pressing Esc also gets you there: the first press clears the selection, the next one switches back to Select. Pick markups One markup: click it. A dashed box and handles appear, and Properties shows it. Add or remove one: hold Shift and click. Fully inside a window: drag from left to right on an empty part of the sheet. Only markups entirely inside the box are picked (solid outline). Touching a window: drag from right to left. Anything the box touches is picked (green, dashed outline). Everything on this sheet: Ctrl+A, or More then Select all in the toolbar's Edit group. Nothing: click an empty spot, press Esc, or More then Select none. One markup: tap it. Several: drag one finger across an empty part of the sheet to draw a window. Left to right picks what is fully inside; right to left picks what it touches. Tapping with Shift held only works with a keyboard attached. Without one, use a window. One finger never pans in Select; use two fingers, or the Pan tool. Read-only comments that came with the file show a gray dashed box and no handles. They can be selected and replied to, not changed. Move and resize Move: drag a selected markup. Hold Shift to move only along multiples of 45°. Several selected markups move together. Resize: with one markup selected, drag a handle. Square handles resize the box (hold Shift to keep its proportions); round handles move one corner point of a line, polyline, polygon, cloud or measurement (hold Shift for 45°); diamond handles move a callout's tip and bend. Pen strokes scale from their box. Nudge: press the arrow keys to move the selection one screen pixel; hold Shift for ten. Each press is one undo step. Change what it says or how it looks Text, callouts and notes: double-click the markup, or press Enter, or choose Edit on the sheet in Properties. Type, then click outside or press Ctrl+Enter. Esc cancels the edit. Stamps: pick a different stamp in Properties (double-click opens Properties for you). Style: Color, Line width, Fill, Opacity and Text size under \"Style\" in Properties. With several selected, Properties shows the kinds you picked and changes them all at once. Copy, paste and duplicate The toolbar's Edit group has Copy and Paste; its More menu has all of these, each with its key. Command Key What it does Cut Ctrl+X Copies the selected markups and deletes them Copy Ctrl+C Copies the selected markups Paste Ctrl+V Puts a copy on the sheet you are on (where, below) Paste in place Ctrl+Shift+V Puts the copy at exactly the spot it was copied from: on the same sheet, on top of the original; on another sheet, at the same place on the paper Duplicate Ctrl+D Makes a copy of the selection a little down and to the right, without touching what you copied before Where Paste puts the copy: Ctrl+V with the pointer on the sheet puts the copy centered where the pointer is. Move the pointer, press Ctrl+V again, and you place another. The Paste button (the pointer is on the toolbar then), or Ctrl+V with the pointer off the sheet, puts it a little down and to the right of the original. Each paste of the same copy steps a little further, so copies never hide one another. On another sheet the first paste lands where the original was on its sheet. If that spot is out of view, the copy lands in the middle of the view. To make Ctrl+V always paste beside the original, choose Beside the copy under Paste in MarkupCraft settings. The pasted markups are selected, so you can drag them straight away. Each cut, paste or duplicate is one undo step: Ctrl+Z takes the whole paste away. Pasted copies are new markups: they get your name, today's date, the status Open, no punch number and an empty thread. Their kind, shape, style, text and comment come along. Read-only comments that came with the file cannot be copied or cut. Between tabs: what you copy is also kept in this browser, so another MarkupCraft tab in the same browser can paste it. Copying markups does not use the computer's clipboard: you cannot paste them into another program, and text you copied elsewhere does not paste as a markup. Right-click menu: right-click a markup (or empty paper) for Cut, Copy, Paste here, Paste in place, Duplicate, Delete, Save selection as a stamp…, Flatten into the page when saved, Insert a picture here… and Select all. Right-clicking a markup that is not selected selects it first. Dragging with the right button still pans. On a touch screen, a long press on a markup opens the same menu (on text, it starts editing the text). The menu key or Shift+F10 opens it at the selection. Page content selected with the Content tool copies and pastes too: see Change the page's own lines, pictures and text. To copy part of the page itself as a picture (a rectangle, an ellipse or a freeform shape), or as a see-through overlay for another sheet or app, see Copy an area of the page, or an overlay. Delete Press Delete or Backspace, choose More then Delete in the toolbar, or Delete in Properties. The status bar says how many were deleted. Read-only comments from the file are never deleted (\"kept as in the file\"). Undo and redo Ctrl+Z undoes, Ctrl+Y or Ctrl+Shift+Z redoes. The toolbar has Undo and Redo. The status bar says what was undone (\"Undid move markup.\"). Undo covers every change to markups, statuses, replies, punch items, sheet scales and sheet names, up to 500 steps. History is cleared when you close the set. Copy markups to other sheets To put the same stamp, note or cloud on several sheets, select it (or several markups) and choose Copy to other sheets… in Properties. Tick the sheets, or All sheets, and choose Copy. Each sheet gets a copy in the same place on the page, signed with your name. One Ctrl+Z removes them all.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"stamps-and-notes","scope":"markupcraft","title":"Stamps and sticky notes","summary":"Stamp a sheet Approved, Revise and resubmit or your own wording, and leave sticky notes that open as pop-up comments.","section":"tasks","order":50,"keywords":["stamp","picture","image","paste picture","screenshot","clipboard","logo","photo stamp","signature","sign","approved","approved as noted","revise and resubmit","rejected","reviewed","void","custom stamp","sticky note","note","comment","pop-up"],"headings":[{"id":"stamp-a-sheet","text":"Stamp a sheet","level":2},{"id":"change-a-stamp-later","text":"Change a stamp later","level":3},{"id":"stamps-you-use-often","text":"Stamps you use often","level":3},{"id":"stamp-a-picture-or-your-signature","text":"Stamp a picture or your signature","level":2},{"id":"paste-a-picture-from-the-clipboard","text":"Paste a picture from the clipboard","level":2},{"id":"insert-a-picture-from-a-file","text":"Insert a picture from a file","level":2},{"id":"make-a-stamp-from-a-selection","text":"Make a stamp from a selection","level":2},{"id":"sticky-notes","text":"Sticky notes","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"stamp-a-sheet\"\u003EStamp a sheet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStamp\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"S\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E on the right, pick the stamp under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStamp\u003C/span\u003E:\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EApproved\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EApproved as noted\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERevise and resubmit\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERejected\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EReviewed\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EVoid\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ECustom text… (type up to 60 characters in the box that appears, then press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or click elsewhere)\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick on the sheet where the stamp goes. Or drag a box to choose its size.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EResult: a framed stamp in capitals with a second line &quot;By &lt;your name&gt;, &lt;date&gt;&quot;. A clicked stamp is sized for the screen at your zoom, kept between half an inch and an inch and a quarter tall on the printed sheet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe stamp you picked stays chosen for the next one, also after you reopen MarkupCraft in this browser.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EColors follow the meaning:\u003C/strong\u003E the Approved stamps are green, Revise and resubmit orange, Rejected and Void red, Reviewed blue, and custom stamps purple. If you choose another color in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E, that color is kept.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"change-a-stamp-later\"\u003EChange a stamp later\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESelect it and pick another stamp in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E. If it still had its automatic color, the color changes with the meaning.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe name and date on the stamp are the stamp&#039;s author and the day it was made. They do not change when you edit it, and a stamp&#039;s author cannot be changed. To stamp in someone else&#039;s name, they set their name and stamp it themselves.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe date on a stamp is the date in Coordinated Universal Time (UTC). Late in the evening in the Americas, or early in the morning in Asia and Australia, it can show the next or the previous day compared with your clock. The created time in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E is in your own time zone.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"stamps-you-use-often\"\u003EStamps you use often\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe tool chest comes with an &quot;Approved as noted&quot; stamp tool, and you can save any stamp (its wording and color) as a tool of its own. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/markupcraft/tool-chest\" data-topic=\"markupcraft/tool-chest\"\u003EThe tool chest\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EStamps with filled-in fields (date received, project number) are not built yet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"stamp-a-picture-or-your-signature\"\u003EStamp a picture or your signature\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe stamp list in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E ends with two more choices:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPicture…\u003C/span\u003E asks for a PNG, JPEG or SVG file on this computer, or a page of a PDF, such as a company seal or a logo. Large pictures are scaled down to 1600 pixels on their longer side; an SVG or a PDF page becomes a picture of that size.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMy signature…\u003C/span\u003E opens a signature box: sign with a mouse, a pen or a finger, and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUse this signature\u003C/span\u003E. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EClear\u003C/span\u003E starts again. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUse a picture of a signature…\u003C/span\u003E takes a scan or photo of your signature instead: MarkupCraft makes the white paper see-through and trims the picture to the ink (for a PNG it asks first).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThen click the sheet to place it (a picture 3 in wide, a signature 2 in wide), or drag a box to fit it inside; the proportions are kept. Resize it with its handles like any markup (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E keeps the proportions).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe last picture is kept in this browser, so it is ready next time; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EChoose another picture…\u003C/span\u003E replaces it. Every signature you draw or load is kept in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EYour signatures\u003C/span\u003E (up to eight, newest first): click one to use it, or × to remove it. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/markupcraft/browser-storage\" data-topic=\"markupcraft/browser-storage\"\u003EWhat stays in this browser\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn the saved PDF a picture is a stamp comment showing the image, so any PDF viewer shows and prints it. A signature drawn here is a picture of your signature, not a certified digital signature.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"paste-a-picture-from-the-clipboard\"\u003EPaste a picture from the clipboard\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECopy a picture in another program, or take a screenshot to the clipboard, then press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command V\"\u003E⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E over the sheet. It lands where the pointer is, at most 3 in wide or tall with its proportions kept, as a picture markup you can move and resize. In the saved PDF it is a stamp comment showing the image, like the pictures above.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaste a picture\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit\u003C/span\u003E group does the same from the menu; the browser may ask first. When the clipboard holds no picture, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command V\"\u003E⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E pastes copied markups as before.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"insert-a-picture-from-a-file\"\u003EInsert a picture from a file\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EInsert a picture from a file…\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit\u003C/span\u003E group (or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EInsert a picture here…\u003C/span\u003E in the right-click menu), and pick a PNG, JPEG or SVG file or a PDF (a PDF with several pages asks which one). You can also drag a PNG, JPEG or SVG file onto the sheet: it lands where you drop it. The picture is a markup, at most 3 in wide or tall: move and resize it like any other.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo make a picture part of the page, tick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFlatten into the page when saved\u003C/span\u003E in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/markupcraft/flatten\" data-topic=\"markupcraft/flatten\"\u003ESave a flattened copy\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"make-a-stamp-from-a-selection\"\u003EMake a stamp from a selection\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESelect markups (or page content with the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EContent\u003C/span\u003E tool) and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave selection as a stamp…\u003C/span\u003E in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E, in the right-click menu or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave as a stamp…\u003C/span\u003E in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E. Give it a name. It goes into the tool chest: click it there, then click on any sheet to place everything you selected, together, centered where you click. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/markupcraft/tool-chest\" data-topic=\"markupcraft/tool-chest\"\u003EThe tool chest\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"sticky-notes\"\u003ESticky notes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENote\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"N\"\u003EN\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EN\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick where the note goes. A small note icon appears with a box beside it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType the note. Click outside or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Return\"\u003E⌘ Return\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Enter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to keep it; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E throws it away.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUnlike text and callouts, a note is kept even if you type nothing, so you can place the icon now and write later.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe note&#039;s words are its \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENote\u003C/span\u003E field in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E. Double-click the icon (or select it and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) to edit them on the sheet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOn the sheet you see only the icon. Other PDF viewers show a standard sticky note that opens to the text when clicked.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChange the icon&#039;s color with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EColor\u003C/span\u003E in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFor back-and-forth discussion, use the reply thread on any markup instead: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/markupcraft/statuses-and-replies\" data-topic=\"markupcraft/statuses-and-replies\"\u003EStatuses and replies\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Stamp a sheet Choose Stamp (S). In Properties on the right, pick the stamp under Stamp: Approved Approved as noted Revise and resubmit Rejected Reviewed Void Custom text… (type up to 60 characters in the box that appears, then press Enter or click elsewhere) Click on the sheet where the stamp goes. Or drag a box to choose its size. Result: a framed stamp in capitals with a second line \"By \u003Cyour name\u003E, \u003Cdate\u003E\". A clicked stamp is sized for the screen at your zoom, kept between half an inch and an inch and a quarter tall on the printed sheet. The stamp you picked stays chosen for the next one, also after you reopen MarkupCraft in this browser. Colors follow the meaning: the Approved stamps are green, Revise and resubmit orange, Rejected and Void red, Reviewed blue, and custom stamps purple. If you choose another color in Properties, that color is kept. Change a stamp later Select it and pick another stamp in Properties. If it still had its automatic color, the color changes with the meaning. The name and date on the stamp are the stamp's author and the day it was made. They do not change when you edit it, and a stamp's author cannot be changed. To stamp in someone else's name, they set their name and stamp it themselves. The date on a stamp is the date in Coordinated Universal Time (UTC). Late in the evening in the Americas, or early in the morning in Asia and Australia, it can show the next or the previous day compared with your clock. The created time in Properties is in your own time zone. Stamps you use often The tool chest comes with an \"Approved as noted\" stamp tool, and you can save any stamp (its wording and color) as a tool of its own. See The tool chest. Stamps with filled-in fields (date received, project number) are not built yet. Stamp a picture or your signature The stamp list in Properties ends with two more choices: Picture… asks for a PNG, JPEG or SVG file on this computer, or a page of a PDF, such as a company seal or a logo. Large pictures are scaled down to 1600 pixels on their longer side; an SVG or a PDF page becomes a picture of that size. My signature… opens a signature box: sign with a mouse, a pen or a finger, and choose Use this signature. Clear starts again. Use a picture of a signature… takes a scan or photo of your signature instead: MarkupCraft makes the white paper see-through and trims the picture to the ink (for a PNG it asks first). Then click the sheet to place it (a picture 3 in wide, a signature 2 in wide), or drag a box to fit it inside; the proportions are kept. Resize it with its handles like any markup (Shift keeps the proportions). The last picture is kept in this browser, so it is ready next time; Choose another picture… replaces it. Every signature you draw or load is kept in Your signatures (up to eight, newest first): click one to use it, or × to remove it. See What stays in this browser. In the saved PDF a picture is a stamp comment showing the image, so any PDF viewer shows and prints it. A signature drawn here is a picture of your signature, not a certified digital signature. Paste a picture from the clipboard Copy a picture in another program, or take a screenshot to the clipboard, then press Ctrl+V over the sheet. It lands where the pointer is, at most 3 in wide or tall with its proportions kept, as a picture markup you can move and resize. In the saved PDF it is a stamp comment showing the image, like the pictures above. More › Paste a picture in the Edit group does the same from the menu; the browser may ask first. When the clipboard holds no picture, Ctrl+V pastes copied markups as before. Insert a picture from a file Choose More › Insert a picture from a file… in the Edit group (or Insert a picture here… in the right-click menu), and pick a PNG, JPEG or SVG file or a PDF (a PDF with several pages asks which one). You can also drag a PNG, JPEG or SVG file onto the sheet: it lands where you drop it. The picture is a markup, at most 3 in wide or tall: move and resize it like any other. To make a picture part of the page, tick Flatten into the page when saved in Properties. See Save a flattened copy. Make a stamp from a selection Select markups (or page content with the Content tool) and choose Save selection as a stamp… in More, in the right-click menu or Save as a stamp… in Properties. Give it a name. It goes into the tool chest: click it there, then click on any sheet to place everything you selected, together, centered where you click. See The tool chest. Sticky notes Choose Note (N). Click where the note goes. A small note icon appears with a box beside it. Type the note. Click outside or press Ctrl+Enter to keep it; Esc throws it away. Unlike text and callouts, a note is kept even if you type nothing, so you can place the icon now and write later. The note's words are its Note field in Properties. Double-click the icon (or select it and press Enter) to edit them on the sheet. On the sheet you see only the icon. Other PDF viewers show a standard sticky note that opens to the text when clicked. Change the icon's color with Color in Properties. For back-and-forth discussion, use the reply thread on any markup instead: see Statuses and replies.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"what-changed-in-words","scope":"markupcraft","title":"List what changed between revisions","summary":"Compare two plan sets and get a plain list, sheet by sheet, of where drawing and words changed, ready to read, copy or hand to your assistant.","section":"tasks","order":55,"keywords":["what changed","changes","revision list","compare list","plain list","narrate","assistant","delta","revision summary"],"headings":[],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EThe compare tools show the changes on the sheet. This one writes them down.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen both revisions (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/markupcraft/open-a-plan-set\" data-topic=\"markupcraft/open-a-plan-set\"\u003EOpen a plan set\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECompare\u003C/span\u003E tab, then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECompare a whole set\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose the earlier and the later file and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EList the changes\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe list starts with a count (&quot;2 of 14 matched sheets changed; 1 new; 1 dropped&quot;) and then covers each changed sheet, biggest change first: where on the sheet (upper left, centre…), how big, whether lines were added, removed or both, how many markups sit there, and which words appeared or disappeared. On a calibrated sheet the size is in real units as well as inches on the paper.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe list says what is on the page; it does not say what it means. A drawn line moving could be a wall or a leader. Your connected assistant can read the list with the \u003Ccode\u003Erevisions_changes\u003C/code\u003E tool and put it in words, and you can look at the sheet for the rest. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECopy the list\u003C/span\u003E puts it on the clipboard and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECSV\u003C/span\u003E writes one row per change.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESheets are paired by sheet number, as in \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/markupcraft/slip-sheet-a-revised-set\" data-topic=\"markupcraft/slip-sheet-a-revised-set\"\u003ESlip-sheet a revised set\u003C/a\u003E. Changes smaller than a few points are ignored, and text that is part of a picture (a scan) has no words to compare.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"The compare tools show the changes on the sheet. This one writes them down. Open both revisions (see Open a plan set). Open the Compare tab, then Compare a whole set. Choose the earlier and the later file and choose List the changes. The list starts with a count (\"2 of 14 matched sheets changed; 1 new; 1 dropped\") and then covers each changed sheet, biggest change first: where on the sheet (upper left, centre…), how big, whether lines were added, removed or both, how many markups sit there, and which words appeared or disappeared. On a calibrated sheet the size is in real units as well as inches on the paper. The list says what is on the page; it does not say what it means. A drawn line moving could be a wall or a leader. Your connected assistant can read the list with the revisions_changes tool and put it in words, and you can look at the sheet for the rest. Copy the list puts it on the clipboard and CSV writes one row per change. Sheets are paired by sheet number, as in Slip-sheet a revised set. Changes smaller than a few points are ignored, and text that is part of a picture (a scan) has no words to compare.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"calibrate-and-measure","scope":"markupcraft","title":"Calibrate a sheet and measure","summary":"Set each sheet's scale from a preset or a known dimension, then measure lengths, areas with perimeters, and counts.","section":"tasks","order":60,"keywords":["calibrate","scale","set scale","measure","length","distance","dimension","area","square feet","perimeter","count","takeoff","quantity","ruler","arithmetic","calculation","plus","fraction"],"headings":[{"id":"choose-a-scale-from-the-list","text":"Choose a scale from the list","level":2},{"id":"calibrate-from-a-known-dimension","text":"Calibrate from a known dimension","level":2},{"id":"measure-a-length","text":"Measure a length","level":2},{"id":"sketch-to-scale","text":"Sketch to scale","level":2},{"id":"measure-an-angle","text":"Measure an angle","level":2},{"id":"measure-an-area-and-its-perimeter","text":"Measure an area and its perimeter","level":2},{"id":"cut-outs","text":"Cut-outs","level":3},{"id":"count-things","text":"Count things","level":2},{"id":"change-or-remove-a-scale","text":"Change or remove a scale","level":2},{"id":"where-the-numbers-go","text":"Where the numbers go","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003ELengths and areas need to know the sheet&#039;s scale. MarkupCraft keeps a scale \u003Cstrong\u003Eper sheet\u003C/strong\u003E: calibrate each sheet you measure on. Counts need no scale.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe status bar shows the current sheet&#039;s scale, or &quot;Not calibrated&quot;.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"choose-a-scale-from-the-list\"\u003EChoose a scale from the list\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUse this when the PDF is at full size (a 24 × 36 sheet that prints at 24 × 36) and the drawing states its scale.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the scale button in the status bar (&quot;Not calibrated&quot;). The &quot;Sheet scale&quot; box opens.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUnder \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScale\u003C/span\u003E, pick the scale: architectural (1/32&quot; = 1&#039;-0&quot; to 3&quot; = 1&#039;-0&quot;), engineering (1&quot; = 10&#039; to 1&quot; = 100&#039;) or metric (1:1 to 1:1000).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESet scale\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EResult: the status bar shows the scale, for example \u003Ccode\u003E1/4&quot; = 1&#039;-0&quot;\u003C/code\u003E. Architectural scales show lengths in feet and inches, engineering scales in decimal feet, metric scales up to 1:50 in millimeters and from 1:100 in meters.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-warning\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003EWarning\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA preset is only right if the PDF has not been shrunk. A half-size set, or a sheet that was &quot;fit to page&quot; when it was made, measures wrong with a preset. Calibrate from a known dimension instead, and check one dimension either way.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"calibrate-from-a-known-dimension\"\u003ECalibrate from a known dimension\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUse this for half-size sets, scanned sheets, details at odd scales, or to check a preset.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFind a long dimension you trust, or a graphic scale bar.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECalibrate\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMeasure\u003C/span\u003E group (or choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPick two points\u003C/span\u003E in the &quot;Sheet scale&quot; box).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick one end of the dimension, then the other. (Or drag from one end to the other.) Zoom in first for accuracy; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E keeps the line at 45° steps.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe box says how far apart the points are on the paper and asks &quot;How far apart are they really?&quot;. Type the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReal distance\u003C/span\u003E, for example \u003Ccode\u003E24&#039;-0&quot;\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E12&#039; 6 1/2&quot;\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E7.2 m\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E900 mm\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPick how lengths are shown under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShow lengths in\u003C/span\u003E: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFeet and inches\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDecimal feet\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EInches\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMeters\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMillimeters\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESet scale\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EResult: the status bar shows &quot;Calibrated: 24&#039;-0&quot; over 1.00 in&quot; (the real length and the paper length), and the tool you were going to measure with comes back on. Every way of typing a length is in \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/markupcraft/units-and-scales\" data-topic=\"markupcraft/units-and-scales\"\u003EUnits and lengths\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe real distance can also be a small calculation: \u003Ccode\u003E12&#039;6&quot;+3/4&quot;\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E(24&#039;-0&quot;)/3\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E2*450mm\u003C/code\u003E. Use \u003Ccode\u003E+ - * /\u003C/code\u003E and brackets; a number with no unit counts in the unit under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShow lengths in\u003C/span\u003E (feet for \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFeet and inches\u003C/span\u003E). \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESet scale\u003C/span\u003E uses the result.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf MarkupCraft cannot read what you typed, the box says so: &#039;That is not a length MarkupCraft understands. Try 24&#039;-0&quot;, 12&#039; 6 1/2&quot;, 7.2 m or 900 mm.&#039; A calculation it cannot work out says why and where, for example &quot;A bracket is not closed (character 5).&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"measure-a-length\"\u003EMeasure a length\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELength\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"M\"\u003EM\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EM\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the start, then the end. Or drag from start to end. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E keeps it at 45° steps.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EResult: a dimension line with ticks and the length written on it. The status bar repeats it (&quot;Length: 12&#039;-6 1/2&quot; (1/4&quot; = 1&#039;-0&quot;).&quot;), and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E shows it under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELength\u003C/span\u003E. Feet and inches are rounded to the nearest 1/16&quot;.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf the sheet has no scale yet, choosing \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELength\u003C/span\u003E switches to \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECalibrate\u003C/span\u003E and the status bar explains: &quot;Calibrate this sheet first: pick two points a known distance apart, or choose its scale. Then measure.&quot; After you set the scale, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELength\u003C/span\u003E comes back.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"sketch-to-scale\"\u003ESketch to scale\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn a calibrated sheet you can type real lengths while you draw a \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELine\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArrow\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELength\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPolyline\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPolygon\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFree cloud\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArea\u003C/span\u003E or a space:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the first point.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPoint the way the next segment goes and type its length, such as \u003Ccode\u003E16\u003C/code\u003E (in the sheet&#039;s unit), \u003Ccode\u003E12&#039;6&quot;\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E3.5 m\u003C/code\u003E. A box opens by the pointer as you type.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. The point lands that far away, in the pointer&#039;s direction.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo set the direction exactly, add \u003Ccode\u003E&lt;\u003C/code\u003E and an angle in degrees: \u003Ccode\u003E8 &lt; 90\u003C/code\u003E goes 8 feet straight up, \u003Ccode\u003E4&lt;0\u003C/code\u003E to the right, \u003Ccode\u003E10 &lt; 225\u003C/code\u003E down and to the left, as the sheet is seen (turned pages included). Keep typing lengths for the next corners, and finish the shape as usual. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E closes the box without placing a point.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"measure-an-angle\"\u003EMeasure an angle\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAngle\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"X\"\u003EX\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EX\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the end of one arm, then the corner, then the end of the other arm.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EResult: the two arms, a small arc at the corner and the angle in degrees, to a tenth (0° to 180°). An angle needs no scale. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E shows it under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAngle\u003C/span\u003E, and the list, CSV and BCF carry it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArc\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"O\"\u003EO\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EO\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) on a calibrated sheet also shows its length along the curve in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"measure-an-area-and-its-perimeter\"\u003EMeasure an area and its perimeter\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArea\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Q\"\u003EQ\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EQ\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick each corner of the room or region.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFinish with a double-click, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or a right-click (double-tap on touch). \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Delete\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EBackspace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E removes the last corner.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EResult: a shaded outline with the area and &quot;P&quot; with the perimeter in the middle, for example &quot;128.00 sq ft&quot; and &quot;P 48&#039;-0&quot;&quot;. Feet scales give square feet, inches square inches, meters square meters, millimeters square millimeters. An area needs at least three corners.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"cut-outs\"\u003ECut-outs\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo leave out a column, a shaft or a courtyard, select the area and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd a cut-out\u003C/span\u003E in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E. Click the cut-out&#039;s corners inside the area and finish as for an area. The cut-out stays open (unshaded), its area comes off, and its edges are added to the perimeter: a 16&#039; × 8&#039; room with a 4&#039; × 4&#039; cut-out reads &quot;112.00 sq ft&quot; and &quot;P 64&#039;-0&quot;&quot;. Add as many as you need; they cannot overlap each other or cross the area&#039;s outline. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERemove cut-outs\u003C/span\u003E takes them all out, and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E undoes one.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"count-things\"\u003ECount things\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECount\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"U\"\u003EU\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EU\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E, type a \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGroup name\u003C/span\u003E, for example &quot;Floor drains&quot;. (It stays for next time.)\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick each item on the sheet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EResult: each click adds a numbered dot, the first dot carries a tag like &quot;Floor drains: 7&quot;, and the status bar keeps the running total.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EStart another group:\u003C/strong\u003E press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E once (it clears the selection), change the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGroup name\u003C/span\u003E, and click again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAdd to an existing group:\u003C/strong\u003E select the group first, then choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECount\u003C/span\u003E and click.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERemove a mark:\u003C/strong\u003E with the group selected, hold \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and click the dot. Removing the last dot removes the group. On a touch screen without a keyboard, use \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUndo\u003C/span\u003E instead.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERename a group:\u003C/strong\u003E select it and edit \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGroup name\u003C/span\u003E in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA count group belongs to one sheet. Counts on several sheets show as separate groups in the markups list.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"change-or-remove-a-scale\"\u003EChange or remove a scale\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EClick the scale button in the status bar. The box shows the current scale; pick another, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPick two points\u003C/span\u003E, or choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERemove scale\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ELengths and areas already drawn are \u003Cstrong\u003Ere-measured\u003C/strong\u003E with the new scale at once. Undo brings the old scale back.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"where-the-numbers-go\"\u003EWhere the numbers go\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E shows the selected measurement&#039;s values.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMarkups\u003C/span\u003E tab lists each value, and its CSV has a &quot;Measurement&quot; column. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/markupcraft/markups-list-and-export\" data-topic=\"markupcraft/markups-list-and-export\"\u003EThe markups list\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe saved PDF keeps the scale on the page and writes lengths and areas as measurement comments, so other PDF programs that understand measurements can show them. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/markupcraft/what-the-pdf-keeps\" data-topic=\"markupcraft/what-the-pdf-keeps\"\u003EWhat is saved in the PDF\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFor a full quantity takeoff with items, assemblies and prices, use TakeoffCraft: MarkupCraft measures for review, not for estimating.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Lengths and areas need to know the sheet's scale. MarkupCraft keeps a scale per sheet: calibrate each sheet you measure on. Counts need no scale. The status bar shows the current sheet's scale, or \"Not calibrated\". Choose a scale from the list Use this when the PDF is at full size (a 24 × 36 sheet that prints at 24 × 36) and the drawing states its scale. Click the scale button in the status bar (\"Not calibrated\"). The \"Sheet scale\" box opens. Under Scale, pick the scale: architectural (1/32\" = 1'-0\" to 3\" = 1'-0\"), engineering (1\" = 10' to 1\" = 100') or metric (1:1 to 1:1000). Choose Set scale. Result: the status bar shows the scale, for example 1/4\" = 1'-0\". Architectural scales show lengths in feet and inches, engineering scales in decimal feet, metric scales up to 1:50 in millimeters and from 1:100 in meters. A preset is only right if the PDF has not been shrunk. A half-size set, or a sheet that was \"fit to page\" when it was made, measures wrong with a preset. Calibrate from a known dimension instead, and check one dimension either way. Calibrate from a known dimension Use this for half-size sets, scanned sheets, details at odd scales, or to check a preset. Find a long dimension you trust, or a graphic scale bar. Choose Calibrate in the Measure group (or choose Pick two points in the \"Sheet scale\" box). Click one end of the dimension, then the other. (Or drag from one end to the other.) Zoom in first for accuracy; Shift keeps the line at 45° steps. The box says how far apart the points are on the paper and asks \"How far apart are they really?\". Type the Real distance, for example 24'-0\", 12' 6 1/2\", 7.2 m or 900 mm. Pick how lengths are shown under Show lengths in: Feet and inches, Decimal feet, Inches, Meters or Millimeters. Choose Set scale. Result: the status bar shows \"Calibrated: 24'-0\" over 1.00 in\" (the real length and the paper length), and the tool you were going to measure with comes back on. Every way of typing a length is in Units and lengths. The real distance can also be a small calculation: 12'6\"+3/4\", (24'-0\")/3 or 2*450mm. Use + - * / and brackets; a number with no unit counts in the unit under Show lengths in (feet for Feet and inches). Set scale uses the result. If MarkupCraft cannot read what you typed, the box says so: 'That is not a length MarkupCraft understands. Try 24'-0\", 12' 6 1/2\", 7.2 m or 900 mm.' A calculation it cannot work out says why and where, for example \"A bracket is not closed (character 5).\" Measure a length Choose Length (M). Click the start, then the end. Or drag from start to end. Shift keeps it at 45° steps. Result: a dimension line with ticks and the length written on it. The status bar repeats it (\"Length: 12'-6 1/2\" (1/4\" = 1'-0\").\"), and Properties shows it under Length. Feet and inches are rounded to the nearest 1/16\". If the sheet has no scale yet, choosing Length switches to Calibrate and the status bar explains: \"Calibrate this sheet first: pick two points a known distance apart, or choose its scale. Then measure.\" After you set the scale, Length comes back. Sketch to scale On a calibrated sheet you can type real lengths while you draw a Line, Arrow, Length, Polyline, Polygon, Free cloud, Area or a space: Click the first point. Point the way the next segment goes and type its length, such as 16 (in the sheet's unit), 12'6\" or 3.5 m. A box opens by the pointer as you type. Press Enter. The point lands that far away, in the pointer's direction. To set the direction exactly, add \u003C and an angle in degrees: 8 \u003C 90 goes 8 feet straight up, 4\u003C0 to the right, 10 \u003C 225 down and to the left, as the sheet is seen (turned pages included). Keep typing lengths for the next corners, and finish the shape as usual. Esc closes the box without placing a point. Measure an angle Choose Angle (X). Click the end of one arm, then the corner, then the end of the other arm. Result: the two arms, a small arc at the corner and the angle in degrees, to a tenth (0° to 180°). An angle needs no scale. Properties shows it under Angle, and the list, CSV and BCF carry it. An Arc (O) on a calibrated sheet also shows its length along the curve in Properties. Measure an area and its perimeter Choose Area (Q). Click each corner of the room or region. Finish with a double-click, Enter or a right-click (double-tap on touch). Backspace removes the last corner. Result: a shaded outline with the area and \"P\" with the perimeter in the middle, for example \"128.00 sq ft\" and \"P 48'-0\"\". Feet scales give square feet, inches square inches, meters square meters, millimeters square millimeters. An area needs at least three corners. Cut-outs To leave out a column, a shaft or a courtyard, select the area and choose Add a cut-out in Properties. Click the cut-out's corners inside the area and finish as for an area. The cut-out stays open (unshaded), its area comes off, and its edges are added to the perimeter: a 16' × 8' room with a 4' × 4' cut-out reads \"112.00 sq ft\" and \"P 64'-0\"\". Add as many as you need; they cannot overlap each other or cross the area's outline. Remove cut-outs takes them all out, and Ctrl+Z undoes one. Count things Choose Count (U). In Properties, type a Group name, for example \"Floor drains\". (It stays for next time.) Click each item on the sheet. Result: each click adds a numbered dot, the first dot carries a tag like \"Floor drains: 7\", and the status bar keeps the running total. Start another group: press Esc once (it clears the selection), change the Group name, and click again. Add to an existing group: select the group first, then choose Count and click. Remove a mark: with the group selected, hold Shift and click the dot. Removing the last dot removes the group. On a touch screen without a keyboard, use Ctrl+Z or Undo instead. Rename a group: select it and edit Group name in Properties. A count group belongs to one sheet. Counts on several sheets show as separate groups in the markups list. Change or remove a scale Click the scale button in the status bar. The box shows the current scale; pick another, choose Pick two points, or choose Remove scale. Lengths and areas already drawn are re-measured with the new scale at once. Undo brings the old scale back. Where the numbers go Properties shows the selected measurement's values. The Markups tab lists each value, and its CSV has a \"Measurement\" column. See The markups list. The saved PDF keeps the scale on the page and writes lengths and areas as measurement comments, so other PDF programs that understand measurements can show them. See What is saved in the PDF. For a full quantity takeoff with items, assemblies and prices, use TakeoffCraft: MarkupCraft measures for review, not for estimating.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"redline-to-revision","scope":"markupcraft","title":"Send markups to the drafter as a redline","summary":"Write the open markups as a redline file for whoever draws the changes, then load their answers so each markup shows Accepted or Rejected.","section":"tasks","order":60,"keywords":["redline","revision","drafter","accept","reject","answers","cc-redline","revision cloud","apply markups"],"headings":[{"id":"send","text":"Send","level":2},{"id":"answers","text":"Answers","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EReviewing is half the job; the drawing still has to change. The redline file carries each open markup to the drafter and the drafter&#039;s answers carry the result back.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"send\"\u003ESend\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETasks\u003C/span\u003E tab and open \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERedline to revision\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType the revision the markups are against (for example B).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport redline file\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou get a \u003Ccode\u003E.redline.json\u003C/code\u003E file with one item per Open markup: its sheet, where it is (as fractions of the sheet, so a re-plot at another size still lines up, and in points), its words, author and thread, and its task if it has one. Read-only comments from other programs, pictures and legends are left out.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"answers\"\u003EAnswers\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe drafting side answers each item Accepted, Rejected or Done, with a note, in a \u003Ccode\u003E.redline-answers.json\u003C/code\u003E file. Choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELoad answers file…\u003C/span\u003E and pick it: each markup gets that status and the drafter&#039;s note in its thread, and a task whose markup was accepted or done is finished. One undo step takes all the answers back.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe file format is in \u003Ccode\u003Edocs/REDLINE-FORMAT.md\u003C/code\u003E. The CadCraft side (reading the package, accepting or rejecting each item, revision clouds and the log) is built separately.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Reviewing is half the job; the drawing still has to change. The redline file carries each open markup to the drafter and the drafter's answers carry the result back. Send Open the Tasks tab and open Redline to revision. Type the revision the markups are against (for example B). Choose Export redline file. You get a .redline.json file with one item per Open markup: its sheet, where it is (as fractions of the sheet, so a re-plot at another size still lines up, and in points), its words, author and thread, and its task if it has one. Read-only comments from other programs, pictures and legends are left out. Answers The drafting side answers each item Accepted, Rejected or Done, with a note, in a .redline-answers.json file. Choose Load answers file… and pick it: each markup gets that status and the drafter's note in its thread, and a task whose markup was accepted or done is finished. One undo step takes all the answers back. The file format is in docs/REDLINE-FORMAT.md. The CadCraft side (reading the package, accepting or rejecting each item, revision clouds and the log) is built separately.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"markups-that-act","scope":"markupcraft","title":"Make a markup a task","summary":"Give a markup an assignee (a person or your assistant), a due day and a link to the drawing or model element it is about, and see it close when that really changes.","section":"tasks","order":70,"keywords":["task","tasks","assignee","assistant","due","overdue","link","closes","changed","element","drawing area","markups that act","MCP"],"headings":[{"id":"make-a-task","text":"Make a task","level":2},{"id":"watch-for-a-change","text":"Watch for a change","level":2},{"id":"the-list","text":"The list","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EA task is a markup that has to be done by someone. It keeps its place on the sheet and its thread, and it adds a title, an assignee, a due day, a state and, if you want, a link to the thing it is about.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"make-a-task\"\u003EMake a task\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESelect one or more markups.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETasks\u003C/span\u003E tab and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMake selected a task\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFill in the title (it starts from the markup&#039;s text), who it is for (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPerson\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAssistant\u003C/span\u003E, and a name), an optional due day, and what to watch.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAn assistant task is work for the assistant you connected to MarkupCraft (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/markupcraft/use-with-ai-assistants\" data-topic=\"markupcraft/use-with-ai-assistants\"\u003EUse MarkupCraft with an AI assistant\u003C/a\u003E). MarkupCraft contains no assistant of its own: it only lists the work and takes the assistant&#039;s answers. Choosing \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAssigned to\u003C/span\u003E, then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAssistant\u003C/span\u003E in the list shows what is waiting for it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"watch-for-a-change\"\u003EWatch for a change\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe drawing inside the markup.\u003C/strong\u003E MarkupCraft keeps the lines inside the markup&#039;s box as they are now. When you open a newer revision of that sheet (a second file with the same sheet number, or the same page position), choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECheck now\u003C/span\u003E: if the lines in that area differ by more than a hair, the task is closed.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA model element.\u003C/strong\u003E Give the app and the element&#039;s id. Your assistant reads the element with the other app&#039;s own tools and reports it with \u003Ccode\u003Etasks_check\u003C/code\u003E. The first report is kept as the starting state; a later report that differs closes the task. An element that was deleted closes it too.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENothing.\u003C/strong\u003E A plain task you finish by hand.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA closed task is marked Done, its markup becomes Completed, and a note in the thread says what changed. One undo step reopens it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELimit:\u003C/strong\u003E the check assumes the newer revision uses the same page origin and page size. A sheet re-plotted at another size will not match, so its tasks do not close. Sheets are paired by sheet number, else by page position; MarkupCraft does not scale one revision to fit the other.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ERe-plotting the same drawing with a new date stamp, or lines that moved less than 1.5 points, does not count as a change. If no newer revision is open, the task stays open and the status bar says so.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-list\"\u003EThe list\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETasks\u003C/span\u003E list can be filtered by state and by who it is for, including \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOverdue\u003C/span\u003E. A task past its due day shows in the Problems panel as a warning. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECSV\u003C/span\u003E exports the list.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETasks are saved in the PDF with the markup, and they travel through Live like the other markup properties.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"A task is a markup that has to be done by someone. It keeps its place on the sheet and its thread, and it adds a title, an assignee, a due day, a state and, if you want, a link to the thing it is about. Make a task Select one or more markups. Open the Tasks tab and choose Make selected a task. Fill in the title (it starts from the markup's text), who it is for (Person or Assistant, and a name), an optional due day, and what to watch. An assistant task is work for the assistant you connected to MarkupCraft (see Use MarkupCraft with an AI assistant). MarkupCraft contains no assistant of its own: it only lists the work and takes the assistant's answers. Choosing Assigned to, then Assistant in the list shows what is waiting for it. Watch for a change The drawing inside the markup. MarkupCraft keeps the lines inside the markup's box as they are now. When you open a newer revision of that sheet (a second file with the same sheet number, or the same page position), choose Check now: if the lines in that area differ by more than a hair, the task is closed. A model element. Give the app and the element's id. Your assistant reads the element with the other app's own tools and reports it with tasks_check. The first report is kept as the starting state; a later report that differs closes the task. An element that was deleted closes it too. Nothing. A plain task you finish by hand. A closed task is marked Done, its markup becomes Completed, and a note in the thread says what changed. One undo step reopens it. Limit: the check assumes the newer revision uses the same page origin and page size. A sheet re-plotted at another size will not match, so its tasks do not close. Sheets are paired by sheet number, else by page position; MarkupCraft does not scale one revision to fit the other. Re-plotting the same drawing with a new date stamp, or lines that moved less than 1.5 points, does not count as a change. If no newer revision is open, the task stays open and the status bar says so. The list The Tasks list can be filtered by state and by who it is for, including Overdue. A task past its due day shows in the Problems panel as a warning. CSV exports the list. Tasks are saved in the PDF with the markup, and they travel through Live like the other markup properties.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"statuses-and-replies","scope":"markupcraft","title":"Statuses and replies","summary":"Mark each comment Open, Accepted, Rejected or Completed, discuss it in a reply thread, and pass the thread on in the PDF.","section":"tasks","order":70,"keywords":["status","review status","accepted","rejected","completed","open","reply","replies","thread","comment","conversation","discussion","respond","back-check"],"headings":[{"id":"set-a-status","text":"Set a status","level":2},{"id":"reply","text":"Reply","level":2},{"id":"add-a-photo","text":"Add a photo","level":2},{"id":"passing-the-review-on","text":"Passing the review on","level":2},{"id":"find-what-is-still-open","text":"Find what is still open","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EEvery markup has a \u003Cstrong\u003Estatus\u003C/strong\u003E and a \u003Cstrong\u003Ereply thread\u003C/strong\u003E. Together they carry a review round: the reviewer marks up, the designer replies and sets statuses, the reviewer back-checks.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"set-a-status\"\u003ESet a status\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESelect the markup (or several).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E, pick a \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStatus\u003C/span\u003E: Open, Accepted, Rejected or Completed.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EResult: the status shows as a chip in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMarkups\u003C/span\u003E list, and a line is added to the thread: &quot;Status set to Accepted by Ana Ruiz.&quot; With several markups selected, the status box says &quot;Mixed&quot; until you pick one, and then it changes all of them.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENew markups start as Open. Pasted or duplicated markups also start as Open.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"reply\"\u003EReply\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESelect the markup.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E, find &quot;Replies&quot; below the style settings.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWrite a reply\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReply\u003C/span\u003E, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Return\"\u003E⌘ Return\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Enter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EResult: the reply is listed with your name and the date and time, and the heading counts it: &quot;Replies (3)&quot;. The \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMarkups\u003C/span\u003E list shows a reply count on the row.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EStatus changes are listed in the same thread and marked as status entries, so the history reads in order.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIf you start typing a reply and click something else, your draft is kept until you come back to that markup (until you close the set).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIf your name is not set, MarkupCraft asks for it before sending.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EYou can reply to read-only comments that came with the file, too.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA reply cannot be edited or deleted once sent. Right after sending, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E takes it back; after that, add a new reply that corrects it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"add-a-photo\"\u003EAdd a photo\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EBelow the reply box, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd photo\u003C/span\u003E adds a picture to the thread: a PNG or JPEG from this computer, or, on a tablet or phone, a photo you take there and then. Whatever you typed in the reply box becomes its caption. The thread shows a thumbnail; choose it to see the photo full size. Large photos are scaled down to 1600 pixels on their longer side.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn the saved PDF each photo also travels as an attached file beside the markup (a paperclip), so other PDF viewers can open it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"passing-the-review-on\"\u003EPassing the review on\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThere is no shared online session yet: the review travels in the PDF.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave\u003C/span\u003E. The copy holds every markup with its status and its replies.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESend that copy the way you send drawings today.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe next person opens it in MarkupCraft, replies, sets statuses and saves again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOther PDF programs see the replies as replies to the comment, and the statuses as review states (Accepted, Rejected, Completed; Open shows as no state). Their own replies and review states come back into MarkupCraft when you open the file: the newest status wins.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf two people mark up separate copies at the same time, there is no merge. You can open both copies in one set and copy a few markups across with \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command C\"\u003E⌘C\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+C\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command V\"\u003E⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, but a pasted copy is a new markup: it carries your name, a new date and the status Open, and lands slightly offset. Agree who marks up which sheets to avoid this.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"find-what-is-still-open\"\u003EFind what is still open\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMarkups\u003C/span\u003E tab, set \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStatus\u003C/span\u003E to &quot;Open&quot; to list only open items, and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAuthor\u003C/span\u003E to one reviewer. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/markupcraft/markups-list-and-export\" data-topic=\"markupcraft/markups-list-and-export\"\u003EThe markups list\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EStatus (Open, Accepted, Rejected, Completed) is about the comment. A punch item has its own separate status for the work in the field; see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/markupcraft/punch-list\" data-topic=\"markupcraft/punch-list\"\u003EMake a punch list\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Every markup has a status and a reply thread. Together they carry a review round: the reviewer marks up, the designer replies and sets statuses, the reviewer back-checks. Set a status Select the markup (or several). In Properties, pick a Status: Open, Accepted, Rejected or Completed. Result: the status shows as a chip in the Markups list, and a line is added to the thread: \"Status set to Accepted by Ana Ruiz.\" With several markups selected, the status box says \"Mixed\" until you pick one, and then it changes all of them. New markups start as Open. Pasted or duplicated markups also start as Open. Reply Select the markup. In Properties, find \"Replies\" below the style settings. Type in Write a reply. Choose Reply, or press Ctrl+Enter. Result: the reply is listed with your name and the date and time, and the heading counts it: \"Replies (3)\". The Markups list shows a reply count on the row. Status changes are listed in the same thread and marked as status entries, so the history reads in order. If you start typing a reply and click something else, your draft is kept until you come back to that markup (until you close the set). If your name is not set, MarkupCraft asks for it before sending. You can reply to read-only comments that came with the file, too. A reply cannot be edited or deleted once sent. Right after sending, Ctrl+Z takes it back; after that, add a new reply that corrects it. Add a photo Below the reply box, Add photo adds a picture to the thread: a PNG or JPEG from this computer, or, on a tablet or phone, a photo you take there and then. Whatever you typed in the reply box becomes its caption. The thread shows a thumbnail; choose it to see the photo full size. Large photos are scaled down to 1600 pixels on their longer side. In the saved PDF each photo also travels as an attached file beside the markup (a paperclip), so other PDF viewers can open it. Passing the review on There is no shared online session yet: the review travels in the PDF. Choose Save. The copy holds every markup with its status and its replies. Send that copy the way you send drawings today. The next person opens it in MarkupCraft, replies, sets statuses and saves again. Other PDF programs see the replies as replies to the comment, and the statuses as review states (Accepted, Rejected, Completed; Open shows as no state). Their own replies and review states come back into MarkupCraft when you open the file: the newest status wins. If two people mark up separate copies at the same time, there is no merge. You can open both copies in one set and copy a few markups across with Ctrl+C and Ctrl+V, but a pasted copy is a new markup: it carries your name, a new date and the status Open, and lands slightly offset. Agree who marks up which sheets to avoid this. Find what is still open In the Markups tab, set Status to \"Open\" to list only open items, and Author to one reviewer. See The markups list. Status (Open, Accepted, Rejected, Completed) is about the comment. A punch item has its own separate status for the work in the field; see Make a punch list.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"punch-list","scope":"markupcraft","title":"Make a punch list","summary":"Turn markups into numbered punch items with an assignee, a due date and a status, and export the list.","section":"tasks","order":80,"keywords":["punch list","punch","snag list","deficiency","field issues","observation","assign","assignee","due date","closeout","walkthrough","number"],"headings":[{"id":"add-items","text":"Add items","level":2},{"id":"fill-in-the-details","text":"Fill in the details","level":2},{"id":"take-an-item-off","text":"Take an item off","level":2},{"id":"export-the-list","text":"Export the list","level":2},{"id":"on-site-with-a-tablet","text":"On site with a tablet","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EA punch item is a markup with a number, an assignee, a due date and an item status. The number is drawn on the sheet next to the markup, so the printed sheet and the list match.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"add-items\"\u003EAdd items\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMark up each issue on the sheets: a cloud, a callout, a note, anything.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESelect one or more of them (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/markupcraft/select-and-edit\" data-topic=\"markupcraft/select-and-edit\"\u003ESelect, move and change markups\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEither open the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPunch list\u003C/span\u003E tab and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd selected to the punch list\u003C/span\u003E, or choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd to the punch list\u003C/span\u003E in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EResult: each markup gets the next number (1, 2, 3…), in sheet order and then in the order they were drawn. A numbered badge appears on the sheet, and the status bar says &quot;Added 3 items to the punch list.&quot; Markups that already have a number, and read-only comments, are skipped.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"fill-in-the-details\"\u003EFill in the details\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPunch list\u003C/span\u003E tab, each item shows its number, subject, text, sheet and status, with three fields under it. The same fields are in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E under &quot;Punch list&quot; when the markup is selected:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAssignee\u003C/span\u003E: a name or trade, as free text.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDue\u003C/span\u003E: a date, from the date picker.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EItem status\u003C/span\u003E: Open, In progress, Ready for review or Closed.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EClick an item&#039;s heading to jump to it on its sheet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe item status is separate from the markup&#039;s review status (Open, Accepted, Rejected, Completed). Use the item status for the work, and the review status for the comment.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"take-an-item-off\"\u003ETake an item off\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESelect the markup and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETake off the punch list\u003C/span\u003E in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E. The markup stays; only the number goes. While the set is open, numbers are not reused: the next item still gets the next new number, so a gap means an item was taken off. When a file is opened again, numbering carries on after the highest number in it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"export-the-list\"\u003EExport the list\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport CSV\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPunch list\u003C/span\u003E tab. You get &quot;&lt;name&gt; punch list.csv&quot; with the columns No., Sheet, File, Page, Item, Comment, Assignee, Due, Status, Markup status, Author and Created, in number order. Open it in any spreadsheet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe punch numbers, assignees, due dates and item statuses are also saved inside the marked-up PDF, so they come back when you reopen it. Other PDF viewers show the number badge as part of the markup. The markups list&#039;s BCF export carries the assignee and due date too (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/markupcraft/bcf-basics\" data-topic=\"markupcraft/bcf-basics\"\u003EBCF basics\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"on-site-with-a-tablet\"\u003EOn site with a tablet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOpen the set on an iPad, walk the job, and drop a \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENote\u003C/span\u003E or draw a \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECloud\u003C/span\u003E at each issue. Add them to the punch list as you go, and save before you leave. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/markupcraft/review-on-a-tablet\" data-topic=\"markupcraft/review-on-a-tablet\"\u003EReview on an iPad or touch screen\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA punch list as a formatted PDF or spreadsheet report, and photos attached to items, are not built yet.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"A punch item is a markup with a number, an assignee, a due date and an item status. The number is drawn on the sheet next to the markup, so the printed sheet and the list match. Add items Mark up each issue on the sheets: a cloud, a callout, a note, anything. Select one or more of them (see Select, move and change markups). Either open the Punch list tab and choose Add selected to the punch list, or choose Add to the punch list in Properties. Result: each markup gets the next number (1, 2, 3…), in sheet order and then in the order they were drawn. A numbered badge appears on the sheet, and the status bar says \"Added 3 items to the punch list.\" Markups that already have a number, and read-only comments, are skipped. Fill in the details In the Punch list tab, each item shows its number, subject, text, sheet and status, with three fields under it. The same fields are in Properties under \"Punch list\" when the markup is selected: Assignee: a name or trade, as free text. Due: a date, from the date picker. Item status: Open, In progress, Ready for review or Closed. Click an item's heading to jump to it on its sheet. The item status is separate from the markup's review status (Open, Accepted, Rejected, Completed). Use the item status for the work, and the review status for the comment. Take an item off Select the markup and choose Take off the punch list in Properties. The markup stays; only the number goes. While the set is open, numbers are not reused: the next item still gets the next new number, so a gap means an item was taken off. When a file is opened again, numbering carries on after the highest number in it. Export the list Choose Export CSV in the Punch list tab. You get \"\u003Cname\u003E punch list.csv\" with the columns No., Sheet, File, Page, Item, Comment, Assignee, Due, Status, Markup status, Author and Created, in number order. Open it in any spreadsheet. The punch numbers, assignees, due dates and item statuses are also saved inside the marked-up PDF, so they come back when you reopen it. Other PDF viewers show the number badge as part of the markup. The markups list's BCF export carries the assignee and due date too (see BCF basics). On site with a tablet Open the set on an iPad, walk the job, and drop a Note or draw a Cloud at each issue. Add them to the punch list as you go, and save before you leave. See Review on an iPad or touch screen. A punch list as a formatted PDF or spreadsheet report, and photos attached to items, are not built yet.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"field-records","scope":"markupcraft","title":"Track RFIs, submittals and daily logs","summary":"Put RFIs, submittals and daily logs on the sheets, with numbers, assignees, due dates and threads; filter what is overdue; export CSV or BCF; work offline and merge copies.","section":"tasks","order":85,"keywords":["rfi","request for information","submittal","daily log","field report","site diary","construction","overdue","due date","assigned to me","thread","attachment","merge","offline","tablet","bcf","csv","field"],"headings":[{"id":"add-one","text":"Add one","level":2},{"id":"answer-and-track","text":"Answer and track","level":2},{"id":"find-what-matters","text":"Find what matters","level":2},{"id":"export","text":"Export","level":2},{"id":"save-offline-and-merging-copies","text":"Save, offline and merging copies","level":2},{"id":"limits","text":"Limits","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EField\u003C/span\u003E tab keeps three kinds of record beside your markups. Each is tied to a sheet; an RFI or a submittal also has a pin at the place on the sheet it is about.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERFI\u003C/strong\u003E: a question for the design team. Statuses: Open, Answered, Closed.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESubmittal\u003C/strong\u003E: something to be reviewed before work goes ahead. Statuses: Open, In review, Approved, Approved as noted, Revise and resubmit, Closed.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDaily log\u003C/strong\u003E: the day&#039;s weather, the number of people on site and the work done. Statuses: Draft, Final.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEvery record has a number of its own kind (RFI-0001, SUB-0001, LOG-0001), a title, text, an assignee, a due date, a thread of replies and a list of attachment names.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"add-one\"\u003EAdd one\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EField\u003C/span\u003E tab and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERFI\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESubmittal\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETap or click the sheet where it belongs. A pin appears and the record opens in the tab.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFill in the title, the question or description, who it is assigned to and when it is due.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA daily log is added to the open sheet without a pin. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlace pin\u003C/span\u003E puts one on it later; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMove pin\u003C/span\u003E moves any pin. Tapping a pin on the sheet opens its record.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"answer-and-track\"\u003EAnswer and track\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChange the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStatus\u003C/span\u003E and the change is logged in the thread. Write a reply and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESend reply\u003C/span\u003E (Ctrl+Enter also sends). Attachments are names only: type one or choose files, and the list tells the team what goes with the record. The files themselves stay where they are.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA record whose due date has passed and that is still waiting (an RFI that is not Answered or Closed, a submittal that is not decided) is marked \u003Cstrong\u003EOverdue\u003C/strong\u003E, in red, on the list and on its pin.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"find-what-matters\"\u003EFind what matters\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe filters sit above the list: type, status, due date (overdue, due in 7 days, has or has no due date), this sheet or all sheets, a text search, and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAssigned to me\u003C/span\u003E, which compares the assignee with the name MarkupCraft knows you by (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/markupcraft/set-your-name\" data-topic=\"markupcraft/set-your-name\"\u003ESet your name\u003C/a\u003E). The line under the filters counts what is shown and what is overdue.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"export\"\u003EExport\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECSV\u003C/span\u003E writes the listed records as a spreadsheet. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBCF\u003C/span\u003E writes them as BCF topics, each with a snapshot of its place on the sheet, so other BCF tools can list them. Both follow the filters.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"save-offline-and-merging-copies\"\u003ESave, offline and merging copies\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ERecords are saved inside the PDF when you save it, and they come back when you open it. Nothing needs a network: add, edit, reply and save on a tablet in a basement, then bring the PDF back. Older PDFs, and PDFs from other programs, open as they always did.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo bring two copies together, open one and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMerge\u003C/span\u003E, then pick the other copy of the same PDF. Records are joined by their ids:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ea record only one copy has is added;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ea record in both takes its fields (title, text, status, assignee, due date) from the copy changed last, as a whole, so if two people changed different fields of the same record, the later copy&#039;s version wins; threads and attachment names from both copies are joined;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eif both copies numbered a new record the same, the one that arrives gets the next free number;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ea record removed in one copy stays removed unless it was edited after.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA merge is one undo step.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"limits\"\u003ELimits\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERecords are not shared by Live TeamWork yet; use Merge.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThere are no teams, notifications or cloud projects for records in this version.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPages deleted from the PDF take their records with them (the page confirmation counts them).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"The Field tab keeps three kinds of record beside your markups. Each is tied to a sheet; an RFI or a submittal also has a pin at the place on the sheet it is about. RFI: a question for the design team. Statuses: Open, Answered, Closed. Submittal: something to be reviewed before work goes ahead. Statuses: Open, In review, Approved, Approved as noted, Revise and resubmit, Closed. Daily log: the day's weather, the number of people on site and the work done. Statuses: Draft, Final. Every record has a number of its own kind (RFI-0001, SUB-0001, LOG-0001), a title, text, an assignee, a due date, a thread of replies and a list of attachment names. Add one Open the Field tab and choose RFI or Submittal. Tap or click the sheet where it belongs. A pin appears and the record opens in the tab. Fill in the title, the question or description, who it is assigned to and when it is due. A daily log is added to the open sheet without a pin. Place pin puts one on it later; Move pin moves any pin. Tapping a pin on the sheet opens its record. Answer and track Change the Status and the change is logged in the thread. Write a reply and choose Send reply (Ctrl+Enter also sends). Attachments are names only: type one or choose files, and the list tells the team what goes with the record. The files themselves stay where they are. A record whose due date has passed and that is still waiting (an RFI that is not Answered or Closed, a submittal that is not decided) is marked Overdue, in red, on the list and on its pin. Find what matters The filters sit above the list: type, status, due date (overdue, due in 7 days, has or has no due date), this sheet or all sheets, a text search, and Assigned to me, which compares the assignee with the name MarkupCraft knows you by (see Set your name). The line under the filters counts what is shown and what is overdue. Export CSV writes the listed records as a spreadsheet. BCF writes them as BCF topics, each with a snapshot of its place on the sheet, so other BCF tools can list them. Both follow the filters. Save, offline and merging copies Records are saved inside the PDF when you save it, and they come back when you open it. Nothing needs a network: add, edit, reply and save on a tablet in a basement, then bring the PDF back. Older PDFs, and PDFs from other programs, open as they always did. To bring two copies together, open one and choose Merge, then pick the other copy of the same PDF. Records are joined by their ids: a record only one copy has is added; a record in both takes its fields (title, text, status, assignee, due date) from the copy changed last, as a whole, so if two people changed different fields of the same record, the later copy's version wins; threads and attachment names from both copies are joined; if both copies numbered a new record the same, the one that arrives gets the next free number; a record removed in one copy stays removed unless it was edited after. A merge is one undo step. Limits Records are not shared by Live TeamWork yet; use Merge. There are no teams, notifications or cloud projects for records in this version. Pages deleted from the PDF take their records with them (the page confirmation counts them).","lang":"en"},{"slug":"voice-notes-and-decisions","scope":"markupcraft","title":"Pin voice notes and decisions on a sheet","summary":"Record a short voice note, or write down a decision and what it replaces, and keep it pinned on the plan.","section":"tasks","order":90,"keywords":["voice note","record","microphone","audio","decision","decided","superseded","site visit","pin","note"],"headings":[{"id":"a-voice-note","text":"A voice note","level":2},{"id":"a-decision","text":"A decision","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EBoth are notes on the sheet, so they show in the Markups list, in the thread and in other PDF programs as sticky notes.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"a-voice-note\"\u003EA voice note\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EShow the sheet and open the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENotes\u003C/span\u003E tab.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERecord a voice note\u003C/span\u003E. The browser asks to use the microphone the first time.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStop recording\u003C/span\u003E. Recordings stop by themselves at two minutes.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe note is pinned at the centre of the sheet: drag it where it belongs, and type a caption in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlay\u003C/span\u003E in the list plays it. The recording is saved inside the PDF.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThis is recording only. MarkupCraft does not turn speech into text, and neither does an assistant connected through the tools: it can see that a voice note exists, how long it is, who made it and its caption, but it cannot hear it. Type the words that matter into the caption.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"a-decision\"\u003EA decision\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPin a decision\u003C/span\u003E, write it in a sentence or two and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPin it\u003C/span\u003E. If it replaces an earlier decision, pick that one in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EIt replaces\u003C/span\u003E: the old one stays on the sheet, marked Replaced, and points to the new one. One undo step takes it back.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Both are notes on the sheet, so they show in the Markups list, in the thread and in other PDF programs as sticky notes. A voice note Show the sheet and open the Notes tab. Choose Record a voice note. The browser asks to use the microphone the first time. Choose Stop recording. Recordings stop by themselves at two minutes. The note is pinned at the centre of the sheet: drag it where it belongs, and type a caption in Properties. Play in the list plays it. The recording is saved inside the PDF. This is recording only. MarkupCraft does not turn speech into text, and neither does an assistant connected through the tools: it can see that a voice note exists, how long it is, who made it and its caption, but it cannot hear it. Type the words that matter into the caption. A decision Choose Pin a decision, write it in a sentence or two and choose Pin it. If it replaces an earlier decision, pick that one in It replaces: the old one stays on the sheet, marked Replaced, and points to the new one. One undo step takes it back.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"markups-list-and-export","scope":"markupcraft","title":"The markups list, filters and exports","summary":"See every markup in the set, filter by sheet, author, status and type, jump to one, and export the list as CSV or BCF.","section":"tasks","order":90,"keywords":["markups list","list","summary","xlsx","spreadsheet workbook","workbook","pdf report","import bcf","filter","sort","report","export","csv","spreadsheet","bcf","bcfzip","issues","comments list","jump"],"headings":[{"id":"open-the-list","text":"Open the list","level":2},{"id":"jump-to-a-markup","text":"Jump to a markup","level":2},{"id":"filter","text":"Filter","level":2},{"id":"export-to-csv","text":"Export to CSV","level":2},{"id":"a-legend-on-the-sheet","text":"A legend on the sheet","level":2},{"id":"your-own-columns-and-sorting","text":"Your own columns, and sorting","level":2},{"id":"export-a-summary-workbook-or-pdf","text":"Export a summary: workbook or PDF","level":2},{"id":"export-to-bcf","text":"Export to BCF","level":2},{"id":"import-bcf","text":"Import BCF","level":2},{"id":"what-the-list-does-not-do-yet","text":"What the list does not do yet","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"open-the-list\"\u003EOpen the list\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChoose the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMarkups\u003C/span\u003E tab on the right. Every markup of every open file is listed, in sheet order and then in the order they were made. Each row shows:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ean icon in the markup&#039;s color, its punch number if it has one (&quot;#4&quot;), and its subject,\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eits text, stamp wording or measured value, and its comment,\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ethe sheet, the author and the date,\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eits status (or &quot;Read-only&quot; for comments that came with the file), and a reply count when it has replies.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe line above the list counts them: &quot;38 markups&quot;, or &quot;12 of 38 markups&quot; when filtered.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"jump-to-a-markup\"\u003EJump to a markup\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EClick a row. MarkupCraft shows that sheet, selects the markup, and zooms to it when it is off-screen or too small to see. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E has its details and thread when you switch back to that tab.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"filter\"\u003EFilter\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFour menus above the list. They combine.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMenu\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EChoices\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESheet\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAll sheets, This sheet (follows you as you change sheets), or any sheet that has markups\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAuthor\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAnyone, or each author in the set\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStatus\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAny status, Open, Accepted, Rejected, Completed\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EType\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAny type, or each kind of markup in the set\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EExamples:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWhat is still open on this sheet: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESheet\u003C/span\u003E &quot;This sheet&quot;, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStatus\u003C/span\u003E &quot;Open&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOne consultant&#039;s comments: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAuthor\u003C/span\u003E their name.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAll measurements: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EType\u003C/span\u003E &quot;Length&quot; or &quot;Area&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe list shows up to 2000 rows; narrow it with a filter in very large sets.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"export-to-csv\"\u003EExport to CSV\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECSV\u003C/span\u003E at the top of the tab. You get &quot;&lt;name&gt; markups.csv&quot; with \u003Cstrong\u003Ethe markups as currently filtered\u003C/strong\u003E, one per row, with these columns:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPunch no., Sheet, File, Page, Type, Subject, Text, Comment, Measurement, Author, Created, Modified, Status, Replies, Last reply, Color, Read-only.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E&quot;Text&quot; holds the words of text boxes and callouts, a stamp&#039;s wording, or a count group&#039;s name.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E&quot;Measurement&quot; holds lengths, &quot;area, perimeter&quot; for areas, and &quot;group: n&quot; for counts.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDates are full timestamps in UTC.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo export everything, set every filter back to its first choice first.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"a-legend-on-the-sheet\"\u003EA legend on the sheet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELegend\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMarkups\u003C/span\u003E tab puts a legend at the top right of what you see: a box listing each kind of markup on the sheet (by subject and color, such as &quot;Cloud&quot;, &quot;Question&quot; or &quot;Outlets&quot;) with how many there are. A count group counts its marks. The legend keeps counting as you draw, move or delete markups, and it prints and saves with the counts of that moment.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EMove and resize it like any markup. In its \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E, change its \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETitle\u003C/span\u003E, or set \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECounts\u003C/span\u003E to \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EThe whole set\u003C/span\u003E to count every sheet. Spaces, other legends and comments from other programs are not counted.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"your-own-columns-and-sorting\"\u003EYour own columns, and sorting\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EColumns\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMarkups\u003C/span\u003E tab adds columns of your own to every markup: a \u003Cstrong\u003Etext\u003C/strong\u003E, a \u003Cstrong\u003Echoice\u003C/strong\u003E (such as Trade: Mechanical, Electrical, Plumbing), a \u003Cstrong\u003Enumber\u003C/strong\u003E (such as Cost) or a \u003Cstrong\u003Edate\u003C/strong\u003E. Give each a name, pick its kind, list the choices for a choice column, and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave columns\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFill them in under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E: each column has a field there. A number column takes numbers only, a date column a date.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe list shows the values under each markup, the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EColumn\u003C/span\u003E filter picks one value (or the markups where it is empty), and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESort\u003C/span\u003E orders the list by a column, by author, status, kind or date, instead of by sheet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ECSV and XLSX exports carry the columns after the usual ones. The columns are saved with the PDF and come back when you reopen it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"export-a-summary-workbook-or-pdf\"\u003EExport a summary: workbook or PDF\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EXLSX\u003C/span\u003E downloads &quot;&lt;name&gt; markup summary.xlsx&quot; with three sheets: \u003Cstrong\u003EMarkups\u003C/strong\u003E (one row per listed markup, the same columns as the CSV), \u003Cstrong\u003EBy sheet\u003C/strong\u003E (how many markups each sheet has, open and closed, and how many of each kind, with totals) and \u003Cstrong\u003EPunch list\u003C/strong\u003E (when there are punch items).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPDF\u003C/span\u003E downloads &quot;&lt;name&gt; markup summary.pdf&quot;: a report to print or send, with the set&#039;s name, the date and a table of the listed markups (number, sheet, kind, subject, text or comment, measured value, author, status and the latest reply). Long text wraps; the table header repeats on each page.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EBoth take the list as it is filtered, so filter first to report one sheet, one author or the open items only.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"export-to-bcf\"\u003EExport to BCF\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBCF\u003C/span\u003E. MarkupCraft makes a picture of each listed markup on its sheet (&quot;Making snapshots for BCF (3 of 12)…&quot;), then downloads &quot;&lt;name&gt; markups.bcfzip&quot;. Each markup becomes one BCF topic with its status, author, dates, replies as comments, assignee and due date from its punch item, and the snapshot.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUse it to bring drawing review comments into BIM coordination tools that read BCF. What carries over, and what does not, is in \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/markupcraft/bcf-basics\" data-topic=\"markupcraft/bcf-basics\"\u003EBCF basics\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"import-bcf\"\u003EImport BCF\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EImport BCF\u003C/span\u003E reads a .bcfzip (or .bcf) file from a BIM tool:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIssues that came from MarkupCraft&#039;s own BCF export find their markups again: new replies are added to the thread, and a new status (Closed counts as Completed) is set. Replies already there are not added twice.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOther issues become notes, with their title, description, author and comments. A note goes on the sheet whose number or name the issue&#039;s labels give, else on the open sheet; a BIM camera has no place on paper, so such notes are stacked at the top left of the sheet for you to move.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe whole import is one step: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E takes it back. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/markupcraft/bcf-basics\" data-topic=\"markupcraft/bcf-basics\"\u003EBCF basics\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-the-list-does-not-do-yet\"\u003EWhat the list does not do yet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EGrouping the list (sort, then filter, instead).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEditing values in the list (edit in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E; punch fields are editable in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPunch list\u003C/span\u003E tab).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"Open the list Choose the Markups tab on the right. Every markup of every open file is listed, in sheet order and then in the order they were made. Each row shows: an icon in the markup's color, its punch number if it has one (\"#4\"), and its subject, its text, stamp wording or measured value, and its comment, the sheet, the author and the date, its status (or \"Read-only\" for comments that came with the file), and a reply count when it has replies. The line above the list counts them: \"38 markups\", or \"12 of 38 markups\" when filtered. Jump to a markup Click a row. MarkupCraft shows that sheet, selects the markup, and zooms to it when it is off-screen or too small to see. Properties has its details and thread when you switch back to that tab. Filter Four menus above the list. They combine. Menu Choices Sheet All sheets, This sheet (follows you as you change sheets), or any sheet that has markups Author Anyone, or each author in the set Status Any status, Open, Accepted, Rejected, Completed Type Any type, or each kind of markup in the set Examples: What is still open on this sheet: Sheet \"This sheet\", Status \"Open\". One consultant's comments: Author their name. All measurements: Type \"Length\" or \"Area\". The list shows up to 2000 rows; narrow it with a filter in very large sets. Export to CSV Choose CSV at the top of the tab. You get \"\u003Cname\u003E markups.csv\" with the markups as currently filtered, one per row, with these columns: Punch no., Sheet, File, Page, Type, Subject, Text, Comment, Measurement, Author, Created, Modified, Status, Replies, Last reply, Color, Read-only. \"Text\" holds the words of text boxes and callouts, a stamp's wording, or a count group's name. \"Measurement\" holds lengths, \"area, perimeter\" for areas, and \"group: n\" for counts. Dates are full timestamps in UTC. To export everything, set every filter back to its first choice first. A legend on the sheet Legend in the Markups tab puts a legend at the top right of what you see: a box listing each kind of markup on the sheet (by subject and color, such as \"Cloud\", \"Question\" or \"Outlets\") with how many there are. A count group counts its marks. The legend keeps counting as you draw, move or delete markups, and it prints and saves with the counts of that moment. Move and resize it like any markup. In its Properties, change its Title, or set Counts to The whole set to count every sheet. Spaces, other legends and comments from other programs are not counted. Your own columns, and sorting Columns in the Markups tab adds columns of your own to every markup: a text, a choice (such as Trade: Mechanical, Electrical, Plumbing), a number (such as Cost) or a date. Give each a name, pick its kind, list the choices for a choice column, and choose Save columns. Fill them in under Properties: each column has a field there. A number column takes numbers only, a date column a date. The list shows the values under each markup, the Column filter picks one value (or the markups where it is empty), and Sort orders the list by a column, by author, status, kind or date, instead of by sheet. CSV and XLSX exports carry the columns after the usual ones. The columns are saved with the PDF and come back when you reopen it. Export a summary: workbook or PDF XLSX downloads \"\u003Cname\u003E markup summary.xlsx\" with three sheets: Markups (one row per listed markup, the same columns as the CSV), By sheet (how many markups each sheet has, open and closed, and how many of each kind, with totals) and Punch list (when there are punch items). PDF downloads \"\u003Cname\u003E markup summary.pdf\": a report to print or send, with the set's name, the date and a table of the listed markups (number, sheet, kind, subject, text or comment, measured value, author, status and the latest reply). Long text wraps; the table header repeats on each page. Both take the list as it is filtered, so filter first to report one sheet, one author or the open items only. Export to BCF Choose BCF. MarkupCraft makes a picture of each listed markup on its sheet (\"Making snapshots for BCF (3 of 12)…\"), then downloads \"\u003Cname\u003E markups.bcfzip\". Each markup becomes one BCF topic with its status, author, dates, replies as comments, assignee and due date from its punch item, and the snapshot. Use it to bring drawing review comments into BIM coordination tools that read BCF. What carries over, and what does not, is in BCF basics. Import BCF Import BCF reads a .bcfzip (or .bcf) file from a BIM tool: Issues that came from MarkupCraft's own BCF export find their markups again: new replies are added to the thread, and a new status (Closed counts as Completed) is set. Replies already there are not added twice. Other issues become notes, with their title, description, author and comments. A note goes on the sheet whose number or name the issue's labels give, else on the open sheet; a BIM camera has no place on paper, so such notes are stacked at the top left of the sheet for you to move. The whole import is one step: Ctrl+Z takes it back. See BCF basics. What the list does not do yet Grouping the list (sort, then filter, instead). Editing values in the list (edit in Properties; punch fields are editable in the Punch list tab).","lang":"en"},{"slug":"layers","scope":"markupcraft","title":"Markup layers","summary":"Put markups on named layers, then show, hide or lock each layer; other PDF viewers can switch them too.","section":"tasks","order":95,"keywords":["layers","layer","hide","show","lock","group markups","discipline","mechanical","electrical","optional content","ocg","visibility"],"headings":[{"id":"make-a-layer-and-draw-on-it","text":"Make a layer and draw on it","level":2},{"id":"show-hide-and-lock","text":"Show, hide and lock","level":2},{"id":"in-the-saved-pdf","text":"In the saved PDF","level":2},{"id":"good-to-know","text":"Good to know","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003ELayers group markups, for example by discipline or by reviewer. Hide a layer to see the drawing under it, or lock it so its markups cannot be moved by accident.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"make-a-layer-and-draw-on-it\"\u003EMake a layer and draw on it\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELayers\u003C/span\u003E, at the top of the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESheets\u003C/span\u003E panel.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew layer\u003C/span\u003E, type a name such as \u003Ccode\u003EMechanical\u003C/code\u003E, and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd layer\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe new layer becomes the current layer: every markup you draw from now on goes on it. Choose another layer&#039;s name, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENo layer\u003C/span\u003E, to change where new markups go.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo move a markup to another layer, select it and pick the layer under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELayer\u003C/span\u003E in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew layer…\u003C/span\u003E at the end of that list makes one on the spot. Moving can be undone with \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"show-hide-and-lock\"\u003EShow, hide and lock\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach layer&#039;s row has two buttons:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe \u003Cstrong\u003Eeye\u003C/strong\u003E shows or hides the layer. Hidden markups are not drawn and cannot be picked; they still count in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMarkups\u003C/span\u003E list.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe \u003Cstrong\u003Elock\u003C/strong\u003E keeps the layer&#039;s markups on the sheet but stops them from being picked, moved or deleted.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe pencil button renames a layer and moves its markups along. Filter the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMarkups\u003C/span\u003E list by \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELayer\u003C/span\u003E to see one layer&#039;s markups.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"in-the-saved-pdf\"\u003EIn the saved PDF\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach layer becomes a PDF layer (optional content). Other PDF viewers list the layers in their layers panel and can switch them; a layer you hid starts hidden there too. Layers the drawing already had, such as CAD layers, stay as they were. When you reopen the copy in MarkupCraft, the markups come back on their layers, hidden and locked as you left them.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"good-to-know\"\u003EGood to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EHiding and locking are not edits: they are not undone with \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, but they are saved with the PDF.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ECopy and paste keep a markup&#039;s layer.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA flattened copy draws every markup into the page, whatever its layer. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/markupcraft/flatten\" data-topic=\"markupcraft/flatten\"\u003ESave a flattened copy\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"Layers group markups, for example by discipline or by reviewer. Hide a layer to see the drawing under it, or lock it so its markups cannot be moved by accident. Make a layer and draw on it Open Layers, at the top of the Sheets panel. Choose New layer, type a name such as Mechanical, and choose Add layer. The new layer becomes the current layer: every markup you draw from now on goes on it. Choose another layer's name, or No layer, to change where new markups go. To move a markup to another layer, select it and pick the layer under Layer in Properties. New layer… at the end of that list makes one on the spot. Moving can be undone with Ctrl+Z. Show, hide and lock Each layer's row has two buttons: The eye shows or hides the layer. Hidden markups are not drawn and cannot be picked; they still count in the Markups list. The lock keeps the layer's markups on the sheet but stops them from being picked, moved or deleted. The pencil button renames a layer and moves its markups along. Filter the Markups list by Layer to see one layer's markups. In the saved PDF Each layer becomes a PDF layer (optional content). Other PDF viewers list the layers in their layers panel and can switch them; a layer you hid starts hidden there too. Layers the drawing already had, such as CAD layers, stay as they were. When you reopen the copy in MarkupCraft, the markups come back on their layers, hidden and locked as you left them. Good to know Hiding and locking are not edits: they are not undone with Ctrl+Z, but they are saved with the PDF. Copy and paste keep a markup's layer. A flattened copy draws every markup into the page, whatever its layer. See Save a flattened copy.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"spaces","scope":"markupcraft","title":"Spaces","summary":"Name the rooms or zones of a sheet, then filter, count and export markups by the space they are in.","section":"tasks","order":96,"keywords":["spaces","space","room","zone","wing","area of work","location","filter by room","group by room","export by space"],"headings":[{"id":"make-a-space","text":"Make a space","level":2},{"id":"what-is-in-a-space","text":"What is in a space","level":2},{"id":"in-the-saved-pdf","text":"In the saved PDF","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EA space is a named zone on a sheet: a room, a wing, a phase of work. Every markup inside it belongs to it, so you can list, filter and export the markups of one room.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"make-a-space\"\u003EMake a space\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESpaces\u003C/span\u003E, at the top of the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESheets\u003C/span\u003E panel, and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew space\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the corners of the zone, then double-click or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType its name, such as \u003Ccode\u003EKitchen\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003ELevel 2 east\u003C/code\u003E, and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd space\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe space shows as a light green zone with a dashed outline and its name. The status bar says how many markups are in it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-is-in-a-space\"\u003EWhat is in a space\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA markup is in the space that holds the middle of the markup. When spaces sit inside each other (a room in a wing), the smaller one wins. Move a markup and it moves between spaces. Select a space to change its outline with its handles, or its name under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EName\u003C/span\u003E in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E shows a markup&#039;s space.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMarkups\u003C/span\u003E list filters by \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E, including \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENo space\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ECSV and XLSX exports get a \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E column once the set has spaces.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESpaces\u003C/span\u003E panel lists every space with how many markups are in it; choose one to go to it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"in-the-saved-pdf\"\u003EIn the saved PDF\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA space is a polygon comment with its name as its text, drawn with a dashed border, so other viewers show it too. It reopens in MarkupCraft as a space.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"A space is a named zone on a sheet: a room, a wing, a phase of work. Every markup inside it belongs to it, so you can list, filter and export the markups of one room. Make a space Open Spaces, at the top of the Sheets panel, and choose New space. Click the corners of the zone, then double-click or press Enter. Type its name, such as Kitchen or Level 2 east, and choose Add space. The space shows as a light green zone with a dashed outline and its name. The status bar says how many markups are in it. What is in a space A markup is in the space that holds the middle of the markup. When spaces sit inside each other (a room in a wing), the smaller one wins. Move a markup and it moves between spaces. Select a space to change its outline with its handles, or its name under Name in Properties. Properties shows a markup's space. The Markups list filters by Space, including No space. CSV and XLSX exports get a Space column once the set has spaces. The Spaces panel lists every space with how many markups are in it; choose one to go to it. In the saved PDF A space is a polygon comment with its name as its text, drawn with a dashed border, so other viewers show it too. It reopens in MarkupCraft as a space.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"tool-chest","scope":"markupcraft","title":"The tool chest","summary":"Save a styled markup as a reusable tool, draw with it, and share your chest with the office as a file.","section":"tasks","order":100,"keywords":["tool chest","tools","tool set","custom tools","saved markups","favorites","office standard","import","export","share","json"],"headings":[{"id":"the-tools-you-start-with","text":"The tools you start with","level":2},{"id":"draw-with-a-tool","text":"Draw with a tool","level":2},{"id":"save-your-own","text":"Save your own","level":2},{"id":"stamps-made-from-a-selection","text":"Stamps made from a selection","level":2},{"id":"remove-a-tool","text":"Remove a tool","level":2},{"id":"where-the-chest-is-kept","text":"Where the chest is kept","level":2},{"id":"share-a-chest-with-your-office","text":"Share a chest with your office","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETools\u003C/span\u003E tab holds your \u003Cstrong\u003Etool chest\u003C/strong\u003E: markups saved with their look, ready to draw again. A tool keeps the markup&#039;s kind, color, line width, fill, opacity, text size and subject, plus a stamp&#039;s wording, a count&#039;s group name, or the starting text of a text box or callout.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-tools-you-start-with\"\u003EThe tools you start with\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA new chest has six: &quot;Revision cloud&quot; (red), &quot;Question cloud&quot; (blue), &quot;Yellow highlighter&quot;, &quot;RFI callout&quot; (blue, starts with &quot;RFI: &quot;), &quot;Field verify&quot; (an orange callout that says &quot;Field verify.&quot;) and &quot;Approved as noted&quot; (a stamp).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"draw-with-a-tool\"\u003EDraw with a tool\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETools\u003C/span\u003E tab.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick a tool. It looks pressed, and the matching toolbar tool turns on.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDraw as usual. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E says &#039;Drawing with &quot;&lt;tool&gt;&quot; from the tool chest.&#039;\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhile a chest tool is on, changes you make in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E before drawing apply to that tool for now, without changing the saved tool or your normal tool settings. Choosing a toolbar button, or pressing a tool&#039;s key, goes back to the plain tool.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"save-your-own\"\u003ESave your own\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDraw a markup and style it the way you want: color, line, fill, subject, stamp wording.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESelect it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave selected as a tool\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETools\u003C/span\u003E tab, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave as a tool\u003C/span\u003E in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType a \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETool name\u003C/span\u003E and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave tool\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EResult: the tool is added at the end of the chest, and the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETools\u003C/span\u003E tab opens to show it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"stamps-made-from-a-selection\"\u003EStamps made from a selection\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESelect several markups (or page content with the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EContent\u003C/span\u003E tool) and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave selection as a stamp…\u003C/span\u003E in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E or in the right-click menu. The chest gets one stamp that holds them all (&quot;Stamp: 3 markups together&quot;). Click it, then click on any sheet: the markups land together, centered where you click, as new markups with your name; page content is drawn into the page there. These stamps are saved and shared with the chest like any tool.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"remove-a-tool\"\u003ERemove a tool\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChoose the × at the end of the tool&#039;s row, then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERemove\u003C/span\u003E. Markups already drawn with it stay as they are.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"where-the-chest-is-kept\"\u003EWhere the chest is kept\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe chest is stored \u003Cstrong\u003Ein this browser, on this computer\u003C/strong\u003E. Another browser or computer has its own chest (starting with the six defaults), a private window forgets it when it closes, and clearing this site&#039;s data in the browser&#039;s settings erases it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf the browser will not store it (storage turned off, or full), the status bar says: &quot;The tool chest could not be kept in this browser (storage is off or full). Export it to keep it.&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:safari\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003ESafari\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESafari can clear a website&#039;s stored data when you have not used the site for a while. Export your chest now and then so you can import it again.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EExport your chest as a backup\u003C/strong\u003E, especially after building tools you rely on.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"share-a-chest-with-your-office\"\u003EShare a chest with your office\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESet up the tools once.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport\u003C/span\u003E. You get &quot;MarkupCraft tool chest.json&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPut that file where the office keeps its standards, or send it around.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEveryone else chooses \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EImport\u003C/span\u003E in their \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETools\u003C/span\u003E tab and picks the file.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EResult: &quot;Imported 12 tools from &quot;MarkupCraft tool chest.json&quot;.&quot; Tools that came from the same chest replace their older copies, so importing an updated office chest refreshes those tools instead of doubling them; new tools are added. A chest holds up to 500 tools.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EMarkupCraft checks every value in an imported file and skips what it cannot use (&quot;2 skipped (not usable)&quot;). Files that are not a chest are refused: &quot;This file is not a MarkupCraft tool chest.&quot; or &quot;This file is not a tool chest (it is not JSON).&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETool chest files from other markup programs cannot be imported.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"The Tools tab holds your tool chest: markups saved with their look, ready to draw again. A tool keeps the markup's kind, color, line width, fill, opacity, text size and subject, plus a stamp's wording, a count's group name, or the starting text of a text box or callout. The tools you start with A new chest has six: \"Revision cloud\" (red), \"Question cloud\" (blue), \"Yellow highlighter\", \"RFI callout\" (blue, starts with \"RFI: \"), \"Field verify\" (an orange callout that says \"Field verify.\") and \"Approved as noted\" (a stamp). Draw with a tool Open the Tools tab. Click a tool. It looks pressed, and the matching toolbar tool turns on. Draw as usual. Properties says 'Drawing with \"\u003Ctool\u003E\" from the tool chest.' While a chest tool is on, changes you make in Properties before drawing apply to that tool for now, without changing the saved tool or your normal tool settings. Choosing a toolbar button, or pressing a tool's key, goes back to the plain tool. Save your own Draw a markup and style it the way you want: color, line, fill, subject, stamp wording. Select it. Choose Save selected as a tool in the Tools tab, or Save as a tool in Properties. Type a Tool name and choose Save tool. Result: the tool is added at the end of the chest, and the Tools tab opens to show it. Stamps made from a selection Select several markups (or page content with the Content tool) and choose Save selection as a stamp… in More or in the right-click menu. The chest gets one stamp that holds them all (\"Stamp: 3 markups together\"). Click it, then click on any sheet: the markups land together, centered where you click, as new markups with your name; page content is drawn into the page there. These stamps are saved and shared with the chest like any tool. Remove a tool Choose the × at the end of the tool's row, then Remove. Markups already drawn with it stay as they are. Where the chest is kept The chest is stored in this browser, on this computer. Another browser or computer has its own chest (starting with the six defaults), a private window forgets it when it closes, and clearing this site's data in the browser's settings erases it. If the browser will not store it (storage turned off, or full), the status bar says: \"The tool chest could not be kept in this browser (storage is off or full). Export it to keep it.\" Safari can clear a website's stored data when you have not used the site for a while. Export your chest now and then so you can import it again. Export your chest as a backup, especially after building tools you rely on. Share a chest with your office Set up the tools once. Choose Export. You get \"MarkupCraft tool chest.json\". Put that file where the office keeps its standards, or send it around. Everyone else chooses Import in their Tools tab and picks the file. Result: \"Imported 12 tools from \"MarkupCraft tool chest.json\".\" Tools that came from the same chest replace their older copies, so importing an updated office chest refreshes those tools instead of doubling them; new tools are added. A chest holds up to 500 tools. MarkupCraft checks every value in an imported file and skips what it cannot use (\"2 skipped (not usable)\"). Files that are not a chest are refused: \"This file is not a MarkupCraft tool chest.\" or \"This file is not a tool chest (it is not JSON).\" Tool chest files from other markup programs cannot be imported.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"compare-revisions","scope":"markupcraft","title":"Compare two revisions","summary":"Overlay an old and a new issue of a sheet in two colors, line them up by two points, and cloud what changed.","section":"tasks","order":110,"keywords":["compare","overlay","overlay pages","revision","diff","differences","changes","old new","align","backcheck","issue","bulletin"],"headings":[{"id":"compare-two-sheets","text":"Compare two sheets","level":2},{"id":"fade-between-old-and-new","text":"Fade between old and new","level":2},{"id":"line-the-sheets-up","text":"Line the sheets up","level":2},{"id":"changed-lines-only","text":"Changed lines only","level":2},{"id":"mark-the-changes","text":"Mark the changes","level":2},{"id":"compare-a-whole-set","text":"Compare a whole set","level":2},{"id":"stop-comparing","text":"Stop comparing","level":2},{"id":"good-to-know","text":"Good to know","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003ECompare lays two sheets over each other. Lines only on the old sheet show in red, lines only on the new sheet in blue, and lines on both in a muted gray. The colors are named in the legend under the sheet: &quot;Only on the old sheet&quot;, &quot;Only on the new sheet&quot;, &quot;On both&quot;.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"compare-two-sheets\"\u003ECompare two sheets\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen both issues. They can be pages of the same PDF or of different PDFs; open them together as one set (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/markupcraft/several-files\" data-topic=\"markupcraft/several-files\"\u003EWork with several files at once\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECompare\u003C/span\u003E tab on the right.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPick the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOld sheet\u003C/span\u003E and the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew sheet\u003C/span\u003E. The new sheet starts as the one you are on. When several files are open, each sheet&#039;s file name is shown after its name.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECompare\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EResult: MarkupCraft shows the new sheet with the old one mixed in, the legend appears, and the tab says &quot;Comparing &lt;old&gt; (old) with &lt;new&gt; (new). Markups on the new sheet stay editable.&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"fade-between-old-and-new\"\u003EFade between old and new\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDrag the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOld and new\u003C/span\u003E slider. All the way left shows only the old sheet, all the way right only the new one, the middle shows both. Sliding back and forth is often the quickest way to see a small change.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"line-the-sheets-up\"\u003ELine the sheets up\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf the two issues were plotted with a different margin, size or rotation, everything looks changed. Fix it:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EBy two points (best):\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAlign by two points\u003C/span\u003E. The view shows only the old sheet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick a sharp feature you can find on both sheets, such as a column grid intersection. The tab counts the steps (&quot;1 of 4&quot;).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick a second feature, far from the first, such as the opposite grid corner.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe view switches to the new sheet. Click the first feature again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the second feature again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EResult: &quot;Lined up: old sheet scaled 100.00%, turned 0.00°. Nudge to fine-tune.&quot; The old sheet is moved, scaled and turned to fit. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E cancels while you are picking. Zoom in on each feature before clicking for a precise fit.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EBy nudging:\u003C/strong\u003E use the four arrow buttons under &quot;Line up&quot; to move the old sheet. Choose the step (0.25, 1, 5 or 20 pt; 72 pt is one inch on the paper). The tab says how far it has moved.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReset\u003C/span\u003E removes the alignment and the nudges.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"changed-lines-only\"\u003EChanged lines only\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUnder the alignment buttons, pick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EChanged lines\u003C/span\u003E instead of \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPicture overlay\u003C/span\u003E. MarkupCraft then compares the two sheets&#039; own lines, not their pictures: the new sheet shows as it is, with the lines that are only on the old sheet in red and the lines only on the new one in blue. A line that only got shorter shows just the missing part. Line the sheets up first (see above): the comparison uses the alignment, so a sheet that was only shifted shows no changes. The status line says how much changed.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThis works for PDFs plotted from CAD, where the drawing is made of lines. Scanned sheets have no lines to compare: use the picture overlay for them. Text set in a font (not drawn as strokes) is not compared.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"mark-the-changes\"\u003EMark the changes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhile comparing, you are on the new sheet and every tool works. Draw clouds around the changes as you find them; they belong to the new sheet and stay when you stop comparing.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECloud the changes\u003C/span\u003E does it for you: it groups the changed lines that sit close together and draws a revision cloud round each group on the new sheet, with &quot;Changed since &lt;old sheet&gt;&quot; as its comment. One \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E removes them all; delete the ones you do not need.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"compare-a-whole-set\"\u003ECompare a whole set\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen the new issue arrives as its own PDF, open both PDFs and open \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECompare two sets\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECompare\u003C/span\u003E tab. Pick the old file and the new file, then choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECompare the sets\u003C/span\u003E. Sheets are paired by their number (as in \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/markupcraft/slip-sheet-a-revised-set\" data-topic=\"markupcraft/slip-sheet-a-revised-set\"\u003Eslip-sheeting\u003C/a\u003E), compared by their lines, and listed with the most changed first. Choose a sheet in the list to compare that pair with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EChanged lines\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECloud all changes\u003C/span\u003E clouds every group of changed lines on every changed sheet, in one step you can undo. The set comparison does not line sheets up first, so a sheet that was shifted on the page shows as changed: compare that pair on its own and use \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAlign by two points\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"stop-comparing\"\u003EStop comparing\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStop comparing\u003C/span\u003E, or go to another sheet. The alignment is remembered for the same pair of sheets until you close the set.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"good-to-know\"\u003EGood to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003ECompare works on what is drawn, pixel by pixel. It does not know that a line moved; it shows the old position in one color and the new position in the other.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EText that reflowed, a changed title block date or a different plot style shows up as changes too.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA scanned sheet compared with a plotted one is noisy: scanning blurs and shifts lines.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EComparing text written in a font, and a list of what changed, are not built yet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"Compare lays two sheets over each other. Lines only on the old sheet show in red, lines only on the new sheet in blue, and lines on both in a muted gray. The colors are named in the legend under the sheet: \"Only on the old sheet\", \"Only on the new sheet\", \"On both\". Compare two sheets Open both issues. They can be pages of the same PDF or of different PDFs; open them together as one set (see Work with several files at once). Choose the Compare tab on the right. Pick the Old sheet and the New sheet. The new sheet starts as the one you are on. When several files are open, each sheet's file name is shown after its name. Choose Compare. Result: MarkupCraft shows the new sheet with the old one mixed in, the legend appears, and the tab says \"Comparing \u003Cold\u003E (old) with \u003Cnew\u003E (new). Markups on the new sheet stay editable.\" Fade between old and new Drag the Old and new slider. All the way left shows only the old sheet, all the way right only the new one, the middle shows both. Sliding back and forth is often the quickest way to see a small change. Line the sheets up If the two issues were plotted with a different margin, size or rotation, everything looks changed. Fix it: By two points (best): Choose Align by two points. The view shows only the old sheet. Click a sharp feature you can find on both sheets, such as a column grid intersection. The tab counts the steps (\"1 of 4\"). Click a second feature, far from the first, such as the opposite grid corner. The view switches to the new sheet. Click the first feature again. Click the second feature again. Result: \"Lined up: old sheet scaled 100.00%, turned 0.00°. Nudge to fine-tune.\" The old sheet is moved, scaled and turned to fit. Esc cancels while you are picking. Zoom in on each feature before clicking for a precise fit. By nudging: use the four arrow buttons under \"Line up\" to move the old sheet. Choose the step (0.25, 1, 5 or 20 pt; 72 pt is one inch on the paper). The tab says how far it has moved. Reset removes the alignment and the nudges. Changed lines only Under the alignment buttons, pick Changed lines instead of Picture overlay. MarkupCraft then compares the two sheets' own lines, not their pictures: the new sheet shows as it is, with the lines that are only on the old sheet in red and the lines only on the new one in blue. A line that only got shorter shows just the missing part. Line the sheets up first (see above): the comparison uses the alignment, so a sheet that was only shifted shows no changes. The status line says how much changed. This works for PDFs plotted from CAD, where the drawing is made of lines. Scanned sheets have no lines to compare: use the picture overlay for them. Text set in a font (not drawn as strokes) is not compared. Mark the changes While comparing, you are on the new sheet and every tool works. Draw clouds around the changes as you find them; they belong to the new sheet and stay when you stop comparing. Cloud the changes does it for you: it groups the changed lines that sit close together and draws a revision cloud round each group on the new sheet, with \"Changed since \u003Cold sheet\u003E\" as its comment. One Ctrl+Z removes them all; delete the ones you do not need. Compare a whole set When the new issue arrives as its own PDF, open both PDFs and open Compare two sets in the Compare tab. Pick the old file and the new file, then choose Compare the sets. Sheets are paired by their number (as in slip-sheeting), compared by their lines, and listed with the most changed first. Choose a sheet in the list to compare that pair with Changed lines. Cloud all changes clouds every group of changed lines on every changed sheet, in one step you can undo. The set comparison does not line sheets up first, so a sheet that was shifted on the page shows as changed: compare that pair on its own and use Align by two points. Stop comparing Choose Stop comparing, or go to another sheet. The alignment is remembered for the same pair of sheets until you close the set. Good to know Compare works on what is drawn, pixel by pixel. It does not know that a line moved; it shows the old position in one color and the new position in the other. Text that reflowed, a changed title block date or a different plot style shows up as changes too. A scanned sheet compared with a plotted one is noisy: scanning blurs and shifts lines. Comparing text written in a font, and a list of what changed, are not built yet.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"slip-sheet-a-revised-set","scope":"markupcraft","title":"Slip-sheet a revised set","summary":"Open the new issue of a plan set beside the old one and carry every open markup onto the sheet with the same number.","section":"tasks","order":112,"keywords":["slip sheet","slip-sheet","revised set","new issue","revision","supersede","superseded","carry forward","replace sheets","reissue","addendum","bulletin"],"headings":[{"id":"slip-sheet","text":"Slip-sheet","level":2},{"id":"how-sheets-are-matched","text":"How sheets are matched","level":2},{"id":"good-to-know","text":"Good to know","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EWhen a new issue of the drawings arrives, slip-sheeting moves your review onto it: each new sheet takes the markups of the old sheet with the same number, and the old sheet is stamped &quot;Superseded&quot;.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"slip-sheet\"\u003ESlip-sheet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen the current set, with your markups.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPages\u003C/span\u003E, then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESlip-sheet a revised set…\u003C/span\u003E, and pick the new PDFs. They open beside the old set.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe dialog lists each new sheet and the old sheet it replaces. Untick a row to leave that sheet&#039;s markups where they are. Sheets with a new number have nothing to carry.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose whether to leave closed markups (Accepted, Rejected, Completed) on the old sheets, and whether to stamp the old sheets.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECarry markups forward\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EResult: the markups appear on the new sheets in the same place, with their replies, statuses and authors. A punch item moves to the new sheet and keeps its number. The old sheets keep their markups as a record, under a red &quot;Superseded&quot; stamp. The status bar sums up what moved.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EJust open the files\u003C/span\u003E opens the new set without carrying anything, for example to compare first. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/markupcraft/compare-revisions\" data-topic=\"markupcraft/compare-revisions\"\u003ECompare two revisions\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"how-sheets-are-matched\"\u003EHow sheets are matched\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EBy sheet number\u003C/strong\u003E, read as the sheets list shows it: a number you typed with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERename sheet\u003C/span\u003E, the PDF&#039;s page label, or the title block. Case, spaces, dots and dashes do not matter: &quot;A-101&quot; matches &quot;a101&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIf two open files have a sheet with that number, the newest one is replaced, so you can slip-sheet issue after issue.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EScanned sets\u003C/strong\u003E have no numbers to read. When a new file has none, its pages are matched in page order to an open file with the same number of pages. Check those rows before you carry.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"good-to-know\"\u003EGood to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOne \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E takes the whole slip-sheet back: the copies, the stamps and the moved punch items. The new files stay open.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIf a new sheet is a different size, the markups are scaled with the page, and the status bar says how many to check.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe scale of an old sheet comes along when the new sheet is the same size and has none.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EComments other programs made stay on the old sheets: they are read-only here.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESave the new files to keep the carried markups. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/markupcraft/save-the-marked-up-pdf\" data-topic=\"markupcraft/save-the-marked-up-pdf\"\u003ESave the marked-up PDF\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"When a new issue of the drawings arrives, slip-sheeting moves your review onto it: each new sheet takes the markups of the old sheet with the same number, and the old sheet is stamped \"Superseded\". Slip-sheet Open the current set, with your markups. Choose Pages, then Slip-sheet a revised set…, and pick the new PDFs. They open beside the old set. The dialog lists each new sheet and the old sheet it replaces. Untick a row to leave that sheet's markups where they are. Sheets with a new number have nothing to carry. Choose whether to leave closed markups (Accepted, Rejected, Completed) on the old sheets, and whether to stamp the old sheets. Choose Carry markups forward. Result: the markups appear on the new sheets in the same place, with their replies, statuses and authors. A punch item moves to the new sheet and keeps its number. The old sheets keep their markups as a record, under a red \"Superseded\" stamp. The status bar sums up what moved. Just open the files opens the new set without carrying anything, for example to compare first. See Compare two revisions. How sheets are matched By sheet number, read as the sheets list shows it: a number you typed with Rename sheet, the PDF's page label, or the title block. Case, spaces, dots and dashes do not matter: \"A-101\" matches \"a101\". If two open files have a sheet with that number, the newest one is replaced, so you can slip-sheet issue after issue. Scanned sets have no numbers to read. When a new file has none, its pages are matched in page order to an open file with the same number of pages. Check those rows before you carry. Good to know One Ctrl+Z takes the whole slip-sheet back: the copies, the stamps and the moved punch items. The new files stay open. If a new sheet is a different size, the markups are scaled with the page, and the status bar says how many to check. The scale of an old sheet comes along when the new sheet is the same size and has none. Comments other programs made stay on the old sheets: they are read-only here. Save the new files to keep the carried markups. See Save the marked-up PDF.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"find-text","scope":"markupcraft","title":"Find text in the drawings","summary":"Search the words on every sheet, jump to each hit, and cloud them all in one step.","section":"tasks","order":115,"keywords":["find","search","text","word","words","ctrl f","look for","note","keynote","callout","cloud all","highlight hits","ocr","scan","scanned","read text","select text","copy text","tesseract"],"headings":[{"id":"find","text":"Find","level":2},{"id":"cloud-every-hit","text":"Cloud every hit","level":2},{"id":"read-the-text-on-a-scan-ocr","text":"Read the text on a scan (OCR)","level":2},{"id":"select-and-copy-text","text":"Select and copy text","level":2},{"id":"good-to-know","text":"Good to know","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFind text\u003C/span\u003E, at the top of the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESheets\u003C/span\u003E panel, searches the words drawn on the sheets themselves. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESearch sheets\u003C/span\u003E below it only searches sheet names.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"find\"\u003EFind\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command F\"\u003E⌘F\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+F\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or open \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFind text\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType the words, such as \u003Ccode\u003Ehood\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003Everify in field\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAll sheets\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EThis sheet\u003C/span\u003E, and tick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMatch case\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWhole words\u003C/span\u003E if you need them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFind\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EResult: every hit is listed with its sheet number and the words around it, and is marked in yellow on its sheet. Choose a hit to open its sheet and zoom to it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA phrase is found even when the drawing splits it over two pieces of text or two lines.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"cloud-every-hit\"\u003ECloud every hit\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECloud all\u003C/span\u003E. A revision cloud goes round each hit in the list, on every sheet, with &quot;Found: &lt;your words&gt;&quot; as its comment. One \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E removes them all.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"read-the-text-on-a-scan-ocr\"\u003ERead the text on a scan (OCR)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA scanned sheet is only a picture: until its words are read, there is nothing to find. When the count says sheets have no text, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERead text (OCR)…\u003C/span\u003E under it, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPages\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERead text (OCR)…\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPick the pages: this sheet, every page of the file, or every page of every open file.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPick the language of the text. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAlso English\u003C/span\u003E reads English words too.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERead text\u003C/span\u003E. A bar shows the page being read; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStop\u003C/span\u003E ends the run and keeps the pages already read.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EResult: the words can be found, selected and copied like any other text. It all runs on your computer: nothing is sent anywhere. It works without a connection in Studio, and in the browser once the app is saved for offline (English and your interface language) or a language has been used once. Pages that already have text are left as they are.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen you save, the words go into the PDF as invisible text behind the picture, apart from your markups, so other PDF programs can search and copy them too. Until you save, the file shows as unsaved.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"select-and-copy-text\"\u003ESelect and copy text\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect text to copy\u003C/span\u003E. Drag from one word to another to select the words between them, drag from empty paper to select the words in a box, or double-click one word. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command A\"\u003E⌘A\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+A\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E selects every word on the sheet. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command C\"\u003E⌘C\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+C\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E copies them, keeping the lines. Any other tool ends it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"good-to-know\"\u003EGood to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDrawings whose letters are drawn as lines have no text either; reading them as a picture works best on printed text such as a scanned book or specification.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe reader is Tesseract, built into the app with its language data for English and the app&#039;s languages. The first run loads it (about 6 MB, more for a second language).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EHow well it reads depends on the scan: clean pages at 300 dpi read almost word for word; old, faint or low-resolution scans miss more words.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe list shows the first 500 hits; Cloud all clouds up to 2,000.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe hits are cleared when you close the set, and for one file when you change its pages.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"Find text, at the top of the Sheets panel, searches the words drawn on the sheets themselves. Search sheets below it only searches sheet names. Find Press Ctrl+F, or open Find text. Type the words, such as hood or verify in field. Pick All sheets or This sheet, and tick Match case or Whole words if you need them. Press Enter or choose Find. Result: every hit is listed with its sheet number and the words around it, and is marked in yellow on its sheet. Choose a hit to open its sheet and zoom to it. A phrase is found even when the drawing splits it over two pieces of text or two lines. Cloud every hit Choose Cloud all. A revision cloud goes round each hit in the list, on every sheet, with \"Found: \u003Cyour words\u003E\" as its comment. One Ctrl+Z removes them all. Read the text on a scan (OCR) A scanned sheet is only a picture: until its words are read, there is nothing to find. When the count says sheets have no text, choose Read text (OCR)… under it, or Pages › Read text (OCR)…. Pick the pages: this sheet, every page of the file, or every page of every open file. Pick the language of the text. Also English reads English words too. Choose Read text. A bar shows the page being read; Stop ends the run and keeps the pages already read. Result: the words can be found, selected and copied like any other text. It all runs on your computer: nothing is sent anywhere. It works without a connection in Studio, and in the browser once the app is saved for offline (English and your interface language) or a language has been used once. Pages that already have text are left as they are. When you save, the words go into the PDF as invisible text behind the picture, apart from your markups, so other PDF programs can search and copy them too. Until you save, the file shows as unsaved. Select and copy text Choose More › Select text to copy. Drag from one word to another to select the words between them, drag from empty paper to select the words in a box, or double-click one word. Ctrl+A selects every word on the sheet. Ctrl+C copies them, keeping the lines. Any other tool ends it. Good to know Drawings whose letters are drawn as lines have no text either; reading them as a picture works best on printed text such as a scanned book or specification. The reader is Tesseract, built into the app with its language data for English and the app's languages. The first run loads it (about 6 MB, more for a second language). How well it reads depends on the scan: clean pages at 300 dpi read almost word for word; old, faint or low-resolution scans miss more words. The list shows the first 500 hits; Cloud all clouds up to 2,000. The hits are cleared when you close the set, and for one file when you change its pages.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"sheet-links","scope":"markupcraft","title":"Sheet links","summary":"Make a markup jump to another sheet, and turn every sheet callout like 3/A-501 into a link in one pass.","section":"tasks","order":117,"keywords":["hyperlink","link","links","jump","sheet callout","detail callout","section callout","3/A-501","go to sheet","back","auto link","batch link"],"headings":[{"id":"link-a-markup-to-a-sheet","text":"Link a markup to a sheet","level":2},{"id":"link-every-sheet-callout-at-once","text":"Link every sheet callout at once","level":2},{"id":"in-the-saved-pdf","text":"In the saved PDF","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EA sheet link takes you from a markup to another sheet of the set: from a detail bubble to the detail, from a note to the sheet it refers to.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"link-a-markup-to-a-sheet\"\u003ELink a markup to a sheet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESelect the markup.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E, pick the sheet under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELink to sheet\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA small arrow appears at the markup&#039;s top right. To follow it, hold \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command\"\u003E⌘\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and click the markup, or select it and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGo to …\u003C/span\u003E in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Option ←\"\u003E⌥←\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EAlt+←\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E goes back to the sheet you came from.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA link names the sheet by its number, so it still works after you reopen the set or slip-sheet a new issue: it opens the newest sheet with that number. If that sheet is not open, the status bar says so.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"link-every-sheet-callout-at-once\"\u003ELink every sheet callout at once\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFind text\u003C/span\u003E at the top of the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESheets\u003C/span\u003E panel and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELink sheet callouts…\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMarkupCraft reads the text of every sheet and lists each callout that names another open sheet, such as &quot;3/A-501&quot;, &quot;A-501&quot; or &quot;2 / A501&quot;. Callouts already linked, and a sheet&#039;s own number in its title block, are left out.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUntick any you do not want, then choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECreate the links\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach callout gets a thin blue box that is a sheet link. One \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E removes them all.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESheets need numbers for this: from their title block, their page label, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERename sheet\u003C/span\u003E. Scanned sheets have no text to read; link their callouts one by one.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"in-the-saved-pdf\"\u003EIn the saved PDF\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA link to a sheet in the same PDF is also written as a PDF link, so other viewers jump there on a click too. Links to a sheet in another file of the set work in MarkupCraft only.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"A sheet link takes you from a markup to another sheet of the set: from a detail bubble to the detail, from a note to the sheet it refers to. Link a markup to a sheet Select the markup. In Properties, pick the sheet under Link to sheet. A small arrow appears at the markup's top right. To follow it, hold Ctrl and click the markup, or select it and choose Go to … in Properties. Alt+← goes back to the sheet you came from. A link names the sheet by its number, so it still works after you reopen the set or slip-sheet a new issue: it opens the newest sheet with that number. If that sheet is not open, the status bar says so. Link every sheet callout at once Open Find text at the top of the Sheets panel and choose Link sheet callouts…. MarkupCraft reads the text of every sheet and lists each callout that names another open sheet, such as \"3/A-501\", \"A-501\" or \"2 / A501\". Callouts already linked, and a sheet's own number in its title block, are left out. Untick any you do not want, then choose Create the links. Each callout gets a thin blue box that is a sheet link. One Ctrl+Z removes them all. Sheets need numbers for this: from their title block, their page label, or Rename sheet. Scanned sheets have no text to read; link their callouts one by one. In the saved PDF A link to a sheet in the same PDF is also written as a PDF link, so other viewers jump there on a click too. Links to a sheet in another file of the set work in MarkupCraft only.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"save-the-marked-up-pdf","scope":"markupcraft","title":"Save the marked-up PDF","summary":"Download a copy with every markup written in as standard PDF comments, check what other PDF readers see, and keep working on it later.","section":"tasks","order":120,"keywords":["save","download","export pdf","marked up","annotations","comments","share","send","issue","keep working","reopen","round trip","print"],"headings":[{"id":"save","text":"Save","level":2},{"id":"keep-working-on-it-later","text":"Keep working on it later","level":2},{"id":"what-other-pdf-readers-see","text":"What other PDF readers see","level":2},{"id":"characters-that-print-as","text":"Characters that print as \"?\"","level":2},{"id":"files-that-cannot-be-saved","text":"Files that cannot be saved","level":2},{"id":"flattening-and-printing","text":"Flattening and printing","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"save\"\u003ESave\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave\u003C/span\u003E in the toolbar, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIf several files are open, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EThis file\u003C/span\u003E (the file of the sheet you are on) or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEvery changed file\u003C/span\u003E. Each copy downloads separately.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWait for &quot;Saving &lt;file&gt;…&quot; to finish. The first save in a session also loads the PDF writer.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EResult: your browser downloads &quot;&lt;name&gt; (marked up).pdf&quot;, and the status bar reports it: &#039;Saved A-Plans (marked up).pdf (24 markups, 18.2 MB). The original file is unchanged.&#039; The dot after \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave\u003C/span\u003E goes away.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EYour original PDF is never changed.\u003C/strong\u003E MarkupCraft writes a new file. Where it lands depends on your browser&#039;s download settings (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/markupcraft/files#where-downloads-go\" data-topic=\"markupcraft/files\" data-hash=\"where-downloads-go\"\u003EFiles MarkupCraft opens and saves\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"keep-working-on-it-later\"\u003EKeep working on it later\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOpen the &quot;(marked up)&quot; copy next time, not the original. Everything comes back editable: markups, statuses, replies, punch items, each sheet&#039;s scale and any sheet names you typed. Saving it again downloads a file with the same &quot;(marked up)&quot; name; your browser may add a number such as &quot;(1)&quot; if the old download is still in the folder. Replace or tidy old copies yourself.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EMarkupCraft does not save the PDF on its own. While you work it keeps a recovery copy in this browser, so a crash or a closed tab does not lose your markups: the next time you open MarkupCraft it asks &quot;Recover your work?&quot;. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/recover\" data-topic=\"basics/recover\"\u003ERecover your work\u003C/a\u003E. Still save before closing; if you try to leave with unsaved markups, the browser asks &quot;Leave site?&quot; first (iPad Safari does not).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-other-pdf-readers-see\"\u003EWhat other PDF readers see\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEvery markup is written as a standard PDF comment (annotation) with its own drawing, so any PDF viewer shows it, prints it, and lists it in its comments panel:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe look is the same\u003C/strong\u003E as in MarkupCraft: clouds with their scallops, arrowheads, stamps, count dots, punch number badges, measurement labels.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe details are standard:\u003C/strong\u003E author, date, subject, the comment text, and colors.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EClouds\u003C/strong\u003E are rectangle or polygon comments with a cloudy border; \u003Cstrong\u003Etext boxes\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003Ecallouts\u003C/strong\u003E are text box comments; \u003Cstrong\u003Enotes\u003C/strong\u003E are sticky notes; \u003Cstrong\u003Epen\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003Ehighlighter\u003C/strong\u003E strokes are ink; \u003Cstrong\u003Estamps\u003C/strong\u003E are stamp comments.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELengths and areas\u003C/strong\u003E are measurement comments, and each calibrated sheet carries its scale, so viewers that understand measurements can show the values.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EReplies\u003C/strong\u003E are replies to the comment, and \u003Cstrong\u003Estatuses\u003C/strong\u003E are review states (Open shows as no state).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESheet numbers\u003C/strong\u003E you typed become the page labels other viewers show.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA few things only MarkupCraft reads back: the punch item&#039;s assignee and due date, item status, and some style details. Other programs keep them unless they rewrite the comment.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn another program, comments can be moved or edited like any other. If someone does that and sends the file back, MarkupCraft uses the comment&#039;s new position when it opens the file.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFull detail: \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/markupcraft/what-the-pdf-keeps\" data-topic=\"markupcraft/what-the-pdf-keeps\"\u003EWhat is saved in the PDF\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"characters-that-print-as\"\u003ECharacters that print as &quot;?&quot;\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EMarkup text is written with the standard Helvetica font that every PDF viewer has. It covers English and Western European letters (é, ñ, ü, ø, and the like), but not other scripts, symbols outside that set, or emoji. Those characters show fine in MarkupCraft but print as &quot;?&quot; in the saved copy, and the save message counts them: &quot;3 characters outside the Western European set print as &quot;?&quot; in the copy.&quot; Spell such words out, or use a plain substitute (for example &quot;dia.&quot; for the diameter sign).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"files-that-cannot-be-saved\"\u003EFiles that cannot be saved\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EProtected PDFs:\u003C/strong\u003E &quot;This PDF is protected (encrypted), so a marked-up copy cannot be written. Ask for an unprotected copy.&quot; You can still review it on screen, export the CSV and the BCF.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIf saving fails for another reason, a box called &quot;Could not save &lt;file&gt;&quot; shows the reason. Your markups are still in the tab; nothing is lost until you close it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"flattening-and-printing\"\u003EFlattening and printing\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThere is no flatten (burning markups into the page) and no print command yet. To print, open the saved copy in any PDF viewer and print with comments shown.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Save Choose Save in the toolbar, or press Ctrl+S. If several files are open, choose This file (the file of the sheet you are on) or Every changed file. Each copy downloads separately. Wait for \"Saving \u003Cfile\u003E…\" to finish. The first save in a session also loads the PDF writer. Result: your browser downloads \"\u003Cname\u003E (marked up).pdf\", and the status bar reports it: 'Saved A-Plans (marked up).pdf (24 markups, 18.2 MB). The original file is unchanged.' The dot after Save goes away. Your original PDF is never changed. MarkupCraft writes a new file. Where it lands depends on your browser's download settings (see Files MarkupCraft opens and saves). Keep working on it later Open the \"(marked up)\" copy next time, not the original. Everything comes back editable: markups, statuses, replies, punch items, each sheet's scale and any sheet names you typed. Saving it again downloads a file with the same \"(marked up)\" name; your browser may add a number such as \"(1)\" if the old download is still in the folder. Replace or tidy old copies yourself. MarkupCraft does not save the PDF on its own. While you work it keeps a recovery copy in this browser, so a crash or a closed tab does not lose your markups: the next time you open MarkupCraft it asks \"Recover your work?\". See Recover your work. Still save before closing; if you try to leave with unsaved markups, the browser asks \"Leave site?\" first (iPad Safari does not). What other PDF readers see Every markup is written as a standard PDF comment (annotation) with its own drawing, so any PDF viewer shows it, prints it, and lists it in its comments panel: The look is the same as in MarkupCraft: clouds with their scallops, arrowheads, stamps, count dots, punch number badges, measurement labels. The details are standard: author, date, subject, the comment text, and colors. Clouds are rectangle or polygon comments with a cloudy border; text boxes and callouts are text box comments; notes are sticky notes; pen and highlighter strokes are ink; stamps are stamp comments. Lengths and areas are measurement comments, and each calibrated sheet carries its scale, so viewers that understand measurements can show the values. Replies are replies to the comment, and statuses are review states (Open shows as no state). Sheet numbers you typed become the page labels other viewers show. A few things only MarkupCraft reads back: the punch item's assignee and due date, item status, and some style details. Other programs keep them unless they rewrite the comment. In another program, comments can be moved or edited like any other. If someone does that and sends the file back, MarkupCraft uses the comment's new position when it opens the file. Full detail: What is saved in the PDF. Characters that print as \"?\" Markup text is written with the standard Helvetica font that every PDF viewer has. It covers English and Western European letters (é, ñ, ü, ø, and the like), but not other scripts, symbols outside that set, or emoji. Those characters show fine in MarkupCraft but print as \"?\" in the saved copy, and the save message counts them: \"3 characters outside the Western European set print as \"?\" in the copy.\" Spell such words out, or use a plain substitute (for example \"dia.\" for the diameter sign). Files that cannot be saved Protected PDFs: \"This PDF is protected (encrypted), so a marked-up copy cannot be written. Ask for an unprotected copy.\" You can still review it on screen, export the CSV and the BCF. If saving fails for another reason, a box called \"Could not save \u003Cfile\u003E\" shows the reason. Your markups are still in the tab; nothing is lost until you close it. Flattening and printing There is no flatten (burning markups into the page) and no print command yet. To print, open the saved copy in any PDF viewer and print with comments shown.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"flatten","scope":"markupcraft","title":"Save a flattened copy","summary":"Draw your markups into the pages of a separate copy, so nobody can move or change them.","section":"tasks","order":122,"keywords":["flatten","flattened","lock","burn in","merge markups","final","issue","permanent","cannot edit","print ready"],"headings":[{"id":"save-a-flattened-copy","text":"Save a flattened copy","level":2},{"id":"flatten-some-markups-every-time-you-save","text":"Flatten some markups every time you save","level":2},{"id":"good-to-know","text":"Good to know","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EA flattened copy carries the markups as part of the drawing, not as comments. Every viewer and printer shows them exactly, and nobody can move, edit or delete them. Use it for a final issue; keep the normal marked-up copy for review.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"save-a-flattened-copy\"\u003ESave a flattened copy\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen a sheet of the file you want to flatten.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPages\u003C/span\u003E, then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave a flattened copy…\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEvery markup in this file\u003C/span\u003E, or, with markups selected, only those. The others stay comments in the copy.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave the copy\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EResult: your browser downloads &quot;&lt;name&gt; (flattened).pdf&quot;. The markups in MarkupCraft stay editable, and your original PDF is unchanged.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"flatten-some-markups-every-time-you-save\"\u003EFlatten some markups every time you save\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo make a picture, a signature or any markup part of the page in the copy you save, select it and tick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFlatten into the page when saved\u003C/span\u003E in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E (or choose it in the right-click menu). Here it stays a markup: you can still move, resize or delete it. Each time you save (and when you extract pages), it is drawn into the page itself, and the status bar says how many were. Untick it to save it as a markup again.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"good-to-know\"\u003EGood to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EReplies, statuses and punch items of a flattened markup are not kept in the copy: it is a picture of the markup now.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EComments that came with the file from other programs stay comments.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPrinting flattens a copy for you each time. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/markupcraft/pages-and-printing\" data-topic=\"markupcraft/pages-and-printing\"\u003EPrint, and change the pages\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"A flattened copy carries the markups as part of the drawing, not as comments. Every viewer and printer shows them exactly, and nobody can move, edit or delete them. Use it for a final issue; keep the normal marked-up copy for review. Save a flattened copy Open a sheet of the file you want to flatten. Choose Pages, then Save a flattened copy…. Choose Every markup in this file, or, with markups selected, only those. The others stay comments in the copy. Choose Save the copy. Result: your browser downloads \"\u003Cname\u003E (flattened).pdf\". The markups in MarkupCraft stay editable, and your original PDF is unchanged. Flatten some markups every time you save To make a picture, a signature or any markup part of the page in the copy you save, select it and tick Flatten into the page when saved in Properties (or choose it in the right-click menu). Here it stays a markup: you can still move, resize or delete it. Each time you save (and when you extract pages), it is drawn into the page itself, and the status bar says how many were. Untick it to save it as a markup again. Good to know Replies, statuses and punch items of a flattened markup are not kept in the copy: it is a picture of the markup now. Comments that came with the file from other programs stay comments. Printing flattens a copy for you each time. See Print, and change the pages.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"pages-and-printing","scope":"markupcraft","title":"Print, and change the pages of a PDF","summary":"Print sheets with their markups, and rotate, move, insert, delete or extract pages of an open PDF.","section":"tasks","order":125,"keywords":["print","printer","paper","pages","page","rotate","turn","reorder","move page","insert","add page","blank page","delete page","remove page","extract","split","page operations"],"headings":[{"id":"print","text":"Print","level":2},{"id":"turn-a-page","text":"Turn a page","level":2},{"id":"move-a-page","text":"Move a page","level":2},{"id":"insert-pages","text":"Insert pages","level":2},{"id":"delete-pages","text":"Delete pages","level":2},{"id":"extract-pages","text":"Extract pages","level":2},{"id":"good-to-know","text":"Good to know","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPages\u003C/span\u003E menu in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFile\u003C/span\u003E group works on the PDF that the open sheet belongs to. Your PDF on this computer is never changed: the changes are part of the copy you save.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"print\"\u003EPrint\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command P\"\u003E⌘P\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+P\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPages\u003C/span\u003E, then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPrint…\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWhen more than one sheet is open, pick what to print: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EThis sheet\u003C/span\u003E, every page of this file, the whole set, or the pages you type (such as \u003Ccode\u003E1-3, 5\u003C/code\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPrint\u003C/span\u003E. Your browser&#039;s printer dialog opens: pick the printer, the paper and the scale there.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe markups print as they look on the sheet: they are drawn into the pages of the copy that goes to the printer. Comments other programs made print the way their PDF says.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:safari,firefox\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003ESafari or Firefox\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf your browser cannot print from inside the page, the sheets open as a PDF in a new tab. Print from there.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"turn-a-page\"\u003ETurn a page\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPages\u003C/span\u003E, then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERotate page clockwise\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERotate page counterclockwise\u003C/span\u003E. The open sheet turns a quarter turn. Its markups stay where they were on the drawing, and text you typed turns with the page so it still reads upright.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"move-a-page\"\u003EMove a page\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPages\u003C/span\u003E, then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMove page earlier\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMove page later\u003C/span\u003E. The open sheet swaps places with its neighbor in its own PDF. Its markups, its scale and its sheet number go with it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"insert-pages\"\u003EInsert pages\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EInsert pages from a PDF…\u003C/span\u003E puts every page of another PDF after the open sheet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EInsert a blank page\u003C/span\u003E adds an empty page of the same size after the open sheet, for sketches and notes. In a set made with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew\u003C/span\u003E, it has the set&#039;s grid too.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn a set made with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd a blank page\u003C/span\u003E under the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESheets\u003C/span\u003E list adds one more page like the others at the end. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/markupcraft/open-a-plan-set#start-with-blank-pages\" data-topic=\"markupcraft/open-a-plan-set\" data-hash=\"start-with-blank-pages\"\u003EStart with blank pages\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EInserted pages get their page number as their label until you rename them. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/markupcraft/open-a-plan-set#rename-a-sheet\" data-topic=\"markupcraft/open-a-plan-set\" data-hash=\"rename-a-sheet\"\u003ERename a sheet\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"delete-pages\"\u003EDelete pages\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPages\u003C/span\u003E, then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDelete pages…\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType the page numbers of this PDF, such as \u003Ccode\u003E3\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E2-4\u003C/code\u003E. The open sheet&#039;s page is filled in.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDelete pages\u003C/span\u003E. If those pages hold markups, MarkupCraft says how many and asks again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA PDF keeps at least one page. To drop a whole file, close the set and open the files you still need.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"extract-pages\"\u003EExtract pages\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPages\u003C/span\u003E, then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExtract pages…\u003C/span\u003E, and type the pages. They download as a new PDF named &quot;&lt;file&gt; (pages 1-3).pdf&quot;, with their markups as comments you can keep editing. The open file stays as it is.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"good-to-know\"\u003EGood to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUndo stops at a page change.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E undoes markups drawn after it, but not the page change itself or anything before it. Close without saving to start over from your original PDF.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPage numbers count the pages of one PDF, not the whole set. Moving a page keeps it inside its own file.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe changed pages are unsaved work: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave\u003C/span\u003E downloads the reorganized copy, and leaving asks first.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"The Pages menu in the File group works on the PDF that the open sheet belongs to. Your PDF on this computer is never changed: the changes are part of the copy you save. Print Press Ctrl+P, or choose Pages, then Print…. When more than one sheet is open, pick what to print: This sheet, every page of this file, the whole set, or the pages you type (such as 1-3, 5). Choose Print. Your browser's printer dialog opens: pick the printer, the paper and the scale there. The markups print as they look on the sheet: they are drawn into the pages of the copy that goes to the printer. Comments other programs made print the way their PDF says. If your browser cannot print from inside the page, the sheets open as a PDF in a new tab. Print from there. Turn a page Choose Pages, then Rotate page clockwise or Rotate page counterclockwise. The open sheet turns a quarter turn. Its markups stay where they were on the drawing, and text you typed turns with the page so it still reads upright. Move a page Choose Pages, then Move page earlier or Move page later. The open sheet swaps places with its neighbor in its own PDF. Its markups, its scale and its sheet number go with it. Insert pages Insert pages from a PDF… puts every page of another PDF after the open sheet. Insert a blank page adds an empty page of the same size after the open sheet, for sketches and notes. In a set made with New, it has the set's grid too. In a set made with New, Add a blank page under the Sheets list adds one more page like the others at the end. See Start with blank pages. Inserted pages get their page number as their label until you rename them. See Rename a sheet. Delete pages Choose Pages, then Delete pages…. Type the page numbers of this PDF, such as 3 or 2-4. The open sheet's page is filled in. Choose Delete pages. If those pages hold markups, MarkupCraft says how many and asks again. A PDF keeps at least one page. To drop a whole file, close the set and open the files you still need. Extract pages Choose Pages, then Extract pages…, and type the pages. They download as a new PDF named \"\u003Cfile\u003E (pages 1-3).pdf\", with their markups as comments you can keep editing. The open file stays as it is. Good to know Undo stops at a page change. Ctrl+Z undoes markups drawn after it, but not the page change itself or anything before it. Close without saving to start over from your original PDF. Page numbers count the pages of one PDF, not the whole set. Moving a page keeps it inside its own file. The changed pages are unsaved work: Save downloads the reorganized copy, and leaving asks first.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"page-content","scope":"markupcraft","title":"Change the page's own lines, pictures and text","summary":"Move, resize, recolor or delete what the PDF itself draws, move a line's points, replace a picture, and change the words of a line of text.","section":"tasks","order":128,"keywords":["page content","base content","edit pdf","edit drawing","vector","path","shape","line","picture","image","replace image","text","words","font","points","nodes","move","resize","recolor","delete","content tool"],"headings":[{"id":"pick-what-to-change","text":"Pick what to change","level":2},{"id":"move-resize-recolor-delete","text":"Move, resize, recolor, delete","level":2},{"id":"copy-cut-and-paste-page-content","text":"Copy, cut and paste page content","level":2},{"id":"move-a-line-s-points","text":"Move a line's points","level":2},{"id":"replace-a-picture","text":"Replace a picture","level":2},{"id":"change-the-words-of-a-line-of-text","text":"Change the words of a line of text","level":2},{"id":"what-markupcraft-leaves-as-it-is","text":"What MarkupCraft leaves as it is","level":2},{"id":"put-markups-into-the-page","text":"Put markups into the page","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EMarkups sit on top of a sheet. The \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EContent\u003C/span\u003E tool works on the sheet itself: the lines, pictures and lines of text the PDF draws. Your PDF on this computer is never changed: the edits are part of the copy you save.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"pick-what-to-change\"\u003EPick what to change\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EContent\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E group, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"B\"\u003EB\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EB\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUnder \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPick\u003C/span\u003E in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E, turn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShapes\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPictures\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EText\u003C/span\u003E on or off. Only the kinds that are on can be picked, so you can pick the text of a busy drawing without catching its lines.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick something to select it. Shift+click adds or removes. Drag a box over empty paper to select several: dragging to the right takes what is fully inside the box, dragging to the left takes what it touches.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA shape inside a clipped area is picked only where it shows.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"move-resize-recolor-delete\"\u003EMove, resize, recolor, delete\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMove:\u003C/strong\u003E drag a selected object, or press the arrow keys (hold Shift for bigger steps). Hold Shift while dragging to move straight across or straight up.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EResize:\u003C/strong\u003E drag one of the eight squares around the selection. It grows or shrinks from the opposite side. Hold Shift to keep the proportions.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERecolor:\u003C/strong\u003E for lines and shapes, set \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELine color\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFill color\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELine width (pt)\u003C/span\u003E in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDelete:\u003C/strong\u003E press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn Delete\"\u003Efn Delete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDelete from the page\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUndo:\u003C/strong\u003E each change is one step: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E takes it back, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Y\"\u003E⌘Y\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Y\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E puts it back.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPut back as it was\u003C/span\u003E undoes every change to the selected objects at once; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPut this page back as it was\u003C/span\u003E does it for the whole page.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"copy-cut-and-paste-page-content\"\u003ECopy, cut and paste page content\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESelect lines, pictures or text with the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EContent\u003C/span\u003E tool, then:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command C\"\u003E⌘C\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+C\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E copies, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command X\"\u003E⌘X\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+X\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E cuts (copies and takes them out of the page), \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command D\"\u003E⌘D\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+D\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E puts a copy beside them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command V\"\u003E⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E pastes as page content, beside the original or where the pointer is (as \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/markupcraft/settings\" data-topic=\"markupcraft/settings\"\u003ESettings\u003C/a\u003E say); \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift Command V\"\u003E⇧⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E pastes in exactly the same place. Paste on another page, in another open PDF or in another MarkupCraft tab of this browser.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit\u003C/span\u003E group&#039;s menu and the right-click menu have the same commands.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA paste keeps lines as lines, words as words in their own font, and pictures as pictures. It is one piece: move it, resize it by its squares, delete it, or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E it away. It becomes part of the page in the copy you save. Whatever you copied last, markups or page content, is what \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command V\"\u003E⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E pastes.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhat the copy holds is cut to the box round the selection: something MarkupCraft can&#039;t take out of the copy (see below) and that sits inside that box comes along.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"move-a-line-s-points\"\u003EMove a line&#039;s points\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDouble-click a line or curve (or select it and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit points\u003C/span\u003E). Its points show as squares and its curve handles as circles. Drag a point or a handle; a point brings the handles next to it along, so the curve keeps its shape around it. Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or click away from the line, when you are done.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ERectangles have no points to drag: resize them with the squares around them.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"replace-a-picture\"\u003EReplace a picture\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESelect a picture and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReplace picture…\u003C/span\u003E, then pick a PNG, JPEG or SVG file (or a PDF: its first page). The new picture fills the old one&#039;s place, keeping its own proportions.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"change-the-words-of-a-line-of-text\"\u003EChange the words of a line of text\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDouble-click a line of text, type the new words in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWords\u003C/span\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. The words are written in the line&#039;s own font and size, and the line keeps its length, so the text after it does not move.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EMost PDFs carry only the letters they use from each font. When you type a letter the file&#039;s copy of the font does not have, MarkupCraft prints that letter in Helvetica and says so. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E shows which letters the font in the file has.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EText is changed one line at a time; it does not flow into the next line.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-markupcraft-leaves-as-it-is\"\u003EWhat MarkupCraft leaves as it is\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EScanned pages\u003C/strong\u003E are one picture: you can move, resize, replace or delete the picture, but what is in it can&#039;t be edited.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EEncrypted and signed PDFs:\u003C/strong\u003E changing the page would break a signature, so the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EContent\u003C/span\u003E tool says so and changes nothing. Markups still work.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EClipping areas\u003C/strong\u003E, objects split between two parts of the page&#039;s content, and color blends that fill a whole area stay as they are; MarkupCraft says why when you try.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EWords in a standard font the PDF does not describe\u003C/strong\u003E (such as a Times font with no letter widths in the file) can&#039;t be changed, because MarkupCraft could not keep the line&#039;s spacing. The line can still be moved.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"put-markups-into-the-page\"\u003EPut markups into the page\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ELines, rectangles, ellipses, polylines, clouds and pen strokes you drew as markups can become part of the page itself: select them and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPut into the page\u003C/span\u003E in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E. Text, stamps, pictures and see-through colors stay markups.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Markups sit on top of a sheet. The Content tool works on the sheet itself: the lines, pictures and lines of text the PDF draws. Your PDF on this computer is never changed: the edits are part of the copy you save. Pick what to change Choose Content in the Select group, or press B. Under Pick in Properties, turn Shapes, Pictures and Text on or off. Only the kinds that are on can be picked, so you can pick the text of a busy drawing without catching its lines. Click something to select it. Shift+click adds or removes. Drag a box over empty paper to select several: dragging to the right takes what is fully inside the box, dragging to the left takes what it touches. A shape inside a clipped area is picked only where it shows. Move, resize, recolor, delete Move: drag a selected object, or press the arrow keys (hold Shift for bigger steps). Hold Shift while dragging to move straight across or straight up. Resize: drag one of the eight squares around the selection. It grows or shrinks from the opposite side. Hold Shift to keep the proportions. Recolor: for lines and shapes, set Line color, Fill color and Line width (pt) in Properties. Delete: press Delete, or choose Delete from the page. Undo: each change is one step: Ctrl+Z takes it back, Ctrl+Y puts it back. Put back as it was undoes every change to the selected objects at once; Put this page back as it was does it for the whole page. Copy, cut and paste page content Select lines, pictures or text with the Content tool, then: Ctrl+C copies, Ctrl+X cuts (copies and takes them out of the page), Ctrl+D puts a copy beside them. Ctrl+V pastes as page content, beside the original or where the pointer is (as Settings say); Ctrl+Shift+V pastes in exactly the same place. Paste on another page, in another open PDF or in another MarkupCraft tab of this browser. The Edit group's menu and the right-click menu have the same commands. A paste keeps lines as lines, words as words in their own font, and pictures as pictures. It is one piece: move it, resize it by its squares, delete it, or Ctrl+Z it away. It becomes part of the page in the copy you save. Whatever you copied last, markups or page content, is what Ctrl+V pastes. What the copy holds is cut to the box round the selection: something MarkupCraft can't take out of the copy (see below) and that sits inside that box comes along. Move a line's points Double-click a line or curve (or select it and choose Edit points). Its points show as squares and its curve handles as circles. Drag a point or a handle; a point brings the handles next to it along, so the curve keeps its shape around it. Press Esc, or click away from the line, when you are done. Rectangles have no points to drag: resize them with the squares around them. Replace a picture Select a picture and choose Replace picture…, then pick a PNG, JPEG or SVG file (or a PDF: its first page). The new picture fills the old one's place, keeping its own proportions. Change the words of a line of text Double-click a line of text, type the new words in Words and press Enter. The words are written in the line's own font and size, and the line keeps its length, so the text after it does not move. Most PDFs carry only the letters they use from each font. When you type a letter the file's copy of the font does not have, MarkupCraft prints that letter in Helvetica and says so. Properties shows which letters the font in the file has. Text is changed one line at a time; it does not flow into the next line. What MarkupCraft leaves as it is Scanned pages are one picture: you can move, resize, replace or delete the picture, but what is in it can't be edited. Encrypted and signed PDFs: changing the page would break a signature, so the Content tool says so and changes nothing. Markups still work. Clipping areas, objects split between two parts of the page's content, and color blends that fill a whole area stay as they are; MarkupCraft says why when you try. Words in a standard font the PDF does not describe (such as a Times font with no letter widths in the file) can't be changed, because MarkupCraft could not keep the line's spacing. The line can still be moved. Put markups into the page Lines, rectangles, ellipses, polylines, clouds and pen strokes you drew as markups can become part of the page itself: select them and choose Put into the page in Properties. Text, stamps, pictures and see-through colors stay markups.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"work-live","scope":"markupcraft","title":"Review one plan set together with Live TeamWork","summary":"What changes in MarkupCraft while you are in a Live TeamWork session from CadCraft Studio: who is where, everyone's markups, replies and statuses on the same set, your own undo, and joining from a browser on the same network.","section":"tasks","order":128,"keywords":["live","live teamwork","studio live","together","collaborate","review together","session","share live","join","replies","status","punch","undo","host"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn","level":2},{"id":"everyone-marks-up-at-once","text":"Everyone marks up at once","level":2},{"id":"see-who-is-where","text":"See who is where","level":2},{"id":"when-a-change-is-taken-back","text":"When a change is taken back","level":2},{"id":"undo-undoes-your-own-work","text":"Undo undoes your own work","level":2},{"id":"join-from-a-browser","text":"Join from a browser","level":2},{"id":"when-the-session-ends","text":"When the session ends","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\"\u003EWhat you will learn\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhat happens to markups while several people review one plan set at the same time. CadCraft Studio starts and joins the Live TeamWork session (Share live, Join); this page is about the set itself.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"everyone-marks-up-at-once\"\u003EEveryone marks up at once\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe host&#039;s PDFs come to everyone who joins, so all of you look at the same sheets. Draw, move, restyle and delete markups as usual. Your own changes show at once; other people&#039;s appear a moment after they make them.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETwo people change one markup:\u003C/strong\u003E different things (one the colour, the other the line weight) are both kept. The same thing goes to the later change.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EReplies:\u003C/strong\u003E everyone&#039;s replies stay on the thread, in the order they were written. Statuses travel the same way.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EScales and sheet names\u003C/strong\u003E set by anyone apply for everyone.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPunch numbers\u003C/strong\u003E never repeat: if two people add a punch item at the same moment, one of them is taken back and can add it again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhat stays yours alone: layers you hide or lock, custom columns, the view, your tool and your name as the author.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"see-who-is-where\"\u003ESee who is where\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach reviewer has a colour. On the sheet you are looking at you see the others&#039; pointers with their names, and a dashed box in their colour around the markups they have picked. A strip of the reviewers sits at the top right of the sheet: it says which sheet each one is on, and clicking a name takes you to that sheet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn the markup list, a dot in the reviewer&#039;s colour sits beside the author&#039;s name, so you can tell at a glance who wrote what. The author filter finds one reviewer&#039;s markups. Save the PDF and every markup carries its author, so the reviewed set shows who wrote what.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPointers and colours are for the session only: they are not saved in the PDF.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"when-a-change-is-taken-back\"\u003EWhen a change is taken back\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe host&#039;s copy checks every change. One that can&#039;t go in is taken back on your screen, and the status bar says why, for example when someone deleted that markup just before you.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"undo-undoes-your-own-work\"\u003EUndo undoes your own work\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E undoes your own last change and leaves everyone else&#039;s alone. When someone changed the same thing since, nothing is undone and the status bar says who.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"join-from-a-browser\"\u003EJoin from a browser\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA teammate without Studio can scan the picture under the host&#039;s code with a phone, tablet or computer on the same network. MarkupCraft opens in the browser and fills with the host&#039;s plan set once the host lets you in. Invite links, for joining from another network, are not open yet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"when-the-session-ends\"\u003EWhen the session ends\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOnly the host saves the marked-up PDFs. Everyone else keeps an unsaved copy: save it if you want to keep it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPDFs you open after joining stay on your own screen: the others don&#039;t get them.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E","text":"What you will learn What happens to markups while several people review one plan set at the same time. CadCraft Studio starts and joins the Live TeamWork session (Share live, Join); this page is about the set itself. Everyone marks up at once The host's PDFs come to everyone who joins, so all of you look at the same sheets. Draw, move, restyle and delete markups as usual. Your own changes show at once; other people's appear a moment after they make them. Two people change one markup: different things (one the colour, the other the line weight) are both kept. The same thing goes to the later change. Replies: everyone's replies stay on the thread, in the order they were written. Statuses travel the same way. Scales and sheet names set by anyone apply for everyone. Punch numbers never repeat: if two people add a punch item at the same moment, one of them is taken back and can add it again. What stays yours alone: layers you hide or lock, custom columns, the view, your tool and your name as the author. See who is where Each reviewer has a colour. On the sheet you are looking at you see the others' pointers with their names, and a dashed box in their colour around the markups they have picked. A strip of the reviewers sits at the top right of the sheet: it says which sheet each one is on, and clicking a name takes you to that sheet. In the markup list, a dot in the reviewer's colour sits beside the author's name, so you can tell at a glance who wrote what. The author filter finds one reviewer's markups. Save the PDF and every markup carries its author, so the reviewed set shows who wrote what. Pointers and colours are for the session only: they are not saved in the PDF. When a change is taken back The host's copy checks every change. One that can't go in is taken back on your screen, and the status bar says why, for example when someone deleted that markup just before you. Undo undoes your own work Ctrl+Z undoes your own last change and leaves everyone else's alone. When someone changed the same thing since, nothing is undone and the status bar says who. Join from a browser A teammate without Studio can scan the picture under the host's code with a phone, tablet or computer on the same network. MarkupCraft opens in the browser and fills with the host's plan set once the host lets you in. Invite links, for joining from another network, are not open yet. When the session ends Only the host saves the marked-up PDFs. Everyone else keeps an unsaved copy: save it if you want to keep it. PDFs you open after joining stay on your own screen: the others don't get them.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"copy-an-area","scope":"markupcraft","title":"Copy an area of the page, or an overlay","summary":"Copy part of a sheet with a rectangle, an ellipse or a freeform shape and paste it into another sheet, CadCraft, BimCraft, DetailCraft or any program, at its size; copy it as a see-through overlay, and line a picture up with two points.","section":"tasks","order":129,"keywords":["copy area","area","crop","snip","snapshot","clip","region","rectangle","ellipse","circle","freeform","lasso","picture","image","png","overlay","transparent","see-through","tint","opacity","paste","true size","scale","line up","align","two points","cadcraft","bimcraft","detailcraft"],"headings":[{"id":"pick-the-area","text":"Pick the area","level":2},{"id":"copy","text":"Copy","level":2},{"id":"copy-as-overlay","text":"Copy as overlay","level":2},{"id":"paste-it","text":"Paste it","level":2},{"id":"line-up-a-picture-with-two-points","text":"Line up a picture with two points","level":2},{"id":"good-to-know","text":"Good to know","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECopy an area of the page…\u003C/span\u003E. A bar opens over the sheet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"pick-the-area\"\u003EPick the area\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERectangle\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEllipse\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFreeform\u003C/span\u003E in the bar, then drag on the sheet:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERectangle:\u003C/strong\u003E drag a box round the part to copy.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EEllipse:\u003C/strong\u003E drag a box; the ellipse inside it is copied and the corners outside it are see-through.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFreeform:\u003C/strong\u003E draw round the part to copy; the shape closes itself. Outside it is see-through.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDrag again to start over, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"copy\"\u003ECopy\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EInclude markups\u003C/span\u003E copies your markups with the page; untick it for the clean drawing.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EResolution\u003C/span\u003E: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAs on screen\u003C/span\u003E, 72, 150 or 300 dpi. The picture is kept under 3 MB; when a big area at a high resolution would pass that, the resolution is lowered and the status line says to what.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECopy\u003C/span\u003E (or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command C\"\u003E⌘C\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+C\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) puts the area on the clipboard as a PNG picture, which pastes into Word, email, other PDF programs and drawing programs.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave as PNG\u003C/span\u003E downloads the picture instead, for a program that drops the see-through parts on paste.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"copy-as-overlay\"\u003ECopy as overlay\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECopy as overlay\u003C/span\u003E also makes the white paper see-through, so only the lines are left. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EColour\u003C/span\u003E gives every line one colour (red, blue, green, orange or purple) so the overlay stands out from the drawing under it; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOwn colours\u003C/span\u003E keeps them. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStrength\u003C/span\u003E is how strong it starts when pasted in our apps (50% unless you change it).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"paste-it\"\u003EPaste it\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EIn MarkupCraft\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command V\"\u003E⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E on any sheet pastes the area as a picture markup at the pointer. It comes in at its size:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eon paper, the same size it had on the sheet it came from;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ewhen both sheets have a scale, the same size in the drawing: an area from a 1:50 sheet comes in at half its paper size on a 1:100 sheet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAn overlay starts at its strength; change it with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpacity\u003C/span\u003E in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E. Move, resize, lock and delete it like any picture, and tick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFlatten into the page when saved\u003C/span\u003E to draw it into the page.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EIn CadCraft, BimCraft and DetailCraft\u003C/strong\u003E the copy also knows its size, so a paste comes in at the drawing&#039;s real size when the sheet had a scale, or asks for one when it did not. See each app&#039;s help on pictures.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"line-up-a-picture-with-two-points\"\u003ELine up a picture with two points\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESelect one picture or overlay, then choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELine up a picture with two points\u003C/span\u003E:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick a point on the picture.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick where that point goes.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick a second point on the picture, away from the first.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick where that point goes.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe picture is scaled, moved and turned so both points land where you clicked. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E stops; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E undoes it in one step.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"good-to-know\"\u003EGood to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe size travels with the copy only between our apps on the same site, and only for a copy made in the last few hours; another program gets a plain picture.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIf the browser does not let MarkupCraft use the system clipboard, the status line says so: paste in our apps still works, and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave as PNG\u003C/span\u003E works for other programs.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELine up turns the picture by redrawing its pixels, so a turned picture is slightly softer than the original.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"Choose More › Copy an area of the page…. A bar opens over the sheet. Pick the area Pick Rectangle, Ellipse or Freeform in the bar, then drag on the sheet: Rectangle: drag a box round the part to copy. Ellipse: drag a box; the ellipse inside it is copied and the corners outside it are see-through. Freeform: draw round the part to copy; the shape closes itself. Outside it is see-through. Drag again to start over, or press Esc. Copy Include markups copies your markups with the page; untick it for the clean drawing. Resolution: As on screen, 72, 150 or 300 dpi. The picture is kept under 3 MB; when a big area at a high resolution would pass that, the resolution is lowered and the status line says to what. Copy (or Ctrl+C or Enter) puts the area on the clipboard as a PNG picture, which pastes into Word, email, other PDF programs and drawing programs. Save as PNG downloads the picture instead, for a program that drops the see-through parts on paste. Copy as overlay Copy as overlay also makes the white paper see-through, so only the lines are left. Colour gives every line one colour (red, blue, green, orange or purple) so the overlay stands out from the drawing under it; Own colours keeps them. Strength is how strong it starts when pasted in our apps (50% unless you change it). Paste it In MarkupCraft, Ctrl+V on any sheet pastes the area as a picture markup at the pointer. It comes in at its size: on paper, the same size it had on the sheet it came from; when both sheets have a scale, the same size in the drawing: an area from a 1:50 sheet comes in at half its paper size on a 1:100 sheet. An overlay starts at its strength; change it with Opacity in Properties. Move, resize, lock and delete it like any picture, and tick Flatten into the page when saved to draw it into the page. In CadCraft, BimCraft and DetailCraft the copy also knows its size, so a paste comes in at the drawing's real size when the sheet had a scale, or asks for one when it did not. See each app's help on pictures. Line up a picture with two points Select one picture or overlay, then choose More › Line up a picture with two points: Click a point on the picture. Click where that point goes. Click a second point on the picture, away from the first. Click where that point goes. The picture is scaled, moved and turned so both points land where you clicked. Esc stops; Ctrl+Z undoes it in one step. Good to know The size travels with the copy only between our apps on the same site, and only for a copy made in the last few hours; another program gets a plain picture. If the browser does not let MarkupCraft use the system clipboard, the status line says so: paste in our apps still works, and Save as PNG works for other programs. Line up turns the picture by redrawing its pixels, so a turned picture is slightly softer than the original.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"several-files","scope":"markupcraft","title":"Work with several files at once","summary":"Open many PDFs as one set, find sheets across files, copy markups between them, and save each file's copy.","section":"tasks","order":130,"keywords":["several files","multiple pdfs","plan set","set","batch","many files","combine","disciplines","architectural structural mechanical","save all","close"],"headings":[{"id":"open-them-together","text":"Open them together","level":2},{"id":"across-the-whole-set","text":"Across the whole set","level":2},{"id":"save","text":"Save","level":2},{"id":"close","text":"Close","level":2},{"id":"big-sets","text":"Big sets","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EA plan set often arrives as one PDF per discipline, or one PDF per sheet. MarkupCraft treats everything you open as \u003Cstrong\u003Eone set\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"open-them-together\"\u003EOpen them together\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESelect all the PDFs in the file picker at once, or drop them all on the sheet area. To add more later, open them the same way; they join the end of the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESheets\u003C/span\u003E list in the order you open them.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EResult: &quot;Opened 6 files (84 sheets in the set).&quot; Every page is a sheet. Sheets with a sheet number show &quot;Sheet A-101&quot; under their name; others show the file and page.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThere is no saved &quot;set&quot; file: next time, open the same PDFs again (the &quot;(marked up)&quot; copies, if you saved).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"across-the-whole-set\"\u003EAcross the whole set\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFind a sheet:\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESearch sheets\u003C/span\u003E looks in sheet numbers, sheet names and file names of every file. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn ↑\"\u003Efn ↑\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EPage Up\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn ↓\"\u003Efn ↓\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EPage Down\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E walk through all sheets of all files in order.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMarkups list:\u003C/strong\u003E the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMarkups\u003C/span\u003E tab lists markups from every file. Filter by \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESheet\u003C/span\u003E to narrow it. The CSV has a File column.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPunch list:\u003C/strong\u003E one numbering for the whole set.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECompare:\u003C/strong\u003E the old and the new sheet can come from different files.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECopy and paste:\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command C\"\u003E⌘C\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+C\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E on one sheet and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command V\"\u003E⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E on another, in any file. The copy lands at the same place on the page, slightly offset.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"save\"\u003ESave\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave\u003C/span\u003E. With more than one file open, MarkupCraft asks:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EThis file\u003C/span\u003E: a marked-up copy of the file of the sheet you are on.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEvery changed file\u003C/span\u003E: a copy of each file that has unsaved changes. Each downloads separately, as &quot;&lt;file name&gt; (marked up).pdf&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECancel\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:chromium\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EChrome or Edge\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe first time you save several files at once, Chrome and Edge may ask whether this site may download multiple files. Choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAllow\u003C/span\u003E; otherwise only the first copy arrives.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:safari\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003ESafari\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESafari may ask once for each file. On an iPad each copy goes to the Files app.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA file whose markups were saved stops counting as changed; the others still do.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECSV, BCF and punch list exports are named after the \u003Cstrong\u003Efirst\u003C/strong\u003E file you opened.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"close\"\u003EClose\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EClose\u003C/span\u003E closes the whole set; there is no way to close just one file yet. If anything is unsaved, it asks:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECancel\u003C/span\u003E: nothing happens.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EClose without saving\u003C/span\u003E: unsaved markups are lost.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave copies first\u003C/span\u003E: saves every changed file, then closes.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EClosing also clears undo history.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"big-sets\"\u003EBig sets\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEvery page&#039;s text is read on open to find sheet numbers, so a 100-sheet set takes a while. The status bar counts sheets as it goes.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESheet pictures in the list are drawn as you scroll to them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEach open PDF is held in the browser tab&#039;s memory. If the tab slows down, close other heavy tabs, or review one discipline at a time.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpening the same file twice gives you two copies of its sheets. If that happens, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EClose\u003C/span\u003E and open the set again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"A plan set often arrives as one PDF per discipline, or one PDF per sheet. MarkupCraft treats everything you open as one set. Open them together Select all the PDFs in the file picker at once, or drop them all on the sheet area. To add more later, open them the same way; they join the end of the Sheets list in the order you open them. Result: \"Opened 6 files (84 sheets in the set).\" Every page is a sheet. Sheets with a sheet number show \"Sheet A-101\" under their name; others show the file and page. There is no saved \"set\" file: next time, open the same PDFs again (the \"(marked up)\" copies, if you saved). Across the whole set Find a sheet: Search sheets looks in sheet numbers, sheet names and file names of every file. Page Up and Page Down walk through all sheets of all files in order. Markups list: the Markups tab lists markups from every file. Filter by Sheet to narrow it. The CSV has a File column. Punch list: one numbering for the whole set. Compare: the old and the new sheet can come from different files. Copy and paste: Ctrl+C on one sheet and Ctrl+V on another, in any file. The copy lands at the same place on the page, slightly offset. Save Choose Save. With more than one file open, MarkupCraft asks: This file: a marked-up copy of the file of the sheet you are on. Every changed file: a copy of each file that has unsaved changes. Each downloads separately, as \"\u003Cfile name\u003E (marked up).pdf\". Cancel The first time you save several files at once, Chrome and Edge may ask whether this site may download multiple files. Choose Allow; otherwise only the first copy arrives. Safari may ask once for each file. On an iPad each copy goes to the Files app. A file whose markups were saved stops counting as changed; the others still do. CSV, BCF and punch list exports are named after the first file you opened. Close Close closes the whole set; there is no way to close just one file yet. If anything is unsaved, it asks: Cancel: nothing happens. Close without saving: unsaved markups are lost. Save copies first: saves every changed file, then closes. Closing also clears undo history. Big sets Every page's text is read on open to find sheet numbers, so a 100-sheet set takes a while. The status bar counts sheets as it goes. Sheet pictures in the list are drawn as you scroll to them. Each open PDF is held in the browser tab's memory. If the tab slows down, close other heavy tabs, or review one discipline at a time. Opening the same file twice gives you two copies of its sheets. If that happens, choose Close and open the set again.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"use-with-ai-assistants","scope":"markupcraft","title":"Use MarkupCraft with an AI assistant","summary":"Let your own AI assistant or script list the markups of a set, add simple ones, set their status, reply and export the list: permissions, connecting through CadCraft Studio, and the activity log.","section":"tasks","order":135,"keywords":["ai","assistant","agent","mcp","model context protocol","script","automation","markups","review","permissions","disconnect","activity"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn","level":2},{"id":"turn-it-on","text":"Turn it on","level":2},{"id":"connect-your-assistant","text":"Connect your assistant","level":2},{"id":"you-stay-in-charge","text":"You stay in charge","level":2},{"id":"what-an-assistant-can-ask-for","text":"What an assistant can ask for","level":2},{"id":"if-something-doesn-t-work","text":"If something doesn't work","level":2},{"id":"see-also","text":"See also","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\"\u003EWhat you will learn\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHow to let an AI assistant (or a script you wrote) work with MarkupCraft, and how to decide what it is allowed to touch.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EMarkupCraft has \u003Cstrong\u003Eno AI of its own\u003C/strong\u003E and sends nothing anywhere. You bring the assistant: any program that speaks the Model Context Protocol (MCP). MarkupCraft only gives it tools, and you choose which.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"turn-it-on\"\u003ETurn it on\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E (the gear in the app bar, or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command ,\"\u003E⌘,\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+,\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) and go to the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMarkupCraft\u003C/span\u003E tab.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUnder \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAssistants\u003C/span\u003E, tick \u003Cstrong\u003ELet AI assistants and scripts use MarkupCraft\u003C/strong\u003E. It is off until you do.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose what it may do: \u003Cstrong\u003ERead\u003C/strong\u003E is ticked to start with; tick \u003Cstrong\u003EEdit\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003ESave\u003C/strong\u003E or \u003Cstrong\u003EExport\u003C/strong\u003E only when you need them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETools outside the permissions you ticked are not offered to the assistant, and if it asks for one anyway, MarkupCraft refuses and writes it in the activity log. Turning the switch off ends every connection at once; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReset MarkupCraft settings\u003C/span\u003E turns it off too.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"connect-your-assistant\"\u003EConnect your assistant\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAssistants connect through \u003Cstrong\u003ECadCraft Studio\u003C/strong\u003E on your computer: Studio runs a small server only this computer can reach, protected by a token it makes each time it starts. In Studio, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EConnect an assistant\u003C/span\u003E and copy the address and the token into your assistant (Studio shows the exact settings to paste). Never share the token.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA browser tab can&#039;t open a connection for an assistant: in the browser apps the switch has no effect until you use CadCraft Studio on your computer.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"you-stay-in-charge\"\u003EYou stay in charge\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EEvery change is one undo step\u003C/strong\u003E, labelled with the assistant&#039;s name for it (for example &quot;Assistant: add cloudrect&quot;). \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E takes back the assistant&#039;s last change as a whole.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EYou know when one is connected.\u003C/strong\u003E A button in the app bar names the assistant while it is connected; click it to see what it may do, or to \u003Cstrong\u003EDisconnect\u003C/strong\u003E it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EActivity.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMarkupCraft\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAssistants\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EActivity\u003C/span\u003E lists every call: when, which tool, done or refused. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave the log\u003C/span\u003E keeps a copy.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-an-assistant-can-ask-for\"\u003EWhat an assistant can ask for\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ETool\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EPermission\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it does\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Eapp_info\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Esheets_list\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERead\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe sets open, their sheets and how many markups each has\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Emarkups_list\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERead\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe markups with type, sheet, text, comment, measurement, status, author and replies, filtered by sheet, status, type or author\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Emarkup_add\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEdit\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAdds a rectangle, cloud, ellipse, text box, note, line or arrow to a sheet\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Emarkup_status\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Emarkup_reply\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEdit\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESets a markup&#039;s status (logged in its thread, signed by the assistant); adds a comment to a thread\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Emarkups_export\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EExport\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMarkups\u003C/span\u003E list&#039;s own CSV or BCF of what the list shows (its filters as you set them); in Studio it can write the file to a folder you allowed\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Etasks_list\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERead\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe markups that are tasks: assignee (person or assistant), due day, state, link, how it closed. \u003Ccode\u003Eassignee: &quot;assistant&quot;\u003C/code\u003E lists the work waiting for an assistant\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Etask_set\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Etask_clear\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEdit\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMake a markup a task or change it (title, assignee, due, state, link); take the task off\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Etasks_check\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEdit\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClose tasks whose linked drawing area or model element really changed; pass the element states you read from the other app\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Erevisions_changes\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERead\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe plain list of what changed between two open plan sets, to put in your own words\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Epins_list\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Edecision_add\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERead, Edit\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EVoice notes (length, who, when, caption; never the sound, no transcript) and decisions; pin a decision and replace an earlier one\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Eredline_export\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eredline_answer\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EExport, Edit\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWrite the redline file for the drafter; apply the drafter&#039;s answers\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA markup an assistant adds names it as the author: the name the assistant program gives itself, followed by &quot;(assistant)&quot;, or by &quot;(assistant for &lt;your name&gt;)&quot; once you have set your reviewer name. Its status changes and replies are signed the same way. Each is one undo step. Saving the marked-up PDF stays with you: use \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave\u003C/span\u003E as usual.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWithout opening MarkupCraft, the CadCraft MCP server lists the markups of a PDF in a folder you name (\u003Ccode\u003Emarkups_list\u003C/code\u003E), and \u003Ccode\u003Ecadcraft info Set.pdf\u003C/code\u003E prints them on the command line.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"if-something-doesn-t-work\"\u003EIf something doesn&#039;t work\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe assistant sees no MarkupCraft tools:\u003C/strong\u003E check that the switch is on in this window, and that the window is open in Studio.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;needs the edit permission&quot;:\u003C/strong\u003E tick \u003Cstrong\u003EEdit\u003C/strong\u003E under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAssistants\u003C/span\u003E, then ask again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;401&quot; or &quot;token&quot; errors:\u003C/strong\u003E the token changed (Studio restarted, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew token\u003C/span\u003E). Copy the new one into the assistant&#039;s settings.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"see-also\"\u003ESee also\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/assistants-recipes\" data-topic=\"basics/assistants-recipes\"\u003ERecipes for AI assistants\u003C/a\u003E: worked examples, each with the tools it calls.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you will learn How to let an AI assistant (or a script you wrote) work with MarkupCraft, and how to decide what it is allowed to touch. MarkupCraft has no AI of its own and sends nothing anywhere. You bring the assistant: any program that speaks the Model Context Protocol (MCP). MarkupCraft only gives it tools, and you choose which. Turn it on Open Settings (the gear in the app bar, or Ctrl+,) and go to the MarkupCraft tab. Under Assistants, tick Let AI assistants and scripts use MarkupCraft. It is off until you do. Choose what it may do: Read is ticked to start with; tick Edit, Save or Export only when you need them. Tools outside the permissions you ticked are not offered to the assistant, and if it asks for one anyway, MarkupCraft refuses and writes it in the activity log. Turning the switch off ends every connection at once; Reset MarkupCraft settings turns it off too. Connect your assistant Assistants connect through CadCraft Studio on your computer: Studio runs a small server only this computer can reach, protected by a token it makes each time it starts. In Studio, choose Connect an assistant and copy the address and the token into your assistant (Studio shows the exact settings to paste). Never share the token. A browser tab can't open a connection for an assistant: in the browser apps the switch has no effect until you use CadCraft Studio on your computer. You stay in charge Every change is one undo step, labelled with the assistant's name for it (for example \"Assistant: add cloudrect\"). Ctrl+Z takes back the assistant's last change as a whole. You know when one is connected. A button in the app bar names the assistant while it is connected; click it to see what it may do, or to Disconnect it. Activity. Settings › MarkupCraft › Assistants › Activity lists every call: when, which tool, done or refused. Save the log keeps a copy. What an assistant can ask for Tool Permission What it does app_info, sheets_list Read The sets open, their sheets and how many markups each has markups_list Read The markups with type, sheet, text, comment, measurement, status, author and replies, filtered by sheet, status, type or author markup_add Edit Adds a rectangle, cloud, ellipse, text box, note, line or arrow to a sheet markup_status, markup_reply Edit Sets a markup's status (logged in its thread, signed by the assistant); adds a comment to a thread markups_export Export The Markups list's own CSV or BCF of what the list shows (its filters as you set them); in Studio it can write the file to a folder you allowed tasks_list Read The markups that are tasks: assignee (person or assistant), due day, state, link, how it closed. assignee: \"assistant\" lists the work waiting for an assistant task_set, task_clear Edit Make a markup a task or change it (title, assignee, due, state, link); take the task off tasks_check Edit Close tasks whose linked drawing area or model element really changed; pass the element states you read from the other app revisions_changes Read The plain list of what changed between two open plan sets, to put in your own words pins_list, decision_add Read, Edit Voice notes (length, who, when, caption; never the sound, no transcript) and decisions; pin a decision and replace an earlier one redline_export, redline_answer Export, Edit Write the redline file for the drafter; apply the drafter's answers A markup an assistant adds names it as the author: the name the assistant program gives itself, followed by \"(assistant)\", or by \"(assistant for \u003Cyour name\u003E)\" once you have set your reviewer name. Its status changes and replies are signed the same way. Each is one undo step. Saving the marked-up PDF stays with you: use Save as usual. Without opening MarkupCraft, the CadCraft MCP server lists the markups of a PDF in a folder you name (markups_list), and cadcraft info Set.pdf prints them on the command line. If something doesn't work The assistant sees no MarkupCraft tools: check that the switch is on in this window, and that the window is open in Studio. \"needs the edit permission\": tick Edit under Assistants, then ask again. \"401\" or \"token\" errors: the token changed (Studio restarted, or New token). Copy the new one into the assistant's settings. See also Recipes for AI assistants: worked examples, each with the tools it calls.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"review-on-a-tablet","scope":"markupcraft","title":"Review on an iPad or touch screen","summary":"Pinch, pan and draw with your fingers or a stylus, what acts on lift, what needs a keyboard, and how to keep your work safe on a tablet.","section":"tasks","order":140,"keywords":["ipad","tablet","touch","touch screen","pinch","zoom","two fingers","apple pencil","stylus","finger","field","site","surface","gestures","mobile"],"headings":[{"id":"gestures","text":"Gestures","level":2},{"id":"what-happens-on-lift","text":"What happens on lift","level":2},{"id":"selecting-with-a-finger","text":"Selecting with a finger","level":2},{"id":"things-that-need-a-keyboard","text":"Things that need a keyboard","level":2},{"id":"stylus-and-apple-pencil","text":"Stylus and Apple Pencil","level":2},{"id":"opening-and-saving-on-an-ipad","text":"Opening and saving on an iPad","level":2},{"id":"keep-your-work-safe-on-a-tablet","text":"Keep your work safe on a tablet","level":2},{"id":"on-a-phone","text":"On a phone","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EMarkupCraft works with touch: on an iPad (Safari or Chrome), on a touch-screen laptop, or on a Windows or Android tablet with a recent browser. In landscape a tablet shows the full layout.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"gestures\"\u003EGestures\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EGesture\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it does\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOne finger\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDraws with the current tool, or selects with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETwo fingers, spread or pinch\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EZooms around the point between your fingers\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETwo fingers, moved together\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPans; you can zoom and pan in one movement\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA second finger while drawing\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECancels what the first finger started and starts zooming instead\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDouble-tap, or touch and hold\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFinishes a polyline, polygon, free cloud or area\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETouch and hold a text, callout or note, with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpens its text to edit\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOne finger with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPan\u003C/span\u003E on\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPans\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn a touch screen the toolbar buttons, menus, tabs and dialog buttons are larger, so they are easy to hit with a finger.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-happens-on-lift\"\u003EWhat happens on lift\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESo that the first finger of a pinch never leaves a mark, some tools act when you \u003Cstrong\u003Elift\u003C/strong\u003E your finger, not when you touch:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECount\u003C/span\u003E: the dot is added on lift.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENote\u003C/span\u003E: the note is placed on lift.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPolyline\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPolygon\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFree cloud\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArea\u003C/span\u003E: each point is added on lift.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELine\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArrow\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELength\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECalibrate\u003C/span\u003E: when you tap the start and then tap the end, the end is placed on lift.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf your finger slides more than a little before lifting, it does not count as a tap and nothing is added. Tap cleanly; to correct, use \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUndo\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETools you drag (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPen\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHighlight\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECloud\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERectangle\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEllipse\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECallout\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EText\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStamp\u003C/span\u003E) start as soon as you touch; a second finger cancels them.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"selecting-with-a-finger\"\u003ESelecting with a finger\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETap a markup to select it; drag it to move it; drag a handle to resize. After a finger touches the sheet, markups and handles take a bigger target and the handles draw larger.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDrag across empty sheet to draw a selection window (left to right: fully inside; right to left: touching).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E, one finger does \u003Cstrong\u003Enot\u003C/strong\u003E pan. Use two fingers, or choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPan\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"things-that-need-a-keyboard\"\u003EThings that need a keyboard\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWithout a keyboard there is no \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. Here is what to do instead:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKeyboard action\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWithout a keyboard\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to cancel or go back to Select\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETap \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E in the toolbar\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E for 45° lines, squares and circles\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDraw carefully; adjust with the handles\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E-click to add to a selection\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUse a selection window\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E-click to remove a count dot\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUndo\u003C/span\u003E right after a wrong tap\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Return\"\u003E⌘ Return\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Enter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to finish typing\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETap outside the text box\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to finish a polyline, or to edit a markup&#039;s text\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETouch and hold, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit on the sheet\u003C/span\u003E in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn Delete\"\u003Efn Delete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E in the toolbar or in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EStarting a new count group with \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETap empty sheet with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E, then tap \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECount\u003C/span\u003E again\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA keyboard case or Bluetooth keyboard brings back every shortcut; on an iPad, the ⌘ key works for \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and the rest.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EButtons do not show their tooltips on touch. The key for each tool is listed in \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/markupcraft/keyboard\" data-topic=\"markupcraft/keyboard\"\u003EKeyboard and mouse\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"stylus-and-apple-pencil\"\u003EStylus and Apple Pencil\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA stylus (Apple Pencil, Surface Pen and the like) works like a mouse: it draws and selects, and acts at once on touch-down. Use your fingers for pinch and pan. Pressure does not change line width.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"opening-and-saving-on-an-ipad\"\u003EOpening and saving on an iPad\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen\u003C/span\u003E shows the Files picker: iCloud Drive, On My iPad, and any storage app you have connected.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave\u003C/span\u003E asks to download; the copy lands in Files, under Downloads (or the folder set in Safari&#039;s settings).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"keep-your-work-safe-on-a-tablet\"\u003EKeep your work safe on a tablet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-warning\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003EWarning\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EiPad Safari does not warn you before leaving a page with unsaved work, and iPadOS can reload a tab in the background to free memory. MarkupCraft keeps a recovery copy in the browser and offers it back when you open it again (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/recover\" data-topic=\"basics/recover\"\u003ERecover your work\u003C/a\u003E), but \u003Cstrong\u003Esave after each sheet or each walk-round\u003C/strong\u003E: a private tab keeps no copy.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EYour name, tool settings and tool chest are kept in that browser on that iPad, like on a computer. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/markupcraft/browser-storage\" data-topic=\"markupcraft/browser-storage\"\u003EWhat stays in this browser\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EBefore a site visit with no signal:\u003C/strong\u003E while you are still online, open MarkupCraft in Safari (or Chrome) on the iPad and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave for offline\u003C/span\u003E in the bar at the top. Wait until it says &quot;Offline ready&quot; (it counts &quot;Saving N of M&quot;; the PDF reader and writer are about 6 MB). On site, open the same address or a bookmark of it: opening, marking up and saving PDFs work with no connection. If it says &quot;Offline not available&quot;, you are in a private tab; use a normal one. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/offline\" data-topic=\"basics/offline\"\u003EWorking offline\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESafari removes an offline copy you have not used for some weeks. Before a trip, click &quot;Offline ready&quot; and choose &quot;Save again (get the newest version)&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EVery large plan sets use a lot of memory. On a tablet, open only the sheets you need for the visit.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"on-a-phone\"\u003EOn a phone\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIt works, but the screen is small: the parts stack (toolbar, sheet, status bar, panels, then the sheets list), and the toolbar scrolls sideways. Use a phone to look and to add a quick note, and a tablet or computer for real review.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"MarkupCraft works with touch: on an iPad (Safari or Chrome), on a touch-screen laptop, or on a Windows or Android tablet with a recent browser. In landscape a tablet shows the full layout. Gestures Gesture What it does One finger Draws with the current tool, or selects with Select Two fingers, spread or pinch Zooms around the point between your fingers Two fingers, moved together Pans; you can zoom and pan in one movement A second finger while drawing Cancels what the first finger started and starts zooming instead Double-tap, or touch and hold Finishes a polyline, polygon, free cloud or area Touch and hold a text, callout or note, with Select Opens its text to edit One finger with Pan on Pans On a touch screen the toolbar buttons, menus, tabs and dialog buttons are larger, so they are easy to hit with a finger. What happens on lift So that the first finger of a pinch never leaves a mark, some tools act when you lift your finger, not when you touch: Count: the dot is added on lift. Note: the note is placed on lift. Polyline, Polygon, Free cloud and Area: each point is added on lift. Line, Arrow, Length and Calibrate: when you tap the start and then tap the end, the end is placed on lift. If your finger slides more than a little before lifting, it does not count as a tap and nothing is added. Tap cleanly; to correct, use Undo. Tools you drag (Pen, Highlight, Cloud, Rectangle, Ellipse, Callout, Text, Stamp) start as soon as you touch; a second finger cancels them. Selecting with a finger Tap a markup to select it; drag it to move it; drag a handle to resize. After a finger touches the sheet, markups and handles take a bigger target and the handles draw larger. Drag across empty sheet to draw a selection window (left to right: fully inside; right to left: touching). In Select, one finger does not pan. Use two fingers, or choose Pan. Things that need a keyboard Without a keyboard there is no Shift, Esc or Enter. Here is what to do instead: Keyboard action Without a keyboard Esc to cancel or go back to Select Tap Select in the toolbar Shift for 45° lines, squares and circles Draw carefully; adjust with the handles Shift-click to add to a selection Use a selection window Shift-click to remove a count dot Undo right after a wrong tap Ctrl+Enter to finish typing Tap outside the text box Enter to finish a polyline, or to edit a markup's text Touch and hold, or Edit on the sheet in Properties Delete Delete in the toolbar or in Properties Starting a new count group with Esc Tap empty sheet with Select, then tap Count again A keyboard case or Bluetooth keyboard brings back every shortcut; on an iPad, the ⌘ key works for Ctrl+Z and the rest. Buttons do not show their tooltips on touch. The key for each tool is listed in Keyboard and mouse. Stylus and Apple Pencil A stylus (Apple Pencil, Surface Pen and the like) works like a mouse: it draws and selects, and acts at once on touch-down. Use your fingers for pinch and pan. Pressure does not change line width. Opening and saving on an iPad Open shows the Files picker: iCloud Drive, On My iPad, and any storage app you have connected. Save asks to download; the copy lands in Files, under Downloads (or the folder set in Safari's settings). Keep your work safe on a tablet iPad Safari does not warn you before leaving a page with unsaved work, and iPadOS can reload a tab in the background to free memory. MarkupCraft keeps a recovery copy in the browser and offers it back when you open it again (see Recover your work), but save after each sheet or each walk-round: a private tab keeps no copy. Your name, tool settings and tool chest are kept in that browser on that iPad, like on a computer. See What stays in this browser. Before a site visit with no signal: while you are still online, open MarkupCraft in Safari (or Chrome) on the iPad and choose Save for offline in the bar at the top. Wait until it says \"Offline ready\" (it counts \"Saving N of M\"; the PDF reader and writer are about 6 MB). On site, open the same address or a bookmark of it: opening, marking up and saving PDFs work with no connection. If it says \"Offline not available\", you are in a private tab; use a normal one. See Working offline. Safari removes an offline copy you have not used for some weeks. Before a trip, click \"Offline ready\" and choose \"Save again (get the newest version)\". Very large plan sets use a lot of memory. On a tablet, open only the sheets you need for the visit. On a phone It works, but the screen is small: the parts stack (toolbar, sheet, status bar, panels, then the sheets list), and the toolbar scrolls sideways. Use a phone to look and to add a quick note, and a tablet or computer for real review.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"coming-from","scope":"markupcraft","title":"Coming from other markup software","summary":"How the habits of a desktop PDF markup program map to MarkupCraft, and the differences that catch people out.","section":"switching","order":10,"keywords":["switch","switching","migrate","familiar","desktop pdf markup","tool chest","markups list","overlay","sessions","profile","shortcuts","differences"],"headings":[{"id":"the-five-differences-that-matter","text":"The five differences that matter","level":2},{"id":"habit-by-habit","text":"Habit by habit","level":2},{"id":"keys-that-differ","text":"Keys that differ","level":2},{"id":"things-that-work-differently-than-you-might-expect","text":"Things that work differently than you might expect","level":2},{"id":"using-both","text":"Using both","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EIf you review drawings in Bluebeam Revu (or comment on PDFs in another desktop PDF program), most of MarkupCraft will feel familiar: clouds, callouts, a tool chest, a markups list, statuses with replies, calibrated measurements, sheet overlays. The way files are handled is the big difference, so read the next section even if you skip the rest.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-five-differences-that-matter\"\u003EThe five differences that matter\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESave makes a new file.\u003C/strong\u003E MarkupCraft runs in the browser and cannot write over the PDF on your disk. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave\u003C/span\u003E downloads &quot;&lt;name&gt; (marked up).pdf&quot; and the original stays as it was. Next time, open the &quot;(marked up)&quot; copy, and tidy old copies yourself.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe PDF is saved when you save.\u003C/strong\u003E Until then a recovery copy in this browser keeps your markups through a crash or a closed tab, and MarkupCraft offers it back next time. It is a safety net: save the PDF to keep the review.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EYour profile lives in the browser.\u003C/strong\u003E Your author name, tool settings and tool chest are kept in this browser on this computer. A different browser or computer starts fresh, and clearing site data erases them. Export your tool chest as a backup. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/markupcraft/browser-storage\" data-topic=\"markupcraft/browser-storage\"\u003EWhat stays in this browser\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENo shared sessions yet.\u003C/strong\u003E There is no live, shared review session and no cloud project. The review travels in the PDF: save, send the copy, the next person replies and saves.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EScale is per sheet, and only per sheet.\u003C/strong\u003E There are no viewports inside a sheet and no &quot;apply this scale to all pages&quot;. Calibrate each sheet you measure on.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"habit-by-habit\"\u003EHabit by habit\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EYou are used to\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EIn MarkupCraft\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThumbnails panel, page labels\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESheets\u003C/span\u003E on the left. Names come from page labels or are read from the title block; fix them with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERename sheet\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpening several PDFs as a set\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpen them all at once; they become one list. No set file is saved: open the same PDFs next time.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETool chest with tool sets\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETools\u003C/span\u003E tab: one chest per browser, saved tools with style and subject. Share by \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EImport\u003C/span\u003E as a JSON file. Tool set files from other programs cannot be imported.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EProperties panel\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E tab: status, subject, comment, style, replies and punch item in one place.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMarkups list with columns\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMarkups\u003C/span\u003E tab with filters for sheet, author, status and type. Export CSV or BCF. No custom columns and no PDF or workbook summary yet.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EStatus and review state\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStatus\u003C/span\u003E: Open, Accepted, Rejected, Completed. No custom status sets. Saved as standard review states.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EReplies on a markup\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe &quot;Replies&quot; thread in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E, with status changes logged in it. A sent reply cannot be edited.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECalibrate, then length, area, count\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECalibrate\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELength\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"M\"\u003EM\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EM\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArea\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Q\"\u003EQ\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EQ\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, gives area and perimeter), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECount\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"U\"\u003EU\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EU\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E). No angle, radius, volume, polylength or cut-outs yet.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOverlay pages\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECompare\u003C/span\u003E tab: red for old, blue for new, align by two points. No automatic &quot;compare documents&quot; clouding.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EStamps\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStamp\u003C/span\u003E with six built-in stamps and custom wording, signed with your name and the date. No image or signature stamps yet.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPunch or issue tracking\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPunch list\u003C/span\u003E tab: numbered items with assignee, due date and status; CSV export.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELive collaboration session\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENot available yet. Pass the saved PDF around.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFlatten\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENot built yet. Markups stay as comments other viewers can show and print.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELayers for markups\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENot built yet. Use subjects, authors and the list filters to sort comments.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHyperlinks between sheets\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENot built yet.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESpacebar to pan, wheel to zoom\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe same: hold \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and drag; the wheel zooms at the pointer. The wheel never scrolls the page.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"keys-that-differ\"\u003EKeys that differ\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EMany one-letter keys are what you might expect, but check these before your fingers do it for you:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"K\"\u003EK\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EK\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E is \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECallout\u003C/span\u003E, and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Q\"\u003EQ\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EQ\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E is \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArea\u003C/span\u003E. If you are used to Q for callouts, it will start an area here.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"C\"\u003EC\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EC\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E is the box \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECloud\u003C/span\u003E; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift C\"\u003E⇧C\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift+C\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E is \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFree cloud\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"M\"\u003EM\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EM\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E is \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELength\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"U\"\u003EU\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EU\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E is \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECount\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Y\"\u003EY\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EY\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E is \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPolyline\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"G\"\u003EG\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EG\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E is \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPolygon\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"I\"\u003EI\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EI\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E is \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHighlight\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command D\"\u003E⌘D\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+D\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E duplicates the selection (instead of the browser&#039;s bookmark).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command +\"\u003E⌘+\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl++\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command −\"\u003E⌘−\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+−\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E zoom the sheet, not the browser page.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAny key can be changed: see below.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf your fingers know Bluebeam Revu, pick the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPDF review\u003C/span\u003E menu style in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMarkupCraft\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMenus, keys and mouse\u003C/span\u003E: a menu bar (File, Edit, View, Document, Markup, Measure, Window) over a row of review tools, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Q\"\u003EQ\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EQ\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E for a callout, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"H\"\u003EH\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EH\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E for the highlighter, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"N\"\u003EN\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EN\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E for a polygon, and the measurements on \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Option Shift\"\u003E⌥⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EAlt+Shift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E with a letter. Any key can be changed there too. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/markupcraft/menus-keys-and-mouse\" data-topic=\"markupcraft/menus-keys-and-mouse\"\u003EMenus, keys and mouse\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe full list is in \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/markupcraft/keyboard\" data-topic=\"markupcraft/keyboard\"\u003EKeyboard and mouse\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"things-that-work-differently-than-you-might-expect\"\u003EThings that work differently than you might expect\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETools stay on.\u003C/strong\u003E After a cloud, you are still drawing clouds. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E once clears the selection, twice returns to \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EText and callouts need text.\u003C/strong\u003E Finish with nothing typed and no markup is added. Notes are kept empty.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMeasurements need a scale first.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELength\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArea\u003C/span\u003E switch to \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECalibrate\u003C/span\u003E on an uncalibrated sheet and come back after.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EComments from other programs.\u003C/strong\u003E Rectangles, ellipses, lines, polylines, polygons, ink, text boxes and sticky notes made elsewhere become editable MarkupCraft markups, and are rewritten in MarkupCraft&#039;s way when you save (their look can change slightly). Text highlights, other programs&#039; stamps and other special kinds stay read-only: you can reply to them, not change them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECharacters outside Western European letters\u003C/strong\u003E print as &quot;?&quot; in the saved PDF. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/markupcraft/save-the-marked-up-pdf\" data-topic=\"markupcraft/save-the-marked-up-pdf\"\u003ESave the marked-up PDF\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EStamp dates\u003C/strong\u003E use UTC, so a stamp made late in the evening in the Americas can show tomorrow&#039;s date.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMeasurements carry over\u003C/strong\u003E as standard measurement comments, and each calibrated sheet keeps its scale, so a program that reads PDF measurements can usually show them. Exact behavior depends on that program.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"using-both\"\u003EUsing both\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou can go back and forth. Comments made elsewhere open in MarkupCraft, and the &quot;(marked up)&quot; PDF opens in other PDF programs with every markup, reply and status visible. What only MarkupCraft reads back (punch assignee and due date, item status) is kept in the file for MarkupCraft.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"If you review drawings in Bluebeam Revu (or comment on PDFs in another desktop PDF program), most of MarkupCraft will feel familiar: clouds, callouts, a tool chest, a markups list, statuses with replies, calibrated measurements, sheet overlays. The way files are handled is the big difference, so read the next section even if you skip the rest. The five differences that matter Save makes a new file. MarkupCraft runs in the browser and cannot write over the PDF on your disk. Save downloads \"\u003Cname\u003E (marked up).pdf\" and the original stays as it was. Next time, open the \"(marked up)\" copy, and tidy old copies yourself. The PDF is saved when you save. Until then a recovery copy in this browser keeps your markups through a crash or a closed tab, and MarkupCraft offers it back next time. It is a safety net: save the PDF to keep the review. Your profile lives in the browser. Your author name, tool settings and tool chest are kept in this browser on this computer. A different browser or computer starts fresh, and clearing site data erases them. Export your tool chest as a backup. See What stays in this browser. No shared sessions yet. There is no live, shared review session and no cloud project. The review travels in the PDF: save, send the copy, the next person replies and saves. Scale is per sheet, and only per sheet. There are no viewports inside a sheet and no \"apply this scale to all pages\". Calibrate each sheet you measure on. Habit by habit You are used to In MarkupCraft Thumbnails panel, page labels Sheets on the left. Names come from page labels or are read from the title block; fix them with Rename sheet. Opening several PDFs as a set Open them all at once; they become one list. No set file is saved: open the same PDFs next time. Tool chest with tool sets Tools tab: one chest per browser, saved tools with style and subject. Share by Export and Import as a JSON file. Tool set files from other programs cannot be imported. Properties panel Properties tab: status, subject, comment, style, replies and punch item in one place. Markups list with columns Markups tab with filters for sheet, author, status and type. Export CSV or BCF. No custom columns and no PDF or workbook summary yet. Status and review state Status: Open, Accepted, Rejected, Completed. No custom status sets. Saved as standard review states. Replies on a markup The \"Replies\" thread in Properties, with status changes logged in it. A sent reply cannot be edited. Calibrate, then length, area, count Calibrate, Length (M), Area (Q, gives area and perimeter), Count (U). No angle, radius, volume, polylength or cut-outs yet. Overlay pages Compare tab: red for old, blue for new, align by two points. No automatic \"compare documents\" clouding. Stamps Stamp with six built-in stamps and custom wording, signed with your name and the date. No image or signature stamps yet. Punch or issue tracking Punch list tab: numbered items with assignee, due date and status; CSV export. Live collaboration session Not available yet. Pass the saved PDF around. Flatten Not built yet. Markups stay as comments other viewers can show and print. Layers for markups Not built yet. Use subjects, authors and the list filters to sort comments. Hyperlinks between sheets Not built yet. Spacebar to pan, wheel to zoom The same: hold Space and drag; the wheel zooms at the pointer. The wheel never scrolls the page. Keys that differ Many one-letter keys are what you might expect, but check these before your fingers do it for you: K is Callout, and Q is Area. If you are used to Q for callouts, it will start an area here. C is the box Cloud; Shift+C is Free cloud. M is Length, U is Count, Y is Polyline, G is Polygon, I is Highlight. Ctrl+D duplicates the selection (instead of the browser's bookmark). Ctrl++ and Ctrl+− zoom the sheet, not the browser page. Any key can be changed: see below. If your fingers know Bluebeam Revu, pick the PDF review menu style in Settings › MarkupCraft › Menus, keys and mouse: a menu bar (File, Edit, View, Document, Markup, Measure, Window) over a row of review tools, Q for a callout, H for the highlighter, N for a polygon, and the measurements on Alt+Shift with a letter. Any key can be changed there too. See Menus, keys and mouse. The full list is in Keyboard and mouse. Things that work differently than you might expect Tools stay on. After a cloud, you are still drawing clouds. Esc once clears the selection, twice returns to Select. Text and callouts need text. Finish with nothing typed and no markup is added. Notes are kept empty. Measurements need a scale first. Length and Area switch to Calibrate on an uncalibrated sheet and come back after. Comments from other programs. Rectangles, ellipses, lines, polylines, polygons, ink, text boxes and sticky notes made elsewhere become editable MarkupCraft markups, and are rewritten in MarkupCraft's way when you save (their look can change slightly). Text highlights, other programs' stamps and other special kinds stay read-only: you can reply to them, not change them. Characters outside Western European letters print as \"?\" in the saved PDF. See Save the marked-up PDF. Stamp dates use UTC, so a stamp made late in the evening in the Americas can show tomorrow's date. Measurements carry over as standard measurement comments, and each calibrated sheet keeps its scale, so a program that reads PDF measurements can usually show them. Exact behavior depends on that program. Using both You can go back and forth. Comments made elsewhere open in MarkupCraft, and the \"(marked up)\" PDF opens in other PDF programs with every markup, reply and status visible. What only MarkupCraft reads back (punch assignee and due date, item status) is kept in the file for MarkupCraft.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"keyboard","scope":"markupcraft","title":"Keyboard and mouse","summary":"Every key, mouse action and touch gesture MarkupCraft understands, grouped, plus the shortcuts the browser keeps.","section":"reference","order":10,"keywords":["keyboard","shortcuts","hotkeys","keys","mouse","wheel","right click","middle button","trackpad","touch","gestures","pinch","space pan"],"headings":[{"id":"tools","text":"Tools","level":2},{"id":"while-drawing","text":"While drawing","level":2},{"id":"selecting-and-editing","text":"Selecting and editing","level":2},{"id":"cut-copy-and-paste","text":"Cut, copy and paste","level":2},{"id":"help","text":"Help","level":2},{"id":"files-and-sheets","text":"Files and sheets","level":2},{"id":"zoom-and-pan","text":"Zoom and pan","level":2},{"id":"touch","text":"Touch","level":2},{"id":"shortcuts-the-browser-keeps","text":"Shortcuts the browser keeps","level":2},{"id":"when-a-key-does-nothing","text":"When a key does nothing","level":2},{"id":"where-move-lives","text":"Where Move lives","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EShortcuts written with \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command\"\u003E⌘\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E accept either ⌘ or Control on a Mac, and Ctrl on Windows and Linux. Tool letters work only when you are not typing in a box; while you type, letters are just letters. See also \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/keys\" data-topic=\"basics/keys\"\u003EKeys, mice and trackpads\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"tools\"\u003ETools\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKey\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ETool\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"V\"\u003EV\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EV\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"H\"\u003EH\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EH\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPan\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"B\"\u003EB\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EB\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EContent\u003C/span\u003E: the page&#039;s own lines, pictures and text (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/markupcraft/page-content\" data-topic=\"markupcraft/page-content\"\u003EChange the page&#039;s own content\u003C/a\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"P\"\u003EP\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EP\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPen\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"I\"\u003EI\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EI\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHighlight\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"L\"\u003EL\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EL\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELine\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"A\"\u003EA\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EA\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArrow\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"R\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERectangle\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"E\"\u003EE\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EE\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEllipse\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Y\"\u003EY\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EY\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPolyline\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"G\"\u003EG\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EG\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPolygon\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"C\"\u003EC\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EC\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECloud\u003C/span\u003E (drag a box)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift C\"\u003E⇧C\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift+C\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFree cloud\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"K\"\u003EK\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EK\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECallout\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"T\"\u003ET\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ET\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EText\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"N\"\u003EN\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EN\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENote\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"S\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStamp\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"M\"\u003EM\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EM\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELength\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Q\"\u003EQ\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EQ\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArea\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"U\"\u003EU\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EU\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECount\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"O\"\u003EO\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EO\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"X\"\u003EX\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EX\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAngle\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECalibrate\u003C/span\u003E has no key: use its toolbar button or the scale button in the status bar. Tool letters are ignored when \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Option\"\u003E⌥\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EAlt\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E is held.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"while-drawing\"\u003EWhile drawing\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKey or action\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it does\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E while drawing\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELines, arrows, lengths and polyline sides at 45° steps; rectangles, ellipses and box clouds square; pen and highlighter one straight stroke\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDouble-click, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or right-click\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFinish a polyline, polygon, free cloud or area\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Delete\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EBackspace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERemove the last point of a polyline, polygon, free cloud or area\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECancel what you are drawing; with nothing in progress, clear the selection; with nothing selected, go back to \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Return\"\u003E⌘ Return\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Enter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFinish typing in a text box, callout or note (or click outside it)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E while typing\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECancel the text you were typing\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E-click with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECount\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERemove the nearest dot of the selected count group\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"selecting-and-editing\"\u003ESelecting and editing\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKey or action\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it does\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESelect a markup\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E-click\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAdd or remove a markup from the selection\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrag left to right on empty sheet\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESelect what is fully inside the window\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrag right to left on empty sheet\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESelect what the window touches\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command A\"\u003E⌘A\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+A\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESelect every markup on this sheet\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrag a selected markup\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMove it (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E: along 45° steps)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrag a handle\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EResize or reshape (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E: keep proportions, or 45°)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EArrow keys\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENudge the selection one screen pixel (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E: ten)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDouble-click, or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEdit the text of a text box, callout or note; for a stamp, open \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn Delete\"\u003Efn Delete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Delete\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EBackspace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDelete the selection\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESelect nothing\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUndo\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Y\"\u003E⌘Y\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Y\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift Command Z\"\u003E⇧⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERedo\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"cut-copy-and-paste\"\u003ECut, copy and paste\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe same commands are in the toolbar&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit\u003C/span\u003E group (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECopy\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaste\u003C/span\u003E) and its \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E menu, each with its key. Every one is a single undo step. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/markupcraft/select-and-edit#copy-paste-and-duplicate\" data-topic=\"markupcraft/select-and-edit\" data-hash=\"copy-paste-and-duplicate\"\u003ECopy, paste and duplicate\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKey\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it does\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command X\"\u003E⌘X\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+X\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECut: copy the selection and delete it\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command C\"\u003E⌘C\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+C\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECopy the selection\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command V\"\u003E⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPaste. With the pointer on the sheet, the copy lands centered where it points; otherwise beside the original. Settings can make it always paste beside the original.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift Command V\"\u003E⇧⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPaste in place: the same spot on the sheet, on this sheet or another\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command D\"\u003E⌘D\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+D\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDuplicate the selection beside it (the clipboard is left alone)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"help\"\u003EHelp\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKey\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it does\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F1\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpen or close this help (a panel at the side; the sheet stays usable). Also \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHelp\u003C/span\u003E in the bar at the top.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E in the help\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClose the help\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"os:mac\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EMac\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn most Mac keyboards, press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F1\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E with \u003Cstrong\u003Efn\u003C/strong\u003E (or the globe key), or use \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHelp\u003C/span\u003E. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/keys#function-keys-on-a-mac\" data-topic=\"basics/keys\" data-hash=\"function-keys-on-a-mac\"\u003EKeys, mice and trackpads\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"files-and-sheets\"\u003EFiles and sheets\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKey\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it does\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command N\"\u003E⌘N\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+N\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENew blank pages (when the browser lets the page have the key; see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/markupcraft/open-a-plan-set#start-with-blank-pages\" data-topic=\"markupcraft/open-a-plan-set\" data-hash=\"start-with-blank-pages\"\u003EStart with blank pages\u003C/a\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command O\"\u003E⌘O\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+O\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpen PDFs\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command P\"\u003E⌘P\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+P\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPrint (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/markupcraft/pages-and-printing\" data-topic=\"markupcraft/pages-and-printing\"\u003EPrint, and change the pages\u003C/a\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command F\"\u003E⌘F\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+F\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFind text in the drawings (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/markupcraft/find-text\" data-topic=\"markupcraft/find-text\"\u003EFind text\u003C/a\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command\"\u003E⌘\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E+click a linked markup\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFollow its sheet link; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Option ←\"\u003E⌥←\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EAlt+←\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E goes back (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/markupcraft/sheet-links\" data-topic=\"markupcraft/sheet-links\"\u003ESheet links\u003C/a\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESave a marked-up copy\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn ↓\"\u003Efn ↓\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EPage Down\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E / \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn ↑\"\u003Efn ↑\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EPage Up\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENext / previous sheet\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESearch sheets\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpen the first matching sheet\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Return\"\u003E⌘ Return\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Enter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWrite a reply\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESend the reply\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"zoom-and-pan\"\u003EZoom and pan\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKey or action\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it does\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMouse wheel\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EZoom in or out around the pointer (the wheel never scrolls)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control\"\u003E⌃\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E + wheel\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EZoom faster\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"+\"\u003E+\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E+\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"−\"\u003E−\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E−\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E (also with \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command\"\u003E⌘\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EZoom in or out around the middle; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"=\"\u003E=\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E=\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E works as plus\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command 0\"\u003E⌘0\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+0\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFit page\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command 9\"\u003E⌘9\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+9\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFit width\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrag with the middle button\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPan\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrag with the right button\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPan (when you are not in the middle of a polyline)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHold \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and drag\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPan\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EArrow keys with nothing selected\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPan\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control Shift F\"\u003E⌃⇧F\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+F\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EScreen mode: normal, full screen, full screen with bars hidden (Control key on every computer)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"os:mac\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EMac\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOn a trackpad:\u003C/strong\u003E two-finger scrolling zooms. Pinch also zooms in Chrome, Edge and Firefox. Pan with \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E held while you drag, or with the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPan\u003C/span\u003E tool. A two-finger click is a right-click and finishes a polyline. Control-click may act as a plain click here rather than a right-click, so use a two-finger click.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"input:touch\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003ETouch\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"touch\"\u003ETouch\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EGesture\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it does\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOne finger\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDraw with the current tool, or select\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETwo fingers\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPinch to zoom around their middle, move to pan\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESecond finger during a stroke\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECancels the stroke and zooms instead\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDouble-tap\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFinish a polyline, polygon, free cloud or area\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECount dots, notes, polyline points and the second point of a two-point tool are placed when the finger lifts. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/markupcraft/review-on-a-tablet\" data-topic=\"markupcraft/review-on-a-tablet\"\u003EReview on an iPad or touch screen\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"shortcuts-the-browser-keeps\"\u003EShortcuts the browser keeps\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command W\"\u003E⌘W\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+W\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command T\"\u003E⌘T\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+T\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command N\"\u003E⌘N\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+N\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Q\"\u003E⌘Q\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Q\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E belong to the browser. Closing the tab or window with unsaved markups loses them, after the browser&#039;s &quot;Leave site?&quot; question on a computer.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMost browsers keep \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command N\"\u003E⌘N\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+N\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E for a new window, so in a browser tab use \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew\u003C/span\u003E in the toolbar; the desktop app and Studio pass the key to MarkupCraft.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMarkupCraft takes \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command O\"\u003E⌘O\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+O\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command D\"\u003E⌘D\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+D\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command A\"\u003E⌘A\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+A\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command V\"\u003E⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift Command V\"\u003E⇧⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command 0\"\u003E⌘0\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+0\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command +\"\u003E⌘+\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl++\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command −\"\u003E⌘−\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+−\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E away from the browser while the app has the keyboard, and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command C\"\u003E⌘C\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+C\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command X\"\u003E⌘X\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+X\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E while markups are selected. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command ,\"\u003E⌘,\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+,\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E opens \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E. To zoom the whole browser page instead, use the browser&#039;s menu.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command 9\"\u003E⌘9\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+9\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E jumps to the last browser tab in some browsers before MarkupCraft sees it. If that happens, use \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFit width\u003C/span\u003E under the sheet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E in full screen also leaves full screen.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"when-a-key-does-nothing\"\u003EWhen a key does nothing\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EYou are typing in a box.\u003C/strong\u003E Click on the sheet first. (A click with a drawing tool draws; press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E afterward or use \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.)\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA dialog is open.\u003C/strong\u003E Keys go to the dialog until it closes.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EYou just clicked a toolbar button\u003C/strong\u003E and the arrow keys or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E act on that button instead. Move the pointer over the sheet and use the wheel or middle button, or press a tool letter instead of clicking buttons.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"where-move-lives\"\u003EWhere Move lives\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThere is no Move command: select a markup and drag it (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E keeps 45° steps). A pin is moved with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMove pin\u003C/span\u003E, and a page with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMove page earlier\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMove page later\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Shortcuts written with Ctrl accept either ⌘ or Control on a Mac, and Ctrl on Windows and Linux. Tool letters work only when you are not typing in a box; while you type, letters are just letters. See also Keys, mice and trackpads. Tools Key Tool V Select H Pan B Content: the page's own lines, pictures and text (see Change the page's own content) P Pen I Highlight L Line A Arrow R Rectangle E Ellipse Y Polyline G Polygon C Cloud (drag a box) Shift+C Free cloud K Callout T Text N Note S Stamp M Length Q Area U Count O Arc X Angle Calibrate has no key: use its toolbar button or the scale button in the status bar. Tool letters are ignored when Alt is held. While drawing Key or action What it does Shift while drawing Lines, arrows, lengths and polyline sides at 45° steps; rectangles, ellipses and box clouds square; pen and highlighter one straight stroke Double-click, Enter or right-click Finish a polyline, polygon, free cloud or area Backspace Remove the last point of a polyline, polygon, free cloud or area Esc Cancel what you are drawing; with nothing in progress, clear the selection; with nothing selected, go back to Select Ctrl+Enter Finish typing in a text box, callout or note (or click outside it) Esc while typing Cancel the text you were typing Shift-click with Count Remove the nearest dot of the selected count group Selecting and editing Key or action What it does Click Select a markup Shift-click Add or remove a markup from the selection Drag left to right on empty sheet Select what is fully inside the window Drag right to left on empty sheet Select what the window touches Ctrl+A Select every markup on this sheet Drag a selected markup Move it (Shift: along 45° steps) Drag a handle Resize or reshape (Shift: keep proportions, or 45°) Arrow keys Nudge the selection one screen pixel (Shift: ten) Double-click, or Enter Edit the text of a text box, callout or note; for a stamp, open Properties Delete or Backspace Delete the selection Esc Select nothing Ctrl+Z Undo Ctrl+Y or Ctrl+Shift+Z Redo Cut, copy and paste The same commands are in the toolbar's Edit group (Copy, Paste) and its More menu, each with its key. Every one is a single undo step. See Copy, paste and duplicate. Key What it does Ctrl+X Cut: copy the selection and delete it Ctrl+C Copy the selection Ctrl+V Paste. With the pointer on the sheet, the copy lands centered where it points; otherwise beside the original. Settings can make it always paste beside the original. Ctrl+Shift+V Paste in place: the same spot on the sheet, on this sheet or another Ctrl+D Duplicate the selection beside it (the clipboard is left alone) Help Key What it does F1 Open or close this help (a panel at the side; the sheet stays usable). Also Help in the bar at the top. Esc in the help Close the help On most Mac keyboards, press F1 with fn (or the globe key), or use Help. See Keys, mice and trackpads. Files and sheets Key What it does Ctrl+N New blank pages (when the browser lets the page have the key; see Start with blank pages) Ctrl+O Open PDFs Ctrl+P Print (see Print, and change the pages) Ctrl+F Find text in the drawings (see Find text) Ctrl+click a linked markup Follow its sheet link; Alt+← goes back (see Sheet links) Ctrl+S Save a marked-up copy Page Down / Page Up Next / previous sheet Enter in Search sheets Open the first matching sheet Ctrl+Enter in Write a reply Send the reply Zoom and pan Key or action What it does Mouse wheel Zoom in or out around the pointer (the wheel never scrolls) Ctrl + wheel Zoom faster + or − (also with Ctrl) Zoom in or out around the middle; = works as plus Ctrl+0 Fit page Ctrl+9 Fit width Drag with the middle button Pan Drag with the right button Pan (when you are not in the middle of a polyline) Hold Space and drag Pan Arrow keys with nothing selected Pan Ctrl+Shift+F Screen mode: normal, full screen, full screen with bars hidden (Control key on every computer) On a trackpad: two-finger scrolling zooms. Pinch also zooms in Chrome, Edge and Firefox. Pan with Space held while you drag, or with the Pan tool. A two-finger click is a right-click and finishes a polyline. Control-click may act as a plain click here rather than a right-click, so use a two-finger click. Touch Gesture What it does One finger Draw with the current tool, or select Two fingers Pinch to zoom around their middle, move to pan Second finger during a stroke Cancels the stroke and zooms instead Double-tap Finish a polyline, polygon, free cloud or area Count dots, notes, polyline points and the second point of a two-point tool are placed when the finger lifts. See Review on an iPad or touch screen. Shortcuts the browser keeps Ctrl+W, Ctrl+T, Ctrl+N and Ctrl+Q belong to the browser. Closing the tab or window with unsaved markups loses them, after the browser's \"Leave site?\" question on a computer. Most browsers keep Ctrl+N for a new window, so in a browser tab use New in the toolbar; the desktop app and Studio pass the key to MarkupCraft. MarkupCraft takes Ctrl+O, Ctrl+S, Ctrl+D, Ctrl+A, Ctrl+V, Ctrl+Shift+V, Ctrl+0, Ctrl++ and Ctrl+− away from the browser while the app has the keyboard, and Ctrl+C and Ctrl+X while markups are selected. Ctrl+, opens Settings. To zoom the whole browser page instead, use the browser's menu. Ctrl+9 jumps to the last browser tab in some browsers before MarkupCraft sees it. If that happens, use Fit width under the sheet. Esc in full screen also leaves full screen. When a key does nothing You are typing in a box. Click on the sheet first. (A click with a drawing tool draws; press Esc afterward or use Ctrl+Z.) A dialog is open. Keys go to the dialog until it closes. You just clicked a toolbar button and the arrow keys or Space act on that button instead. Move the pointer over the sheet and use the wheel or middle button, or press a tool letter instead of clicking buttons. Where Move lives There is no Move command: select a markup and drag it (Shift keeps 45° steps). A pin is moved with Move pin, and a page with Move page earlier or Move page later.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"menus-keys-and-mouse","scope":"markupcraft","title":"Menus, keys and mouse","summary":"Pick MarkupCraft's own toolbar, a PDF review menu bar or a ribbon, give any tool your own key, move tools between menus, and set what the wheel and the mouse buttons do.","section":"reference","order":11,"keywords":["menu style","menus","menu bar","toolbar","ribbon","tabs","pdf review","keyboard shortcuts","hotkeys","keys","customize","remap","rebind","mouse","wheel","invert","zoom direction","middle button","right button","pan","export","import","switching","familiar"],"headings":[{"id":"the-three-styles","text":"The three styles","level":2},{"id":"make-it-your-own","text":"Make it your own","level":2},{"id":"mouse","text":"Mouse","level":2},{"id":"take-it-with-you","text":"Take it with you","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EMarkupCraft&#039;s tools are the same whichever way you reach them. A \u003Cstrong\u003Emenu style\u003C/strong\u003E only decides where they sit on screen, what they are called there and which keys pick them. Choose one in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command ,\"\u003E⌘,\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+,\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMarkupCraft\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMenus, keys and mouse\u003C/span\u003E; the change is immediate, and this browser remembers it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-three-styles\"\u003EThe three styles\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EStyle\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat you get\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMarkupCraft\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMarkupCraft&#039;s own toolbar (File, Select, Draw, Revise, Annotate, Measure, Edit) and its one-letter keys: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"V\"\u003EV\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EV\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E select, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"H\"\u003EH\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EH\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E pan, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"P\"\u003EP\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EP\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E pen, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"I\"\u003EI\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EI\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E highlighter, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"L\"\u003EL\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EL\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E line, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"A\"\u003EA\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EA\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E arrow, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"R\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E rectangle, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"E\"\u003EE\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EE\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E ellipse, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"C\"\u003EC\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EC\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E cloud, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"K\"\u003EK\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EK\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E callout, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"T\"\u003ET\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ET\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E text, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"N\"\u003EN\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EN\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E note, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"S\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E stamp, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"M\"\u003EM\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EM\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E length, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Q\"\u003EQ\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EQ\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E area, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"U\"\u003EU\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EU\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E count. The start.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPDF review\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA menu bar (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFile\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EView\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDocument\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMarkup\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMeasure\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReview\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWindow\u003C/span\u003E) over a row of review tools, with keys set out the way PDF review programs set theirs: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"V\"\u003EV\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EV\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E select, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift V\"\u003E⇧V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E pan, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"H\"\u003EH\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EH\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E highlighter, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Q\"\u003EQ\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EQ\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E callout, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"N\"\u003EN\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EN\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E polygon, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift N\"\u003E⇧N\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift+N\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E polyline, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift T\"\u003E⇧T\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift+T\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E note, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift A\"\u003E⇧A\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift+A\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E arc, and the measurements on \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Option Shift\"\u003E⌥⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EAlt+Shift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Option Shift K\"\u003E⌥⇧K\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EAlt+Shift+K\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E calibrate, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Option Shift L\"\u003E⌥⇧L\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EAlt+Shift+L\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E length, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Option Shift G\"\u003E⌥⇧G\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EAlt+Shift+G\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E angle, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Option Shift A\"\u003E⌥⇧A\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EAlt+Shift+A\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E area, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Option Shift C\"\u003E⌥⇧C\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EAlt+Shift+C\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E count. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Option L\"\u003E⌥L\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EAlt+L\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E opens the markups list. The middle button pans; a right click does not.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERibbon\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETabs named for the work: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMark up\u003C/span\u003E (select, draw, clouds, callouts, text, notes, stamps), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMeasure\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReview\u003C/span\u003E (the markups list and its exports, BCF issues, the punch list, field records, the tool chest, compare), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPages\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EView\u003C/span\u003E, with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFile\u003C/span\u003E and quick buttons for open, save, undo and redo. The tools keep MarkupCraft&#039;s own one-letter keys. A button for a panel that is not open opens that panel first.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEvery style keeps the \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command\"\u003E⌘\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E keys: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Y\"\u003E⌘Y\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Y\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command O\"\u003E⌘O\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+O\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command P\"\u003E⌘P\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+P\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command C\"\u003E⌘C\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+C\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command X\"\u003E⌘X\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+X\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command V\"\u003E⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command D\"\u003E⌘D\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+D\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command A\"\u003E⌘A\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+A\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command F\"\u003E⌘F\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+F\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command 0\"\u003E⌘0\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+0\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E fit page and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command 9\"\u003E⌘9\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+9\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E fit width; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn ↑\"\u003Efn ↑\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EPage Up\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn ↓\"\u003Efn ↓\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EPage Down\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E change sheet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA key that belongs to MarkupCraft&#039;s own layout but not to the style you picked does nothing, and the status bar says where its tool went (&quot;In PDF review, Callout is Q&quot;). \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn Delete\"\u003Efn Delete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Tab\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and the arrows always keep their meaning. While you type in a text box, callout or note, every key is the text&#039;s, and numbers typed while drawing still open the length box.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"make-it-your-own\"\u003EMake it your own\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPick the style closest to what you want, then click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECustomize…\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EKeys and menus\u003C/span\u003E, find a tool (the search box helps). \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd a key\u003C/span\u003E and press the key, or two keys in a row for a two-letter key. Click a key to take it off.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWith the menu bar, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWhere it sits\u003C/span\u003E moves the tool to another menu, or to \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EKeys only\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMouse\u003C/span\u003E, set the wheel and the buttons that pan the sheet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EGive it a name and click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESaving a built-in style makes your own copy (&quot;My PDF review&quot;); the built-in one never changes. A key given to two tools is shown in red and has to be fixed before you can save.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"mouse\"\u003EMouse\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMouse wheel\u003C/strong\u003E: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScroll up zooms in\u003C/span\u003E (the start) or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScroll up zooms out\u003C/span\u003E, and how fast. A trackpad pinch always zooms the way your fingers move.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPan the sheet with\u003C/strong\u003E: the middle button, the right button, or both. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and drag, and the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPan\u003C/span\u003E tool, always pan; the left button otherwise draws and selects, so ticking it changes nothing here.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMarkupCraft has no 3D view, so the 3D buttons do nothing here.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"take-it-with-you\"\u003ETake it with you\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport\u003C/span\u003E saves the style in use as a small \u003Ccode\u003E.ccinput.json\u003C/code\u003E file; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EImport…\u003C/span\u003E adds one, from another computer or a colleague. An imported file can only point at MarkupCraft&#039;s own tools: anything else in it is left out and counted in the status bar. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDelete this style\u003C/span\u003E removes one of your own.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThese settings stay in this browser. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUse this style in every app\u003C/span\u003E under the style cards makes every app open in its matching style: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPDF review\u003C/span\u003E goes with the other apps&#039; menu-bar styles, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERibbon\u003C/span\u003E with their ribbons, MarkupCraft&#039;s own toolbar with theirs. An app without a matching style keeps its own.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe workspace layouts in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGlobal\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELayout\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERibbon and project browser\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EToolbox and info box\u003C/span\u003E) build their ribbon from the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERibbon\u003C/span\u003E style&#039;s tabs and their toolbox from MarkupCraft&#039;s toolbar, so every tool runs the same way in them.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ERelated: \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/markupcraft/keyboard\" data-topic=\"markupcraft/keyboard\"\u003EKeyboard and mouse\u003C/a\u003E · \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/markupcraft/settings\" data-topic=\"markupcraft/settings\"\u003EMarkupCraft settings\u003C/a\u003E · \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/markupcraft/coming-from\" data-topic=\"markupcraft/coming-from\"\u003EComing from other markup software\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/p\u003E","text":"MarkupCraft's tools are the same whichever way you reach them. A menu style only decides where they sit on screen, what they are called there and which keys pick them. Choose one in Settings (Ctrl+,) › MarkupCraft › Menus, keys and mouse; the change is immediate, and this browser remembers it. The three styles Style What you get MarkupCraft MarkupCraft's own toolbar (File, Select, Draw, Revise, Annotate, Measure, Edit) and its one-letter keys: V select, H pan, P pen, I highlighter, L line, A arrow, R rectangle, E ellipse, C cloud, K callout, T text, N note, S stamp, M length, Q area, U count. The start. PDF review A menu bar (File, Edit, View, Document, Markup, Measure, Review, Window) over a row of review tools, with keys set out the way PDF review programs set theirs: V select, Shift+V pan, H highlighter, Q callout, N polygon, Shift+N polyline, Shift+T note, Shift+A arc, and the measurements on Alt+Shift: Alt+Shift+K calibrate, Alt+Shift+L length, Alt+Shift+G angle, Alt+Shift+A area, Alt+Shift+C count. Alt+L opens the markups list. The middle button pans; a right click does not. Ribbon Tabs named for the work: Mark up (select, draw, clouds, callouts, text, notes, stamps), Measure, Edit, Review (the markups list and its exports, BCF issues, the punch list, field records, the tool chest, compare), Pages and View, with File and quick buttons for open, save, undo and redo. The tools keep MarkupCraft's own one-letter keys. A button for a panel that is not open opens that panel first. Every style keeps the Ctrl keys: Ctrl+Z, Ctrl+Y, Ctrl+S, Ctrl+O, Ctrl+P, Ctrl+C, Ctrl+X, Ctrl+V, Ctrl+D, Ctrl+A, Ctrl+F, Ctrl+0 fit page and Ctrl+9 fit width; Page Up and Page Down change sheet. A key that belongs to MarkupCraft's own layout but not to the style you picked does nothing, and the status bar says where its tool went (\"In PDF review, Callout is Q\"). Delete, Esc, Enter, Tab, Space and the arrows always keep their meaning. While you type in a text box, callout or note, every key is the text's, and numbers typed while drawing still open the length box. Make it your own Pick the style closest to what you want, then click Customize…. On Keys and menus, find a tool (the search box helps). Add a key and press the key, or two keys in a row for a two-letter key. Click a key to take it off. With the menu bar, Where it sits moves the tool to another menu, or to Keys only. On Mouse, set the wheel and the buttons that pan the sheet. Give it a name and click Save. Saving a built-in style makes your own copy (\"My PDF review\"); the built-in one never changes. A key given to two tools is shown in red and has to be fixed before you can save. Mouse Mouse wheel: Scroll up zooms in (the start) or Scroll up zooms out, and how fast. A trackpad pinch always zooms the way your fingers move. Pan the sheet with: the middle button, the right button, or both. Space and drag, and the Pan tool, always pan; the left button otherwise draws and selects, so ticking it changes nothing here. MarkupCraft has no 3D view, so the 3D buttons do nothing here. Take it with you Export saves the style in use as a small .ccinput.json file; Import… adds one, from another computer or a colleague. An imported file can only point at MarkupCraft's own tools: anything else in it is left out and counted in the status bar. Delete this style removes one of your own. These settings stay in this browser. Use this style in every app under the style cards makes every app open in its matching style: PDF review goes with the other apps' menu-bar styles, Ribbon with their ribbons, MarkupCraft's own toolbar with theirs. An app without a matching style keeps its own. The workspace layouts in Settings › Global › Layout (Ribbon and project browser, Toolbox and info box) build their ribbon from the Ribbon style's tabs and their toolbox from MarkupCraft's toolbar, so every tool runs the same way in them. Related: Keyboard and mouse · MarkupCraft settings · Coming from other markup software","lang":"en"},{"slug":"markup-types","scope":"markupcraft","title":"Every markup type","summary":"The seventeen kinds of markup, their keys, how each is drawn, its default look, and what it becomes in the saved PDF.","section":"reference","order":20,"keywords":["markup types","tools list","annotation types","cloud","callout","text","note","stamp","pen","highlighter","line","arrow","rectangle","ellipse","polyline","polygon","length","area","count"],"headings":[{"id":"the-tools","text":"The tools","level":2},{"id":"what-every-markup-carries","text":"What every markup carries","level":2},{"id":"read-only-comments","text":"Read-only comments","level":2},{"id":"not-built-yet","text":"Not built yet","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"the-tools\"\u003EThe tools\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EToolbar\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKey\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EHow to draw\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EDefault look\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EIn the saved PDF\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPen\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"P\"\u003EP\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EP\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrag\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERed, 1.5 pt\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EInk\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHighlight\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"I\"\u003EI\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EI\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrag\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EYellow, 14 pt wide, see-through, tints what is under it\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EInk (marked as a highlighter stroke)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELine\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"L\"\u003EL\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EL\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrag, or click start and end\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERed, 2 pt\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELine\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArrow\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"A\"\u003EA\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EA\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrag, or click start and end\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERed, 2 pt, closed arrowhead at the end\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELine with an arrowhead\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"O\"\u003EO\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EO\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick the start, a point on the arc, then the end\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERed, 2 pt\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPolyline along the arc\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERectangle\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"R\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ER\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrag a box\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERed, 2 pt, no fill\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESquare\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEllipse\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"E\"\u003EE\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EE\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrag a box\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERed, 2 pt, no fill\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECircle\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPolyline\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Y\"\u003EY\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EY\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick points, double-click to finish\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERed, 2 pt\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPolyline\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPolygon\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"G\"\u003EG\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EG\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick points, double-click to finish\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERed, 2 pt, no fill\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPolygon\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECloud\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"C\"\u003EC\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EC\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrag a box\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERed, 1.5 pt scallops\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESquare with a cloudy border\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFree cloud\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift C\"\u003E⇧C\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift+C\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick points, double-click to finish\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERed, 1.5 pt scallops\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPolygon with a cloudy border\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECallout\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"K\"\u003EK\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EK\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPress on the point, drag to the text, type\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERed text and leader, 1 pt, 12 pt text\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EText box with a callout leader\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EText\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"T\"\u003ET\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ET\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick or drag a box, type\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERed 12 pt text, no border\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EText box\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENote\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"N\"\u003EN\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EN\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick, type\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EYellow note icon\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESticky note\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStamp\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"S\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPick a stamp, click or drag\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFramed capitals plus &quot;By &lt;name&gt;, &lt;date&gt;&quot;; colored by meaning\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EStamp\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELength\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"M\"\u003EM\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EM\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick start and end (needs a scale)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBlue dimension line with ticks and the length\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELine marked as a dimension, with a measurement\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAngle\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"X\"\u003EX\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EX\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick one arm&#039;s end, the corner, then the other arm&#039;s end\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBlue arms, a small arc at the corner and the angle in degrees\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPolyline with the angle as its text\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArea\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Q\"\u003EQ\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EQ\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick corners, double-click to finish (needs a scale)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBlue outline, light blue fill, area and perimeter\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPolygon marked as an area, with a measurement\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECount\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"U\"\u003EU\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EU\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick each item\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOrange numbered dots and a &quot;group: n&quot; tag\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOne ink comment per group, a ring per item\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn touch, &quot;double-click to finish&quot; is a double-tap, and &quot;click&quot; is a tap that acts when you lift (for points, notes and counts).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-every-markup-carries\"\u003EWhat every markup carries\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EField\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhere you see it\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAuthor\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E, the list, the stamp&#039;s second line, other PDF viewers\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECreated and modified\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E (your time zone), the list, the CSV\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EStatus\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpen, Accepted, Rejected or Completed\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESubject\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA short label; defaults to the kind (&quot;Cloud&quot;, &quot;Text box&quot;, &quot;Length measurement&quot;)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EComment\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELonger notes. For a note, its text is its comment.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EText\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EText boxes and callouts only\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EReplies\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe thread, with status changes\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPunch item\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENumber, assignee, due date, item status, when added to the punch list\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EStyle\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EColor, line width, fill and fill opacity (closed shapes, text and callouts), opacity, text size (text, callouts and measurements)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"read-only-comments\"\u003ERead-only comments\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EComments that came with the file and that MarkupCraft cannot edit (text highlights, other programs&#039; stamps and other special kinds) are listed with the type &quot;Other&quot; and the status chip &quot;Read-only&quot;. They keep exactly the look the file gives them. You can select them, see their author, subject and comment, and reply to them.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"not-built-yet\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFile attachments are not built yet. Any markup can link to a sheet; see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/markupcraft/sheet-links\" data-topic=\"markupcraft/sheet-links\"\u003ESheet links\u003C/a\u003E. Pictures and signatures are placed with the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStamp\u003C/span\u003E tool; see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/markupcraft/stamps-and-notes\" data-topic=\"markupcraft/stamps-and-notes\"\u003EStamps and notes\u003C/a\u003E. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/markupcraft/not-built-yet\" data-topic=\"markupcraft/not-built-yet\"\u003EWhat is not built yet\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"The tools Toolbar Key How to draw Default look In the saved PDF Pen P Drag Red, 1.5 pt Ink Highlight I Drag Yellow, 14 pt wide, see-through, tints what is under it Ink (marked as a highlighter stroke) Line L Drag, or click start and end Red, 2 pt Line Arrow A Drag, or click start and end Red, 2 pt, closed arrowhead at the end Line with an arrowhead Arc O Click the start, a point on the arc, then the end Red, 2 pt Polyline along the arc Rectangle R Drag a box Red, 2 pt, no fill Square Ellipse E Drag a box Red, 2 pt, no fill Circle Polyline Y Click points, double-click to finish Red, 2 pt Polyline Polygon G Click points, double-click to finish Red, 2 pt, no fill Polygon Cloud C Drag a box Red, 1.5 pt scallops Square with a cloudy border Free cloud Shift+C Click points, double-click to finish Red, 1.5 pt scallops Polygon with a cloudy border Callout K Press on the point, drag to the text, type Red text and leader, 1 pt, 12 pt text Text box with a callout leader Text T Click or drag a box, type Red 12 pt text, no border Text box Note N Click, type Yellow note icon Sticky note Stamp S Pick a stamp, click or drag Framed capitals plus \"By \u003Cname\u003E, \u003Cdate\u003E\"; colored by meaning Stamp Length M Click start and end (needs a scale) Blue dimension line with ticks and the length Line marked as a dimension, with a measurement Angle X Click one arm's end, the corner, then the other arm's end Blue arms, a small arc at the corner and the angle in degrees Polyline with the angle as its text Area Q Click corners, double-click to finish (needs a scale) Blue outline, light blue fill, area and perimeter Polygon marked as an area, with a measurement Count U Click each item Orange numbered dots and a \"group: n\" tag One ink comment per group, a ring per item On touch, \"double-click to finish\" is a double-tap, and \"click\" is a tap that acts when you lift (for points, notes and counts). What every markup carries Field Where you see it Author Properties, the list, the stamp's second line, other PDF viewers Created and modified Properties (your time zone), the list, the CSV Status Open, Accepted, Rejected or Completed Subject A short label; defaults to the kind (\"Cloud\", \"Text box\", \"Length measurement\") Comment Longer notes. For a note, its text is its comment. Text Text boxes and callouts only Replies The thread, with status changes Punch item Number, assignee, due date, item status, when added to the punch list Style Color, line width, fill and fill opacity (closed shapes, text and callouts), opacity, text size (text, callouts and measurements) Read-only comments Comments that came with the file and that MarkupCraft cannot edit (text highlights, other programs' stamps and other special kinds) are listed with the type \"Other\" and the status chip \"Read-only\". They keep exactly the look the file gives them. You can select them, see their author, subject and comment, and reply to them. Not built yet File attachments are not built yet. Any markup can link to a sheet; see Sheet links. Pictures and signatures are placed with the Stamp tool; see Stamps and notes. See What is not built yet.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"units-and-scales","scope":"markupcraft","title":"Units, lengths and scales","summary":"Every way to type a length, the units lengths and areas are shown in, rounding, and the list of preset scales.","section":"reference","order":30,"keywords":["units","feet","inches","feet and inches","metric","meters","millimeters","centimeters","scale","preset","architectural scale","engineering scale","ratio","square feet","rounding","fractions"],"headings":[{"id":"typing-a-length","text":"Typing a length","level":2},{"id":"how-lengths-and-areas-are-shown","text":"How lengths and areas are shown","level":2},{"id":"preset-scales","text":"Preset scales","level":2},{"id":"scales-other-programs-saved","text":"Scales other programs saved","level":2},{"id":"paper-positions","text":"Paper positions","level":2},{"id":"accuracy","text":"Accuracy","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"typing-a-length\"\u003ETyping a length\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen you calibrate from two points, you type the real distance. MarkupCraft reads all of these:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EYou type\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EIt means\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E24&#039;-0&quot;\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E24 feet\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E12&#039; 6 1/2&quot;\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E12&#039;-6 1/2&quot;\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E12 feet 6½ inches\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E12&#039; 6\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E12 feet 6 inches (inch mark left off)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E6&quot;\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E1/2&quot;\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Einches\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E12.5&#039;\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E12.5 ft\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E12½ feet\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E3 ft 6 in\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E3 feet 6 inches (also feet, foot, inch, inches)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E3.5 m\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E90 cm\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E900 mm\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Emetric (also meter, metre, millimeter, centimetre…)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E24\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ea bare number, in the unit chosen under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShow lengths in\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECurly quotes (’ ”) and two apostrophes (&#039;&#039;) for inches work too. Zero and negative lengths are refused.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-warning\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003EWarning\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA comma is read as a decimal point: \u003Ccode\u003E1,5 m\u003C/code\u003E is 1.5 meters. Do not type thousands separators: \u003Ccode\u003E1,200 mm\u003C/code\u003E is read as 1.2 millimeters. Type \u003Ccode\u003E1200 mm\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"how-lengths-and-areas-are-shown\"\u003EHow lengths and areas are shown\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShow lengths in\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EA length shows as\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EAn area shows as\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFeet and inches\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E12&#039;-6 1/2&quot;\u003C/code\u003E, rounded to 1/16&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Esquare feet, two decimals\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDecimal feet\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E12.54&#039;\u003C/code\u003E, two decimals\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Esquare feet, two decimals\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EInches\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E150.5&quot;\u003C/code\u003E, up to two decimals\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Esquare inches, one decimal\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMeters\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E3.820 m\u003C/code\u003E, three decimals\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Esquare meters, three decimals\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMillimeters\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E3820 mm\u003C/code\u003E, whole millimeters\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Esquare millimeters, whole\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe perimeter of an area uses the length format.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"preset-scales\"\u003EPreset scales\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe presets assume the PDF is at its full printed size: 72 PDF points to the inch, as a CAD program plots it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EGroup\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EScales\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ELengths shown in\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EArchitectural\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E1/32&quot; = 1&#039;-0&quot;, 1/16&quot;, 3/32&quot;, 1/8&quot;, 3/16&quot;, 1/4&quot;, 3/8&quot;, 1/2&quot;, 3/4&quot;, 1&quot;, 1 1/2&quot;, 3&quot; = 1&#039;-0&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFeet and inches\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEngineering\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E1&quot; = 10&#039;, 20&#039;, 30&#039;, 40&#039;, 50&#039;, 60&#039;, 100&#039;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDecimal feet\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMetric\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E1:1, 1:5, 1:10, 1:20, 1:25, 1:50\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMillimeters\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMetric\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E1:100, 1:200, 1:250, 1:500, 1:1000\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMeters\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo use a preset scale but see lengths in another unit, calibrate from two points instead and pick the unit there.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"scales-other-programs-saved\"\u003EScales other programs saved\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen a PDF already carries a scale for a page from another program (a measurement scale stored on the page), MarkupCraft uses it if it is in feet, inches, meters, centimeters or millimeters. Its label is shown as the file states it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"paper-positions\"\u003EPaper positions\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe status bar shows where the pointer is on the paper, in inches from the sheet&#039;s top-left corner, whatever the sheet&#039;s scale. Compare&#039;s nudge steps are in points (72 points to an inch).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"accuracy\"\u003EAccuracy\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA measurement is as good as the calibration and your clicks. Calibrate over the longest known dimension you can find, zoom in to click its ends, then check one other dimension. A PDF that was scaled when it was made (a &quot;fit to page&quot; plot or a half-size set) is still accurate after calibrating from a dimension, but not with a preset.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Typing a length When you calibrate from two points, you type the real distance. MarkupCraft reads all of these: You type It means 24'-0\" 24 feet 12' 6 1/2\" or 12'-6 1/2\" 12 feet 6½ inches 12' 6 12 feet 6 inches (inch mark left off) 6\" or 1/2\" inches 12.5' or 12.5 ft 12½ feet 3 ft 6 in 3 feet 6 inches (also feet, foot, inch, inches) 3.5 m, 90 cm, 900 mm metric (also meter, metre, millimeter, centimetre…) 24 a bare number, in the unit chosen under Show lengths in Curly quotes (’ ”) and two apostrophes ('') for inches work too. Zero and negative lengths are refused. A comma is read as a decimal point: 1,5 m is 1.5 meters. Do not type thousands separators: 1,200 mm is read as 1.2 millimeters. Type 1200 mm. How lengths and areas are shown Show lengths in A length shows as An area shows as Feet and inches 12'-6 1/2\", rounded to 1/16\" square feet, two decimals Decimal feet 12.54', two decimals square feet, two decimals Inches 150.5\", up to two decimals square inches, one decimal Meters 3.820 m, three decimals square meters, three decimals Millimeters 3820 mm, whole millimeters square millimeters, whole The perimeter of an area uses the length format. Preset scales The presets assume the PDF is at its full printed size: 72 PDF points to the inch, as a CAD program plots it. Group Scales Lengths shown in Architectural 1/32\" = 1'-0\", 1/16\", 3/32\", 1/8\", 3/16\", 1/4\", 3/8\", 1/2\", 3/4\", 1\", 1 1/2\", 3\" = 1'-0\" Feet and inches Engineering 1\" = 10', 20', 30', 40', 50', 60', 100' Decimal feet Metric 1:1, 1:5, 1:10, 1:20, 1:25, 1:50 Millimeters Metric 1:100, 1:200, 1:250, 1:500, 1:1000 Meters To use a preset scale but see lengths in another unit, calibrate from two points instead and pick the unit there. Scales other programs saved When a PDF already carries a scale for a page from another program (a measurement scale stored on the page), MarkupCraft uses it if it is in feet, inches, meters, centimeters or millimeters. Its label is shown as the file states it. Paper positions The status bar shows where the pointer is on the paper, in inches from the sheet's top-left corner, whatever the sheet's scale. Compare's nudge steps are in points (72 points to an inch). Accuracy A measurement is as good as the calibration and your clicks. Calibrate over the longest known dimension you can find, zoom in to click its ends, then check one other dimension. A PDF that was scaled when it was made (a \"fit to page\" plot or a half-size set) is still accurate after calibrating from a dimension, but not with a preset.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"what-the-pdf-keeps","scope":"markupcraft","title":"What is saved in the PDF","summary":"Exactly what the marked-up copy contains, what other PDF programs see, and what is read back when you open a file.","section":"reference","order":40,"keywords":["pdf annotations","comments","saved data","round trip","page labels","measurement","review state","replies","interoperability","compatibility","reopen","private data"],"headings":[{"id":"each-markup","text":"Each markup","level":2},{"id":"threads-and-statuses","text":"Threads and statuses","level":2},{"id":"each-sheet","text":"Each sheet","level":2},{"id":"text-and-characters","text":"Text and characters","level":2},{"id":"calc-sheets-and-reasons","text":"Calc sheets and reasons","level":2},{"id":"what-happens-to-comments-that-were-already-there","text":"What happens to comments that were already there","level":2},{"id":"what-is-not-changed","text":"What is not changed","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EThe marked-up copy is the original PDF with comments (PDF annotations) added. The pages themselves, their drawings, text, bookmarks and links are copied as they were.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"each-markup\"\u003EEach markup\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMarkupCraft\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ESaved as\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EOther PDF viewers show\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERectangle, box cloud\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESquare annotation (cloud: cloudy border)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA rectangle or cloud comment\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEllipse\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECircle annotation\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAn ellipse comment\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPolygon, free cloud\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPolygon annotation (cloud: cloudy border)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA polygon or cloud comment\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPolyline\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPolyLine annotation\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA polyline comment\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELine, arrow\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELine annotation (arrow: closed arrowhead)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA line or arrow comment\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELength\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELine annotation marked as a dimension, with a measurement\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA length measurement, where the viewer supports them\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EArea\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPolygon annotation marked as an area, with a measurement\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAn area measurement, where supported; otherwise a polygon\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECount group\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOne ink annotation, a ring per item; its text is &quot;group: n&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAn ink comment\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPen, highlighter\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EInk annotation (highlighter marked as such)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAn ink comment\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EText\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFreeText annotation\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA text box comment\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECallout\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFreeText annotation with a callout leader\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA callout comment\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENote\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EText annotation\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA sticky note that opens to its text\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EStamp\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EStamp annotation\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA stamp comment\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEvery one is saved with:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003Eits own appearance\u003C/strong\u003E, drawn exactly as MarkupCraft draws it on screen, so it looks the same in any viewer and prints the same, punch number badge included;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003Ethe author\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003Ethe created and modified dates\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003Ethe subject\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003Ethe comment text\u003C/strong\u003E (for text boxes and callouts, the text and the comment), \u003Cstrong\u003Ethe color\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003Ethe opacity\u003C/strong\u003E;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003Eits MarkupCraft details\u003C/strong\u003E in a private entry that other programs ignore: status, punch item, style and type, so it reopens exactly.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"threads-and-statuses\"\u003EThreads and statuses\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEach reply is saved as a reply comment linked to its markup, with its author, date and text. Replies have no icon of their own on the sheet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEach status change is saved as a reply carrying a review state. Other review tools show it as Accepted, Rejected or Completed; Open is saved as no state.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA markup with a status other than Open but no logged status change gets one review-state reply, so other programs see the status too.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EReplies on read-only comments from the original file are saved the same way, linked to that comment.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"each-sheet\"\u003EEach sheet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EScale:\u003C/strong\u003E a calibrated sheet gets a measurement viewport covering the page (the standard way to store a page scale) and MarkupCraft&#039;s own copy of the calibration.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESheet number:\u003C/strong\u003E a number typed with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERename sheet\u003C/span\u003E becomes the page label. When any page of a file has one, every page of that file gets a label: its typed number, else its old label, else its page number.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESheet title:\u003C/strong\u003E a typed title is saved for MarkupCraft only; other viewers do not show it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"text-and-characters\"\u003EText and characters\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EText is written in Helvetica, which every PDF viewer has. Letters outside the Western European set (Windows-1252) print as &quot;?&quot; in the copy; the save message counts them.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"calc-sheets-and-reasons\"\u003ECalc sheets and reasons\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECalc sheets and reasons are kept in a private entry of the PDF named \u003Ccode\u003E/MarkupCraftShell\u003C/code\u003E. Other PDF programs ignore it. An older MarkupCraft also ignores it, and drops it the next time it saves that file.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-happens-to-comments-that-were-already-there\"\u003EWhat happens to comments that were already there\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen you open a PDF that already has comments:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMarkupCraft&#039;s own comments are read back exactly. If another program moved or resized one, its new position wins.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERectangles, ellipses, lines, polylines, polygons, ink, text boxes and sticky notes from other programs become editable markups. \u003Cstrong\u003EWhen you save, MarkupCraft removes the originals and writes them again its own way\u003C/strong\u003E, with their pop-ups and replies. Their look can change slightly.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EReview states from other programs set the status; the newest wins.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEverything else (text highlights, other programs&#039; stamps and special kinds) is \u003Cstrong\u003Ekept untouched\u003C/strong\u003E in the copy, read-only.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELinks and form fields are kept untouched and are not listed.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESaving the same file again never doubles anything: the markups MarkupCraft read are replaced, not added again.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-is-not-changed\"\u003EWhat is not changed\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYour original file on disk is never changed. The copy is not optimized for size, so it can be somewhat larger than the original. Protected (encrypted) PDFs cannot be saved.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"The marked-up copy is the original PDF with comments (PDF annotations) added. The pages themselves, their drawings, text, bookmarks and links are copied as they were. Each markup MarkupCraft Saved as Other PDF viewers show Rectangle, box cloud Square annotation (cloud: cloudy border) A rectangle or cloud comment Ellipse Circle annotation An ellipse comment Polygon, free cloud Polygon annotation (cloud: cloudy border) A polygon or cloud comment Polyline PolyLine annotation A polyline comment Line, arrow Line annotation (arrow: closed arrowhead) A line or arrow comment Length Line annotation marked as a dimension, with a measurement A length measurement, where the viewer supports them Area Polygon annotation marked as an area, with a measurement An area measurement, where supported; otherwise a polygon Count group One ink annotation, a ring per item; its text is \"group: n\" An ink comment Pen, highlighter Ink annotation (highlighter marked as such) An ink comment Text FreeText annotation A text box comment Callout FreeText annotation with a callout leader A callout comment Note Text annotation A sticky note that opens to its text Stamp Stamp annotation A stamp comment Every one is saved with: its own appearance, drawn exactly as MarkupCraft draws it on screen, so it looks the same in any viewer and prints the same, punch number badge included; the author, the created and modified dates, the subject, the comment text (for text boxes and callouts, the text and the comment), the color and the opacity; its MarkupCraft details in a private entry that other programs ignore: status, punch item, style and type, so it reopens exactly. Threads and statuses Each reply is saved as a reply comment linked to its markup, with its author, date and text. Replies have no icon of their own on the sheet. Each status change is saved as a reply carrying a review state. Other review tools show it as Accepted, Rejected or Completed; Open is saved as no state. A markup with a status other than Open but no logged status change gets one review-state reply, so other programs see the status too. Replies on read-only comments from the original file are saved the same way, linked to that comment. Each sheet Scale: a calibrated sheet gets a measurement viewport covering the page (the standard way to store a page scale) and MarkupCraft's own copy of the calibration. Sheet number: a number typed with Rename sheet becomes the page label. When any page of a file has one, every page of that file gets a label: its typed number, else its old label, else its page number. Sheet title: a typed title is saved for MarkupCraft only; other viewers do not show it. Text and characters Text is written in Helvetica, which every PDF viewer has. Letters outside the Western European set (Windows-1252) print as \"?\" in the copy; the save message counts them. Calc sheets and reasons Calc sheets and reasons are kept in a private entry of the PDF named /MarkupCraftShell. Other PDF programs ignore it. An older MarkupCraft also ignores it, and drops it the next time it saves that file. What happens to comments that were already there When you open a PDF that already has comments: MarkupCraft's own comments are read back exactly. If another program moved or resized one, its new position wins. Rectangles, ellipses, lines, polylines, polygons, ink, text boxes and sticky notes from other programs become editable markups. When you save, MarkupCraft removes the originals and writes them again its own way, with their pop-ups and replies. Their look can change slightly. Review states from other programs set the status; the newest wins. Everything else (text highlights, other programs' stamps and special kinds) is kept untouched in the copy, read-only. Links and form fields are kept untouched and are not listed. Saving the same file again never doubles anything: the markups MarkupCraft read are replaced, not added again. What is not changed Your original file on disk is never changed. The copy is not optimized for size, so it can be somewhat larger than the original. Protected (encrypted) PDFs cannot be saved.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"bcf-basics","scope":"markupcraft","title":"BCF basics","summary":"What a BCF export from MarkupCraft contains, how to use it with BIM coordination tools, and its limits.","section":"reference","order":50,"keywords":["bcf","bcfzip","bim collaboration format","issues","coordination","clash","topics","viewpoint","snapshot","openbim","buildingsmart","export issues"],"headings":[{"id":"make-one","text":"Make one","level":2},{"id":"what-is-in-it","text":"What is in it","level":2},{"id":"limits-to-know","text":"Limits to know","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EBCF (BIM Collaboration Format) is an open file format for passing issues between BIM tools. Many model coordination and authoring programs can import a \u003Ccode\u003E.bcfzip\u003C/code\u003E file as a list of issues. MarkupCraft writes \u003Cstrong\u003EBCF 2.1\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"make-one\"\u003EMake one\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMarkups\u003C/span\u003E tab.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFilter the list to what you want to send, for example \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStatus\u003C/span\u003E &quot;Open&quot;. Only the listed markups are exported.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBCF\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWait while it makes a snapshot of each markup (&quot;Making snapshots for BCF (5 of 20)…&quot;).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EResult: &quot;&lt;name&gt; markups.bcfzip&quot; downloads, and the status bar says how many topics it holds and the file size.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-is-in-it\"\u003EWhat is in it\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach markup becomes one \u003Cstrong\u003Etopic\u003C/strong\u003E:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ETopic field\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EFilled with\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETitle\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPunch number, subject and sheet, for example &quot;#4 Cloud on A-101 — Floor plan&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EType\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe markup kind (Cloud, Callout, Length…)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EStatus\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpen, Accepted, Rejected or Completed\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELabels\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe markup kind and the sheet\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAuthor and dates\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe markup&#039;s author, created and modified dates\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAssigned to, due date\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFrom its punch item, when it has one\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDescription\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EIts text or stamp wording, measured value, comment, and the sheet and file\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EComments\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOne per reply, with author and date\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESnapshot\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA picture of the markup on its part of the sheet\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EViewpoint\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA top-down view of the markup&#039;s place on the sheet\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EExporting the same markup again gives the same topic ID, so a tool that imports BCF can recognize it as the same issue.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"limits-to-know\"\u003ELimits to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA sheet is not a model.\u003C/strong\u003E The viewpoint points at the markup&#039;s position on the paper, measured in meters of paper. It does not take a BIM tool to a place in the model. Use the snapshot and the sheet name to find the spot.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EStatuses and types are MarkupCraft&#039;s own\u003C/strong\u003E (Open, Accepted, Rejected, Completed; Cloud, Callout…). The file declares them, as BCF allows, but a tool with fixed lists of its own may map or ignore them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EImporting brings comments, not model views.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EImport BCF\u003C/span\u003E adds replies and statuses to the markups you exported, and makes notes of other issues; it cannot show a model camera. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/markupcraft/markups-list-and-export#import-bcf\" data-topic=\"markupcraft/markups-list-and-export\" data-hash=\"import-bcf\"\u003EMarkups list and export\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESending markups back as a PDF is often simpler for drawing reviews. BCF is for getting drawing comments into a model coordination workflow.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"BCF (BIM Collaboration Format) is an open file format for passing issues between BIM tools. Many model coordination and authoring programs can import a .bcfzip file as a list of issues. MarkupCraft writes BCF 2.1. Make one Open the Markups tab. Filter the list to what you want to send, for example Status \"Open\". Only the listed markups are exported. Choose BCF. Wait while it makes a snapshot of each markup (\"Making snapshots for BCF (5 of 20)…\"). Result: \"\u003Cname\u003E markups.bcfzip\" downloads, and the status bar says how many topics it holds and the file size. What is in it Each markup becomes one topic: Topic field Filled with Title Punch number, subject and sheet, for example \"#4 Cloud on A-101 — Floor plan\" Type The markup kind (Cloud, Callout, Length…) Status Open, Accepted, Rejected or Completed Labels The markup kind and the sheet Author and dates The markup's author, created and modified dates Assigned to, due date From its punch item, when it has one Description Its text or stamp wording, measured value, comment, and the sheet and file Comments One per reply, with author and date Snapshot A picture of the markup on its part of the sheet Viewpoint A top-down view of the markup's place on the sheet Exporting the same markup again gives the same topic ID, so a tool that imports BCF can recognize it as the same issue. Limits to know A sheet is not a model. The viewpoint points at the markup's position on the paper, measured in meters of paper. It does not take a BIM tool to a place in the model. Use the snapshot and the sheet name to find the spot. Statuses and types are MarkupCraft's own (Open, Accepted, Rejected, Completed; Cloud, Callout…). The file declares them, as BCF allows, but a tool with fixed lists of its own may map or ignore them. Importing brings comments, not model views. Import BCF adds replies and statuses to the markups you exported, and makes notes of other issues; it cannot show a model camera. See Markups list and export. Sending markups back as a PDF is often simpler for drawing reviews. BCF is for getting drawing comments into a model coordination workflow.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"browser-storage","scope":"markupcraft","title":"What stays in this browser","summary":"Your name, settings, tool chest and the last markups you copied are kept in this browser on this computer; your markups are kept only in the PDFs you save.","section":"reference","order":60,"keywords":["local storage","browser storage","settings","preferences","profile","clear data","private window","incognito","backup","another computer","sync","reset"],"headings":[{"id":"what-is-kept-in-the-browser","text":"What is kept in the browser","level":2},{"id":"what-that-means","text":"What that means","level":2},{"id":"what-is-not-kept-in-the-browser","text":"What is not kept in the browser","level":2},{"id":"back-up-and-move-your-setup","text":"Back up and move your setup","level":2},{"id":"start-fresh","text":"Start fresh","level":2},{"id":"the-offline-copy","text":"The offline copy","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EMarkupCraft has no account and no server storage. A few small things are kept in your browser&#039;s storage for this website, and everything else lives in your files.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-is-kept-in-the-browser\"\u003EWhat is kept in the browser\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ESet where\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKept as\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EYour author name\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe name button in the status bar, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOne name\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMarkupCraft settings\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMarkupCraft\u003C/span\u003E tab: color and line weight of new markups, where paste puts copies, units (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/markupcraft/settings\" data-topic=\"markupcraft/settings\"\u003EMarkupCraft settings\u003C/a\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOne set\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETool settings\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E while a drawing tool is on: color, line, fill, opacity and text size per tool, the chosen stamp, the count group name\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOne set\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETool chest\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETools\u003C/span\u003E tab\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUp to 500 tools\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWhat you last copied\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECopy\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECut\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe copied markups, so another tab can paste them; replaced by the next copy\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EStamp picture and signature\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPicture…\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMy signature…\u003C/span\u003E in the stamp list\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe last picture and your signature\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERecovery copies\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EKept for you while a PDF has unsaved markups\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe PDF and its markups, removed when you save, close or discard it; kept up to 30 days\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-that-means\"\u003EWhat that means\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPer browser, per computer.\u003C/strong\u003E Chrome and Firefox on the same computer each have their own. Your laptop and the office desktop each have their own. Nothing syncs between them, even if you sign in to the browser.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPrivate and incognito windows\u003C/strong\u003E start empty and forget everything when they close.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EClearing site data erases it.\u003C/strong\u003E Clearing cookies and site data for this website, or for all sites, in the browser&#039;s settings removes your name, settings and chest. Some cleanup tools and some company policies do this automatically.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENothing is uploaded.\u003C/strong\u003E These values never leave the computer, except your name inside the PDFs you save. Recovery copies stay in this browser too.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:safari\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003ESafari\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESafari can delete a website&#039;s stored data after some weeks without a visit. Open MarkupCraft now and then, and keep an exported tool chest.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-is-not-kept-in-the-browser\"\u003EWhat is not kept in the browser\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EYour markups, for good.\u003C/strong\u003E They belong in the &quot;(marked up)&quot; PDF you save. The recovery copy is a safety net for a crash or a closed tab, not a place to keep work: it goes when you save, and clearing site data removes it. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/recover\" data-topic=\"basics/recover\"\u003ERecover your work\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EYour open files.\u003C/strong\u003E Each time you open MarkupCraft, open the PDFs again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe sheet you were on, zoom, filters and undo history.\u003C/strong\u003E These last until you close the set or the tab.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"back-up-and-move-your-setup\"\u003EBack up and move your setup\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETool chest:\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETools\u003C/span\u003E, then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport\u003C/span\u003E. On the other browser or computer, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETools\u003C/span\u003E, then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EImport\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EName:\u003C/strong\u003E type it again on the other browser (click the name button in the status bar).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETool settings:\u003C/strong\u003E set them again, or save each styled markup as a tool so it travels with the chest.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"start-fresh\"\u003EStart fresh\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo reset everything, clear this website&#039;s data in your browser&#039;s settings. Your name, settings and chest go; the six default tools come back.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-offline-copy\"\u003EThe offline copy\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave for offline\u003C/span\u003E in the bar at the top stores the app&#039;s own files (with the PDF reader and writer, about 6 MB) in the browser too, separately from these settings. Clearing site data removes that copy as well; click &quot;Offline ready&quot; and choose &quot;Remove the offline copy&quot; to remove only it. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/offline\" data-topic=\"basics/offline\"\u003EWorking offline\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECloud saving and sync between computers are not available yet.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"MarkupCraft has no account and no server storage. A few small things are kept in your browser's storage for this website, and everything else lives in your files. What is kept in the browser What Set where Kept as Your author name The name button in the status bar, or Settings One name MarkupCraft settings Settings, MarkupCraft tab: color and line weight of new markups, where paste puts copies, units (see MarkupCraft settings) One set Tool settings Properties while a drawing tool is on: color, line, fill, opacity and text size per tool, the chosen stamp, the count group name One set Tool chest The Tools tab Up to 500 tools What you last copied Copy or Cut The copied markups, so another tab can paste them; replaced by the next copy Stamp picture and signature Picture… and My signature… in the stamp list The last picture and your signature Recovery copies Kept for you while a PDF has unsaved markups The PDF and its markups, removed when you save, close or discard it; kept up to 30 days What that means Per browser, per computer. Chrome and Firefox on the same computer each have their own. Your laptop and the office desktop each have their own. Nothing syncs between them, even if you sign in to the browser. Private and incognito windows start empty and forget everything when they close. Clearing site data erases it. Clearing cookies and site data for this website, or for all sites, in the browser's settings removes your name, settings and chest. Some cleanup tools and some company policies do this automatically. Nothing is uploaded. These values never leave the computer, except your name inside the PDFs you save. Recovery copies stay in this browser too. Safari can delete a website's stored data after some weeks without a visit. Open MarkupCraft now and then, and keep an exported tool chest. What is not kept in the browser Your markups, for good. They belong in the \"(marked up)\" PDF you save. The recovery copy is a safety net for a crash or a closed tab, not a place to keep work: it goes when you save, and clearing site data removes it. See Recover your work. Your open files. Each time you open MarkupCraft, open the PDFs again. The sheet you were on, zoom, filters and undo history. These last until you close the set or the tab. Back up and move your setup Tool chest: Tools, then Export. On the other browser or computer, Tools, then Import. Name: type it again on the other browser (click the name button in the status bar). Tool settings: set them again, or save each styled markup as a tool so it travels with the chest. Start fresh To reset everything, clear this website's data in your browser's settings. Your name, settings and chest go; the six default tools come back. The offline copy Save for offline in the bar at the top stores the app's own files (with the PDF reader and writer, about 6 MB) in the browser too, separately from these settings. Clearing site data removes that copy as well; click \"Offline ready\" and choose \"Remove the offline copy\" to remove only it. See Working offline. Cloud saving and sync between computers are not available yet.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"settings","scope":"markupcraft","title":"MarkupCraft settings","summary":"Change your name, the color and line weight of new markups, where paste puts copies, and the units for lengths and the sheet position.","section":"reference","order":65,"keywords":["settings","preferences","options","name","author","color","colour","default color","line weight","line width","thick","thin","paste","paste at pointer","offset","units","metric","millimeters","inches","feet","reset"],"headings":[{"id":"your-name","text":"Your name","level":2},{"id":"new-markups","text":"New markups","level":2},{"id":"paste","text":"Paste","level":2},{"id":"measurements","text":"Measurements","level":2},{"id":"reset","text":"Reset","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EOpen \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E with the gear in the bar at the top, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command ,\"\u003E⌘,\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+,\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. The first tab, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMarkupCraft\u003C/span\u003E, holds the settings that belong to this app; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E holds the ones every CadCraft app shares (theme, toolbar, panels). MarkupCraft&#039;s settings exist only in the app: you change them here, and this browser remembers them (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/markupcraft/browser-storage\" data-topic=\"markupcraft/browser-storage\"\u003EWhat stays in this browser\u003C/a\u003E). A change takes effect at once; there is nothing to save.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"your-name\"\u003EYour name\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EName on markups\u003C/span\u003E is the name every new markup and reply carries, and that saved PDFs show as the comment&#039;s author. It is the same name as the button at the right end of the status bar; change it in either place. Markups already drawn keep the name they were made with. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/markupcraft/set-your-name\" data-topic=\"markupcraft/set-your-name\"\u003ESet your author name\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"new-markups\"\u003ENew markups\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMarkup color\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/strong\u003E is the color new pens, lines, arrows, rectangles, ellipses, polylines, polygons, clouds, callouts and text start with (red at the start). Highlighters, notes, stamps, lengths, areas and counts keep their own colors, because their colors say what they are.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EThin lines\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENormal\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHeavy lines\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/strong\u003E make the lines of those same markups half as wide, as they are, or half again as wide.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA color or line width you choose for one tool in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E (with that tool on and nothing selected) wins over these. Tools from the \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/markupcraft/tool-chest\" data-topic=\"markupcraft/tool-chest\"\u003Etool chest\u003C/a\u003E keep their own style. Markups already drawn do not change: select them and change them in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"paste\"\u003EPaste\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhere \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command V\"\u003E⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E puts what you copied:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAt the pointer\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/strong\u003E (the start): with the pointer on the sheet, the copy lands centered where it points. With the pointer elsewhere, it lands beside the original.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBeside the copy\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/strong\u003E: always a little down and to the right of the original, a step further with each paste.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaste\u003C/span\u003E button and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift Command V\"\u003E⇧⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaste in place\u003C/span\u003E) work the same with either choice. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/markupcraft/select-and-edit#copy-paste-and-duplicate\" data-topic=\"markupcraft/select-and-edit\" data-hash=\"copy-paste-and-duplicate\"\u003ECopy, paste and duplicate\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"measurements\"\u003EMeasurements\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShow lengths in\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/strong\u003E is the unit the sheet scale dialog offers first when you calibrate from two points: feet and inches, decimal feet, inches, meters or millimeters. Sheets you already calibrated keep their unit. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/markupcraft/calibrate-and-measure\" data-topic=\"markupcraft/calibrate-and-measure\"\u003ECalibrate a sheet and measure\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPosition on the sheet\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/strong\u003E chooses inches or millimeters for the pointer position at the bottom left of the screen. It is a position on the paper, not a real-world distance.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"reset\"\u003EReset\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReset MarkupCraft settings\u003C/span\u003E at the bottom of the dialog puts these settings, and the panel and toolbar settings on \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E, back to how they start. It keeps your name, since that says who you are rather than how you like to work, and it keeps the colors you picked in each tool&#039;s own panel.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Open Settings with the gear in the bar at the top, or press Ctrl+,. The first tab, MarkupCraft, holds the settings that belong to this app; General holds the ones every CadCraft app shares (theme, toolbar, panels). MarkupCraft's settings exist only in the app: you change them here, and this browser remembers them (see What stays in this browser). A change takes effect at once; there is nothing to save. Your name Name on markups is the name every new markup and reply carries, and that saved PDFs show as the comment's author. It is the same name as the button at the right end of the status bar; change it in either place. Markups already drawn keep the name they were made with. See Set your author name. New markups Markup color is the color new pens, lines, arrows, rectangles, ellipses, polylines, polygons, clouds, callouts and text start with (red at the start). Highlighters, notes, stamps, lengths, areas and counts keep their own colors, because their colors say what they are. Thin lines, Normal, Heavy lines make the lines of those same markups half as wide, as they are, or half again as wide. A color or line width you choose for one tool in Properties (with that tool on and nothing selected) wins over these. Tools from the tool chest keep their own style. Markups already drawn do not change: select them and change them in Properties. Paste Where Ctrl+V puts what you copied: At the pointer (the start): with the pointer on the sheet, the copy lands centered where it points. With the pointer elsewhere, it lands beside the original. Beside the copy: always a little down and to the right of the original, a step further with each paste. The Paste button and Ctrl+Shift+V (Paste in place) work the same with either choice. See Copy, paste and duplicate. Measurements Show lengths in is the unit the sheet scale dialog offers first when you calibrate from two points: feet and inches, decimal feet, inches, meters or millimeters. Sheets you already calibrated keep their unit. See Calibrate a sheet and measure. Position on the sheet chooses inches or millimeters for the pointer position at the bottom left of the screen. It is a position on the paper, not a real-world distance. Reset Reset MarkupCraft settings at the bottom of the dialog puts these settings, and the panel and toolbar settings on General, back to how they start. It keeps your name, since that says who you are rather than how you like to work, and it keeps the colors you picked in each tool's own panel.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"troubleshooting","scope":"markupcraft","title":"Troubleshooting","summary":"Messages MarkupCraft shows and what to do about them, plus fixes for things that seem not to work.","section":"trouble","order":10,"keywords":["problem","error","not working","help","message","wont open","cannot save","password","protected","measure refused","calibrate","offline","blank","slow","lost markups","question marks"],"headings":[{"id":"opening-files","text":"Opening files","level":2},{"id":"drawing-and-selecting","text":"Drawing and selecting","level":2},{"id":"measuring","text":"Measuring","level":2},{"id":"saving","text":"Saving","level":2},{"id":"working-offline","text":"Working offline","level":2},{"id":"lost-things","text":"Lost things","level":2},{"id":"display-and-speed","text":"Display and speed","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EMost problems are explained in the status bar at the bottom of the screen. Read it first.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"opening-files\"\u003EOpening files\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;MarkupCraft opens PDF files. &quot;x&quot; is not a PDF.&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E You picked or dropped something else. Only PDFs open. Export drawings from your CAD program as PDF first.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&#039;&quot;name&quot; needs a password to open. Open an unprotected copy.&#039;\u003C/strong\u003E The PDF is password-protected. Ask the sender for an unprotected copy, or open it in a PDF program where you know the password and save a copy without the password.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&#039;&quot;name&quot; could not be read as a PDF (…)&#039;\u003C/strong\u003E The file is damaged or is not really a PDF (an incomplete download, an email attachment cut off). Download it again. If other PDF programs can open it, use their &quot;save as&quot; to make a clean copy and open that.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;Some files did not open&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E When you open several at once, this box lists the files that failed and why. The others are open.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;The PDF reader could not load: …&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E The PDF engine could not be fetched. Online: reload the page and try again; check that a firewall or content blocker is not blocking the site. Offline: MarkupCraft was not saved for offline use in this browser (the bar at the top does not say &quot;Offline ready&quot;). Get online, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave for offline\u003C/span\u003E, and wait for &quot;Offline ready&quot;. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/offline\" data-topic=\"basics/offline\"\u003EWorking offline\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;Some annotations may show twice on screen&quot; or &quot;Extra annotation details could not be read&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E The file&#039;s existing comments are unusual. They are still opened as well as MarkupCraft can; check them before relying on them. Saving still works.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe sheet has the wrong name, or &quot;page 3&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/markupcraft/open-a-plan-set#how-sheets-get-their-names\" data-topic=\"markupcraft/open-a-plan-set\" data-hash=\"how-sheets-get-their-names\"\u003EHow sheets get their names\u003C/a\u003E. Scanned sheets have no text to read; use \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERename sheet\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe same sheets appear twice\u003C/strong\u003E The same file was opened twice. Choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EClose\u003C/span\u003E (save first if needed) and open the set again.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"drawing-and-selecting\"\u003EDrawing and selecting\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENothing happens when I click on the sheet\u003C/strong\u003E Check which tool is pressed in the toolbar, and read the status bar. With \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERectangle\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEllipse\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECloud\u003C/span\u003E, a click without dragging draws nothing: drag. With \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPolyline\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPolygon\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFree cloud\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArea\u003C/span\u003E, points are being added: finish with a double-click or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;A polygon needs at least 3 points.&quot; (or 2 for a polyline)\u003C/strong\u003E Click more points before finishing.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;Nothing typed, so no text was added.&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E Text boxes and callouts are kept only when they have text. Draw it again and type before clicking away.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;The two points are the same. Click two different points.&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E For lines, arrows, lengths and calibration, the two ends must be apart. Drag, or click twice in different places.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EI cannot move or delete a markup; it has a gray dashed box\u003C/strong\u003E It is a read-only comment that came with the file (for example a text highlight). MarkupCraft keeps it as it is: you can only reply to it. The status bar says &quot;read-only markup(s) are kept as in the file&quot;.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMy keys type letters into a box instead of choosing tools\u003C/strong\u003E The cursor is in a text box (a reply, a field in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E, the sheet search). Click on an empty part of the sheet area first, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E if you are editing markup text.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESpace does not pan, or arrow keys do nothing\u003C/strong\u003E You just clicked a toolbar button, so it still has the keyboard. Drag with the middle mouse button instead, or click the sheet once with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E on, then try again. See also \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/markupcraft/keyboard#when-a-key-does-nothing\" data-topic=\"markupcraft/keyboard\" data-hash=\"when-a-key-does-nothing\"\u003EKeyboard and mouse\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA pinch on the touch screen left a mark\u003C/strong\u003E A drag tool (pen, cloud, rectangle) started before the second finger arrived and finished before it was canceled. Press \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUndo\u003C/span\u003E. Put both fingers down together when you mean to zoom.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"measuring\"\u003EMeasuring\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;Calibrate this sheet first: pick two points a known distance apart, or choose its scale. Then measure.&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E Each sheet needs its own scale before \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELength\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArea\u003C/span\u003E. MarkupCraft has switched to \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECalibrate\u003C/span\u003E: click the two ends of a known dimension and type its length, or click the scale button in the status bar and pick a scale. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/markupcraft/calibrate-and-measure\" data-topic=\"markupcraft/calibrate-and-measure\"\u003ECalibrate a sheet and measure\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&#039;That is not a length MarkupCraft understands.&#039;\u003C/strong\u003E Type it like \u003Ccode\u003E24&#039;-0&quot;\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E12&#039; 6 1/2&quot;\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E7.2 m\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E900 mm\u003C/code\u003E. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/markupcraft/units-and-scales\" data-topic=\"markupcraft/units-and-scales\"\u003EUnits, lengths and scales\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMeasurements are a bit off, or wildly off\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA preset scale on a PDF that is not full size measures wrong. Calibrate from a long known dimension instead.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA thousands separator was read as a decimal point: \u003Ccode\u003E1,200 mm\u003C/code\u003E means 1.2 mm. Type \u003Ccode\u003E1200 mm\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EZoom in when you click the ends.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe drawing may not be to scale (&quot;NTS&quot;).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;Select a count group first, then Shift+click a mark to remove it.&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E Choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E, click the count group, then choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECount\u003C/span\u003E again and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E-click the dot.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"saving\"\u003ESaving\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;This PDF is protected (encrypted), so a marked-up copy cannot be written.&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E The file forbids changes. Ask for an unprotected copy. Meanwhile you can still export the markups list as CSV or BCF.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;The PDF writer could not load: …&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E Usually you are offline and the app was not saved for offline use (no &quot;Offline ready&quot; in the bar at the top). Your markups are still in the tab: keep it open, get online, and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave\u003C/span\u003E again. Then choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave for offline\u003C/span\u003E so it works offline next time; the saved copy includes the PDF writer.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;Could not save &lt;file&gt;&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E The box shows why. Your markups are still in the tab. Try again; if it repeats, export the CSV so the comments are not lost, and report the message.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EI chose Save but only one file arrived\u003C/strong\u003E Your browser blocked the extra downloads. Look for a notice in the address bar and allow this site to download multiple files, then save again.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EI cannot find the saved file\u003C/strong\u003E It went to your browser&#039;s downloads folder. Open the browser&#039;s downloads list (in Chrome and Edge, \u003Cspan class=\"hp\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hpv\" data-v=\"os:mac\" title=\"Mac\"\u003EOption-Command-L\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hp-sep\"\u003E / \u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hpv\" data-v=\"os:windows,linux\" title=\"Windows or Linux\"\u003ECtrl+J\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/span\u003E; in Firefox and Safari, the downloads button in the toolbar) to see where. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/browsers\" data-topic=\"basics/browsers\"\u003EBrowsers and computers\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESome text shows &quot;?&quot; in other PDF viewers\u003C/strong\u003E Characters outside the Western European set print as &quot;?&quot; in the copy. The save message counts them. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/markupcraft/save-the-marked-up-pdf\" data-topic=\"markupcraft/save-the-marked-up-pdf\"\u003ESave the marked-up PDF\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe stamp shows tomorrow&#039;s (or yesterday&#039;s) date\u003C/strong\u003E Stamp dates are in UTC. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/markupcraft/stamps-and-notes\" data-topic=\"markupcraft/stamps-and-notes\"\u003EStamps and sticky notes\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"working-offline\"\u003EWorking offline\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe offline button says &quot;Could not save&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E Some of the app&#039;s files did not download. Check the connection and click the button again; it counts &quot;Saving N of M&quot; until &quot;Offline ready&quot;.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe offline button says &quot;Offline not available&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E This browser window cannot keep an offline copy: a private window, or a computer whose policy blocks it. Open MarkupCraft in a normal window and try again.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOffline, the app is an older version\u003C/strong\u003E The offline copy is the version you saved. When you are online, click &quot;Offline ready&quot; and choose &quot;Save again (get the newest version)&quot;. To free the space, choose &quot;Remove the offline copy&quot;. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/offline\" data-topic=\"basics/offline\"\u003EWorking offline\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe help will not open\u003C/strong\u003E Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F1\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E (on a Mac, with \u003Cstrong\u003Efn\u003C/strong\u003E), or choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHelp\u003C/span\u003E in the bar at the top. It works offline too.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"lost-things\"\u003ELost things\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMy markups are gone after closing or reloading the tab\u003C/strong\u003E Open MarkupCraft again in the same browser: it offers the unsaved work back with &quot;Recover your work?&quot;. Choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERecover\u003C/span\u003E and save the PDF. Copies are kept up to 30 days; a private window, or clearing site data, leaves none. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/recover\" data-topic=\"basics/recover\"\u003ERecover your work\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMy name, tool settings or tool chest are gone\u003C/strong\u003E They are kept in this browser only and were cleared (site data cleared, a private window, another browser or computer, or Safari&#039;s cleanup of sites not visited for a while). Set the name again and import your exported chest. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/markupcraft/browser-storage\" data-topic=\"markupcraft/browser-storage\"\u003EWhat stays in this browser\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;The tool chest could not be kept in this browser (storage is off or full). Export it to keep it.&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E The browser refuses to store data for this site (private mode, storage blocked by a setting or policy, or full). Export the chest after changing it, and import it next time.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"display-and-speed\"\u003EDisplay and speed\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe sheet looks blurry for a moment\u003C/strong\u003E MarkupCraft shows a quick, rough rendering while you zoom or pan, and redraws it sharp a moment after you stop.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EEverything is slow with a big set\u003C/strong\u003E Close other heavy tabs, open fewer files at once, and avoid zooming to the maximum on huge sheets. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/markupcraft/several-files#big-sets\" data-topic=\"markupcraft/several-files\" data-hash=\"big-sets\"\u003EWork with several files at once\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe sheet is dark with light lines\u003C/strong\u003E You are in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModel\u003C/span\u003E theme. Choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaper\u003C/span\u003E in the bar at the top for dark lines on white. Your markups and the saved PDF are not affected.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Most problems are explained in the status bar at the bottom of the screen. Read it first. Opening files \"MarkupCraft opens PDF files. \"x\" is not a PDF.\" You picked or dropped something else. Only PDFs open. Export drawings from your CAD program as PDF first. '\"name\" needs a password to open. Open an unprotected copy.' The PDF is password-protected. Ask the sender for an unprotected copy, or open it in a PDF program where you know the password and save a copy without the password. '\"name\" could not be read as a PDF (…)' The file is damaged or is not really a PDF (an incomplete download, an email attachment cut off). Download it again. If other PDF programs can open it, use their \"save as\" to make a clean copy and open that. \"Some files did not open\" When you open several at once, this box lists the files that failed and why. The others are open. \"The PDF reader could not load: …\" The PDF engine could not be fetched. Online: reload the page and try again; check that a firewall or content blocker is not blocking the site. Offline: MarkupCraft was not saved for offline use in this browser (the bar at the top does not say \"Offline ready\"). Get online, choose Save for offline, and wait for \"Offline ready\". See Working offline. \"Some annotations may show twice on screen\" or \"Extra annotation details could not be read\" The file's existing comments are unusual. They are still opened as well as MarkupCraft can; check them before relying on them. Saving still works. The sheet has the wrong name, or \"page 3\" See How sheets get their names. Scanned sheets have no text to read; use Rename sheet. The same sheets appear twice The same file was opened twice. Choose Close (save first if needed) and open the set again. Drawing and selecting Nothing happens when I click on the sheet Check which tool is pressed in the toolbar, and read the status bar. With Rectangle, Ellipse and Cloud, a click without dragging draws nothing: drag. With Polyline, Polygon, Free cloud and Area, points are being added: finish with a double-click or Enter. \"A polygon needs at least 3 points.\" (or 2 for a polyline) Click more points before finishing. \"Nothing typed, so no text was added.\" Text boxes and callouts are kept only when they have text. Draw it again and type before clicking away. \"The two points are the same. Click two different points.\" For lines, arrows, lengths and calibration, the two ends must be apart. Drag, or click twice in different places. I cannot move or delete a markup; it has a gray dashed box It is a read-only comment that came with the file (for example a text highlight). MarkupCraft keeps it as it is: you can only reply to it. The status bar says \"read-only markup(s) are kept as in the file\". My keys type letters into a box instead of choosing tools The cursor is in a text box (a reply, a field in Properties, the sheet search). Click on an empty part of the sheet area first, or press Esc if you are editing markup text. Space does not pan, or arrow keys do nothing You just clicked a toolbar button, so it still has the keyboard. Drag with the middle mouse button instead, or click the sheet once with Select on, then try again. See also Keyboard and mouse. A pinch on the touch screen left a mark A drag tool (pen, cloud, rectangle) started before the second finger arrived and finished before it was canceled. Press Undo. Put both fingers down together when you mean to zoom. Measuring \"Calibrate this sheet first: pick two points a known distance apart, or choose its scale. Then measure.\" Each sheet needs its own scale before Length or Area. MarkupCraft has switched to Calibrate: click the two ends of a known dimension and type its length, or click the scale button in the status bar and pick a scale. See Calibrate a sheet and measure. 'That is not a length MarkupCraft understands.' Type it like 24'-0\", 12' 6 1/2\", 7.2 m or 900 mm. See Units, lengths and scales. Measurements are a bit off, or wildly off A preset scale on a PDF that is not full size measures wrong. Calibrate from a long known dimension instead. A thousands separator was read as a decimal point: 1,200 mm means 1.2 mm. Type 1200 mm. Zoom in when you click the ends. The drawing may not be to scale (\"NTS\"). \"Select a count group first, then Shift+click a mark to remove it.\" Choose Select, click the count group, then choose Count again and Shift-click the dot. Saving \"This PDF is protected (encrypted), so a marked-up copy cannot be written.\" The file forbids changes. Ask for an unprotected copy. Meanwhile you can still export the markups list as CSV or BCF. \"The PDF writer could not load: …\" Usually you are offline and the app was not saved for offline use (no \"Offline ready\" in the bar at the top). Your markups are still in the tab: keep it open, get online, and choose Save again. Then choose Save for offline so it works offline next time; the saved copy includes the PDF writer. \"Could not save \u003Cfile\u003E\" The box shows why. Your markups are still in the tab. Try again; if it repeats, export the CSV so the comments are not lost, and report the message. I chose Save but only one file arrived Your browser blocked the extra downloads. Look for a notice in the address bar and allow this site to download multiple files, then save again. I cannot find the saved file It went to your browser's downloads folder. Open the browser's downloads list (in Chrome and Edge, Option-Command-L / Ctrl+J; in Firefox and Safari, the downloads button in the toolbar) to see where. See Browsers and computers. Some text shows \"?\" in other PDF viewers Characters outside the Western European set print as \"?\" in the copy. The save message counts them. See Save the marked-up PDF. The stamp shows tomorrow's (or yesterday's) date Stamp dates are in UTC. See Stamps and sticky notes. Working offline The offline button says \"Could not save\" Some of the app's files did not download. Check the connection and click the button again; it counts \"Saving N of M\" until \"Offline ready\". The offline button says \"Offline not available\" This browser window cannot keep an offline copy: a private window, or a computer whose policy blocks it. Open MarkupCraft in a normal window and try again. Offline, the app is an older version The offline copy is the version you saved. When you are online, click \"Offline ready\" and choose \"Save again (get the newest version)\". To free the space, choose \"Remove the offline copy\". See Working offline. The help will not open Press F1 (on a Mac, with fn), or choose Help in the bar at the top. It works offline too. Lost things My markups are gone after closing or reloading the tab Open MarkupCraft again in the same browser: it offers the unsaved work back with \"Recover your work?\". Choose Recover and save the PDF. Copies are kept up to 30 days; a private window, or clearing site data, leaves none. See Recover your work. My name, tool settings or tool chest are gone They are kept in this browser only and were cleared (site data cleared, a private window, another browser or computer, or Safari's cleanup of sites not visited for a while). Set the name again and import your exported chest. See What stays in this browser. \"The tool chest could not be kept in this browser (storage is off or full). Export it to keep it.\" The browser refuses to store data for this site (private mode, storage blocked by a setting or policy, or full). Export the chest after changing it, and import it next time. Display and speed The sheet looks blurry for a moment MarkupCraft shows a quick, rough rendering while you zoom or pan, and redraws it sharp a moment after you stop. Everything is slow with a big set Close other heavy tabs, open fewer files at once, and avoid zooming to the maximum on huge sheets. See Work with several files at once. The sheet is dark with light lines You are in the Model theme. Choose Paper in the bar at the top for dark lines on white. Your markups and the saved PDF are not affected.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"not-built-yet","scope":"markupcraft","title":"What is not built yet","summary":"What this build of MarkupCraft does not do yet, and what to do instead today.","section":"trouble","order":20,"keywords":["missing","roadmap","planned","limitation","not supported","coming","feature request","sessions","cloud","digital signature","web links"],"headings":[{"id":"working-with-other-people","text":"Working with other people","level":2},{"id":"markups","text":"Markups","level":2},{"id":"the-page-s-own-content","text":"The page's own content","level":2},{"id":"measuring","text":"Measuring","level":2},{"id":"sheets-and-files","text":"Sheets and files","level":2},{"id":"lists-and-exports","text":"Lists and exports","level":2},{"id":"compare","text":"Compare","level":2},{"id":"panels","text":"Panels","level":2},{"id":"platforms","text":"Platforms","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EMarkupCraft is in development. This is what the current build does \u003Cstrong\u003Enot\u003C/strong\u003E do, so you can plan around it. It describes today&#039;s build, not promises.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"working-with-other-people\"\u003EWorking with other people\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EDo this today\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EShared review sessions over the internet (invite links are not open yet)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EReview live on your own network in CadCraft Studio with a short code, which is free: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/markupcraft/work-live\" data-topic=\"markupcraft/work-live\"\u003EReview one plan set together, live\u003C/a\u003E. Or save the marked-up PDF and pass it on; each person replies and saves.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECloud saving and sync between computers\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EKeep the PDFs with your project files. Cloud saving is not available yet.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMerging two people&#039;s copies of the markups\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDivide the sheets between reviewers, or copy a few markups across with \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command C\"\u003E⌘C\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+C\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command V\"\u003E⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. RFIs, submittals and daily logs do merge: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/markupcraft/field-records\" data-topic=\"markupcraft/field-records\"\u003ETrack RFIs, submittals and daily logs\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETeams, notifications and cloud projects for RFIs and submittals\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPass the PDF on and use Merge to bring copies together.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERFIs, submittals and daily logs in a live session\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEach person works on their own copy; bring the copies together with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMerge\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMerging two people&#039;s changes to different fields of one record\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe copy changed last wins that record as a whole (its thread and attachment names are joined). Agree who edits which record.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAssistant tools for RFIs, submittals and daily logs\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWork with them in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EField\u003C/span\u003E tab. Assistants can work with markups: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/markupcraft/use-with-ai-assistants\" data-topic=\"markupcraft/use-with-ai-assistants\"\u003EUse MarkupCraft with an AI assistant\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEditing or deleting a reply once sent\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUndo right away, or add a correcting reply.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EChanging who a markup is by\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESet your name before you start.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"markups\"\u003EMarkups\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EDo this today\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EStamps with fill-in fields, certified digital signatures\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUse a custom text stamp, which carries your name and the date, or a picture of your signature.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAttaching files other than photos (documents, videos)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EKeep them alongside the PDF and refer to them in the comment.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELinks to websites or to files that are not open\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWrite the address or file name in the comment.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECustom status sets\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUse the four statuses, and the subject for anything more.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEditing comments that came with the file as read-only (text highlights, other programs&#039; stamps)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EReply to them, or draw a MarkupCraft markup next to them.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPressure-sensitive pen strokes\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESet the line width.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-page-s-own-content\"\u003EThe page&#039;s own content\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EDo this today\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrawing new shapes straight into the page\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDraw them as markups, then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPut into the page\u003C/span\u003E. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/markupcraft/page-content\" data-topic=\"markupcraft/page-content\"\u003EChange the page&#039;s own content\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETurning page objects to any angle\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMove and resize them; turn the whole page with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPages\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EChanging a line of text&#039;s font or size, or text that flows over several lines\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EChange the words one line at a time; for new wording in another font, add a text markup.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEditing what is inside a scanned picture\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EReplace the picture, or mark it up.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEditing encrypted or signed PDFs\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAsk for an unsigned copy; markups work on any PDF.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"measuring\"\u003EMeasuring\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EDo this today\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERadius measurements\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDraw an \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArc\u003C/span\u003E along it, or measure the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELength\u003C/span\u003E across and halve it.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEditing a cut-out&#039;s corners\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERemove the cut-outs and draw them again.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EApplying one scale to many sheets at once\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECalibrate each sheet; picking a preset takes a few seconds per sheet.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESeveral scales on one sheet (viewports)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECalibrate to the drawing you measure most. Changing a sheet&#039;s scale re-measures everything on it, so write down detail values before you recalibrate for them.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESnapping to lines in the drawing\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EZoom in before clicking, or type lengths and angles (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/markupcraft/calibrate-and-measure#sketch-to-scale\" data-topic=\"markupcraft/calibrate-and-measure\" data-hash=\"sketch-to-scale\"\u003ESketch to scale\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA full takeoff with items, assemblies and prices\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUse TakeoffCraft, the CadCraft family&#039;s takeoff app.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"sheets-and-files\"\u003ESheets and files\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EDo this today\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUndoing a page change (rotate, move, insert, delete)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClose without saving and open the original PDF again. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/markupcraft/pages-and-printing\" data-topic=\"markupcraft/pages-and-printing\"\u003EPrint, and change the pages\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClosing one file of a set\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EClose\u003C/span\u003E closes the whole set; reopen the files you need.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESaving a set as a project to reopen in one step\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpen the same PDFs again.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPrinting to a chosen scale, or several sheets per paper\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUse your browser&#039;s printer dialog, or print the saved copy from another PDF viewer.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"lists-and-exports\"\u003ELists and exports\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EDo this today\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPlacing imported BCF issues at their model position\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EImported issues tied to no sheet are stacked at the top left: move them.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EImporting tool sets from other markup programs\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERebuild the tools you need and save them to the chest; export it for the office.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"compare\"\u003ECompare\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EDo this today\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EComparing text set in a font, or a list of what changed\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUse the overlay and the slider, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EChanged lines\u003C/span\u003E for drawn lines.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EComparing scanned sheets by their lines\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUse the picture overlay.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELining up every pair automatically when comparing a whole set\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECompare a shifted pair on its own and use \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAlign by two points\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"panels\"\u003EPanels\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EDo this today\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMoving the toolbar or the status bar\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOnly the two side panels move.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"platforms\"\u003EPlatforms\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDesktop:\u003C/strong\u003E MarkupCraft runs in the browser on Mac, Windows, Linux and iPad, and in CadCraft Studio, the desktop app with all eight apps in one install.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMarkupCraft is in development. Labels and behavior may still change, and this help changes with them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"MarkupCraft is in development. This is what the current build does not do, so you can plan around it. It describes today's build, not promises. Working with other people Not built yet Do this today Shared review sessions over the internet (invite links are not open yet) Review live on your own network in CadCraft Studio with a short code, which is free: see Review one plan set together, live. Or save the marked-up PDF and pass it on; each person replies and saves. Cloud saving and sync between computers Keep the PDFs with your project files. Cloud saving is not available yet. Merging two people's copies of the markups Divide the sheets between reviewers, or copy a few markups across with Ctrl+C and Ctrl+V. RFIs, submittals and daily logs do merge: see Track RFIs, submittals and daily logs. Teams, notifications and cloud projects for RFIs and submittals Pass the PDF on and use Merge to bring copies together. RFIs, submittals and daily logs in a live session Each person works on their own copy; bring the copies together with Merge. Merging two people's changes to different fields of one record The copy changed last wins that record as a whole (its thread and attachment names are joined). Agree who edits which record. Assistant tools for RFIs, submittals and daily logs Work with them in the Field tab. Assistants can work with markups: see Use MarkupCraft with an AI assistant. Editing or deleting a reply once sent Undo right away, or add a correcting reply. Changing who a markup is by Set your name before you start. Markups Not built yet Do this today Stamps with fill-in fields, certified digital signatures Use a custom text stamp, which carries your name and the date, or a picture of your signature. Attaching files other than photos (documents, videos) Keep them alongside the PDF and refer to them in the comment. Links to websites or to files that are not open Write the address or file name in the comment. Custom status sets Use the four statuses, and the subject for anything more. Editing comments that came with the file as read-only (text highlights, other programs' stamps) Reply to them, or draw a MarkupCraft markup next to them. Pressure-sensitive pen strokes Set the line width. The page's own content Not built yet Do this today Drawing new shapes straight into the page Draw them as markups, then Put into the page. See Change the page's own content. Turning page objects to any angle Move and resize them; turn the whole page with Pages. Changing a line of text's font or size, or text that flows over several lines Change the words one line at a time; for new wording in another font, add a text markup. Editing what is inside a scanned picture Replace the picture, or mark it up. Editing encrypted or signed PDFs Ask for an unsigned copy; markups work on any PDF. Measuring Not built yet Do this today Radius measurements Draw an Arc along it, or measure the Length across and halve it. Editing a cut-out's corners Remove the cut-outs and draw them again. Applying one scale to many sheets at once Calibrate each sheet; picking a preset takes a few seconds per sheet. Several scales on one sheet (viewports) Calibrate to the drawing you measure most. Changing a sheet's scale re-measures everything on it, so write down detail values before you recalibrate for them. Snapping to lines in the drawing Zoom in before clicking, or type lengths and angles (see Sketch to scale). A full takeoff with items, assemblies and prices Use TakeoffCraft, the CadCraft family's takeoff app. Sheets and files Not built yet Do this today Undoing a page change (rotate, move, insert, delete) Close without saving and open the original PDF again. See Print, and change the pages. Closing one file of a set Close closes the whole set; reopen the files you need. Saving a set as a project to reopen in one step Open the same PDFs again. Printing to a chosen scale, or several sheets per paper Use your browser's printer dialog, or print the saved copy from another PDF viewer. Lists and exports Not built yet Do this today Placing imported BCF issues at their model position Imported issues tied to no sheet are stacked at the top left: move them. Importing tool sets from other markup programs Rebuild the tools you need and save them to the chest; export it for the office. Compare Not built yet Do this today Comparing text set in a font, or a list of what changed Use the overlay and the slider, or Changed lines for drawn lines. Comparing scanned sheets by their lines Use the picture overlay. Lining up every pair automatically when comparing a whole set Compare a shifted pair on its own and use Align by two points. Panels Not built yet Do this today Moving the toolbar or the status bar Only the two side panels move. Platforms Desktop: MarkupCraft runs in the browser on Mac, Windows, Linux and iPad, and in CadCraft Studio, the desktop app with all eight apps in one install. MarkupCraft is in development. Labels and behavior may still change, and this help changes with them.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"help","scope":"basics","title":"Usare questa guida","summary":"Aprire la guida, cercarvi e vedere i passaggi per il proprio computer.","section":"start","order":10,"keywords":["guida","f1","ricerca","tutorial","manuale"],"headings":[{"id":"aprire-la-guida","text":"Aprire la guida","level":2},{"id":"trovare-un-argomento","text":"Trovare un argomento","level":2},{"id":"passaggi-per-il-proprio-computer","text":"Passaggi per il proprio computer","level":2},{"id":"la-stessa-guida-sul-sito-web","text":"La stessa guida sul sito web","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EOgni app include la propria guida. Fa parte dell&#039;app, quindi si apre anche senza connessione e descrive sempre la versione in uso.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"aprire-la-guida\"\u003EAprire la guida\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPremere \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F1\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E oppure scegliere \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGuida\u003C/span\u003E nella barra in alto dell&#039;app.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPremere \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E o scegliere \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EChiudi\u003C/span\u003E per chiuderla. Il disegno resta com&#039;era.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"os:mac\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EMac\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESulla maggior parte delle tastiere Mac la riga superiore regola luminosità e volume. Premere \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F1\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E insieme al tasto \u003Cstrong\u003Efn\u003C/strong\u003E (o al tasto globo), oppure usare il pulsante \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGuida\u003C/span\u003E. Vedere \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/keys#function-keys-on-a-mac\" data-topic=\"basics/keys\" data-hash=\"function-keys-on-a-mac\"\u003ETasti su un Mac\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"trovare-un-argomento\"\u003ETrovare un argomento\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDigitare nella casella di ricerca in alto nella guida. I risultati si aggiornano mentre si digita e comprendono i nomi dei comandi di altri programmi, quindi digitando ciò che si digiterebbe altrove di solito si trova la pagina giusta.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EGli argomenti sono raggruppati allo stesso modo in ogni app: primi passi, guide passo passo, chi arriva da altri software, riferimento e risoluzione dei problemi.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EI collegamenti in un argomento aprono l&#039;argomento successivo nello stesso pannello. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EIndietro\u003C/span\u003E riporta al punto di partenza.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"passaggi-per-il-proprio-computer\"\u003EPassaggi per il proprio computer\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAlcuni passaggi cambiano tra Mac, Windows e Linux, tra browser e tra uso online e offline. La guida stabilisce quale caso vale per voi e mostra quei passaggi. La riga in alto in un argomento indica la scelta fatta, per esempio &quot;Passaggi per Mac, Chrome o Edge, online&quot;.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EScegliere \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECambia\u003C/span\u003E su quella riga per selezionare un altro computer o browser, per esempio per aiutare un collega.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EScegliere \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMostra tutto\u003C/span\u003E per vedere ogni versione di ogni passaggio, ciascuna con la sua etichetta.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ELe scorciatoie da tastiera sono scritte come le riporta la tastiera in uso: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E si legge ⌘S su Mac e Ctrl+S su Windows e Linux.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"la-stessa-guida-sul-sito-web\"\u003ELa stessa guida sul sito web\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIl sito web mostra gli stessi argomenti all&#039;indirizzo getcadcraft.com/tutorials, con i passaggi di ogni piattaforma. Nell&#039;app, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EApri sul sito web\u003C/span\u003E in fondo a un argomento apre quella pagina quando si è online.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Ogni app include la propria guida. Fa parte dell'app, quindi si apre anche senza connessione e descrive sempre la versione in uso. Aprire la guida Premere F1 oppure scegliere Guida nella barra in alto dell'app. Premere Esc o scegliere Chiudi per chiuderla. Il disegno resta com'era. Sulla maggior parte delle tastiere Mac la riga superiore regola luminosità e volume. Premere F1 insieme al tasto fn (o al tasto globo), oppure usare il pulsante Guida. Vedere Tasti su un Mac. Trovare un argomento Digitare nella casella di ricerca in alto nella guida. I risultati si aggiornano mentre si digita e comprendono i nomi dei comandi di altri programmi, quindi digitando ciò che si digiterebbe altrove di solito si trova la pagina giusta. Gli argomenti sono raggruppati allo stesso modo in ogni app: primi passi, guide passo passo, chi arriva da altri software, riferimento e risoluzione dei problemi. I collegamenti in un argomento aprono l'argomento successivo nello stesso pannello. Indietro riporta al punto di partenza. Passaggi per il proprio computer Alcuni passaggi cambiano tra Mac, Windows e Linux, tra browser e tra uso online e offline. La guida stabilisce quale caso vale per voi e mostra quei passaggi. La riga in alto in un argomento indica la scelta fatta, per esempio \"Passaggi per Mac, Chrome o Edge, online\". Scegliere Cambia su quella riga per selezionare un altro computer o browser, per esempio per aiutare un collega. Scegliere Mostra tutto per vedere ogni versione di ogni passaggio, ciascuna con la sua etichetta. Le scorciatoie da tastiera sono scritte come le riporta la tastiera in uso: Ctrl+S si legge ⌘S su Mac e Ctrl+S su Windows e Linux. La stessa guida sul sito web Il sito web mostra gli stessi argomenti all'indirizzo getcadcraft.com/tutorials, con i passaggi di ogni piattaforma. Nell'app, Apri sul sito web in fondo a un argomento apre quella pagina quando si è online.","lang":"it"},{"slug":"browsers","scope":"basics","title":"Browser e computer","summary":"In quali browser girano le app, che cosa cambia da uno all'altro e di che cosa ha bisogno il computer.","section":"start","order":20,"keywords":["chrome","edge","firefox","safari","brave","browser","mac","windows","linux","ipad","tablet","webgl","requisiti","memoria"],"headings":[{"id":"quale-browser","text":"Quale browser","level":2},{"id":"che-cosa-cambia-da-un-browser-all-altro","text":"Che cosa cambia da un browser all'altro","level":2},{"id":"mac-windows-e-linux","text":"Mac, Windows e Linux","level":2},{"id":"tablet-e-touch-screen","text":"Tablet e touch screen","level":2},{"id":"il-browser-e-il-sistema-pi-vecchi","text":"Il browser e il sistema più vecchi","level":2},{"id":"di-che-cosa-ha-bisogno-il-computer","text":"Di che cosa ha bisogno il computer","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003ELe app girano all&#039;interno di un browser web. Svolgono il lavoro sul computer: i file vengono aperti lì e non ci vengono mai inviati (vedere \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/your-files\" data-topic=\"basics/your-files\"\u003EDove vanno i file\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"quale-browser\"\u003EQuale browser\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUsare una versione aggiornata di uno di questi:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EBrowser\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMotore\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ENote\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EChrome, Edge, Brave, Opera, Vivaldi\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EChromium\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EIl più completo: selezione di cartelle, salvataggio per l&#039;uso offline, gestione dei file.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFirefox\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EGecko\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFunziona. L&#039;apertura di un&#039;intera cartella usa un selettore più semplice. Il salvataggio offline può non funzionare nelle finestre private.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESafari\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWebKit\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFunziona su Mac e iPad. L&#039;apertura di un&#039;intera cartella usa un selettore più semplice. Safari può eliminare una copia offline non usata da alcune settimane.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETutti e tre i motori vanno bene per il lavoro di ogni giorno. Dove un&#039;app si comporta in modo diverso in uno di essi, la sua guida mostra i passaggi per il browser in uso.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"che-cosa-cambia-da-un-browser-all-altro\"\u003EChe cosa cambia da un browser all&#039;altro\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAprire una cartella.\u003C/strong\u003E I browser Chromium mostrano un vero selettore di cartelle e possono leggere direttamente la cartella. Firefox e Safari chiedono di scegliere una cartella e poi leggono ogni file al suo interno, il che richiede più tempo per le cartelle di progetto grandi; trascinare la cartella sull&#039;app funziona in tutti e tre.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESalvare.\u003C/strong\u003E Le app salvano scaricando un file. È il browser a decidere dove vanno i download e se chiedere prima.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:chromium\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EChrome or Edge\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn Chrome o Edge, attivare &quot;Chiedi dove salvare ogni file prima di scaricare&quot; (in Impostazioni, sotto Download) per scegliere ogni volta una cartella.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:firefox\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EFirefox\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn Firefox, aprire Impostazioni, poi Generale, poi File e applicazioni, e scegliere &quot;Chiedi sempre dove salvare i file&quot; per scegliere ogni volta una cartella.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:safari\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003ESafari\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn Safari, aprire Impostazioni, poi Generali, e impostare &quot;Posizione dei file scaricati&quot; su &quot;Chiedi per ogni download&quot; per scegliere ogni volta una cartella.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"mac-windows-e-linux\"\u003EMac, Windows e Linux\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ELe app sono uguali su ogni computer. Cambia la tastiera: le scorciatoie vengono scritte automaticamente per il computer in uso. Vedere \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/keys\" data-topic=\"basics/keys\"\u003ETasti, mouse e trackpad\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"tablet-e-touch-screen\"\u003ETablet e touch screen\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAlcune app accettano il tocco (un dito disegna o seleziona, due dita con il gesto di pizzico eseguono zoom e pan). La guida di ogni app indica se lo prevede. Su iPad usare Safari o Chrome. Una tastiera è utile con le app che usano comandi digitati.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"il-browser-e-il-sistema-pi-vecchi\"\u003EIl browser e il sistema più vecchi\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EQuesti dati sono ricavati dal codice delle app 0.7, non da una stima: ogni file è stato analizzato e ogni funzione del linguaggio usata è stata cercata nei dati di compatibilità pubblicati dei browser. Al di sotto di queste versioni le app non riescono nemmeno ad avviarsi, perché il browser non comprende il codice.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003E\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EVersione più vecchia in grado di avviare le app\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EChrome, Edge, Brave, Opera, Vivaldi\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EChromium 94\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFirefox\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E93\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESafari (Mac, iPad, iPhone)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E16.4 (macOS 13.3, iPadOS e iOS 16.4)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECadCraft Studio porta con sé un proprio motore web su Windows, quindi richiede Windows 10 o 11; su Mac usa quello del sistema, quindi richiede macOS 13.3 o successivo. Impostazioni › Aggiornamenti in Studio mostra su che cosa gira la propria copia.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EQuesta è la versione più vecchia che \u003Cem\u003Esi avvia\u003C/em\u003E, ricavata dal codice. Non è un elenco di vecchi browser in cui abbiamo provato le app, quindi un browser più vecchio che si avvia può comunque non avere una funzione.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"di-che-cosa-ha-bisogno-il-computer\"\u003EDi che cosa ha bisogno il computer\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EQuesti sono minimi previsti per le app, non requisiti misurati:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E8 GB di memoria (16 GB consigliati per grandi modelli federati).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUna scheda grafica con WebGL 2 per le app 3D. Se una vista 3D resta vuota, verificare che l&#039;accelerazione hardware sia attiva nelle impostazioni del browser.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUno schermo largo almeno 1280 pixel per le barre degli strumenti complete; gli schermi più piccoli le dispongono su più righe.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EI file molto grandi (grandi set di tavole, grandi modelli) usano molta memoria in una scheda del browser. Chiudere le altre schede pesanti se un&#039;app rallenta.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Le app girano all'interno di un browser web. Svolgono il lavoro sul computer: i file vengono aperti lì e non ci vengono mai inviati (vedere Dove vanno i file). Quale browser Usare una versione aggiornata di uno di questi: Browser Motore Note Chrome, Edge, Brave, Opera, Vivaldi Chromium Il più completo: selezione di cartelle, salvataggio per l'uso offline, gestione dei file. Firefox Gecko Funziona. L'apertura di un'intera cartella usa un selettore più semplice. Il salvataggio offline può non funzionare nelle finestre private. Safari WebKit Funziona su Mac e iPad. L'apertura di un'intera cartella usa un selettore più semplice. Safari può eliminare una copia offline non usata da alcune settimane. Tutti e tre i motori vanno bene per il lavoro di ogni giorno. Dove un'app si comporta in modo diverso in uno di essi, la sua guida mostra i passaggi per il browser in uso. Che cosa cambia da un browser all'altro Aprire una cartella. I browser Chromium mostrano un vero selettore di cartelle e possono leggere direttamente la cartella. Firefox e Safari chiedono di scegliere una cartella e poi leggono ogni file al suo interno, il che richiede più tempo per le cartelle di progetto grandi; trascinare la cartella sull'app funziona in tutti e tre. Salvare. Le app salvano scaricando un file. È il browser a decidere dove vanno i download e se chiedere prima. In Chrome o Edge, attivare \"Chiedi dove salvare ogni file prima di scaricare\" (in Impostazioni, sotto Download) per scegliere ogni volta una cartella. In Firefox, aprire Impostazioni, poi Generale, poi File e applicazioni, e scegliere \"Chiedi sempre dove salvare i file\" per scegliere ogni volta una cartella. In Safari, aprire Impostazioni, poi Generali, e impostare \"Posizione dei file scaricati\" su \"Chiedi per ogni download\" per scegliere ogni volta una cartella. Mac, Windows e Linux Le app sono uguali su ogni computer. Cambia la tastiera: le scorciatoie vengono scritte automaticamente per il computer in uso. Vedere Tasti, mouse e trackpad. Tablet e touch screen Alcune app accettano il tocco (un dito disegna o seleziona, due dita con il gesto di pizzico eseguono zoom e pan). La guida di ogni app indica se lo prevede. Su iPad usare Safari o Chrome. Una tastiera è utile con le app che usano comandi digitati. Il browser e il sistema più vecchi Questi dati sono ricavati dal codice delle app 0.7, non da una stima: ogni file è stato analizzato e ogni funzione del linguaggio usata è stata cercata nei dati di compatibilità pubblicati dei browser. Al di sotto di queste versioni le app non riescono nemmeno ad avviarsi, perché il browser non comprende il codice. Versione più vecchia in grado di avviare le app Chrome, Edge, Brave, Opera, Vivaldi Chromium 94 Firefox 93 Safari (Mac, iPad, iPhone) 16.4 (macOS 13.3, iPadOS e iOS 16.4) CadCraft Studio porta con sé un proprio motore web su Windows, quindi richiede Windows 10 o 11; su Mac usa quello del sistema, quindi richiede macOS 13.3 o successivo. Impostazioni › Aggiornamenti in Studio mostra su che cosa gira la propria copia. Questa è la versione più vecchia che si avvia, ricavata dal codice. Non è un elenco di vecchi browser in cui abbiamo provato le app, quindi un browser più vecchio che si avvia può comunque non avere una funzione. Di che cosa ha bisogno il computer Questi sono minimi previsti per le app, non requisiti misurati: 8 GB di memoria (16 GB consigliati per grandi modelli federati). Una scheda grafica con WebGL 2 per le app 3D. Se una vista 3D resta vuota, verificare che l'accelerazione hardware sia attiva nelle impostazioni del browser. Uno schermo largo almeno 1280 pixel per le barre degli strumenti complete; gli schermi più piccoli le dispongono su più righe. I file molto grandi (grandi set di tavole, grandi modelli) usano molta memoria in una scheda del browser. Chiudere le altre schede pesanti se un'app rallenta.","lang":"it"},{"slug":"keys","scope":"basics","title":"Tasti, mouse e trackpad","summary":"Come sono scritte le scorciatoie, i tasti funzione su un Mac, il clic destro e il pan senza mouse.","section":"start","order":30,"keywords":["tastiera","scorciatoia","ctrl","command","cmd","opzione","fn","tasti funzione","trackpad","clic destro","pulsante centrale","magic mouse"],"headings":[{"id":"come-sono-scritte-le-scorciatoie","text":"Come sono scritte le scorciatoie","level":2},{"id":"tasti-funzione-su-un-mac","text":"Tasti funzione su un Mac","level":2},{"id":"scorciatoie-che-il-browser-si-tiene","text":"Scorciatoie che il browser si tiene","level":2},{"id":"pulsanti-del-mouse-su-un-trackpad","text":"Pulsanti del mouse su un trackpad","level":2},{"id":"digitare-in-una-casella-di-testo","text":"Digitare in una casella di testo","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"come-sono-scritte-le-scorciatoie\"\u003ECome sono scritte le scorciatoie\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ELa guida scrive ogni scorciatoia come la riporta la tastiera in uso.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EScritto\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ESu Mac\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ESu Windows e Linux\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECommand (⌘) e S\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECtrl e S\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control Shift F\"\u003E⌃⇧F\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+F\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EControl (⌃), Shift (⇧) e F\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECtrl, Shift e F\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Option ←\"\u003E⌥←\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EAlt+←\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpzione (⌥) e ←\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAlt e ←\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Delete\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EBackspace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eil tasto Elimina\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBackspace\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn Delete\"\u003Efn Delete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Efn e Elimina (cancellazione in avanti)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDelete\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EInvio\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EInvio\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIl segno più significa tenere premuti i tasti insieme. &quot;Poi&quot; significa premerne uno dopo l&#039;altro.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ELa maggior parte delle scorciatoie delle app accetta Command o Control su un Mac, quindi \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E funziona in entrambi i modi. Alcune, come lo schermo intero (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control Shift F\"\u003E⌃⇧F\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+F\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), usano il tasto Control su ogni computer; la guida lo indica dove vale.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"tasti-funzione-su-un-mac\"\u003ETasti funzione su un Mac\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESulla maggior parte delle tastiere Mac la riga superiore regola luminosità, volume e contenuti multimediali. Per inviare da F1 a F12:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003Etenere premuto \u003Cstrong\u003Efn\u003C/strong\u003E (o il tasto globo) mentre si preme il tasto, oppure\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eaprire \u003Cstrong\u003EImpostazioni di Sistema\u003C/strong\u003E, poi \u003Cstrong\u003ETastiera\u003C/strong\u003E, poi \u003Cstrong\u003EScorciatoie da tastiera\u003C/strong\u003E, poi \u003Cstrong\u003ETasti funzione\u003C/strong\u003E, e attivare &quot;Usa i tasti F1, F2 ecc. come tasti funzione standard&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAlcuni tasti F sono riservati anche da macOS stesso (per esempio Mission Control). Se un tasto non fa nulla nell&#039;app, provarlo con fn oppure usare il pulsante della barra degli strumenti.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"scorciatoie-che-il-browser-si-tiene\"\u003EScorciatoie che il browser si tiene\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUna pagina web non può sottrarre al browser alcune scorciatoie, qualunque cosa faccia. In ogni app:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command N\"\u003E⌘N\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+N\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command T\"\u003E⌘T\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+T\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E e \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command W\"\u003E⌘W\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+W\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E aprono o chiudono sempre finestre e schede del browser. Le app usano invece pulsanti per Nuovo e Chiudi.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Q\"\u003E⌘Q\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Q\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E su un Mac e \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Option F4\"\u003E⌥ F4\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EAlt+F4\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E su Windows chiudono il browser. In presenza di lavoro non salvato, il browser chiede prima conferma (&quot;Uscire dal sito?&quot;); scegliere di restare, poi salvare.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELo schermo intero del browser (\u003Cspan class=\"hp\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hpv\" data-v=\"os:mac\" title=\"Mac\"\u003EControl-Command-F, o il pulsante verde sulla finestra\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hp-sep\"\u003E / \u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hpv\" data-v=\"os:windows,linux\" title=\"Windows or Linux\"\u003EF11\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/span\u003E) funziona ancora. Le app hanno anche le proprie modalità schermo su \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control Shift F\"\u003E⌃⇧F\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+F\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, che possono nascondere anche le barre dell&#039;app.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"pulsanti-del-mouse-su-un-trackpad\"\u003EPulsanti del mouse su un trackpad\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ELe app usano il pulsante destro e il pulsante centrale. Senza un mouse a tre pulsanti:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"os:mac\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EMac\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EClic destro:\u003C/strong\u003E fare clic con due dita. Attivare &quot;Clic secondario&quot; in \u003Cstrong\u003EImpostazioni di Sistema\u003C/strong\u003E, poi \u003Cstrong\u003ETrackpad\u003C/strong\u003E, se il clic con due dita non fa nulla. Tenendo premuto \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control\"\u003E⌃\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E mentre si fa clic si apre il menu del browser, ma all&#039;interno di un disegno alcune app lo leggono come un normale clic (con Control premuto), quindi preferire il clic con due dita.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETrascinamento con il pulsante centrale (pan):\u003C/strong\u003E ogni app offre un altro modo per il pan, come tenere premuto \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E mentre si trascina o trascinare con il pulsante destro. La pagina sulla tastiera dell&#039;app indica quale.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EZoom con la rotellina:\u003C/strong\u003E lo scorrimento con due dita sul trackpad esegue lo zoom nella maggior parte delle app. Anche il pizzico esegue lo zoom dove l&#039;app lo prevede.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"os:windows,linux\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EWindows or Linux\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EClic destro:\u003C/strong\u003E fare clic con due dita, oppure fare clic nell&#039;angolo in basso a destra del touchpad. Dipende dalle impostazioni del touchpad.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETrascinamento con il pulsante centrale (pan):\u003C/strong\u003E ogni app offre un altro modo per il pan, come tenere premuto \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E mentre si trascina o trascinare con il pulsante destro. La pagina sulla tastiera dell&#039;app indica quale.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EZoom con la rotellina:\u003C/strong\u003E lo scorrimento con due dita sul touchpad esegue lo zoom nella maggior parte delle app.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"digitare-in-una-casella-di-testo\"\u003EDigitare in una casella di testo\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EMentre si digita in una casella (un nome, una nota, una ricerca), le scorciatoie di una sola lettera dell&#039;app sono disattivate, quindi digitando &quot;L&quot; si scrive una L. Premere \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E o fare clic sul disegno per restituire i tasti all&#039;app.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Come sono scritte le scorciatoie La guida scrive ogni scorciatoia come la riporta la tastiera in uso. Scritto Su Mac Su Windows e Linux Ctrl+S Command (⌘) e S Ctrl e S Ctrl+Shift+F Control (⌃), Shift (⇧) e F Ctrl, Shift e F Alt+← Opzione (⌥) e ← Alt e ← Backspace il tasto Elimina Backspace Delete fn e Elimina (cancellazione in avanti) Delete Enter Invio Invio Il segno più significa tenere premuti i tasti insieme. \"Poi\" significa premerne uno dopo l'altro. La maggior parte delle scorciatoie delle app accetta Command o Control su un Mac, quindi Ctrl+Z funziona in entrambi i modi. Alcune, come lo schermo intero (Ctrl+Shift+F), usano il tasto Control su ogni computer; la guida lo indica dove vale. Tasti funzione su un Mac Sulla maggior parte delle tastiere Mac la riga superiore regola luminosità, volume e contenuti multimediali. Per inviare da F1 a F12: tenere premuto fn (o il tasto globo) mentre si preme il tasto, oppure aprire Impostazioni di Sistema, poi Tastiera, poi Scorciatoie da tastiera, poi Tasti funzione, e attivare \"Usa i tasti F1, F2 ecc. come tasti funzione standard\". Alcuni tasti F sono riservati anche da macOS stesso (per esempio Mission Control). Se un tasto non fa nulla nell'app, provarlo con fn oppure usare il pulsante della barra degli strumenti. Scorciatoie che il browser si tiene Una pagina web non può sottrarre al browser alcune scorciatoie, qualunque cosa faccia. In ogni app: Ctrl+N, Ctrl+T e Ctrl+W aprono o chiudono sempre finestre e schede del browser. Le app usano invece pulsanti per Nuovo e Chiudi. Ctrl+Q su un Mac e Alt+F4 su Windows chiudono il browser. In presenza di lavoro non salvato, il browser chiede prima conferma (\"Uscire dal sito?\"); scegliere di restare, poi salvare. Lo schermo intero del browser (Control-Command-F, o il pulsante verde sulla finestra / F11) funziona ancora. Le app hanno anche le proprie modalità schermo su Ctrl+Shift+F, che possono nascondere anche le barre dell'app. Pulsanti del mouse su un trackpad Le app usano il pulsante destro e il pulsante centrale. Senza un mouse a tre pulsanti: Clic destro: fare clic con due dita. Attivare \"Clic secondario\" in Impostazioni di Sistema, poi Trackpad, se il clic con due dita non fa nulla. Tenendo premuto Ctrl mentre si fa clic si apre il menu del browser, ma all'interno di un disegno alcune app lo leggono come un normale clic (con Control premuto), quindi preferire il clic con due dita. Trascinamento con il pulsante centrale (pan): ogni app offre un altro modo per il pan, come tenere premuto Space mentre si trascina o trascinare con il pulsante destro. La pagina sulla tastiera dell'app indica quale. Zoom con la rotellina: lo scorrimento con due dita sul trackpad esegue lo zoom nella maggior parte delle app. Anche il pizzico esegue lo zoom dove l'app lo prevede. Clic destro: fare clic con due dita, oppure fare clic nell'angolo in basso a destra del touchpad. Dipende dalle impostazioni del touchpad. Trascinamento con il pulsante centrale (pan): ogni app offre un altro modo per il pan, come tenere premuto Space mentre si trascina o trascinare con il pulsante destro. La pagina sulla tastiera dell'app indica quale. Zoom con la rotellina: lo scorrimento con due dita sul touchpad esegue lo zoom nella maggior parte delle app. Digitare in una casella di testo Mentre si digita in una casella (un nome, una nota, una ricerca), le scorciatoie di una sola lettera dell'app sono disattivate, quindi digitando \"L\" si scrive una L. Premere Esc o fare clic sul disegno per restituire i tasti all'app.","lang":"it"},{"slug":"find-tools","scope":"basics","title":"Trovare uno strumento o un'impostazione","summary":"Premere Ctrl+K e digitare poche lettere per eseguire uno strumento, cambiare un'impostazione o aprire un argomento della guida.","section":"start","order":32,"keywords":["trova","ricerca","riquadro dei comandi","apertura rapida","esegui comando","ctrl k","cmd k","strumento","impostazione","scorciatoia"],"headings":[{"id":"trovare-ed-eseguire","text":"Trovare ed eseguire","level":2},{"id":"da-sapere","text":"Da sapere","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EOgni app ha una casella di ricerca per i suoi strumenti, le sue impostazioni e questa guida. Non occorre sapere dove si trova uno strumento nella barra degli strumenti.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"trovare-ed-eseguire\"\u003ETrovare ed eseguire\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPremere \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command K\"\u003E⌘K\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+K\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E oppure scegliere \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETrova\u003C/span\u003E nella barra in alto.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDigitare alcune lettere di ciò che serve: &quot;raccordo&quot;, &quot;testo dei pannelli&quot;, &quot;unità&quot;. Ogni parola digitata deve corrispondere, quindi una seconda parola restringe l&#039;elenco.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPremere \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E per eseguire la prima corrispondenza, oppure spostarsi prima con i tasti freccia. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E chiude la casella.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUno strumento viene eseguito come se lo si fosse scelto nella barra degli strumenti. Un&#039;impostazione apre \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EImpostazioni\u003C/span\u003E su quell&#039;impostazione. Un argomento della guida si apre qui, nel pannello della guida.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"da-sapere\"\u003EDa sapere\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003ECiò che è stato usato per ultimo compare per primo la volta successiva, quindi uno strumento usato spesso è a un tasto di distanza.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EGli strumenti che al momento non possono essere eseguiti, per esempio prima di aprire un file, sono nell&#039;elenco ma in grigio.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAnche gli strumenti degli add-on sono nell&#039;elenco, una volta attivato l&#039;add-on.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"Ogni app ha una casella di ricerca per i suoi strumenti, le sue impostazioni e questa guida. Non occorre sapere dove si trova uno strumento nella barra degli strumenti. Trovare ed eseguire Premere Ctrl+K oppure scegliere Trova nella barra in alto. Digitare alcune lettere di ciò che serve: \"raccordo\", \"testo dei pannelli\", \"unità\". Ogni parola digitata deve corrispondere, quindi una seconda parola restringe l'elenco. Premere Enter per eseguire la prima corrispondenza, oppure spostarsi prima con i tasti freccia. Esc chiude la casella. Uno strumento viene eseguito come se lo si fosse scelto nella barra degli strumenti. Un'impostazione apre Impostazioni su quell'impostazione. Un argomento della guida si apre qui, nel pannello della guida. Da sapere Ciò che è stato usato per ultimo compare per primo la volta successiva, quindi uno strumento usato spesso è a un tasto di distanza. Gli strumenti che al momento non possono essere eseguiti, per esempio prima di aprire un file, sono nell'elenco ma in grigio. Anche gli strumenti degli add-on sono nell'elenco, una volta attivato l'add-on.","lang":"it"},{"slug":"arrange-panels","scope":"basics","title":"Disporre i pannelli e usare un secondo monitor","summary":"Ancorare i pannelli laterali a sinistra, a destra o in basso, impilarli, raggrupparli come schede, fissarli o nasconderli, renderli mobili sopra il disegno, rendere la disposizione più densa, scegliere quante righe usa la barra degli strumenti o spostarli nella loro finestra su un altro schermo.","section":"start","order":35,"keywords":["pannelli","ancora","mobile","sposta pannello","trascina","layout","schede","gruppo di schede","impila","fissa","nascondi automaticamente","nascondi pannello","chiudi pannello","compatta","densità della disposizione","densa","righe della barra degli strumenti","a capo","1 riga","2 righe","righe della barra","secondo monitor","due schermi","doppio monitor","più monitor","finestra","pop-up","reimposta layout"],"headings":[{"id":"spostare-un-pannello-nella-finestra","text":"Spostare un pannello nella finestra","level":2},{"id":"raggruppare-i-pannelli-come-schede","text":"Raggruppare i pannelli come schede","level":2},{"id":"fissare-nascondere-e-richiamare","text":"Fissare, nascondere e richiamare","level":2},{"id":"rendere-la-disposizione-pi-densa","text":"Rendere la disposizione più densa","level":2},{"id":"scegliere-quante-righe-usa-la-barra-degli-strumenti","text":"Scegliere quante righe usa la barra degli strumenti","level":2},{"id":"usare-la-tastiera","text":"Usare la tastiera","level":2},{"id":"mettere-i-pannelli-su-un-secondo-monitor","text":"Mettere i pannelli su un secondo monitor","level":2},{"id":"pi-di-una-finestra-dei-pannelli","text":"Più di una finestra dei pannelli","level":2},{"id":"spostare-una-finestra-di-dialogo","text":"Spostare una finestra di dialogo","level":2},{"id":"rimettere-a-posto-i-pannelli","text":"Rimettere a posto i pannelli","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EI pannelli accanto al disegno possono essere spostati. È possibile disporli dove si preferisce oppure assegnare loro una finestra propria su un secondo monitor, così il disegno occupa tutto il primo.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOgni pannello ha una barra del titolo: una sottile barra con il nome del pannello, un menu \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E⋯\u003C/span\u003E, un pulsante di fissaggio e un pulsante di chiusura in cima al pannello. Le versioni precedenti alla 0.7 hanno solo il nome e il menu \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E⋯\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"spostare-un-pannello-nella-finestra\"\u003ESpostare un pannello nella finestra\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETrascinare il pannello dal suo nome in alto.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMentre si trascina, compaiono aree delineate a sinistra, a destra e in basso del disegno. Rilasciare il pannello su una di esse per ancorarlo lì. L&#039;area su cui ci si trova si scurisce.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERilasciarlo in un punto qualsiasi sopra il disegno per renderlo mobile.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EI pannelli possono condividere un lato: si impilano, e ogni linea tra due di essi si trascina per dividere lo spazio (con una linea selezionata, i tasti freccia la spostano, e un doppio clic su di essa divide lo spazio in parti uguali). Trascinare il bordo interno di un lato ancorato per allargarlo o restringerlo; fare doppio clic sul bordo per rimetterlo a posto.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUn pannello mobile resta sopra il disegno. Spostarlo trascinandone il nome, e ridimensionarlo trascinando l&#039;angolo in basso a destra (oppure passare con \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Tab\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E all&#039;angolo e usare i tasti freccia). Fare doppio clic sul nome per ancorarlo di nuovo dove si trovava.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"input:touch\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003ETouch\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETrascinare con un dito sul nome del pannello. Trascinare con un dito l&#039;angolo in basso a destra di un pannello mobile per ridimensionarlo, e i bordi tra i pannelli ancorati allo stesso modo. Per renderlo mobile o ancorarlo senza trascinare, usare il menu \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E⋯\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"raggruppare-i-pannelli-come-schede\"\u003ERaggruppare i pannelli come schede\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDue pannelli possono condividere un&#039;unica cornice e mostrarne uno alla volta. Trascinare un pannello dal suo nome sulla barra del titolo di un altro pannello (la barra è delineata mentre ci si trova sopra) e rilasciare: la cornice ha ora una scheda per ogni pannello, nella sua barra del titolo. Fare clic su una scheda per mostrare quel pannello. Con una scheda selezionata, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"←\"\u003E←\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E←\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E e \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"→\"\u003E→\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E→\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E passano da una scheda all&#039;altra, e \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn ←\"\u003Efn ←\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EHome\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E e \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn →\"\u003Efn →\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnd\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E portano alla prima e all&#039;ultima.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPer far uscire un pannello dal gruppo, scegliere \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEsci dal gruppo di schede\u003C/span\u003E nel suo menu \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E⋯\u003C/span\u003E, oppure trascinarlo altrove. Scegliere \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESchedi con\u003C/span\u003E e il nome di un pannello per raggrupparli senza trascinare.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"fissare-nascondere-e-richiamare\"\u003EFissare, nascondere e richiamare\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIl pulsante di fissaggio nella barra del titolo di un pannello lo rende \u003Cstrong\u003Ea scomparsa automatica\u003C/strong\u003E: il pannello lascia la sua ancora e diventa una piccola scheda su quel bordo. Puntare la scheda, selezionarla o farvi clic: il pannello scorre fuori sopra il disegno. Allontanarsi (o premere \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) e il pannello torna indietro. Premere di nuovo il pulsante di fissaggio, oppure scegliere \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERimuovi fissaggio\u003C/span\u003E, per ancorarlo in modo definitivo.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIl pulsante di chiusura, oppure \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENascondi questo pannello\u003C/span\u003E nel menu, nasconde il pannello e chiude la sua ancora. Una scheda tratteggiata sul bordo lo riporta visibile: fare clic su di essa, oppure selezionarla e premere \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"rendere-la-disposizione-pi-densa\"\u003ERendere la disposizione più densa\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EImpostazioni\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGenerali\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDensità della disposizione\u003C/span\u003E offre due scelte. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EConfortevole\u003C/span\u003E è il valore predefinito. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECompatta\u003C/span\u003E riduce l&#039;altezza delle righe, l&#039;imbottitura dei pannelli, i pulsanti della barra degli strumenti e il testo dei pannelli di quella app, così entra più contenuto nello schermo. L&#039;impostazione è mantenuta per ogni app. Uno schermo touch mantiene i pulsanti grandi in entrambe le scelte, e un telefono mantiene il proprio layout. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReimposta layout\u003C/span\u003E lascia la densità come impostata.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"scegliere-quante-righe-usa-la-barra-degli-strumenti\"\u003EScegliere quante righe usa la barra degli strumenti\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EImpostazioni\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGenerali\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERighe della barra degli strumenti\u003C/span\u003E offre tre scelte. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EA capo\u003C/span\u003E è il valore predefinito: ogni gruppo di strumenti è visibile, su tutte le righe necessarie. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E2 righe\u003C/span\u003E mantiene la barra su due righe e \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E1 riga\u003C/span\u003E su una sola, così il disegno ha più altezza; i gruppi che non entrano scorrono lateralmente all&#039;interno della barra (usare la rotella del mouse, uno scorrimento a due dita, oppure \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Tab\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E fino a uno strumento). Una barra che già entra in due righe resta com&#039;è con \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E2 righe\u003C/span\u003E, perché una griglia la renderebbe solo più alta. L&#039;impostazione è mantenuta per ogni app. Uno schermo touch e un telefono mantengono il proprio layout. CadCraft ha una propria impostazione per la sua barra, in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EImpostazioni\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECadCraft\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"usare-la-tastiera\"\u003EUsare la tastiera\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIl pulsante \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E⋯\u003C/span\u003E di ogni pannello apre un menu: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAncora a sinistra\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAncora a destra\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAncora in basso\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMobile sopra il disegno\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFissa al bordo\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENascondi questo pannello\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESchedi con\u003C/span\u003E un altro pannello, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESposta in una nuova finestra\u003C/span\u003E e \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERimettilo a posto\u003C/span\u003E. Passare con \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Tab\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E al pulsante, premere \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, muoversi con i tasti freccia e premere di nuovo \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E chiude il menu.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"mettere-i-pannelli-su-un-secondo-monitor\"\u003EMettere i pannelli su un secondo monitor\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EScegliere \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E⋯\u003C/span\u003E su un pannello, poi \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESposta tutti i pannelli in una nuova finestra\u003C/span\u003E. Si può anche scegliere \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESposta i pannelli in una nuova finestra\u003C/span\u003E in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EImpostazioni\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESi apre una piccola finestra con dentro i pannelli. Trascinarla sull&#039;altro monitor e renderla grande quanto si desidera.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EContinuare a lavorare: i pannelli agiscono sul disegno come prima, e il disegno riempie la finestra principale.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ELe scorciatoie continuano a funzionare mentre la finestra dei pannelli è in primo piano: i tasti premuti lì, tranne quando si scrive in una casella, vanno al disegno. Domande e finestre di dialogo proposte dall&#039;app si aprono ancora nella finestra principale, quindi controllare lì quando ne compare una. Il tema e la dimensione del testo dei pannelli seguono la finestra principale.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:chromium\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EChrome or Edge\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChrome e Edge possono collocare la finestra sull&#039;altro schermo. Con un secondo schermo collegato, scegliere \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMettila sull&#039;altro schermo\u003C/span\u003E nella finestra dei pannelli (oppure \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMetti i pannelli sull&#039;altro schermo\u003C/span\u003E nel menu \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E⋯\u003C/span\u003E di un pannello). Il browser chiede una volta se questo sito può vedere i propri schermi; dopo averlo consentito, la finestra dei pannelli si apre su quello schermo ogni volta.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:firefox,safari\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EFirefox or Safari\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETrascinare la finestra dei pannelli sull&#039;altro monitor manualmente. La volta successiva si apre nella stessa posizione e con le stesse dimensioni.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"pi-di-una-finestra-dei-pannelli\"\u003EPiù di una finestra dei pannelli\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOgni pannello può avere una finestra propria: fino a quattro finestre dei pannelli, per esempio una per monitor.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003ENel menu \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E⋯\u003C/span\u003E di un pannello, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESposta in una nuova finestra\u003C/span\u003E apre un&#039;altra finestra per quel pannello, e \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESposta nella finestra pannelli 2\u003C/span\u003E (e così via) lo sposta in una già aperta. La seconda finestra si chiama &quot;pannelli 2&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETrascinare un pannello dal suo nome da una finestra a un&#039;altra: rilasciarlo sulla finestra principale per ancorarlo lì o per renderlo mobile (le aree delineate compaiono mentre ci si trova sopra), oppure su un&#039;altra finestra dei pannelli per spostarlo lì. Rilasciarlo altrove e resta dov&#039;era.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUna finestra dei pannelli senza più pannelli si chiude da sola.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDopo un ricaricamento l&#039;app propone di nuovo le finestre dei pannelli. I browser aprono una finestra per ogni clic, quindi scegliere \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EApri la successiva\u003C/span\u003E una volta per ogni finestra.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"spostare-una-finestra-di-dialogo\"\u003ESpostare una finestra di dialogo\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOgni finestra di dialogo può essere spostata fuori dal disegno: premere e trascinare la sua barra del titolo. La finestra ricorda dove la si è lasciata, ciascuna per conto proprio, e torna in quel punto la volta successiva che si apre. Fare doppio clic sulla barra del titolo per centrarla di nuovo.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDalla tastiera, con il fuoco dentro la finestra di dialogo, premere \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Option\"\u003E⌥\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EAlt\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E+\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E+un tasto freccia per spostarla di 16 pixel alla volta; aggiungere \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control\"\u003E⌃\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E per 64 pixel. Una finestra di dialogo che finisce fuori dallo schermo (dopo una finestra o uno schermo più piccoli) viene riportata visibile.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESu un telefono le finestre di dialogo restano fogli a tutta larghezza e non vengono spostate.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn CadCraft Studio, la finestra \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStampa in PDF\u003C/span\u003E di CadCraft, che si può tenere aperta mentre si lavora, ha nella sua barra del titolo un pulsante, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EApri in una finestra separata\u003C/span\u003E. La finestra di dialogo passa in una finestra propria che si può mettere su un altro monitor. Chiudere la finestra di dialogo chiude la finestra, e chiudere la finestra chiude anche la finestra di dialogo.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"rimettere-a-posto-i-pannelli\"\u003ERimettere a posto i pannelli\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EScegliere \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERiporta i pannelli nella finestra principale\u003C/span\u003E nella finestra dei pannelli, oppure semplicemente chiuderla. I pannelli tornano dov&#039;erano.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChiudere o ricaricare la finestra principale chiude anche la finestra dei pannelli. Quando l&#039;app si riapre, segnala che i pannelli erano in una finestra propria: scegliere \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EApri\u003C/span\u003E per riportarla, oppure \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETienili qui\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EImpostazioni\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGenerali\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReimposta layout\u003C/span\u003E rimette ogni pannello dov&#039;era all&#039;inizio.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESe non si apre nulla quando si spostano i pannelli in una nuova finestra, il browser ha bloccato il pop-up. L&#039;app lo segnala e lascia i pannelli dove si trovavano. Consentire i pop-up per getcadcraft.com, poi riprovare.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ELa disposizione è mantenuta per ogni app in questo browser, insieme alle altre impostazioni.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"I pannelli accanto al disegno possono essere spostati. È possibile disporli dove si preferisce oppure assegnare loro una finestra propria su un secondo monitor, così il disegno occupa tutto il primo. Ogni pannello ha una barra del titolo: una sottile barra con il nome del pannello, un menu ⋯, un pulsante di fissaggio e un pulsante di chiusura in cima al pannello. Le versioni precedenti alla 0.7 hanno solo il nome e il menu ⋯. Spostare un pannello nella finestra Trascinare il pannello dal suo nome in alto. Mentre si trascina, compaiono aree delineate a sinistra, a destra e in basso del disegno. Rilasciare il pannello su una di esse per ancorarlo lì. L'area su cui ci si trova si scurisce. Rilasciarlo in un punto qualsiasi sopra il disegno per renderlo mobile. I pannelli possono condividere un lato: si impilano, e ogni linea tra due di essi si trascina per dividere lo spazio (con una linea selezionata, i tasti freccia la spostano, e un doppio clic su di essa divide lo spazio in parti uguali). Trascinare il bordo interno di un lato ancorato per allargarlo o restringerlo; fare doppio clic sul bordo per rimetterlo a posto. Un pannello mobile resta sopra il disegno. Spostarlo trascinandone il nome, e ridimensionarlo trascinando l'angolo in basso a destra (oppure passare con Tab all'angolo e usare i tasti freccia). Fare doppio clic sul nome per ancorarlo di nuovo dove si trovava. Trascinare con un dito sul nome del pannello. Trascinare con un dito l'angolo in basso a destra di un pannello mobile per ridimensionarlo, e i bordi tra i pannelli ancorati allo stesso modo. Per renderlo mobile o ancorarlo senza trascinare, usare il menu ⋯. Raggruppare i pannelli come schede Due pannelli possono condividere un'unica cornice e mostrarne uno alla volta. Trascinare un pannello dal suo nome sulla barra del titolo di un altro pannello (la barra è delineata mentre ci si trova sopra) e rilasciare: la cornice ha ora una scheda per ogni pannello, nella sua barra del titolo. Fare clic su una scheda per mostrare quel pannello. Con una scheda selezionata, ← e → passano da una scheda all'altra, e Home e End portano alla prima e all'ultima. Per far uscire un pannello dal gruppo, scegliere Esci dal gruppo di schede nel suo menu ⋯, oppure trascinarlo altrove. Scegliere Schedi con e il nome di un pannello per raggrupparli senza trascinare. Fissare, nascondere e richiamare Il pulsante di fissaggio nella barra del titolo di un pannello lo rende a scomparsa automatica: il pannello lascia la sua ancora e diventa una piccola scheda su quel bordo. Puntare la scheda, selezionarla o farvi clic: il pannello scorre fuori sopra il disegno. Allontanarsi (o premere Esc) e il pannello torna indietro. Premere di nuovo il pulsante di fissaggio, oppure scegliere Rimuovi fissaggio, per ancorarlo in modo definitivo. Il pulsante di chiusura, oppure Nascondi questo pannello nel menu, nasconde il pannello e chiude la sua ancora. Una scheda tratteggiata sul bordo lo riporta visibile: fare clic su di essa, oppure selezionarla e premere Enter. Rendere la disposizione più densa Impostazioni › Generali › Densità della disposizione offre due scelte. Confortevole è il valore predefinito. Compatta riduce l'altezza delle righe, l'imbottitura dei pannelli, i pulsanti della barra degli strumenti e il testo dei pannelli di quella app, così entra più contenuto nello schermo. L'impostazione è mantenuta per ogni app. Uno schermo touch mantiene i pulsanti grandi in entrambe le scelte, e un telefono mantiene il proprio layout. Reimposta layout lascia la densità come impostata. Scegliere quante righe usa la barra degli strumenti Impostazioni › Generali › Righe della barra degli strumenti offre tre scelte. A capo è il valore predefinito: ogni gruppo di strumenti è visibile, su tutte le righe necessarie. 2 righe mantiene la barra su due righe e 1 riga su una sola, così il disegno ha più altezza; i gruppi che non entrano scorrono lateralmente all'interno della barra (usare la rotella del mouse, uno scorrimento a due dita, oppure Tab fino a uno strumento). Una barra che già entra in due righe resta com'è con 2 righe, perché una griglia la renderebbe solo più alta. L'impostazione è mantenuta per ogni app. Uno schermo touch e un telefono mantengono il proprio layout. CadCraft ha una propria impostazione per la sua barra, in Impostazioni › CadCraft. Usare la tastiera Il pulsante ⋯ di ogni pannello apre un menu: Ancora a sinistra, Ancora a destra, Ancora in basso, Mobile sopra il disegno, Fissa al bordo, Nascondi questo pannello, Schedi con un altro pannello, Sposta in una nuova finestra e Rimettilo a posto. Passare con Tab al pulsante, premere Enter, muoversi con i tasti freccia e premere di nuovo Enter. Esc chiude il menu. Mettere i pannelli su un secondo monitor Scegliere ⋯ su un pannello, poi Sposta tutti i pannelli in una nuova finestra. Si può anche scegliere Sposta i pannelli in una nuova finestra in Impostazioni. Si apre una piccola finestra con dentro i pannelli. Trascinarla sull'altro monitor e renderla grande quanto si desidera. Continuare a lavorare: i pannelli agiscono sul disegno come prima, e il disegno riempie la finestra principale. Le scorciatoie continuano a funzionare mentre la finestra dei pannelli è in primo piano: i tasti premuti lì, tranne quando si scrive in una casella, vanno al disegno. Domande e finestre di dialogo proposte dall'app si aprono ancora nella finestra principale, quindi controllare lì quando ne compare una. Il tema e la dimensione del testo dei pannelli seguono la finestra principale. Chrome e Edge possono collocare la finestra sull'altro schermo. Con un secondo schermo collegato, scegliere Mettila sull'altro schermo nella finestra dei pannelli (oppure Metti i pannelli sull'altro schermo nel menu ⋯ di un pannello). Il browser chiede una volta se questo sito può vedere i propri schermi; dopo averlo consentito, la finestra dei pannelli si apre su quello schermo ogni volta. Trascinare la finestra dei pannelli sull'altro monitor manualmente. La volta successiva si apre nella stessa posizione e con le stesse dimensioni. Più di una finestra dei pannelli Ogni pannello può avere una finestra propria: fino a quattro finestre dei pannelli, per esempio una per monitor. Nel menu ⋯ di un pannello, Sposta in una nuova finestra apre un'altra finestra per quel pannello, e Sposta nella finestra pannelli 2 (e così via) lo sposta in una già aperta. La seconda finestra si chiama \"pannelli 2\". Trascinare un pannello dal suo nome da una finestra a un'altra: rilasciarlo sulla finestra principale per ancorarlo lì o per renderlo mobile (le aree delineate compaiono mentre ci si trova sopra), oppure su un'altra finestra dei pannelli per spostarlo lì. Rilasciarlo altrove e resta dov'era. Una finestra dei pannelli senza più pannelli si chiude da sola. Dopo un ricaricamento l'app propone di nuovo le finestre dei pannelli. I browser aprono una finestra per ogni clic, quindi scegliere Apri la successiva una volta per ogni finestra. Spostare una finestra di dialogo Ogni finestra di dialogo può essere spostata fuori dal disegno: premere e trascinare la sua barra del titolo. La finestra ricorda dove la si è lasciata, ciascuna per conto proprio, e torna in quel punto la volta successiva che si apre. Fare doppio clic sulla barra del titolo per centrarla di nuovo. Dalla tastiera, con il fuoco dentro la finestra di dialogo, premere Alt+Shift+un tasto freccia per spostarla di 16 pixel alla volta; aggiungere Ctrl per 64 pixel. Una finestra di dialogo che finisce fuori dallo schermo (dopo una finestra o uno schermo più piccoli) viene riportata visibile. Su un telefono le finestre di dialogo restano fogli a tutta larghezza e non vengono spostate. In CadCraft Studio, la finestra Stampa in PDF di CadCraft, che si può tenere aperta mentre si lavora, ha nella sua barra del titolo un pulsante, Apri in una finestra separata. La finestra di dialogo passa in una finestra propria che si può mettere su un altro monitor. Chiudere la finestra di dialogo chiude la finestra, e chiudere la finestra chiude anche la finestra di dialogo. Rimettere a posto i pannelli Scegliere Riporta i pannelli nella finestra principale nella finestra dei pannelli, oppure semplicemente chiuderla. I pannelli tornano dov'erano. Chiudere o ricaricare la finestra principale chiude anche la finestra dei pannelli. Quando l'app si riapre, segnala che i pannelli erano in una finestra propria: scegliere Apri per riportarla, oppure Tienili qui. Impostazioni › Generali › Reimposta layout rimette ogni pannello dov'era all'inizio. Se non si apre nulla quando si spostano i pannelli in una nuova finestra, il browser ha bloccato il pop-up. L'app lo segnala e lascia i pannelli dove si trovavano. Consentire i pop-up per getcadcraft.com, poi riprovare. La disposizione è mantenuta per ogni app in questo browser, insieme alle altre impostazioni.","lang":"it"},{"slug":"layout-ribbon","scope":"basics","title":"Lavorare nel layout con barra multifunzione e browser di progetto","summary":"Passare l'app a una barra multifunzione a schede sopra il disegno, con Proprietà e un browser di progetto a sinistra e una scheda per ogni vista aperta, e tornare al layout classico quando si vuole.","section":"start","order":36,"keywords":["layout","area di lavoro","barra multifunzione","schede della barra multifunzione","browser di progetto","browser","palette","palette delle proprietà","schede delle viste","barra di stato","accesso rapido","comprimere la barra multifunzione","ridurre a icona la barra multifunzione","finestra della barra multifunzione","layout classico","cambiare layout","passare a un altro layout","ancorato"],"headings":[{"id":"sceglierlo","text":"Sceglierlo","level":2},{"id":"che-cosa-c-dove","text":"Che cosa c'è dove","level":2},{"id":"usare-la-barra-multifunzione-dalla-tastiera","text":"Usare la barra multifunzione dalla tastiera","level":2},{"id":"comprimere-la-barra-multifunzione-o-darle-una-finestra-propria","text":"Comprimere la barra multifunzione o darle una finestra propria","level":2},{"id":"i-pannelli-restano-i-propri","text":"I pannelli restano i propri","level":2},{"id":"tornare-al-layout-classico","text":"Tornare al layout classico","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EBarra multifunzione e browser di progetto\u003C/strong\u003E è uno dei tre layout dell&#039;area di lavoro dell&#039;app. Una barra multifunzione a schede corre in alto, Proprietà e un browser di progetto condividono il pannello sinistro, ogni vista aperta ha una scheda sopra il disegno e in basso c&#039;è una barra di stato. Gli strumenti sono quelli dell&#039;app: tutto ciò che si può fare nel layout classico funziona anche qui, e il disegno, lo stile dei menu e i tasti restano invariati.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"sceglierlo\"\u003ESceglierlo\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAprire \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EImpostazioni\u003C/span\u003E (l&#039;ingranaggio, oppure \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command ,\"\u003E⌘,\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+,\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EScegliere la scheda \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGlobali\u003C/span\u003E, poi andare a \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELayout\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArea di lavoro\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EScegliere \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBarra multifunzione e browser di progetto\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIl layout cambia subito. La scelta vale per tutte le app in questo browser: da quel momento ogni app si apre con esso, ed è già attivo prima che la pagina venga disegnata, quindi il layout classico non compare mai per primo.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"che-cosa-c-dove\"\u003EChe cosa c&#039;è dove\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EBarra del titolo:\u003C/strong\u003E il nome dell&#039;app, i pulsanti di accesso rapido (apri, salva, annulla, ripristina), il nome del file aperto, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECosa fa ogni strumento qui\u003C/span\u003E (un elenco di tutti gli strumenti con una casella di ricerca, che indica quali sono pronti ora e perché gli altri non lo sono), schermo intero, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGuida\u003C/span\u003E e \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EImpostazioni\u003C/span\u003E. In questo layout la barra del titolo prende il posto della barra dell&#039;app.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EBarra multifunzione:\u003C/strong\u003E gli strumenti dell&#039;app in schede, ciascuna in gruppi. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFile\u003C/span\u003E a sinistra apre, salva e così via. Alcune app aggiungono una scheda propria mentre si lavora in una particolare modalità o area di lavoro; è accesa finché vale e poi scompare.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EBarra delle opzioni:\u003C/strong\u003E sotto la barra multifunzione, ciò che lo strumento in uso richiede, oppure ciò che è selezionato.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPannello sinistro:\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProprietà\u003C/span\u003E sopra e, sotto, le palette (il browser di progetto e il contenuto proprio del pannello) come schede. Trascinare la linea tra le due per ripartire lo spazio.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESchede delle viste:\u003C/strong\u003E una scheda per ogni vista aperta (una tavola, una pianta, una scena…). Si chiude con il suo pulsante di chiusura; il file resta aperto.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EBarra della vista e barra di stato:\u003C/strong\u003E sotto il disegno, i controlli propri della vista (scala, zoom); in basso, i messaggi dell&#039;app.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUno strumento che al momento non può essere eseguito è in grigio e la sua descrizione ne spiega il motivo (per esempio &quot;Aprire prima un file&quot;, oppure che l&#039;app non ha ancora quello strumento). Fare clic su di esso, o premere \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, per leggere il motivo in una finestra.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"usare-la-barra-multifunzione-dalla-tastiera\"\u003EUsare la barra multifunzione dalla tastiera\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECon una scheda della barra multifunzione attiva, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"←\"\u003E←\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E←\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E e \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"→\"\u003E→\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E→\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E passano da una scheda all&#039;altra e \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn ←\"\u003Efn ←\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EHome\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E e \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn →\"\u003Efn →\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnd\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E vanno alla prima e all&#039;ultima. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E termina prima lo strumento in uso, come nel layout classico, e tutte le scorciatoie e \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control Shift F\"\u003E⌃⇧F\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+F\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E (modalità schermo) funzionano come prima.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"comprimere-la-barra-multifunzione-o-darle-una-finestra-propria\"\u003EComprimere la barra multifunzione o darle una finestra propria\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFare doppio clic su una scheda della barra multifunzione per ridurla alle sole schede; un clic su una scheda ne mostra poi gli strumenti finché non ci si sposta. Fare di nuovo doppio clic per tenerla aperta. Anche il pulsante \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESposta la barra multifunzione\u003C/span\u003E, all&#039;estremità destra della barra, contiene \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EComprimi la barra multifunzione\u003C/span\u003E e \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMantieni aperta la barra multifunzione\u003C/span\u003E. Ogni app lo ricorda.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESposta la barra multifunzione\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESposta in una nuova finestra\u003C/span\u003E mette la barra multifunzione in una finestra propria, per esempio su un secondo monitor. I tasti premuti lì raggiungono l&#039;app. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERiporta la barra multifunzione al suo posto\u003C/span\u003E, o la chiusura di quella finestra, la riporta nella finestra principale.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ELe impostazioni della barra degli strumenti valgono per la barra multifunzione: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EImpostazioni\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGlobali\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBarra degli strumenti\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EIcone ed etichette\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESolo icone\u003C/span\u003E (la barra multifunzione mostra allora due righe di piccole icone per gruppo) oppure \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESolo etichette\u003C/span\u003E, e \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMostra i nomi dei gruppi (File, Disegna, …)\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"i-pannelli-restano-i-propri\"\u003EI pannelli restano i propri\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EI pannelli sinistro e destro mantengono tutto ciò che è descritto in \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/arrange-panels\" data-topic=\"basics/arrange-panels\"\u003EDisporre i pannelli\u003C/a\u003E: ancorare, rendere mobili, fissare, nascondere, schede, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESposta in una nuova finestra\u003C/span\u003E e \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReimposta layout\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"tornare-al-layout-classico\"\u003ETornare al layout classico\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAprire \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EImpostazioni\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGlobali\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELayout\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArea di lavoro\u003C/span\u003E e scegliere \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECadCraft classico\u003C/span\u003E. Ogni barra degli strumenti, scheda e pannello torna dov&#039;era.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESu un telefono, o in una finestra larga 760 pixel o meno, l&#039;app usa il layout per telefono qualunque cosa sia stata scelta. Una finestra più larga ripristina il layout scelto.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPer provare un&#039;altra disposizione, vedere \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/layout-toolbox\" data-topic=\"basics/layout-toolbox\"\u003Eil layout con casella degli strumenti e riquadro informazioni\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Barra multifunzione e browser di progetto è uno dei tre layout dell'area di lavoro dell'app. Una barra multifunzione a schede corre in alto, Proprietà e un browser di progetto condividono il pannello sinistro, ogni vista aperta ha una scheda sopra il disegno e in basso c'è una barra di stato. Gli strumenti sono quelli dell'app: tutto ciò che si può fare nel layout classico funziona anche qui, e il disegno, lo stile dei menu e i tasti restano invariati. Sceglierlo Aprire Impostazioni (l'ingranaggio, oppure Ctrl+,). Scegliere la scheda Globali, poi andare a Layout › Area di lavoro. Scegliere Barra multifunzione e browser di progetto. Il layout cambia subito. La scelta vale per tutte le app in questo browser: da quel momento ogni app si apre con esso, ed è già attivo prima che la pagina venga disegnata, quindi il layout classico non compare mai per primo. Che cosa c'è dove Barra del titolo: il nome dell'app, i pulsanti di accesso rapido (apri, salva, annulla, ripristina), il nome del file aperto, Cosa fa ogni strumento qui (un elenco di tutti gli strumenti con una casella di ricerca, che indica quali sono pronti ora e perché gli altri non lo sono), schermo intero, Guida e Impostazioni. In questo layout la barra del titolo prende il posto della barra dell'app. Barra multifunzione: gli strumenti dell'app in schede, ciascuna in gruppi. File a sinistra apre, salva e così via. Alcune app aggiungono una scheda propria mentre si lavora in una particolare modalità o area di lavoro; è accesa finché vale e poi scompare. Barra delle opzioni: sotto la barra multifunzione, ciò che lo strumento in uso richiede, oppure ciò che è selezionato. Pannello sinistro: Proprietà sopra e, sotto, le palette (il browser di progetto e il contenuto proprio del pannello) come schede. Trascinare la linea tra le due per ripartire lo spazio. Schede delle viste: una scheda per ogni vista aperta (una tavola, una pianta, una scena…). Si chiude con il suo pulsante di chiusura; il file resta aperto. Barra della vista e barra di stato: sotto il disegno, i controlli propri della vista (scala, zoom); in basso, i messaggi dell'app. Uno strumento che al momento non può essere eseguito è in grigio e la sua descrizione ne spiega il motivo (per esempio \"Aprire prima un file\", oppure che l'app non ha ancora quello strumento). Fare clic su di esso, o premere Enter, per leggere il motivo in una finestra. Usare la barra multifunzione dalla tastiera Con una scheda della barra multifunzione attiva, ← e → passano da una scheda all'altra e Home e End vanno alla prima e all'ultima. Esc termina prima lo strumento in uso, come nel layout classico, e tutte le scorciatoie e Ctrl+Shift+F (modalità schermo) funzionano come prima. Comprimere la barra multifunzione o darle una finestra propria Fare doppio clic su una scheda della barra multifunzione per ridurla alle sole schede; un clic su una scheda ne mostra poi gli strumenti finché non ci si sposta. Fare di nuovo doppio clic per tenerla aperta. Anche il pulsante Sposta la barra multifunzione, all'estremità destra della barra, contiene Comprimi la barra multifunzione e Mantieni aperta la barra multifunzione. Ogni app lo ricorda. Sposta la barra multifunzione › Sposta in una nuova finestra mette la barra multifunzione in una finestra propria, per esempio su un secondo monitor. I tasti premuti lì raggiungono l'app. Riporta la barra multifunzione al suo posto, o la chiusura di quella finestra, la riporta nella finestra principale. Le impostazioni della barra degli strumenti valgono per la barra multifunzione: Impostazioni › Globali › Barra degli strumenti › Icone ed etichette, Solo icone (la barra multifunzione mostra allora due righe di piccole icone per gruppo) oppure Solo etichette, e Mostra i nomi dei gruppi (File, Disegna, …). I pannelli restano i propri I pannelli sinistro e destro mantengono tutto ciò che è descritto in Disporre i pannelli: ancorare, rendere mobili, fissare, nascondere, schede, Sposta in una nuova finestra e Reimposta layout. Tornare al layout classico Aprire Impostazioni › Globali › Layout › Area di lavoro e scegliere CadCraft classico. Ogni barra degli strumenti, scheda e pannello torna dov'era. Su un telefono, o in una finestra larga 760 pixel o meno, l'app usa il layout per telefono qualunque cosa sia stata scelta. Una finestra più larga ripristina il layout scelto. Per provare un'altra disposizione, vedere il layout con casella degli strumenti e riquadro informazioni.","lang":"it"},{"slug":"layout-toolbox","scope":"basics","title":"Lavorare nel layout con casella degli strumenti e riquadro informazioni","summary":"Passare l'app a una colonna di strumenti a sinistra con posti per i propri, un riquadro informazioni in alto che segue lo strumento in uso o ciò che si è scelto, un navigatore a destra, una scheda per ogni vista, opzioni rapide e una palette di modifica, e tornare al layout classico quando si vuole.","section":"start","order":37,"keywords":["layout","area di lavoro","casella degli strumenti","colonna degli strumenti","riquadro informazioni","barra informazioni","navigatore","mappa del progetto","barra rapida","opzioni rapide","palette di modifica","palette accanto al puntatore","strumenti personali","strumenti preferiti","schede delle viste","finestra della casella degli strumenti","layout classico","cambiare layout","passare a un altro layout"],"headings":[{"id":"sceglierlo","text":"Sceglierlo","level":2},{"id":"che-cosa-c-dove","text":"Che cosa c'è dove","level":2},{"id":"dare-alla-casella-degli-strumenti-una-finestra-propria","text":"Dare alla casella degli strumenti una finestra propria","level":2},{"id":"i-pannelli-restano-i-propri","text":"I pannelli restano i propri","level":2},{"id":"tornare-al-layout-classico","text":"Tornare al layout classico","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECasella degli strumenti e riquadro informazioni\u003C/strong\u003E è uno dei tre layout dell&#039;area di lavoro dell&#039;app. Gli strumenti di disegno stanno in una colonna a sinistra, un riquadro informazioni in alto mostra ciò che lo strumento in uso richiede o ciò che è selezionato, ogni vista ha una scheda e una barra rapida sotto il disegno contiene alcune opzioni di uso quotidiano. Gli strumenti sono quelli dell&#039;app: tutto ciò che si può fare nel layout classico funziona anche qui, e il disegno, lo stile dei menu e i tasti restano invariati.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"sceglierlo\"\u003ESceglierlo\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAprire \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EImpostazioni\u003C/span\u003E (l&#039;ingranaggio, oppure \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command ,\"\u003E⌘,\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+,\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EScegliere la scheda \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGlobali\u003C/span\u003E, poi andare a \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELayout\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArea di lavoro\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EScegliere \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECasella degli strumenti e riquadro informazioni\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIl layout cambia subito. La scelta vale per tutte le app in questo browser: da quel momento ogni app si apre con esso, ed è già attivo prima che la pagina venga disegnata, quindi il layout classico non compare mai per primo.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"che-cosa-c-dove\"\u003EChe cosa c&#039;è dove\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EBarra degli strumenti:\u003C/strong\u003E la barra degli strumenti dell&#039;app resta in alto nell&#039;area di disegno, con i suoi menu.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERiquadro informazioni:\u003C/strong\u003E sotto la barra degli strumenti. Mentre uno strumento è in esecuzione mostra lo strumento e ciò che richiede. Quando si sceglie un elemento ne mostra le impostazioni principali (per un muro: tipo, spessore, lunghezza, linea di posizione e altezza), che si possono modificare direttamente lì: una modifica lì equivale a una nelle Proprietà e \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E la annulla. Senza alcuna selezione riporta &quot;Nessuna selezione&quot;. Ogni app aggiunge qui i propri riquadri, come il layer o il livello corrente, oppure il documento aperto.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECasella degli strumenti:\u003C/strong\u003E la colonna a sinistra, con gli strumenti dell&#039;app in gruppi. Fare clic sul nome di un gruppo per comprimerlo o riaprirlo. Lo strumento in esecuzione è acceso.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EStrumenti personali:\u003C/strong\u003E il primo gruppo della casella degli strumenti, otto posti vuoti per gli strumenti usati più spesso. Fare clic su un posto vuoto per scegliere uno qualsiasi degli strumenti o dei comandi dell&#039;app; oppure fare clic con il pulsante destro su uno strumento nella casella e scegliere \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAggiungi a Strumenti personali\u003C/span\u003E; oppure trascinare uno strumento su un posto. Fare clic con il pulsante destro su un posto per cambiarlo o svuotarlo. I posti vengono conservati per ogni app in questo browser.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENavigatore:\u003C/strong\u003E il pannello destro. In alto c&#039;è il browser di progetto dell&#039;app (piante, sezioni, prospetti, viste 3D, abachi, tavole, secondo ciò che l&#039;app prevede): fare clic su uno per aprirlo. Sotto, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProprietà\u003C/span\u003E e una scheda con gli altri contenuti del pannello destro.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESchede delle viste:\u003C/strong\u003E una scheda per ogni vista del file aperto (una tavola, una pianta, una scena…), sopra il disegno.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EBarra rapida:\u003C/strong\u003E sotto il disegno, le impostazioni della vista e le opzioni di uso quotidiano dell&#039;app, come la scala, il livello di dettaglio, l&#039;intervallo della vista, adatta, pianta, divisione o 3D, snap e unità.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPalette di modifica:\u003C/strong\u003E facendo clic su un elemento del disegno compare accanto al puntatore una piccola palette con i comandi di modifica dell&#039;app per quell&#039;elemento, come sposta, copia, ruota, specchio, duplica o elimina, e \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProprietà\u003C/span\u003E. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, la rotellina del mouse, un altro clic o il suo pulsante di chiusura la fanno sparire.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUno strumento che al momento non può essere eseguito è in grigio e la sua descrizione ne spiega il motivo. Fare clic su di esso, o premere \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, per leggere il motivo in una finestra.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E termina prima lo strumento in uso, come nel layout classico, e tutte le scorciatoie e \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control Shift F\"\u003E⌃⇧F\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+F\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E (modalità schermo) funzionano come prima.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"dare-alla-casella-degli-strumenti-una-finestra-propria\"\u003EDare alla casella degli strumenti una finestra propria\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIl pulsante \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESposta la casella degli strumenti\u003C/span\u003E in alto nella casella contiene \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESposta in una nuova finestra\u003C/span\u003E: la casella degli strumenti si apre in una stretta finestra propria, da affiancare all&#039;app o da mettere su un secondo monitor. I tasti premuti lì raggiungono l&#039;app. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERiporta la casella degli strumenti al suo posto\u003C/span\u003E, o la chiusura di quella finestra, la riporta nella finestra principale.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ELe impostazioni della barra degli strumenti valgono anche per la casella: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EImpostazioni\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGlobali\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBarra degli strumenti\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EIcone ed etichette\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESolo icone\u003C/span\u003E oppure \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESolo etichette\u003C/span\u003E, e \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMostra i nomi dei gruppi (File, Disegna, …)\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"i-pannelli-restano-i-propri\"\u003EI pannelli restano i propri\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EI pannelli sinistro e destro mantengono tutto ciò che è descritto in \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/arrange-panels\" data-topic=\"basics/arrange-panels\"\u003EDisporre i pannelli\u003C/a\u003E: ancorare, rendere mobili, fissare, nascondere, schede, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESposta in una nuova finestra\u003C/span\u003E e \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReimposta layout\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"tornare-al-layout-classico\"\u003ETornare al layout classico\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAprire \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EImpostazioni\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGlobali\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELayout\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArea di lavoro\u003C/span\u003E e scegliere \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECadCraft classico\u003C/span\u003E. Ogni barra degli strumenti, scheda e pannello torna dov&#039;era.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESu un telefono, o in una finestra larga 760 pixel o meno, l&#039;app usa il layout per telefono qualunque cosa sia stata scelta. Una finestra più larga ripristina il layout scelto.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPer provare un&#039;altra disposizione, vedere \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/layout-ribbon\" data-topic=\"basics/layout-ribbon\"\u003Eil layout con barra multifunzione e browser di progetto\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Casella degli strumenti e riquadro informazioni è uno dei tre layout dell'area di lavoro dell'app. Gli strumenti di disegno stanno in una colonna a sinistra, un riquadro informazioni in alto mostra ciò che lo strumento in uso richiede o ciò che è selezionato, ogni vista ha una scheda e una barra rapida sotto il disegno contiene alcune opzioni di uso quotidiano. Gli strumenti sono quelli dell'app: tutto ciò che si può fare nel layout classico funziona anche qui, e il disegno, lo stile dei menu e i tasti restano invariati. Sceglierlo Aprire Impostazioni (l'ingranaggio, oppure Ctrl+,). Scegliere la scheda Globali, poi andare a Layout › Area di lavoro. Scegliere Casella degli strumenti e riquadro informazioni. Il layout cambia subito. La scelta vale per tutte le app in questo browser: da quel momento ogni app si apre con esso, ed è già attivo prima che la pagina venga disegnata, quindi il layout classico non compare mai per primo. Che cosa c'è dove Barra degli strumenti: la barra degli strumenti dell'app resta in alto nell'area di disegno, con i suoi menu. Riquadro informazioni: sotto la barra degli strumenti. Mentre uno strumento è in esecuzione mostra lo strumento e ciò che richiede. Quando si sceglie un elemento ne mostra le impostazioni principali (per un muro: tipo, spessore, lunghezza, linea di posizione e altezza), che si possono modificare direttamente lì: una modifica lì equivale a una nelle Proprietà e Ctrl+Z la annulla. Senza alcuna selezione riporta \"Nessuna selezione\". Ogni app aggiunge qui i propri riquadri, come il layer o il livello corrente, oppure il documento aperto. Casella degli strumenti: la colonna a sinistra, con gli strumenti dell'app in gruppi. Fare clic sul nome di un gruppo per comprimerlo o riaprirlo. Lo strumento in esecuzione è acceso. Strumenti personali: il primo gruppo della casella degli strumenti, otto posti vuoti per gli strumenti usati più spesso. Fare clic su un posto vuoto per scegliere uno qualsiasi degli strumenti o dei comandi dell'app; oppure fare clic con il pulsante destro su uno strumento nella casella e scegliere Aggiungi a Strumenti personali; oppure trascinare uno strumento su un posto. Fare clic con il pulsante destro su un posto per cambiarlo o svuotarlo. I posti vengono conservati per ogni app in questo browser. Navigatore: il pannello destro. In alto c'è il browser di progetto dell'app (piante, sezioni, prospetti, viste 3D, abachi, tavole, secondo ciò che l'app prevede): fare clic su uno per aprirlo. Sotto, Proprietà e una scheda con gli altri contenuti del pannello destro. Schede delle viste: una scheda per ogni vista del file aperto (una tavola, una pianta, una scena…), sopra il disegno. Barra rapida: sotto il disegno, le impostazioni della vista e le opzioni di uso quotidiano dell'app, come la scala, il livello di dettaglio, l'intervallo della vista, adatta, pianta, divisione o 3D, snap e unità. Palette di modifica: facendo clic su un elemento del disegno compare accanto al puntatore una piccola palette con i comandi di modifica dell'app per quell'elemento, come sposta, copia, ruota, specchio, duplica o elimina, e Proprietà. Esc, la rotellina del mouse, un altro clic o il suo pulsante di chiusura la fanno sparire. Uno strumento che al momento non può essere eseguito è in grigio e la sua descrizione ne spiega il motivo. Fare clic su di esso, o premere Enter, per leggere il motivo in una finestra. Esc termina prima lo strumento in uso, come nel layout classico, e tutte le scorciatoie e Ctrl+Shift+F (modalità schermo) funzionano come prima. Dare alla casella degli strumenti una finestra propria Il pulsante Sposta la casella degli strumenti in alto nella casella contiene Sposta in una nuova finestra: la casella degli strumenti si apre in una stretta finestra propria, da affiancare all'app o da mettere su un secondo monitor. I tasti premuti lì raggiungono l'app. Riporta la casella degli strumenti al suo posto, o la chiusura di quella finestra, la riporta nella finestra principale. Le impostazioni della barra degli strumenti valgono anche per la casella: Impostazioni › Globali › Barra degli strumenti › Icone ed etichette, Solo icone oppure Solo etichette, e Mostra i nomi dei gruppi (File, Disegna, …). I pannelli restano i propri I pannelli sinistro e destro mantengono tutto ciò che è descritto in Disporre i pannelli: ancorare, rendere mobili, fissare, nascondere, schede, Sposta in una nuova finestra e Reimposta layout. Tornare al layout classico Aprire Impostazioni › Globali › Layout › Area di lavoro e scegliere CadCraft classico. Ogni barra degli strumenti, scheda e pannello torna dov'era. Su un telefono, o in una finestra larga 760 pixel o meno, l'app usa il layout per telefono qualunque cosa sia stata scelta. Una finestra più larga ripristina il layout scelto. Per provare un'altra disposizione, vedere il layout con barra multifunzione e browser di progetto.","lang":"it"},{"slug":"layout-own","scope":"basics","title":"Disporre il proprio layout e salvare le configurazioni","summary":"Scegliere quale pannello mostra il browser di progetto, le Proprietà o il contenuto proprio del pannello, dove va la casella degli strumenti e che cosa contengono le barre, salvarlo come proprio layout e conservare intere configurazioni con un nome per passare dall'una all'altra.","section":"start","order":39,"keywords":["layout","area di lavoro","disporre","personalizzare","il mio layout","layout proprio","pannelli","editor","browser di progetto","proprietà","casella degli strumenti","barra rapida","riquadro informazioni","ambiente di lavoro","configurazione","profilo","salvare configurazione","cambiare configurazione","esportare configurazione","importare configurazione","ccenv"],"headings":[{"id":"disporre-il-proprio-layout","text":"Disporre il proprio layout","level":2},{"id":"salvare-la-configurazione-come-ambiente-di-lavoro","text":"Salvare la configurazione come ambiente di lavoro","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EOltre ai tre layout predefiniti è possibile disporne uno proprio e salvare l&#039;intera configurazione con un nome, per tornarvi con un solo passaggio.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"disporre-il-proprio-layout\"\u003EDisporre il proprio layout\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAprire \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EImpostazioni\u003C/span\u003E (l&#039;ingranaggio, oppure \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command ,\"\u003E⌘,\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+,\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), scegliere \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGlobali\u003C/span\u003E, poi \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELayout\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EScegliere \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDisponi il proprio layout…\u003C/span\u003E. La finestra parte dal layout in uso.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAssegnargli un nome, poi scegliere:\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESopra il disegno:\u003C/strong\u003E la barra degli strumenti dell&#039;app, una barra multifunzione o nessuna; una barra del titolo; un riquadro informazioni e i controlli che contiene.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAccanto al disegno:\u003C/strong\u003E la casella degli strumenti a sinistra, a destra o assente, con o senza \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStrumenti personali\u003C/span\u003E; quale pannello contiene le palette, quale sta in cima (il browser di progetto o le Proprietà, per esempio) e quali sono schede sotto di esso.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EL&#039;area di disegno:\u003C/strong\u003E schede delle viste, una barra rapida e una barra della vista con i controlli selezionati, la palette di modifica, una barra di stato.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAspetto:\u003C/strong\u003E dimensione, contorni dei pulsanti e stile delle schede.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EScegliere \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUsa questo layout\u003C/span\u003E. L&#039;app passa subito al nuovo layout, e lo usa anche ogni altra app che prevede layout.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EVengono proposte solo le parti presenti in questa app. Il layout è elencato sotto \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDa add-on e file\u003C/span\u003E; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEsporta questo layout\u003C/span\u003E lo salva come file da dare a un collega e il pulsante di rimozione lo elimina. I pannelli che riempie si possono ancora ancorare, rendere mobili e spostare in finestre separate, come in \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/arrange-panels\" data-topic=\"basics/arrange-panels\"\u003EDisporre i pannelli\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"salvare-la-configurazione-come-ambiente-di-lavoro\"\u003ESalvare la configurazione come ambiente di lavoro\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUn ambiente di lavoro è l&#039;intera configurazione per ogni app in questo browser: il layout, lo stile dei menu e i tasti, lo stile della barra degli strumenti, i pannelli e le loro dimensioni, e \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStrumenti personali\u003C/span\u003E. Tema e lingua non ne fanno parte.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESalvare:\u003C/strong\u003E digitare un nome sotto \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAmbienti di lavoro\u003C/span\u003E e scegliere \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESalva la mia configurazione\u003C/span\u003E. Salvando con un nome già esistente, quella configurazione viene sostituita.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPassare a un&#039;altra:\u003C/strong\u003E scegliere una configurazione nell&#039;elenco e poi \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPassa ad essa\u003C/span\u003E. L&#039;app si ricarica con quella configurazione; in presenza di lavoro non salvato, l&#039;app chiede prima conferma, come a ogni ricaricamento.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECondividere:\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEsporta\u003C/span\u003E salva la configurazione scelta come file \u003Ccode\u003E.ccenv.json\u003C/code\u003E; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EImporta una configurazione…\u003C/span\u003E ne aggiunge una da un file. Un file imposta soltanto queste scelte, nient&#039;altro.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERimuovere:\u003C/strong\u003E scegliere una configurazione e poi \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERimuovi\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EÈ possibile conservare fino a dodici configurazioni.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPer provare prima le disposizioni predefinite, vedere \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/layout-toolbox\" data-topic=\"basics/layout-toolbox\"\u003Eil layout con casella degli strumenti e riquadro informazioni\u003C/a\u003E e \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/layout-ribbon\" data-topic=\"basics/layout-ribbon\"\u003Eil layout con barra multifunzione e browser di progetto\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Oltre ai tre layout predefiniti è possibile disporne uno proprio e salvare l'intera configurazione con un nome, per tornarvi con un solo passaggio. Disporre il proprio layout Aprire Impostazioni (l'ingranaggio, oppure Ctrl+,), scegliere Globali, poi Layout. Scegliere Disponi il proprio layout…. La finestra parte dal layout in uso. Assegnargli un nome, poi scegliere: Sopra il disegno: la barra degli strumenti dell'app, una barra multifunzione o nessuna; una barra del titolo; un riquadro informazioni e i controlli che contiene. Accanto al disegno: la casella degli strumenti a sinistra, a destra o assente, con o senza Strumenti personali; quale pannello contiene le palette, quale sta in cima (il browser di progetto o le Proprietà, per esempio) e quali sono schede sotto di esso. L'area di disegno: schede delle viste, una barra rapida e una barra della vista con i controlli selezionati, la palette di modifica, una barra di stato. Aspetto: dimensione, contorni dei pulsanti e stile delle schede. Scegliere Usa questo layout. L'app passa subito al nuovo layout, e lo usa anche ogni altra app che prevede layout. Vengono proposte solo le parti presenti in questa app. Il layout è elencato sotto Da add-on e file; Esporta questo layout lo salva come file da dare a un collega e il pulsante di rimozione lo elimina. I pannelli che riempie si possono ancora ancorare, rendere mobili e spostare in finestre separate, come in Disporre i pannelli. Salvare la configurazione come ambiente di lavoro Un ambiente di lavoro è l'intera configurazione per ogni app in questo browser: il layout, lo stile dei menu e i tasti, lo stile della barra degli strumenti, i pannelli e le loro dimensioni, e Strumenti personali. Tema e lingua non ne fanno parte. Salvare: digitare un nome sotto Ambienti di lavoro e scegliere Salva la mia configurazione. Salvando con un nome già esistente, quella configurazione viene sostituita. Passare a un'altra: scegliere una configurazione nell'elenco e poi Passa ad essa. L'app si ricarica con quella configurazione; in presenza di lavoro non salvato, l'app chiede prima conferma, come a ogni ricaricamento. Condividere: Esporta salva la configurazione scelta come file .ccenv.json; Importa una configurazione… ne aggiunge una da un file. Un file imposta soltanto queste scelte, nient'altro. Rimuovere: scegliere una configurazione e poi Rimuovi. È possibile conservare fino a dodici configurazioni. Per provare prima le disposizioni predefinite, vedere il layout con casella degli strumenti e riquadro informazioni e il layout con barra multifunzione e browser di progetto.","lang":"it"},{"slug":"addons-basics","scope":"basics","title":"Add-ons and your account list","summary":"How add-ons work in every app and edition. Add one once in the Library and it follows your account, loads the first time an app needs it, and updates inside its major version. Sign in on a computer, pin a version, or keep working offline.","section":"start","order":40,"keywords":["add-ons","library","add","remove","update","pin","lab channel","your add-ons","sign in","offline","this file uses","engine","needs","orphan","team add-ons"],"headings":[{"id":"what-an-add-on-is","text":"What an add-on is","level":2},{"id":"your-list","text":"Your list","level":2},{"id":"needs","text":"Needs","level":2},{"id":"files-that-use-add-ons","text":"Files that use add-ons","level":2},{"id":"offline","text":"Offline","level":2},{"id":"ipad-and-iphone","text":"iPad and iPhone","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-an-add-on-is\"\u003EWhat an add-on is\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAn add-on adds features (a new tool or panel) or data (regulation packs, materials, trade packs) to an app. You add it once in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELibrary\u003C/span\u003E and it is on your account list, so it follows you to every edition: the browser, Studio, the portable build and the apps on a tablet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"your-list\"\u003EYour list\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOpen \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd-ons\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EYour add-ons\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESign in\u003C/strong\u003E connects this app to your account. Signed out, the app keeps a list for this device only, and offers to merge it into your account when you sign in. In Studio the sign-in shows a short code to approve in your browser.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERemove\u003C/strong\u003E takes an add-on off the list. It is refused while another add-on you keep needs it, and the screen names that add-on. When you remove something, anything that was brought in only for it is taken off too, and the screen says which.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECheck for updates\u003C/strong\u003E lists every change together. Updates inside the same major version arrive by themselves. A new major version, a changed permission, a pinned version or a lab version always asks you first, and an update is all or nothing: if one file fails its check, nothing changes.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EKeep a version\u003C/strong\u003E pins it. \u003Cstrong\u003EFollow newest\u003C/strong\u003E unpins it. \u003Cstrong\u003ETry lab versions\u003C/strong\u003E moves one add-on to the lab channel.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERoll back\u003C/strong\u003E returns to the version you had.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"needs\"\u003ENeeds\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAn add-on can need other add-ons or a shared engine. Add brings them along and tells you what it also adds, how big it is and which permissions it asks for. Engines are shared: one copy serves everything that needs it, and a copy nothing needs is freed after 30 days.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"files-that-use-add-ons\"\u003EFiles that use add-ons\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA drawing remembers the add-ons whose data it holds. Open it on a computer that does not have them and the app says &quot;This file uses ...&quot; and offers to add them. Until you do, the data stays in the file untouched.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"offline\"\u003EOffline\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDownload everything for offline\u003C/span\u003E fetches every add-on on your list. Once fetched, Studio and the portable build work with no connection. Each download is checked against its checksum and its signature before it is used.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"ipad-and-iphone\"\u003EiPad and iPhone\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAdd-ons that are only data download as usual. Code add-ons made by CadCraft are already inside the app and are switched on from your list. Code add-ons from outside makers are not offered on the iPad and iPhone apps; the browser app (Add to Home Screen) has them.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"What an add-on is An add-on adds features (a new tool or panel) or data (regulation packs, materials, trade packs) to an app. You add it once in the Library and it is on your account list, so it follows you to every edition: the browser, Studio, the portable build and the apps on a tablet. Your list Open Settings › Add-ons › Your add-ons. Sign in connects this app to your account. Signed out, the app keeps a list for this device only, and offers to merge it into your account when you sign in. In Studio the sign-in shows a short code to approve in your browser. Remove takes an add-on off the list. It is refused while another add-on you keep needs it, and the screen names that add-on. When you remove something, anything that was brought in only for it is taken off too, and the screen says which. Check for updates lists every change together. Updates inside the same major version arrive by themselves. A new major version, a changed permission, a pinned version or a lab version always asks you first, and an update is all or nothing: if one file fails its check, nothing changes. Keep a version pins it. Follow newest unpins it. Try lab versions moves one add-on to the lab channel. Roll back returns to the version you had. Needs An add-on can need other add-ons or a shared engine. Add brings them along and tells you what it also adds, how big it is and which permissions it asks for. Engines are shared: one copy serves everything that needs it, and a copy nothing needs is freed after 30 days. Files that use add-ons A drawing remembers the add-ons whose data it holds. Open it on a computer that does not have them and the app says \"This file uses ...\" and offers to add them. Until you do, the data stays in the file untouched. Offline Download everything for offline fetches every add-on on your list. Once fetched, Studio and the portable build work with no connection. Each download is checked against its checksum and its signature before it is used. iPad and iPhone Add-ons that are only data download as usual. Code add-ons made by CadCraft are already inside the app and are switched on from your list. Code add-ons from outside makers are not offered on the iPad and iPhone apps; the browser app (Add to Home Screen) has them.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"offline","scope":"basics","title":"Lavorare offline","summary":"Salvare un'app per l'uso offline, che cosa funziona senza connessione e che cosa ne ha bisogno.","section":"start","order":40,"keywords":["offline","senza internet","modalità aereo","sopralluogo","cache","service worker","installare","scaricare","connessione"],"headings":[{"id":"salvare-un-app-per-l-uso-offline","text":"Salvare un'app per l'uso offline","level":2},{"id":"che-cosa-funziona-offline","text":"Che cosa funziona offline","level":2},{"id":"che-cosa-richiede-una-connessione","text":"Che cosa richiede una connessione","level":2},{"id":"da-sapere","text":"Da sapere","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003ELe app svolgono il lavoro sul computer, quindi quasi tutto funziona senza connessione. Al browser basta aver salvato prima i file dell&#039;app.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"salvare-un-app-per-l-uso-offline\"\u003ESalvare un&#039;app per l&#039;uso offline\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAprire l&#039;app quando si è online.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EScegliere \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESalva per l&#039;uso offline\u003C/span\u003E nella barra in alto.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAttendere &quot;Pronto per l&#039;uso offline&quot;. I file dell&#039;app, i suoi lettori di file e questa guida sono ora salvati nel browser. Le app che leggono file DWG o PDF salvano anche i relativi lettori, quindi la prima volta può occorrere un minuto.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFarlo una volta per app, in ogni browser usato. Per tornare all&#039;app più tardi senza connessione, aprire lo stesso indirizzo usato prima, oppure un suo segnalibro.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"net:offline\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EOffline\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAl momento si è offline. Se questa app è stata salvata per l&#039;uso offline, tutto ciò che segue funziona. In caso contrario, è ancora possibile usare ciò che è già caricato in questa scheda, ma senza ricaricarla finché non si è di nuovo online.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"che-cosa-funziona-offline\"\u003EChe cosa funziona offline\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAprire file dal computer, disegnare, modificare, misurare e verificare.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESalvare ed esportare: ogni salvataggio è un file scaricato sul computer.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EQuesta guida, ricerca compresa.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"che-cosa-richiede-una-connessione\"\u003EChe cosa richiede una connessione\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAprire l&#039;app per la prima volta e salvarla per l&#039;uso offline.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOttenere una versione più recente dell&#039;app. Quando si è online, l&#039;app preleva la versione più recente alla successiva apertura e \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESalva per l&#039;uso offline\u003C/span\u003E aggiorna la copia salvata.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EI collegamenti al sito web, come \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EApri sul sito web\u003C/span\u003E, e i collegamenti ad altre pagine.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIl salvataggio nel cloud e la sincronizzazione tra computer, non ancora disponibili.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"da-sapere\"\u003EDa sapere\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUna copia salvata appartiene a un solo browser su un solo computer. Cancellando i dati dei siti del browser viene rimossa.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELe finestre private di alcuni browser e alcuni computer aziendali con restrizioni non consentono il salvataggio offline. Il pulsante segnala quando non può salvare.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:safari\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003ESafari\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESafari rimuove i dati salvati dei siti non visitati da diverse settimane. Aprire l&#039;app ogni tanto quando si è online, oppure salvarla di nuovo prima di un viaggio.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003ECadCraft Studio, l&#039;app desktop con tutte le otto app in un&#039;unica installazione, è su getcadcraft.com/download.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"Le app svolgono il lavoro sul computer, quindi quasi tutto funziona senza connessione. Al browser basta aver salvato prima i file dell'app. Salvare un'app per l'uso offline Aprire l'app quando si è online. Scegliere Salva per l'uso offline nella barra in alto. Attendere \"Pronto per l'uso offline\". I file dell'app, i suoi lettori di file e questa guida sono ora salvati nel browser. Le app che leggono file DWG o PDF salvano anche i relativi lettori, quindi la prima volta può occorrere un minuto. Farlo una volta per app, in ogni browser usato. Per tornare all'app più tardi senza connessione, aprire lo stesso indirizzo usato prima, oppure un suo segnalibro. Al momento si è offline. Se questa app è stata salvata per l'uso offline, tutto ciò che segue funziona. In caso contrario, è ancora possibile usare ciò che è già caricato in questa scheda, ma senza ricaricarla finché non si è di nuovo online. Che cosa funziona offline Aprire file dal computer, disegnare, modificare, misurare e verificare. Salvare ed esportare: ogni salvataggio è un file scaricato sul computer. Questa guida, ricerca compresa. Che cosa richiede una connessione Aprire l'app per la prima volta e salvarla per l'uso offline. Ottenere una versione più recente dell'app. Quando si è online, l'app preleva la versione più recente alla successiva apertura e Salva per l'uso offline aggiorna la copia salvata. I collegamenti al sito web, come Apri sul sito web, e i collegamenti ad altre pagine. Il salvataggio nel cloud e la sincronizzazione tra computer, non ancora disponibili. Da sapere Una copia salvata appartiene a un solo browser su un solo computer. Cancellando i dati dei siti del browser viene rimossa. Le finestre private di alcuni browser e alcuni computer aziendali con restrizioni non consentono il salvataggio offline. Il pulsante segnala quando non può salvare. Safari rimuove i dati salvati dei siti non visitati da diverse settimane. Aprire l'app ogni tanto quando si è online, oppure salvarla di nuovo prima di un viaggio. CadCraft Studio, l'app desktop con tutte le otto app in un'unica installazione, è su getcadcraft.com/download.","lang":"it"},{"slug":"your-files","scope":"basics","title":"Dove vanno i file","summary":"I file restano sul proprio computer, come funziona il salvataggio e che cosa ricorda il browser.","section":"start","order":50,"keywords":["privacy","caricamento","cloud","salvataggio","scaricamento","archiviazione","locale","sicurezza","riservato"],"headings":[{"id":"i-file-restano-sul-proprio-computer","text":"I file restano sul proprio computer","level":2},{"id":"come-funziona-il-salvataggio","text":"Come funziona il salvataggio","level":2},{"id":"che-cosa-ricorda-il-browser","text":"Che cosa ricorda il browser","level":2},{"id":"salvataggio-nel-cloud","text":"Salvataggio nel cloud","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"i-file-restano-sul-proprio-computer\"\u003EI file restano sul proprio computer\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EQuando si apre un disegno, un modello o un PDF, l&#039;app lo legge all&#039;interno del browser. \u003Cstrong\u003EIl file non viene caricato.\u003C/strong\u003E Non lo vediamo mai e funziona allo stesso modo senza connessione.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"come-funziona-il-salvataggio\"\u003ECome funziona il salvataggio\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUna pagina del browser non può sovrascrivere da sola i file del computer, quindi le app salvano \u003Cstrong\u003Escaricando\u003C/strong\u003E un nuovo file:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIl file scaricato va dove il browser mette i download, di solito la cartella Download, a meno che il browser non sia impostato per chiedere ogni volta (vedere \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/browsers#what-differs-between-browsers\" data-topic=\"basics/browsers\" data-hash=\"what-differs-between-browsers\"\u003EBrowser e computer\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELe app che modificano un file ne salvano una copia con un nuovo nome, come &quot;(modificato)&quot; o &quot;(annotato)&quot;, così l&#039;originale resta intatto.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESe si chiude la scheda con lavoro non salvato, il browser chiede prima conferma (&quot;Uscire dal sito?&quot;).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"che-cosa-ricorda-il-browser\"\u003EChe cosa ricorda il browser\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAlcune app ricordano piccole impostazioni nel browser, come il nome dell&#039;autore o gli stili degli strumenti. Restano in quel browser su quel computer e vengono rimosse cancellando i dati dei siti. La guida di ogni app indica che cosa conserva.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"salvataggio-nel-cloud\"\u003ESalvataggio nel cloud\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIl salvataggio nel cloud e la sincronizzazione tra computer fanno parte della licenza commerciale. Non sono ancora disponibili. Nel frattempo, conservare i file dove si tengono gli altri file di progetto ed eseguirne il backup nel modo consueto.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"I file restano sul proprio computer Quando si apre un disegno, un modello o un PDF, l'app lo legge all'interno del browser. Il file non viene caricato. Non lo vediamo mai e funziona allo stesso modo senza connessione. Come funziona il salvataggio Una pagina del browser non può sovrascrivere da sola i file del computer, quindi le app salvano scaricando un nuovo file: Il file scaricato va dove il browser mette i download, di solito la cartella Download, a meno che il browser non sia impostato per chiedere ogni volta (vedere Browser e computer). Le app che modificano un file ne salvano una copia con un nuovo nome, come \"(modificato)\" o \"(annotato)\", così l'originale resta intatto. Se si chiude la scheda con lavoro non salvato, il browser chiede prima conferma (\"Uscire dal sito?\"). Che cosa ricorda il browser Alcune app ricordano piccole impostazioni nel browser, come il nome dell'autore o gli stili degli strumenti. Restano in quel browser su quel computer e vengono rimosse cancellando i dati dei siti. La guida di ogni app indica che cosa conserva. Salvataggio nel cloud Il salvataggio nel cloud e la sincronizzazione tra computer fanno parte della licenza commerciale. Non sono ancora disponibili. Nel frattempo, conservare i file dove si tengono gli altri file di progetto ed eseguirne il backup nel modo consueto.","lang":"it"},{"slug":"connect-ai-windows","scope":"basics","title":"Connect your AI on Windows","summary":"Let your own AI app work with the CadCraft apps: one click in CadCraft Studio, or a short paste for each AI app. Includes the one-line test that proves it works.","section":"start","order":63,"keywords":["ai","assistant","connect","mcp","claude","cursor","vs code","windsurf","codex","gemini","set up","test","windows"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn","level":2},{"id":"the-easy-way","text":"The easy way","level":2},{"id":"prove-it-works","text":"Prove it works","level":2},{"id":"or-paste-it-yourself","text":"Or paste it yourself","level":2},{"id":"claude-desktop","text":"Claude Desktop","level":3},{"id":"claude-code","text":"Claude Code","level":3},{"id":"cursor","text":"Cursor","level":3},{"id":"windsurf","text":"Windsurf","level":3},{"id":"visual-studio-code","text":"Visual Studio Code","level":3},{"id":"codex-cli","text":"Codex CLI","level":3},{"id":"gemini-cli","text":"Gemini CLI","level":3},{"id":"browser-ai-and-phones","text":"Browser AI and phones","level":2},{"id":"if-it-does-not-work","text":"If it does not work","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\"\u003EWhat you will learn\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHow to let the AI app you already use (Claude, Cursor, Visual Studio Code, Windsurf, Codex, Gemini…) reach your CadCraft apps, how to check that it works in under a minute, and how to take it out again. The apps contain no AI; your AI app asks them to do things, and you decide what it may do. (For those who search for it: this uses the Model Context Protocol, &quot;MCP&quot;.)\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-easy-way\"\u003EThe easy way\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOpen \u003Cstrong\u003ECadCraft Studio › Connect your AI\u003C/strong\u003E. Studio looks for the AI apps on this computer, shows you in plain words what it would add to each one&#039;s settings, and writes it only after you press \u003Cstrong\u003EYes\u003C/strong\u003E. \u003Cstrong\u003EDisconnect\u003C/strong\u003E takes it out again. Nothing secret is written into the AI app&#039;s settings.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"prove-it-works\"\u003EProve it works\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn your AI app, paste this:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EUse the CadCraft tools: list the apps you can reach, then open the sample drawing in my CadCraft folder and tell me how many objects it has, by layer.\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIt should list the CadCraft apps it can reach and then give you a count by layer. It only reads, so it needs no extra permission beyond the \u003Cstrong\u003ERead\u003C/strong\u003E that is on to begin with. In Studio, \u003Cstrong\u003ECheck the connection\u003C/strong\u003E tests each step without the AI and says which one failed.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"or-paste-it-yourself\"\u003EOr paste it yourself\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOne card for each AI app. Each one adds a single entry named \u003Ccode\u003Ecadcraft\u003C/code\u003E and nothing else.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"claude-desktop\"\u003EClaude Desktop\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen %APPDATA%\\Claude\\claude_desktop_config.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAdd the CadCraft entry below (inside &quot;mcpServers&quot; if the file has one).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERestart Claude Desktop completely, then ask it the test prompt.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E{\n  &quot;mcpServers&quot;: {\n    &quot;cadcraft&quot;: {\n      &quot;command&quot;: &quot;npx&quot;,\n      &quot;args&quot;: [\n        &quot;-y&quot;,\n        &quot;@getcadcraft/mcp&quot;,\n        &quot;connect&quot;\n      ]\n    }\n  }\n}\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUndo:\u003C/strong\u003E Delete the &quot;cadcraft&quot; entry from %APPDATA%\\Claude\\claude_desktop_config.json.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"claude-code\"\u003EClaude Code\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen a terminal.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERun the command below.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EStart Claude Code and ask it the test prompt.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Eclaude mcp add cadcraft --scope user -- npx -y @getcadcraft/mcp connect\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUndo:\u003C/strong\u003E claude mcp remove cadcraft --scope user\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"cursor\"\u003ECursor\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen %USERPROFILE%\\.cursor\\mcp.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAdd the CadCraft entry below (inside &quot;mcpServers&quot; if the file has one).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EStart Cursor and ask it the test prompt.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E{\n  &quot;mcpServers&quot;: {\n    &quot;cadcraft&quot;: {\n      &quot;command&quot;: &quot;npx&quot;,\n      &quot;args&quot;: [\n        &quot;-y&quot;,\n        &quot;@getcadcraft/mcp&quot;,\n        &quot;connect&quot;\n      ]\n    }\n  }\n}\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUndo:\u003C/strong\u003E Delete the &quot;cadcraft&quot; entry from %USERPROFILE%\\.cursor\\mcp.json.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThis path comes from the AI app&#039;s public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio&#039;s Connect, which looks first.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"windsurf\"\u003EWindsurf\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen %USERPROFILE%\\.codeium\\windsurf\\mcp_config.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAdd the CadCraft entry below (inside &quot;mcpServers&quot; if the file has one).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EStart Windsurf and ask it the test prompt.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E{\n  &quot;mcpServers&quot;: {\n    &quot;cadcraft&quot;: {\n      &quot;command&quot;: &quot;npx&quot;,\n      &quot;args&quot;: [\n        &quot;-y&quot;,\n        &quot;@getcadcraft/mcp&quot;,\n        &quot;connect&quot;\n      ]\n    }\n  }\n}\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUndo:\u003C/strong\u003E Delete the &quot;cadcraft&quot; entry from %USERPROFILE%\\.codeium\\windsurf\\mcp_config.json.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThis path comes from the AI app&#039;s public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio&#039;s Connect, which looks first.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"visual-studio-code\"\u003EVisual Studio Code\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen %APPDATA%\\Code\\User\\mcp.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAdd the CadCraft entry below (inside &quot;servers&quot; if the file has one).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EStart Visual Studio Code and ask it the test prompt.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E{\n  &quot;servers&quot;: {\n    &quot;cadcraft&quot;: {\n      &quot;type&quot;: &quot;stdio&quot;,\n      &quot;command&quot;: &quot;npx&quot;,\n      &quot;args&quot;: [\n        &quot;-y&quot;,\n        &quot;@getcadcraft/mcp&quot;,\n        &quot;connect&quot;\n      ]\n    }\n  }\n}\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUndo:\u003C/strong\u003E Delete the &quot;cadcraft&quot; entry from %APPDATA%\\Code\\User\\mcp.json.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThis path comes from the AI app&#039;s public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio&#039;s Connect, which looks first.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"codex-cli\"\u003ECodex CLI\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen %USERPROFILE%\\.codex\\config.toml (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAdd the CadCraft entry below (inside &quot;mcp_servers&quot; if the file has one).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EStart Codex CLI and ask it the test prompt.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E[mcp_servers.cadcraft]\ncommand = &quot;npx&quot;\nargs = [&quot;-y&quot;,&quot;@getcadcraft/mcp&quot;,&quot;connect&quot;]\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUndo:\u003C/strong\u003E Delete the &quot;cadcraft&quot; entry from %USERPROFILE%\\.codex\\config.toml.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThis path comes from the AI app&#039;s public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio&#039;s Connect, which looks first.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"gemini-cli\"\u003EGemini CLI\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen %USERPROFILE%\\.gemini\\settings.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAdd the CadCraft entry below (inside &quot;mcpServers&quot; if the file has one).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EStart Gemini CLI and ask it the test prompt.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E{\n  &quot;mcpServers&quot;: {\n    &quot;cadcraft&quot;: {\n      &quot;command&quot;: &quot;npx&quot;,\n      &quot;args&quot;: [\n        &quot;-y&quot;,\n        &quot;@getcadcraft/mcp&quot;,\n        &quot;connect&quot;\n      ]\n    }\n  }\n}\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUndo:\u003C/strong\u003E Delete the &quot;cadcraft&quot; entry from %USERPROFILE%\\.gemini\\settings.json.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThis path comes from the AI app&#039;s public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio&#039;s Connect, which looks first.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"browser-ai-and-phones\"\u003EBrowser AI and phones\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAI apps in a web browser, and on phones and tablets, cannot read a settings file on your computer. They use the \u003Cstrong\u003Ehosted connector\u003C/strong\u003E instead (needs CadCraft Studio open on a computer). Studio › Connect your AI shows the link.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"if-it-does-not-work\"\u003EIf it does not work\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe AI app does not list CadCraft:\u003C/strong\u003E restart it fully (quit, not just close the window), then check the entry is in its settings.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;Could not reach CadCraft Studio&quot;:\u003C/strong\u003E open Studio and the app you want; each app has Settings › Assistants to turn on.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EIt can look but not change:\u003C/strong\u003E Edit, Save and Export are separate permissions you turn on per app in Studio.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you will learn How to let the AI app you already use (Claude, Cursor, Visual Studio Code, Windsurf, Codex, Gemini…) reach your CadCraft apps, how to check that it works in under a minute, and how to take it out again. The apps contain no AI; your AI app asks them to do things, and you decide what it may do. (For those who search for it: this uses the Model Context Protocol, \"MCP\".) The easy way Open CadCraft Studio › Connect your AI. Studio looks for the AI apps on this computer, shows you in plain words what it would add to each one's settings, and writes it only after you press Yes. Disconnect takes it out again. Nothing secret is written into the AI app's settings. Prove it works In your AI app, paste this: Use the CadCraft tools: list the apps you can reach, then open the sample drawing in my CadCraft folder and tell me how many objects it has, by layer. It should list the CadCraft apps it can reach and then give you a count by layer. It only reads, so it needs no extra permission beyond the Read that is on to begin with. In Studio, Check the connection tests each step without the AI and says which one failed. Or paste it yourself One card for each AI app. Each one adds a single entry named cadcraft and nothing else. Claude Desktop Open %APPDATA%\\Claude\\claude_desktop_config.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it). Add the CadCraft entry below (inside \"mcpServers\" if the file has one). Restart Claude Desktop completely, then ask it the test prompt. { \"mcpServers\": { \"cadcraft\": { \"command\": \"npx\", \"args\": [ \"-y\", \"@getcadcraft/mcp\", \"connect\" ] } } } Undo: Delete the \"cadcraft\" entry from %APPDATA%\\Claude\\claude_desktop_config.json. Claude Code Open a terminal. Run the command below. Start Claude Code and ask it the test prompt. claude mcp add cadcraft --scope user -- npx -y @getcadcraft/mcp connect Undo: claude mcp remove cadcraft --scope user Cursor Open %USERPROFILE%\\.cursor\\mcp.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it). Add the CadCraft entry below (inside \"mcpServers\" if the file has one). Start Cursor and ask it the test prompt. { \"mcpServers\": { \"cadcraft\": { \"command\": \"npx\", \"args\": [ \"-y\", \"@getcadcraft/mcp\", \"connect\" ] } } } Undo: Delete the \"cadcraft\" entry from %USERPROFILE%\\.cursor\\mcp.json. This path comes from the AI app's public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio's Connect, which looks first. Windsurf Open %USERPROFILE%\\.codeium\\windsurf\\mcp_config.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it). Add the CadCraft entry below (inside \"mcpServers\" if the file has one). Start Windsurf and ask it the test prompt. { \"mcpServers\": { \"cadcraft\": { \"command\": \"npx\", \"args\": [ \"-y\", \"@getcadcraft/mcp\", \"connect\" ] } } } Undo: Delete the \"cadcraft\" entry from %USERPROFILE%\\.codeium\\windsurf\\mcp_config.json. This path comes from the AI app's public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio's Connect, which looks first. Visual Studio Code Open %APPDATA%\\Code\\User\\mcp.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it). Add the CadCraft entry below (inside \"servers\" if the file has one). Start Visual Studio Code and ask it the test prompt. { \"servers\": { \"cadcraft\": { \"type\": \"stdio\", \"command\": \"npx\", \"args\": [ \"-y\", \"@getcadcraft/mcp\", \"connect\" ] } } } Undo: Delete the \"cadcraft\" entry from %APPDATA%\\Code\\User\\mcp.json. This path comes from the AI app's public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio's Connect, which looks first. Codex CLI Open %USERPROFILE%\\.codex\\config.toml (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it). Add the CadCraft entry below (inside \"mcp_servers\" if the file has one). Start Codex CLI and ask it the test prompt. [mcp_servers.cadcraft] command = \"npx\" args = [\"-y\",\"@getcadcraft/mcp\",\"connect\"] Undo: Delete the \"cadcraft\" entry from %USERPROFILE%\\.codex\\config.toml. This path comes from the AI app's public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio's Connect, which looks first. Gemini CLI Open %USERPROFILE%\\.gemini\\settings.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it). Add the CadCraft entry below (inside \"mcpServers\" if the file has one). Start Gemini CLI and ask it the test prompt. { \"mcpServers\": { \"cadcraft\": { \"command\": \"npx\", \"args\": [ \"-y\", \"@getcadcraft/mcp\", \"connect\" ] } } } Undo: Delete the \"cadcraft\" entry from %USERPROFILE%\\.gemini\\settings.json. This path comes from the AI app's public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio's Connect, which looks first. Browser AI and phones AI apps in a web browser, and on phones and tablets, cannot read a settings file on your computer. They use the hosted connector instead (needs CadCraft Studio open on a computer). Studio › Connect your AI shows the link. If it does not work The AI app does not list CadCraft: restart it fully (quit, not just close the window), then check the entry is in its settings. \"Could not reach CadCraft Studio\": open Studio and the app you want; each app has Settings › Assistants to turn on. It can look but not change: Edit, Save and Export are separate permissions you turn on per app in Studio.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"connect-ai-macos","scope":"basics","title":"Connect your AI on a Mac","summary":"Let your own AI app work with the CadCraft apps: one click in CadCraft Studio, or a short paste for each AI app. Includes the one-line test that proves it works.","section":"start","order":64,"keywords":["ai","assistant","connect","mcp","claude","cursor","vs code","windsurf","codex","gemini","set up","test","macos"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn","level":2},{"id":"the-easy-way","text":"The easy way","level":2},{"id":"prove-it-works","text":"Prove it works","level":2},{"id":"or-paste-it-yourself","text":"Or paste it yourself","level":2},{"id":"claude-desktop","text":"Claude Desktop","level":3},{"id":"claude-code","text":"Claude Code","level":3},{"id":"cursor","text":"Cursor","level":3},{"id":"windsurf","text":"Windsurf","level":3},{"id":"visual-studio-code","text":"Visual Studio Code","level":3},{"id":"codex-cli","text":"Codex CLI","level":3},{"id":"gemini-cli","text":"Gemini CLI","level":3},{"id":"browser-ai-and-phones","text":"Browser AI and phones","level":2},{"id":"if-it-does-not-work","text":"If it does not work","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\"\u003EWhat you will learn\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHow to let the AI app you already use (Claude, Cursor, Visual Studio Code, Windsurf, Codex, Gemini…) reach your CadCraft apps, how to check that it works in under a minute, and how to take it out again. The apps contain no AI; your AI app asks them to do things, and you decide what it may do. (For those who search for it: this uses the Model Context Protocol, &quot;MCP&quot;.)\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-easy-way\"\u003EThe easy way\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOpen \u003Cstrong\u003ECadCraft Studio › Connect your AI\u003C/strong\u003E. Studio looks for the AI apps on this computer, shows you in plain words what it would add to each one&#039;s settings, and writes it only after you press \u003Cstrong\u003EYes\u003C/strong\u003E. \u003Cstrong\u003EDisconnect\u003C/strong\u003E takes it out again. Nothing secret is written into the AI app&#039;s settings.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"prove-it-works\"\u003EProve it works\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn your AI app, paste this:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EUse the CadCraft tools: list the apps you can reach, then open the sample drawing in my CadCraft folder and tell me how many objects it has, by layer.\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIt should list the CadCraft apps it can reach and then give you a count by layer. It only reads, so it needs no extra permission beyond the \u003Cstrong\u003ERead\u003C/strong\u003E that is on to begin with. In Studio, \u003Cstrong\u003ECheck the connection\u003C/strong\u003E tests each step without the AI and says which one failed.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"or-paste-it-yourself\"\u003EOr paste it yourself\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOne card for each AI app. Each one adds a single entry named \u003Ccode\u003Ecadcraft\u003C/code\u003E and nothing else.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"claude-desktop\"\u003EClaude Desktop\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen ~/Library/Application Support/Claude/claude_desktop_config.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAdd the CadCraft entry below (inside &quot;mcpServers&quot; if the file has one).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERestart Claude Desktop completely, then ask it the test prompt.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E{\n  &quot;mcpServers&quot;: {\n    &quot;cadcraft&quot;: {\n      &quot;command&quot;: &quot;npx&quot;,\n      &quot;args&quot;: [\n        &quot;-y&quot;,\n        &quot;@getcadcraft/mcp&quot;,\n        &quot;connect&quot;\n      ]\n    }\n  }\n}\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUndo:\u003C/strong\u003E Delete the &quot;cadcraft&quot; entry from ~/Library/Application Support/Claude/claude_desktop_config.json.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"claude-code\"\u003EClaude Code\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen a terminal.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERun the command below.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EStart Claude Code and ask it the test prompt.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Eclaude mcp add cadcraft --scope user -- npx -y @getcadcraft/mcp connect\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUndo:\u003C/strong\u003E claude mcp remove cadcraft --scope user\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"cursor\"\u003ECursor\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen ~/.cursor/mcp.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAdd the CadCraft entry below (inside &quot;mcpServers&quot; if the file has one).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EStart Cursor and ask it the test prompt.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E{\n  &quot;mcpServers&quot;: {\n    &quot;cadcraft&quot;: {\n      &quot;command&quot;: &quot;npx&quot;,\n      &quot;args&quot;: [\n        &quot;-y&quot;,\n        &quot;@getcadcraft/mcp&quot;,\n        &quot;connect&quot;\n      ]\n    }\n  }\n}\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUndo:\u003C/strong\u003E Delete the &quot;cadcraft&quot; entry from ~/.cursor/mcp.json.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThis path comes from the AI app&#039;s public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio&#039;s Connect, which looks first.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"windsurf\"\u003EWindsurf\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen ~/.codeium/windsurf/mcp_config.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAdd the CadCraft entry below (inside &quot;mcpServers&quot; if the file has one).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EStart Windsurf and ask it the test prompt.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E{\n  &quot;mcpServers&quot;: {\n    &quot;cadcraft&quot;: {\n      &quot;command&quot;: &quot;npx&quot;,\n      &quot;args&quot;: [\n        &quot;-y&quot;,\n        &quot;@getcadcraft/mcp&quot;,\n        &quot;connect&quot;\n      ]\n    }\n  }\n}\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUndo:\u003C/strong\u003E Delete the &quot;cadcraft&quot; entry from ~/.codeium/windsurf/mcp_config.json.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThis path comes from the AI app&#039;s public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio&#039;s Connect, which looks first.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"visual-studio-code\"\u003EVisual Studio Code\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen ~/Library/Application Support/Code/User/mcp.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAdd the CadCraft entry below (inside &quot;servers&quot; if the file has one).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EStart Visual Studio Code and ask it the test prompt.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E{\n  &quot;servers&quot;: {\n    &quot;cadcraft&quot;: {\n      &quot;type&quot;: &quot;stdio&quot;,\n      &quot;command&quot;: &quot;npx&quot;,\n      &quot;args&quot;: [\n        &quot;-y&quot;,\n        &quot;@getcadcraft/mcp&quot;,\n        &quot;connect&quot;\n      ]\n    }\n  }\n}\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUndo:\u003C/strong\u003E Delete the &quot;cadcraft&quot; entry from ~/Library/Application Support/Code/User/mcp.json.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThis path comes from the AI app&#039;s public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio&#039;s Connect, which looks first.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"codex-cli\"\u003ECodex CLI\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen ~/.codex/config.toml (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAdd the CadCraft entry below (inside &quot;mcp_servers&quot; if the file has one).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EStart Codex CLI and ask it the test prompt.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E[mcp_servers.cadcraft]\ncommand = &quot;npx&quot;\nargs = [&quot;-y&quot;,&quot;@getcadcraft/mcp&quot;,&quot;connect&quot;]\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUndo:\u003C/strong\u003E Delete the &quot;cadcraft&quot; entry from ~/.codex/config.toml.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThis path comes from the AI app&#039;s public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio&#039;s Connect, which looks first.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"gemini-cli\"\u003EGemini CLI\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen ~/.gemini/settings.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAdd the CadCraft entry below (inside &quot;mcpServers&quot; if the file has one).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EStart Gemini CLI and ask it the test prompt.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E{\n  &quot;mcpServers&quot;: {\n    &quot;cadcraft&quot;: {\n      &quot;command&quot;: &quot;npx&quot;,\n      &quot;args&quot;: [\n        &quot;-y&quot;,\n        &quot;@getcadcraft/mcp&quot;,\n        &quot;connect&quot;\n      ]\n    }\n  }\n}\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUndo:\u003C/strong\u003E Delete the &quot;cadcraft&quot; entry from ~/.gemini/settings.json.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThis path comes from the AI app&#039;s public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio&#039;s Connect, which looks first.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"browser-ai-and-phones\"\u003EBrowser AI and phones\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAI apps in a web browser, and on phones and tablets, cannot read a settings file on your computer. They use the \u003Cstrong\u003Ehosted connector\u003C/strong\u003E instead (needs CadCraft Studio open on a computer). Studio › Connect your AI shows the link.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"if-it-does-not-work\"\u003EIf it does not work\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe AI app does not list CadCraft:\u003C/strong\u003E restart it fully (quit, not just close the window), then check the entry is in its settings.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;Could not reach CadCraft Studio&quot;:\u003C/strong\u003E open Studio and the app you want; each app has Settings › Assistants to turn on.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EIt can look but not change:\u003C/strong\u003E Edit, Save and Export are separate permissions you turn on per app in Studio.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you will learn How to let the AI app you already use (Claude, Cursor, Visual Studio Code, Windsurf, Codex, Gemini…) reach your CadCraft apps, how to check that it works in under a minute, and how to take it out again. The apps contain no AI; your AI app asks them to do things, and you decide what it may do. (For those who search for it: this uses the Model Context Protocol, \"MCP\".) The easy way Open CadCraft Studio › Connect your AI. Studio looks for the AI apps on this computer, shows you in plain words what it would add to each one's settings, and writes it only after you press Yes. Disconnect takes it out again. Nothing secret is written into the AI app's settings. Prove it works In your AI app, paste this: Use the CadCraft tools: list the apps you can reach, then open the sample drawing in my CadCraft folder and tell me how many objects it has, by layer. It should list the CadCraft apps it can reach and then give you a count by layer. It only reads, so it needs no extra permission beyond the Read that is on to begin with. In Studio, Check the connection tests each step without the AI and says which one failed. Or paste it yourself One card for each AI app. Each one adds a single entry named cadcraft and nothing else. Claude Desktop Open ~/Library/Application Support/Claude/claude_desktop_config.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it). Add the CadCraft entry below (inside \"mcpServers\" if the file has one). Restart Claude Desktop completely, then ask it the test prompt. { \"mcpServers\": { \"cadcraft\": { \"command\": \"npx\", \"args\": [ \"-y\", \"@getcadcraft/mcp\", \"connect\" ] } } } Undo: Delete the \"cadcraft\" entry from ~/Library/Application Support/Claude/claude_desktop_config.json. Claude Code Open a terminal. Run the command below. Start Claude Code and ask it the test prompt. claude mcp add cadcraft --scope user -- npx -y @getcadcraft/mcp connect Undo: claude mcp remove cadcraft --scope user Cursor Open ~/.cursor/mcp.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it). Add the CadCraft entry below (inside \"mcpServers\" if the file has one). Start Cursor and ask it the test prompt. { \"mcpServers\": { \"cadcraft\": { \"command\": \"npx\", \"args\": [ \"-y\", \"@getcadcraft/mcp\", \"connect\" ] } } } Undo: Delete the \"cadcraft\" entry from ~/.cursor/mcp.json. This path comes from the AI app's public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio's Connect, which looks first. Windsurf Open ~/.codeium/windsurf/mcp_config.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it). Add the CadCraft entry below (inside \"mcpServers\" if the file has one). Start Windsurf and ask it the test prompt. { \"mcpServers\": { \"cadcraft\": { \"command\": \"npx\", \"args\": [ \"-y\", \"@getcadcraft/mcp\", \"connect\" ] } } } Undo: Delete the \"cadcraft\" entry from ~/.codeium/windsurf/mcp_config.json. This path comes from the AI app's public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio's Connect, which looks first. Visual Studio Code Open ~/Library/Application Support/Code/User/mcp.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it). Add the CadCraft entry below (inside \"servers\" if the file has one). Start Visual Studio Code and ask it the test prompt. { \"servers\": { \"cadcraft\": { \"type\": \"stdio\", \"command\": \"npx\", \"args\": [ \"-y\", \"@getcadcraft/mcp\", \"connect\" ] } } } Undo: Delete the \"cadcraft\" entry from ~/Library/Application Support/Code/User/mcp.json. This path comes from the AI app's public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio's Connect, which looks first. Codex CLI Open ~/.codex/config.toml (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it). Add the CadCraft entry below (inside \"mcp_servers\" if the file has one). Start Codex CLI and ask it the test prompt. [mcp_servers.cadcraft] command = \"npx\" args = [\"-y\",\"@getcadcraft/mcp\",\"connect\"] Undo: Delete the \"cadcraft\" entry from ~/.codex/config.toml. This path comes from the AI app's public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio's Connect, which looks first. Gemini CLI Open ~/.gemini/settings.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it). Add the CadCraft entry below (inside \"mcpServers\" if the file has one). Start Gemini CLI and ask it the test prompt. { \"mcpServers\": { \"cadcraft\": { \"command\": \"npx\", \"args\": [ \"-y\", \"@getcadcraft/mcp\", \"connect\" ] } } } Undo: Delete the \"cadcraft\" entry from ~/.gemini/settings.json. This path comes from the AI app's public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio's Connect, which looks first. Browser AI and phones AI apps in a web browser, and on phones and tablets, cannot read a settings file on your computer. They use the hosted connector instead (needs CadCraft Studio open on a computer). Studio › Connect your AI shows the link. If it does not work The AI app does not list CadCraft: restart it fully (quit, not just close the window), then check the entry is in its settings. \"Could not reach CadCraft Studio\": open Studio and the app you want; each app has Settings › Assistants to turn on. It can look but not change: Edit, Save and Export are separate permissions you turn on per app in Studio.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"connect-ai-linux","scope":"basics","title":"Connect your AI on Linux","summary":"Let your own AI app work with the CadCraft apps: one click in CadCraft Studio, or a short paste for each AI app. Includes the one-line test that proves it works.","section":"start","order":65,"keywords":["ai","assistant","connect","mcp","claude","cursor","vs code","windsurf","codex","gemini","set up","test","linux"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn","level":2},{"id":"the-easy-way","text":"The easy way","level":2},{"id":"prove-it-works","text":"Prove it works","level":2},{"id":"or-paste-it-yourself","text":"Or paste it yourself","level":2},{"id":"claude-desktop","text":"Claude Desktop","level":3},{"id":"claude-code","text":"Claude Code","level":3},{"id":"cursor","text":"Cursor","level":3},{"id":"windsurf","text":"Windsurf","level":3},{"id":"visual-studio-code","text":"Visual Studio Code","level":3},{"id":"codex-cli","text":"Codex CLI","level":3},{"id":"gemini-cli","text":"Gemini CLI","level":3},{"id":"browser-ai-and-phones","text":"Browser AI and phones","level":2},{"id":"if-it-does-not-work","text":"If it does not work","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\"\u003EWhat you will learn\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHow to let the AI app you already use (Claude, Cursor, Visual Studio Code, Windsurf, Codex, Gemini…) reach your CadCraft apps, how to check that it works in under a minute, and how to take it out again. The apps contain no AI; your AI app asks them to do things, and you decide what it may do. (For those who search for it: this uses the Model Context Protocol, &quot;MCP&quot;.)\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-easy-way\"\u003EThe easy way\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOpen \u003Cstrong\u003ECadCraft Studio › Connect your AI\u003C/strong\u003E. Studio looks for the AI apps on this computer, shows you in plain words what it would add to each one&#039;s settings, and writes it only after you press \u003Cstrong\u003EYes\u003C/strong\u003E. \u003Cstrong\u003EDisconnect\u003C/strong\u003E takes it out again. Nothing secret is written into the AI app&#039;s settings.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"prove-it-works\"\u003EProve it works\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn your AI app, paste this:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EUse the CadCraft tools: list the apps you can reach, then open the sample drawing in my CadCraft folder and tell me how many objects it has, by layer.\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIt should list the CadCraft apps it can reach and then give you a count by layer. It only reads, so it needs no extra permission beyond the \u003Cstrong\u003ERead\u003C/strong\u003E that is on to begin with. In Studio, \u003Cstrong\u003ECheck the connection\u003C/strong\u003E tests each step without the AI and says which one failed.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"or-paste-it-yourself\"\u003EOr paste it yourself\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOne card for each AI app. Each one adds a single entry named \u003Ccode\u003Ecadcraft\u003C/code\u003E and nothing else.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"claude-desktop\"\u003EClaude Desktop\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen ~/.config/Claude/claude_desktop_config.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAdd the CadCraft entry below (inside &quot;mcpServers&quot; if the file has one).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERestart Claude Desktop completely, then ask it the test prompt.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E{\n  &quot;mcpServers&quot;: {\n    &quot;cadcraft&quot;: {\n      &quot;command&quot;: &quot;npx&quot;,\n      &quot;args&quot;: [\n        &quot;-y&quot;,\n        &quot;@getcadcraft/mcp&quot;,\n        &quot;connect&quot;\n      ]\n    }\n  }\n}\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUndo:\u003C/strong\u003E Delete the &quot;cadcraft&quot; entry from ~/.config/Claude/claude_desktop_config.json.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"claude-code\"\u003EClaude Code\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen a terminal.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERun the command below.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EStart Claude Code and ask it the test prompt.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Eclaude mcp add cadcraft --scope user -- npx -y @getcadcraft/mcp connect\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUndo:\u003C/strong\u003E claude mcp remove cadcraft --scope user\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"cursor\"\u003ECursor\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen ~/.cursor/mcp.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAdd the CadCraft entry below (inside &quot;mcpServers&quot; if the file has one).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EStart Cursor and ask it the test prompt.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E{\n  &quot;mcpServers&quot;: {\n    &quot;cadcraft&quot;: {\n      &quot;command&quot;: &quot;npx&quot;,\n      &quot;args&quot;: [\n        &quot;-y&quot;,\n        &quot;@getcadcraft/mcp&quot;,\n        &quot;connect&quot;\n      ]\n    }\n  }\n}\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUndo:\u003C/strong\u003E Delete the &quot;cadcraft&quot; entry from ~/.cursor/mcp.json.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThis path comes from the AI app&#039;s public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio&#039;s Connect, which looks first.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"windsurf\"\u003EWindsurf\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen ~/.codeium/windsurf/mcp_config.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAdd the CadCraft entry below (inside &quot;mcpServers&quot; if the file has one).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EStart Windsurf and ask it the test prompt.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E{\n  &quot;mcpServers&quot;: {\n    &quot;cadcraft&quot;: {\n      &quot;command&quot;: &quot;npx&quot;,\n      &quot;args&quot;: [\n        &quot;-y&quot;,\n        &quot;@getcadcraft/mcp&quot;,\n        &quot;connect&quot;\n      ]\n    }\n  }\n}\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUndo:\u003C/strong\u003E Delete the &quot;cadcraft&quot; entry from ~/.codeium/windsurf/mcp_config.json.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThis path comes from the AI app&#039;s public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio&#039;s Connect, which looks first.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"visual-studio-code\"\u003EVisual Studio Code\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen ~/.config/Code/User/mcp.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAdd the CadCraft entry below (inside &quot;servers&quot; if the file has one).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EStart Visual Studio Code and ask it the test prompt.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E{\n  &quot;servers&quot;: {\n    &quot;cadcraft&quot;: {\n      &quot;type&quot;: &quot;stdio&quot;,\n      &quot;command&quot;: &quot;npx&quot;,\n      &quot;args&quot;: [\n        &quot;-y&quot;,\n        &quot;@getcadcraft/mcp&quot;,\n        &quot;connect&quot;\n      ]\n    }\n  }\n}\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUndo:\u003C/strong\u003E Delete the &quot;cadcraft&quot; entry from ~/.config/Code/User/mcp.json.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThis path comes from the AI app&#039;s public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio&#039;s Connect, which looks first.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"codex-cli\"\u003ECodex CLI\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen ~/.codex/config.toml (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAdd the CadCraft entry below (inside &quot;mcp_servers&quot; if the file has one).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EStart Codex CLI and ask it the test prompt.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E[mcp_servers.cadcraft]\ncommand = &quot;npx&quot;\nargs = [&quot;-y&quot;,&quot;@getcadcraft/mcp&quot;,&quot;connect&quot;]\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUndo:\u003C/strong\u003E Delete the &quot;cadcraft&quot; entry from ~/.codex/config.toml.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThis path comes from the AI app&#039;s public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio&#039;s Connect, which looks first.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"gemini-cli\"\u003EGemini CLI\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen ~/.gemini/settings.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAdd the CadCraft entry below (inside &quot;mcpServers&quot; if the file has one).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EStart Gemini CLI and ask it the test prompt.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E{\n  &quot;mcpServers&quot;: {\n    &quot;cadcraft&quot;: {\n      &quot;command&quot;: &quot;npx&quot;,\n      &quot;args&quot;: [\n        &quot;-y&quot;,\n        &quot;@getcadcraft/mcp&quot;,\n        &quot;connect&quot;\n      ]\n    }\n  }\n}\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUndo:\u003C/strong\u003E Delete the &quot;cadcraft&quot; entry from ~/.gemini/settings.json.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThis path comes from the AI app&#039;s public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio&#039;s Connect, which looks first.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"browser-ai-and-phones\"\u003EBrowser AI and phones\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAI apps in a web browser, and on phones and tablets, cannot read a settings file on your computer. They use the \u003Cstrong\u003Ehosted connector\u003C/strong\u003E instead (needs CadCraft Studio open on a computer). Studio › Connect your AI shows the link.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"if-it-does-not-work\"\u003EIf it does not work\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe AI app does not list CadCraft:\u003C/strong\u003E restart it fully (quit, not just close the window), then check the entry is in its settings.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;Could not reach CadCraft Studio&quot;:\u003C/strong\u003E open Studio and the app you want; each app has Settings › Assistants to turn on.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EIt can look but not change:\u003C/strong\u003E Edit, Save and Export are separate permissions you turn on per app in Studio.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you will learn How to let the AI app you already use (Claude, Cursor, Visual Studio Code, Windsurf, Codex, Gemini…) reach your CadCraft apps, how to check that it works in under a minute, and how to take it out again. The apps contain no AI; your AI app asks them to do things, and you decide what it may do. (For those who search for it: this uses the Model Context Protocol, \"MCP\".) The easy way Open CadCraft Studio › Connect your AI. Studio looks for the AI apps on this computer, shows you in plain words what it would add to each one's settings, and writes it only after you press Yes. Disconnect takes it out again. Nothing secret is written into the AI app's settings. Prove it works In your AI app, paste this: Use the CadCraft tools: list the apps you can reach, then open the sample drawing in my CadCraft folder and tell me how many objects it has, by layer. It should list the CadCraft apps it can reach and then give you a count by layer. It only reads, so it needs no extra permission beyond the Read that is on to begin with. In Studio, Check the connection tests each step without the AI and says which one failed. Or paste it yourself One card for each AI app. Each one adds a single entry named cadcraft and nothing else. Claude Desktop Open ~/.config/Claude/claude_desktop_config.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it). Add the CadCraft entry below (inside \"mcpServers\" if the file has one). Restart Claude Desktop completely, then ask it the test prompt. { \"mcpServers\": { \"cadcraft\": { \"command\": \"npx\", \"args\": [ \"-y\", \"@getcadcraft/mcp\", \"connect\" ] } } } Undo: Delete the \"cadcraft\" entry from ~/.config/Claude/claude_desktop_config.json. Claude Code Open a terminal. Run the command below. Start Claude Code and ask it the test prompt. claude mcp add cadcraft --scope user -- npx -y @getcadcraft/mcp connect Undo: claude mcp remove cadcraft --scope user Cursor Open ~/.cursor/mcp.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it). Add the CadCraft entry below (inside \"mcpServers\" if the file has one). Start Cursor and ask it the test prompt. { \"mcpServers\": { \"cadcraft\": { \"command\": \"npx\", \"args\": [ \"-y\", \"@getcadcraft/mcp\", \"connect\" ] } } } Undo: Delete the \"cadcraft\" entry from ~/.cursor/mcp.json. This path comes from the AI app's public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio's Connect, which looks first. Windsurf Open ~/.codeium/windsurf/mcp_config.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it). Add the CadCraft entry below (inside \"mcpServers\" if the file has one). Start Windsurf and ask it the test prompt. { \"mcpServers\": { \"cadcraft\": { \"command\": \"npx\", \"args\": [ \"-y\", \"@getcadcraft/mcp\", \"connect\" ] } } } Undo: Delete the \"cadcraft\" entry from ~/.codeium/windsurf/mcp_config.json. This path comes from the AI app's public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio's Connect, which looks first. Visual Studio Code Open ~/.config/Code/User/mcp.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it). Add the CadCraft entry below (inside \"servers\" if the file has one). Start Visual Studio Code and ask it the test prompt. { \"servers\": { \"cadcraft\": { \"type\": \"stdio\", \"command\": \"npx\", \"args\": [ \"-y\", \"@getcadcraft/mcp\", \"connect\" ] } } } Undo: Delete the \"cadcraft\" entry from ~/.config/Code/User/mcp.json. This path comes from the AI app's public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio's Connect, which looks first. Codex CLI Open ~/.codex/config.toml (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it). Add the CadCraft entry below (inside \"mcp_servers\" if the file has one). Start Codex CLI and ask it the test prompt. [mcp_servers.cadcraft] command = \"npx\" args = [\"-y\",\"@getcadcraft/mcp\",\"connect\"] Undo: Delete the \"cadcraft\" entry from ~/.codex/config.toml. This path comes from the AI app's public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio's Connect, which looks first. Gemini CLI Open ~/.gemini/settings.json (it is made if it does not exist; keep anything already in it). Add the CadCraft entry below (inside \"mcpServers\" if the file has one). Start Gemini CLI and ask it the test prompt. { \"mcpServers\": { \"cadcraft\": { \"command\": \"npx\", \"args\": [ \"-y\", \"@getcadcraft/mcp\", \"connect\" ] } } } Undo: Delete the \"cadcraft\" entry from ~/.gemini/settings.json. This path comes from the AI app's public guides. If it does not match your install, use Studio's Connect, which looks first. Browser AI and phones AI apps in a web browser, and on phones and tablets, cannot read a settings file on your computer. They use the hosted connector instead (needs CadCraft Studio open on a computer). Studio › Connect your AI shows the link. If it does not work The AI app does not list CadCraft: restart it fully (quit, not just close the window), then check the entry is in its settings. \"Could not reach CadCraft Studio\": open Studio and the app you want; each app has Settings › Assistants to turn on. It can look but not change: Edit, Save and Export are separate permissions you turn on per app in Studio.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"connect-ai-mobile","scope":"basics","title":"Connect your AI on a phone or tablet","summary":"How your own AI app reaches the CadCraft apps from a phone or tablet: through the hosted connector, with CadCraft Studio open on a computer. Includes the one-line test.","section":"start","order":66,"keywords":["ai","assistant","connect","android","ipad","phone","tablet","hosted connector","test"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn","level":2},{"id":"how","text":"How","level":2},{"id":"prove-it-works","text":"Prove it works","level":2},{"id":"what-it-can-and-cannot-do","text":"What it can and cannot do","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\"\u003EWhat you will learn\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAn AI app on a phone or tablet cannot read a settings file on your computer, so it does not use the paste cards. It connects to a \u003Cstrong\u003Ehosted connector\u003C/strong\u003E instead, and CadCraft Studio must be open on a computer you own to answer.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"how\"\u003EHow\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOn the computer, open \u003Cstrong\u003ECadCraft Studio › Connect your AI\u003C/strong\u003E and choose \u003Cstrong\u003EHosted connector\u003C/strong\u003E. Studio shows the address to add and asks you to sign in.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn your AI app, add a connector (a &quot;remote&quot; or &quot;custom&quot; connector) with that address and sign in when asked.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EKeep Studio open. When it is closed the AI app says CadCraft is not reachable.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"prove-it-works\"\u003EProve it works\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn your AI app, paste this:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EUse the CadCraft tools: list the apps you can reach, then open the sample drawing in my CadCraft folder and tell me how many objects it has, by layer.\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-it-can-and-cannot-do\"\u003EWhat it can and cannot do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe hosted connector carries only the tool calls and their answers for the apps and permissions you allowed. It never holds your files, and the answers are marked as data from your files, not as instructions. You can disconnect it in Studio at any time.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"What you will learn An AI app on a phone or tablet cannot read a settings file on your computer, so it does not use the paste cards. It connects to a hosted connector instead, and CadCraft Studio must be open on a computer you own to answer. How On the computer, open CadCraft Studio › Connect your AI and choose Hosted connector. Studio shows the address to add and asks you to sign in. In your AI app, add a connector (a \"remote\" or \"custom\" connector) with that address and sign in when asked. Keep Studio open. When it is closed the AI app says CadCraft is not reachable. Prove it works In your AI app, paste this: Use the CadCraft tools: list the apps you can reach, then open the sample drawing in my CadCraft folder and tell me how many objects it has, by layer. What it can and cannot do The hosted connector carries only the tool calls and their answers for the apps and permissions you allowed. It never holds your files, and the answers are marked as data from your files, not as instructions. You can disconnect it in Studio at any time.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"local-models","scope":"basics","title":"Use an AI that runs on your own computer","summary":"Work with a local AI model and no network: through an AI app that can use tools, .","section":"start","order":66,"keywords":["ai","assistant","local","offline","ollama","lm studio","llama.cpp","jan","air-gapped","private","model","ask box"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn","level":2},{"id":"how","text":"How","level":2},{"id":"good-to-know","text":"Good to know","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\"\u003EWhat you will learn\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHow to use an AI model that runs on your own computer, so nothing you ask leaves it. This suits firms that cannot send drawings to an outside service. The apps contain no AI; the model asks them to do things, and you decide what it may do.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"how\"\u003EHow\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUse an AI app on this computer that can use tools. LM Studio, Jan and llama.cpp&#039;s server can each use tools through the Model Context Protocol (&quot;MCP&quot;). Add the single \u003Ccode\u003Ecadcraft\u003C/code\u003E entry shown in \u003Cstrong\u003EConnect your AI\u003C/strong\u003E, open CadCraft Studio, then ask your question in that app.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EProgram\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ECan use tools through MCP\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELM Studio\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EYes\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ellama.cpp (llama-server)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EYes, start it with \u003Ccode\u003E--jinja\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EJan\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EYes, turn on tool calling for the model\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOllama\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENot on its own: use it as the model, with Jan or an editor in front\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThis list is taken from each program&#039;s own documentation. It has not yet been tried with every program on every system.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"good-to-know\"\u003EGood to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERaise the model&#039;s context length before you start; the default in some programs is small and tool descriptions will not fit.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESmall models call tools less reliably than large hosted ones. If the answer says what it would do but nothing changes, try a larger model or a model marked as able to use tools.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEverything else stays the same: the permission levels, \u003Cstrong\u003ESuggest\u003C/strong\u003E mode, the folders you allowed and the activity list. A model on this computer sends nothing out.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you will learn How to use an AI model that runs on your own computer, so nothing you ask leaves it. This suits firms that cannot send drawings to an outside service. The apps contain no AI; the model asks them to do things, and you decide what it may do. How Use an AI app on this computer that can use tools. LM Studio, Jan and llama.cpp's server can each use tools through the Model Context Protocol (\"MCP\"). Add the single cadcraft entry shown in Connect your AI, open CadCraft Studio, then ask your question in that app. Program Can use tools through MCP LM Studio Yes llama.cpp (llama-server) Yes, start it with --jinja Jan Yes, turn on tool calling for the model Ollama Not on its own: use it as the model, with Jan or an editor in front This list is taken from each program's own documentation. It has not yet been tried with every program on every system. Good to know Raise the model's context length before you start; the default in some programs is small and tool descriptions will not fit. Small models call tools less reliably than large hosted ones. If the answer says what it would do but nothing changes, try a larger model or a model marked as able to use tools. Everything else stays the same: the permission levels, Suggest mode, the folders you allowed and the activity list. A model on this computer sends nothing out.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"engines","scope":"basics","title":"Run an engine on a model","summary":"The Engines panel runs the shared calculation engines (schematics, pipe and power networks, motion, queues, schedule and cost, requirements, risk, live data, circuits, boards, systems, PLC) on a model you paste.","section":"tasks","order":40,"keywords":["engines","schematic","network","power","load flow","robot","kinematics","queue","simulation","schedule","critical path","requirements","risk","fault tree","modbus","circuit","spice","board","pcb","modelica","plc"],"headings":[{"id":"run-one","text":"Run one","level":2},{"id":"controls-on-a-model-or-on-the-3d-scene","text":"Controls on a model, or on the 3D scene","level":2},{"id":"things-to-know","text":"Things to know","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003ESome calculations are the same in every app, so they live in one panel instead of eight. Open \u003Cstrong\u003EEngines\u003C/strong\u003E from the button in the bar at the top, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command K\"\u003E⌘K\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+K\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and type &quot;engines&quot;.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"run-one\"\u003ERun one\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose an engine from the list: schematics, pipe networks and power systems, motion and robots, queues, schedule and cost, requirements, risk, live data, circuits, boards, systems or controls.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose a worked example, or replace the text with your own model. Most engines read JSON; circuits, systems and controls read their own text (a circuit deck, Modelica, structured text).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERun\u003C/span\u003E. The results come back as tables, with the engine&#039;s own problems above them and the limits of its method below.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReset\u003C/span\u003E puts the example text back.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"controls-on-a-model-or-on-the-3d-scene\"\u003EControls on a model, or on the 3D scene\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe controls engine also takes a JSON input that ties a structured-text program to something it can sense and command. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EThe program bound to a flow model\u003C/span\u003E is the queues engine&#039;s model. In ModelCraft, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EThe program bound to the 3D scene\u003C/span\u003E ties it to the placements (groups and components) of the open model: a sensor reads \u003Ccode\u003EName.x\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EName.y\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EName.z\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EName.rotZ\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003EName.hidden\u003C/code\u003E, a command writes one of them, and the program decides. Position sensors are whole numbers, so give each a \u003Ccode\u003Escale\u003C/code\u003E (1000 turns metres into millimetres); a command is on or off, and \u003Ccode\u003Evalues\u003C/code\u003E says what off and on mean (\u003Ccode\u003E[0, 2]\u003C/code\u003E puts the placement at 0 or 2 m). A run changes nothing in the model: the results table shows where each placement would end up. Add \u003Ccode\u003E&quot;keep&quot;: true\u003C/code\u003E to write the final state into the model as one undo step. A placement that is not in the model is reported by name.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"things-to-know\"\u003EThings to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEverything runs in this page. Nothing is uploaded, and nothing is saved in your file.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EProblems the engine finds also appear in the Problems panel, under their own source.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe examples are small on purpose, with answers you can check by hand. They are a way to see what an engine does, not a design.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe results are calculation aids. A person who is responsible for the design checks them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"Some calculations are the same in every app, so they live in one panel instead of eight. Open Engines from the button in the bar at the top, or press Ctrl+K and type \"engines\". Run one Choose an engine from the list: schematics, pipe networks and power systems, motion and robots, queues, schedule and cost, requirements, risk, live data, circuits, boards, systems or controls. Choose a worked example, or replace the text with your own model. Most engines read JSON; circuits, systems and controls read their own text (a circuit deck, Modelica, structured text). Press Run. The results come back as tables, with the engine's own problems above them and the limits of its method below. Reset puts the example text back. Controls on a model, or on the 3D scene The controls engine also takes a JSON input that ties a structured-text program to something it can sense and command. The program bound to a flow model is the queues engine's model. In ModelCraft, The program bound to the 3D scene ties it to the placements (groups and components) of the open model: a sensor reads Name.x, Name.y, Name.z, Name.rotZ or Name.hidden, a command writes one of them, and the program decides. Position sensors are whole numbers, so give each a scale (1000 turns metres into millimetres); a command is on or off, and values says what off and on mean ([0, 2] puts the placement at 0 or 2 m). A run changes nothing in the model: the results table shows where each placement would end up. Add \"keep\": true to write the final state into the model as one undo step. A placement that is not in the model is reported by name. Things to know Everything runs in this page. Nothing is uploaded, and nothing is saved in your file. Problems the engine finds also appear in the Problems panel, under their own source. The examples are small on purpose, with answers you can check by hand. They are a way to see what an engine does, not a design. The results are calculation aids. A person who is responsible for the design checks them.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"assistants-recipes","scope":"basics","title":"Recipes for AI assistants","summary":"Worked examples for using your own AI assistant with the CadCraft apps: what to ask, the tools it calls, and the permissions each needs. Every recipe here is run by our tests with the same calls.","section":"tasks","order":62,"keywords":["ai","assistant","mcp","recipes","examples","prompts","automation","batch","convert","plot","check","takeoff","cut","fill","parameters","script"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn","level":2},{"id":"recipes","text":"Recipes","level":2},{"id":"tidy-a-drawing","text":"Tidy a drawing","level":3},{"id":"plot-a-set-of-sheets","text":"Plot a set of sheets","level":3},{"id":"convert-drawings-for-a-consultant","text":"Convert drawings for a consultant","level":3},{"id":"check-drawings-before-sending-them","text":"Check drawings before sending them","level":3},{"id":"ask-a-building-model-questions","text":"Ask a building model questions","level":3},{"id":"draw-a-room-in-bimcraft","text":"Draw a room in BimCraft","level":3},{"id":"total-a-takeoff","text":"Total a takeoff","level":3},{"id":"site-numbers","text":"Site numbers","level":3},{"id":"try-a-part-variant","text":"Try a part variant","level":3},{"id":"fill-in-a-title-block","text":"Fill in a title block","level":3},{"id":"your-files-and-your-assistant","text":"Your files and your assistant","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\"\u003EWhat you will learn\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EReady-made requests for your own AI assistant, and what it does with them. The apps contain no AI: your assistant reads the files and calls the apps&#039; tools, and you decide which permissions it has. Each recipe names the tools it calls, so you can check its work in the activity log, and each one is run by our tests with exactly these calls, so they keep working.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThere are two ways in. In \u003Cstrong\u003ECadCraft Studio\u003C/strong\u003E, an assistant works with the apps you have open (Settings › Assistants in each app). \u003Cstrong\u003EWithout a window\u003C/strong\u003E, the CadCraft file server for assistants (\u003Ccode\u003Enpx @getcadcraft/mcp --root &lt;folder&gt;\u003C/code\u003E) works on files in folders you name; the \u003Ccode\u003Ecadcraft\u003C/code\u003E command line does the same jobs for your own scripts. The recipes say which way they use; most work both ways.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EStart read-only. Ask the assistant to describe a file before it changes anything; its answers are better when it has looked first, and Read is the only permission that is on to begin with.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"recipes\"\u003ERecipes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"tidy-a-drawing\"\u003ETidy a drawing\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAsk:\u003C/strong\u003E &quot;List the layers with nothing on them and the blocks nobody uses, then purge the unused blocks.&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe assistant opens the drawing and reads it, then runs PURGE:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Edrawing_open {path: &quot;Kitchen/kitchen-equipment.dwg&quot;}            → d1\ndrawing_describe {doc: &quot;d1&quot;}                                    layers with 0 objects, blocks with 0 uses\ncommand_run {doc: &quot;d1&quot;, input: &quot;PURGE&quot;}                         one undo step: &quot;Assistant: PURGE&quot;\ndrawing_save {doc: &quot;d1&quot;, path: &quot;Kitchen/kitchen-tidy.dwg&quot;}\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENeeds Read and Edit, and Save to write the result. Dimension arrowheads count as used, so they stay.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"plot-a-set-of-sheets\"\u003EPlot a set of sheets\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAsk:\u003C/strong\u003E &quot;Plot every sheet of every drawing in the Kitchen folder to PDF.&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Efiles_list {folder: &quot;Kitchen&quot;, extensions: [&quot;dwg&quot;, &quot;dxf&quot;]}\ndrawing_open {path: …}  ·  drawing_describe {doc}                the sheets of each drawing\ndrawing_plot {doc, layout: &quot;&lt;sheet&gt;&quot;, out: &quot;Plots/&lt;drawing&gt; &lt;sheet&gt;.pdf&quot;}\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENeeds Read and Export. From the command line, one PDF per drawing with all its sheets: \u003Ccode\u003Ecadcraft plot Kitchen/*.dwg Plots --all\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"convert-drawings-for-a-consultant\"\u003EConvert drawings for a consultant\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAsk:\u003C/strong\u003E &quot;Save copies of the drawings in Kitchen as AutoCAD 2013 DWG in the Out folder.&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Efiles_list {folder: &quot;Kitchen&quot;, extensions: [&quot;dwg&quot;, &quot;dxf&quot;]}\ndrawing_convert {path: &quot;Kitchen/&lt;file&gt;&quot;, out: &quot;Out/&lt;file&gt;.dwg&quot;, version: &quot;2013&quot;}\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENeeds Read and Export; the originals are not changed. From the command line: \u003Ccode\u003Ecadcraft convert Kitchen/*.dwg Kitchen/*.dxf Out --to dwg --version 2013\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"check-drawings-before-sending-them\"\u003ECheck drawings before sending them\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAsk:\u003C/strong\u003E &quot;Check the drawings in Kitchen: nothing on layer 0, colours by layer, no unused blocks. Give me a table of what fails.&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Edrawing_check {doc, rules: {layers: {noObjectsOn: [&quot;0&quot;]}, objects: {colorByLayer: true}, blocks: {noUnused: true}}}\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENeeds Read. Keep your office&#039;s rules in a file and the command line checks a whole folder: \u003Ccode\u003Ecadcraft check Kitchen/*.dwg --rules office-standards.json\u003C/code\u003E (exit code 1 when something fails, so it fits in a script).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"ask-a-building-model-questions\"\u003EAsk a building model questions\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAsk:\u003C/strong\u003E &quot;How many doors does the house have on each level, and which are external?&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Eifc_open {path: &quot;house.ifc&quot;}                                    → d1\nifc_query {doc: &quot;d1&quot;, class: &quot;IfcDoor&quot;}                         each door with its level\nifc_properties {doc: &quot;d1&quot;, id: &lt;door&gt;}                          Pset_DoorCommon.IsExternal\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENeeds Read. In Studio, BimCraft&#039;s own tools answer the same about the model you have open.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"draw-a-room-in-bimcraft\"\u003EDraw a room in BimCraft\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAsk:\u003C/strong\u003E &quot;Draw a 6 by 4 metre room on Level 1 with a single door in the south wall.&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Ewalls_add {points: [[0, 0], [6000, 0], [6000, 4000], [0, 4000]], closed: true}     one undo step\nopening_add {wall: &quot;&lt;the first wall&#039;s GlobalId&gt;&quot;, kind: &quot;door&quot;}                    one undo step\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENeeds Edit. Walls join and the room follows, as with the Draw tools. Without a window, \u003Ccode\u003Eifc_new\u003C/code\u003E starts a building first and \u003Ccode\u003Eifc_save\u003C/code\u003E writes it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"total-a-takeoff\"\u003ETotal a takeoff\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAsk:\u003C/strong\u003E &quot;Give me the estimate by item and export it to XLSX.&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Etakeoff_open {path: &quot;job.takeoff.json&quot;}  ·  takeoff_estimate {doc}  ·  takeoff_export {doc, out: &quot;job.xlsx&quot;}\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENeeds Read, and Export for the file. Marks on sheets with no scale are listed and never counted as zero, so an incomplete estimate says so.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"site-numbers\"\u003ESite numbers\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAsk:\u003C/strong\u003E &quot;How much cut and fill does the pad need?&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Esite_open {path: &quot;site.civilcraft.json&quot;}  ·  surface_volume {doc}       cut, fill and net, per pad\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENeeds Read. With a level instead of a design: \u003Ccode\u003Esurface_volume {doc, datum: 100}\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"try-a-part-variant\"\u003ETry a part variant\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAsk:\u003C/strong\u003E &quot;Set the length to 40 mm, rebuild, and give me the mass.&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Epart_open {path: &quot;screw.partcraft.json&quot;}  ·  params_list {doc}\nparam_set {doc, name: &quot;l&quot;, value: 40}                           one undo step, the part rebuilt\nmass_properties {doc}                                            volume, mass from the material, centre, inertia\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENeeds Read and Edit; Save (\u003Ccode\u003Epart_save\u003C/code\u003E) to keep it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"fill-in-a-title-block\"\u003EFill in a title block\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAsk:\u003C/strong\u003E &quot;Insert the title block at 0,0 with sheet number A-101 and the title Kitchen plan.&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Eblock_insert {doc, block: &quot;TITLEBLOCK-ARCHD&quot;, at: [0, 0], attributes: {&quot;SHEET-NO&quot;: &quot;A-101&quot;, &quot;SHEET-TITLE&quot;: &quot;Kitchen plan&quot;}}\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENeeds Edit. One undo step; the attributes it does not name keep their defaults.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"your-files-and-your-assistant\"\u003EYour files and your assistant\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe apps never send your files anywhere. \u003Cstrong\u003EBut what your assistant reads goes to whoever runs that assistant:\u003C/strong\u003E a cloud assistant&#039;s maker sees the parts of the drawing it is shown. If that matters for a job, use an assistant that runs on your own computer, or keep that job&#039;s folders out of the ones you allow.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"What you will learn Ready-made requests for your own AI assistant, and what it does with them. The apps contain no AI: your assistant reads the files and calls the apps' tools, and you decide which permissions it has. Each recipe names the tools it calls, so you can check its work in the activity log, and each one is run by our tests with exactly these calls, so they keep working. There are two ways in. In CadCraft Studio, an assistant works with the apps you have open (Settings › Assistants in each app). Without a window, the CadCraft file server for assistants (npx @getcadcraft/mcp --root \u003Cfolder\u003E) works on files in folders you name; the cadcraft command line does the same jobs for your own scripts. The recipes say which way they use; most work both ways. Start read-only. Ask the assistant to describe a file before it changes anything; its answers are better when it has looked first, and Read is the only permission that is on to begin with. Recipes Tidy a drawing Ask: \"List the layers with nothing on them and the blocks nobody uses, then purge the unused blocks.\" The assistant opens the drawing and reads it, then runs PURGE: drawing_open {path: \"Kitchen/kitchen-equipment.dwg\"} → d1 drawing_describe {doc: \"d1\"} layers with 0 objects, blocks with 0 uses command_run {doc: \"d1\", input: \"PURGE\"} one undo step: \"Assistant: PURGE\" drawing_save {doc: \"d1\", path: \"Kitchen/kitchen-tidy.dwg\"} Needs Read and Edit, and Save to write the result. Dimension arrowheads count as used, so they stay. Plot a set of sheets Ask: \"Plot every sheet of every drawing in the Kitchen folder to PDF.\" files_list {folder: \"Kitchen\", extensions: [\"dwg\", \"dxf\"]} drawing_open {path: …} · drawing_describe {doc} the sheets of each drawing drawing_plot {doc, layout: \"\u003Csheet\u003E\", out: \"Plots/\u003Cdrawing\u003E \u003Csheet\u003E.pdf\"} Needs Read and Export. From the command line, one PDF per drawing with all its sheets: cadcraft plot Kitchen/*.dwg Plots --all. Convert drawings for a consultant Ask: \"Save copies of the drawings in Kitchen as AutoCAD 2013 DWG in the Out folder.\" files_list {folder: \"Kitchen\", extensions: [\"dwg\", \"dxf\"]} drawing_convert {path: \"Kitchen/\u003Cfile\u003E\", out: \"Out/\u003Cfile\u003E.dwg\", version: \"2013\"} Needs Read and Export; the originals are not changed. From the command line: cadcraft convert Kitchen/*.dwg Kitchen/*.dxf Out --to dwg --version 2013. Check drawings before sending them Ask: \"Check the drawings in Kitchen: nothing on layer 0, colours by layer, no unused blocks. Give me a table of what fails.\" drawing_check {doc, rules: {layers: {noObjectsOn: [\"0\"]}, objects: {colorByLayer: true}, blocks: {noUnused: true}}} Needs Read. Keep your office's rules in a file and the command line checks a whole folder: cadcraft check Kitchen/*.dwg --rules office-standards.json (exit code 1 when something fails, so it fits in a script). Ask a building model questions Ask: \"How many doors does the house have on each level, and which are external?\" ifc_open {path: \"house.ifc\"} → d1 ifc_query {doc: \"d1\", class: \"IfcDoor\"} each door with its level ifc_properties {doc: \"d1\", id: \u003Cdoor\u003E} Pset_DoorCommon.IsExternal Needs Read. In Studio, BimCraft's own tools answer the same about the model you have open. Draw a room in BimCraft Ask: \"Draw a 6 by 4 metre room on Level 1 with a single door in the south wall.\" walls_add {points: [[0, 0], [6000, 0], [6000, 4000], [0, 4000]], closed: true} one undo step opening_add {wall: \"\u003Cthe first wall's GlobalId\u003E\", kind: \"door\"} one undo step Needs Edit. Walls join and the room follows, as with the Draw tools. Without a window, ifc_new starts a building first and ifc_save writes it. Total a takeoff Ask: \"Give me the estimate by item and export it to XLSX.\" takeoff_open {path: \"job.takeoff.json\"} · takeoff_estimate {doc} · takeoff_export {doc, out: \"job.xlsx\"} Needs Read, and Export for the file. Marks on sheets with no scale are listed and never counted as zero, so an incomplete estimate says so. Site numbers Ask: \"How much cut and fill does the pad need?\" site_open {path: \"site.civilcraft.json\"} · surface_volume {doc} cut, fill and net, per pad Needs Read. With a level instead of a design: surface_volume {doc, datum: 100}. Try a part variant Ask: \"Set the length to 40 mm, rebuild, and give me the mass.\" part_open {path: \"screw.partcraft.json\"} · params_list {doc} param_set {doc, name: \"l\", value: 40} one undo step, the part rebuilt mass_properties {doc} volume, mass from the material, centre, inertia Needs Read and Edit; Save (part_save) to keep it. Fill in a title block Ask: \"Insert the title block at 0,0 with sheet number A-101 and the title Kitchen plan.\" block_insert {doc, block: \"TITLEBLOCK-ARCHD\", at: [0, 0], attributes: {\"SHEET-NO\": \"A-101\", \"SHEET-TITLE\": \"Kitchen plan\"}} Needs Edit. One undo step; the attributes it does not name keep their defaults. Your files and your assistant The apps never send your files anywhere. But what your assistant reads goes to whoever runs that assistant: a cloud assistant's maker sees the parts of the drawing it is shown. If that matters for a job, use an assistant that runs on your own computer, or keep that job's folders out of the ones you allow.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"community-testing","scope":"basics","title":"Community testing","summary":"How to try a test on the Community testing page and send your result.","section":"tasks","order":90,"keywords":["community","testing","test","results","report","feedback","files","passed","failing"],"headings":[{"id":"send-a-result","text":"Send a result","level":2},{"id":"sending-files","text":"Sending files","level":2},{"id":"your-privacy","text":"Your privacy","level":2},{"id":"what-counts-as-known-not-to-work","text":"What counts as \"known not to work\"","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003ESome checks need a real computer, a real file or a real device. The Community testing page lists them so anyone can help.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"send-a-result\"\u003ESend a result\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen the \u003Cstrong\u003ECommunity testing\u003C/strong\u003E page on the website and choose a test.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUse the public build named in \u003Cstrong\u003EBefore you start\u003C/strong\u003E, never a lab build.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFollow the \u003Cstrong\u003ESteps\u003C/strong\u003E and compare with the \u003Cstrong\u003EExpected result\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFill in \u003Cstrong\u003ESend a result\u003C/strong\u003E: passed, still failing, partly works, or could not run it. Say which system you used and what happened.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou do not need an account to send a result. A signed-in account is needed only to send files.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"sending-files\"\u003ESending files\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen a test asks for files, drag them into the framed area (or click it, or press Enter or Space on it to choose them). You can send several at once; each shows its name and size and a Remove button. The form says which file types and sizes we take. We never take programs, scripts or web pages.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"your-privacy\"\u003EYour privacy\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA result sent while you are signed in carries your account so we can write back. It is never shown on the page.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EYour first name is shown next to a result only if you tick \u003Cstrong\u003EShow my first name\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ENotes are shown to others only if you tick \u003Cstrong\u003EAnyone may read my notes\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFiles are seen only by the CadCraft team, kept privately and deleted automatically after the number of days the form states. Remove anything private from a file first.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-counts-as-known-not-to-work\"\u003EWhat counts as &quot;known not to work&quot;\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach test lists what is not built yet. Please do not report those as bugs.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Some checks need a real computer, a real file or a real device. The Community testing page lists them so anyone can help. Send a result Open the Community testing page on the website and choose a test. Use the public build named in Before you start, never a lab build. Follow the Steps and compare with the Expected result. Fill in Send a result: passed, still failing, partly works, or could not run it. Say which system you used and what happened. You do not need an account to send a result. A signed-in account is needed only to send files. Sending files When a test asks for files, drag them into the framed area (or click it, or press Enter or Space on it to choose them). You can send several at once; each shows its name and size and a Remove button. The form says which file types and sizes we take. We never take programs, scripts or web pages. Your privacy A result sent while you are signed in carries your account so we can write back. It is never shown on the page. Your first name is shown next to a result only if you tick Show my first name. Notes are shown to others only if you tick Anyone may read my notes. Files are seen only by the CadCraft team, kept privately and deleted automatically after the number of days the form states. Remove anything private from a file first. What counts as \"known not to work\" Each test lists what is not built yet. Please do not report those as bugs.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"work-together-live","scope":"basics","title":"Work together live","summary":"Share the file open in an app so teammates edit it with you on your own network with a short code, and join someone else's session. Invite links over the internet are not open yet.","section":"tasks","order":90,"keywords":["live","studio live","work together","collaborate","share live","join","code","qr","invite link","link","host","approve","guest","network","relay","license","many people","limit","allow anyway","slow","recent edits","edit log","who changed","history"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn","level":2},{"id":"share-the-file-you-have-open","text":"Share the file you have open","level":2},{"id":"let-people-in","text":"Let people in","level":2},{"id":"when-many-people-join","text":"When many people join","level":2},{"id":"codes-on-your-own-network","text":"Codes on your own network","level":2},{"id":"invite-links-over-the-internet-not-open-yet","text":"Invite links over the internet: not open yet","level":2},{"id":"join-someone-else-s-session","text":"Join someone else's session","level":2},{"id":"who-changed-what-recent-edits","text":"Who changed what: Recent edits","level":2},{"id":"assistants-in-a-session","text":"Assistants in a session","level":2},{"id":"when-the-session-ends","text":"When the session ends","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\"\u003EWhat you will learn\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHow to share a file live from CadCraft Studio, let people in, and join a session someone else shares. What you see in the drawing itself while you work together is on the app&#039;s own page about live sessions.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"share-the-file-you-have-open\"\u003EShare the file you have open\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen the file in its app inside CadCraft Studio on a computer.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELive\u003C/span\u003E in the app&#039;s bar, type the name others should see, then choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShare live\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EStudio shows a code like \u003Ccode\u003EK7-Q4M2X\u003C/code\u003E with a picture to scan and the computer&#039;s address. Give the code to the people you want to invite.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYour file stays on your computer. Only you save it, and everyone sees your saves.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"let-people-in\"\u003ELet people in\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENobody gets in without you. When someone knocks, a card shows their name and device and whether they came on this network or through the internet:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit\u003C/span\u003E lets them change the file with you.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EView only\u003C/span\u003E lets them watch; anything they try to change is taken back.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDeny\u003C/span\u003E turns them away.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe list under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EIn the session\u003C/span\u003E shows everyone. Change a person&#039;s role there, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERemove\u003C/span\u003E them: they keep a copy of what they had, and they can&#039;t come back without a new invitation.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"when-many-people-join\"\u003EWhen many people join\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA session&#039;s usual size is up to 20 people besides you. It is not a hard limit: how many people can work well together depends on their computers, the file and the network, so Studio asks you before a session grows past it instead of deciding for you.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen someone knocks and would make more than 20, their card says so, and Studio explains what to expect: every change goes to everyone, so with many people each change takes longer to reach each person, and slower laptops and connections fall behind first. Then you choose:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAllow anyway\u003C/span\u003E lets more people in for this session. Each person still waits for your \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EView only\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDeny\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EKeep it at 20\u003C/span\u003E turns away the people waiting over the limit; they are told you are keeping the session small.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo allow more before anyone knocks, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAllow more people…\u003C/span\u003E under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EIn the session\u003C/span\u003E. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBack to 20\u003C/span\u003E puts the usual size back. Your choice lasts for this session only; the next one starts at 20 again.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf things slow down, fewer people, a quieter file (fewer people changing it at the same moment) or a better connection (a cable instead of Wi-Fi) helps.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"codes-on-your-own-network\"\u003ECodes on your own network\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA code works for 10 minutes. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew code\u003C/span\u003E makes another; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStop inviting\u003C/span\u003E closes it. People already in stay.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOne-time code\u003C/span\u003E lets one person in per code.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAfter 3 wrong codes in a row, Studio closes the code and makes a new one, and tells you.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESessions on your own network are free, for everyone.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"invite-links-over-the-internet-not-open-yet\"\u003EInvite links over the internet: not open yet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EInvite links aren&#039;t open yet. Sessions on your own network are free. Today the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOver the internet\u003C/span\u003E part of the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELive\u003C/span\u003E panel says so in a plain sentence instead of offering a link.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen invite links open, this is how they will work:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUnder \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOver the internet\u003C/span\u003E, choose how long the link works (1 hour, 8 hours, or until you end the session), then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EInvite by link\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ECopy the link and send it like a meeting link.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe link&#039;s secret part (after \u003Ccode\u003E#\u003C/code\u003E) never reaches getcadcraft.com: the site only passes on encrypted messages it can&#039;t read, and you still approve each person. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew link\u003C/span\u003E makes the old one stop working at once; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStop the link\u003C/span\u003E ends it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EMaking an invite link will need you signed in, with a commercial license in force for the app. Guests will need nothing: no account, no license.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"join-someone-else-s-session\"\u003EJoin someone else&#039;s session\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn CadCraft Studio&#039;s home screen, find \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EJoin a live session\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType the code the host gave you, or type the address shown under their code followed by the code. (Once invite links open, you will paste the host&#039;s link here too.)\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType your name and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EJoin\u003C/span\u003E. Studio opens the right app; it fills with the host&#039;s file once the host lets you in.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWithout Studio, scan the picture under the host&#039;s code with a device on the same network: the app opens in the browser.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn CadCraft, if you already have the same file open in a Studio window, you can join from that window instead: choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELive\u003C/span\u003E, then under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EJoin with the drawing open here\u003C/span\u003E type the code or the address and code, and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EJoin here\u003C/span\u003E. Only the changes made since the file was opened travel. If the host&#039;s drawing is a different one, it replaces the one in that window; your file on disk stays as it is.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EGuest, hotel and school Wi-Fi often keep devices apart, so a code can&#039;t find the host there. Use a network that lets devices see each other, such as the office network. Invite links, for when you are not on the same network, are not open yet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"who-changed-what-recent-edits\"\u003EWho changed what: Recent edits\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe Live panel lists \u003Cstrong\u003ERecent edits\u003C/strong\u003E: who made each change and what it was (the step&#039;s name, such as Line or Move), newest first, with how long ago. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShow more\u003C/span\u003E lists more of them. With something selected in CadCraft or MarkupCraft, the panel also says who changed it last, and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOnly what is selected\u003C/span\u003E keeps just the edits that touched it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe list is made in your own window from the session&#039;s edits while the session runs. It is gone when the session ends: it is never saved in the file or sent anywhere. Everyone in the session can see who made each change. If you joined after people had started, or were away for a while, the edits from before are not in your list, and the panel says so.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"assistants-in-a-session\"\u003EAssistants in a session\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf someone turned on assistants in their app&#039;s settings, their AI assistant can ask to work in the session too. The host&#039;s own assistant joins at once; anyone else&#039;s waits for the host, with a card saying whose assistant it is: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAllow\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDeny\u003C/span\u003E. An assistant edits like its person, with its own undo, and leaves the session when its person leaves.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"when-the-session-ends\"\u003EWhen the session ends\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe host chooses \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEnd the session\u003C/span\u003E, or closes the file. Everyone else keeps an unsaved copy of the file as it was: save it if you want to keep it. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELeave\u003C/span\u003E does the same for you alone.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"What you will learn How to share a file live from CadCraft Studio, let people in, and join a session someone else shares. What you see in the drawing itself while you work together is on the app's own page about live sessions. Share the file you have open Open the file in its app inside CadCraft Studio on a computer. Choose Live in the app's bar, type the name others should see, then choose Share live. Studio shows a code like K7-Q4M2X with a picture to scan and the computer's address. Give the code to the people you want to invite. Your file stays on your computer. Only you save it, and everyone sees your saves. Let people in Nobody gets in without you. When someone knocks, a card shows their name and device and whether they came on this network or through the internet: Edit lets them change the file with you. View only lets them watch; anything they try to change is taken back. Deny turns them away. The list under In the session shows everyone. Change a person's role there, or Remove them: they keep a copy of what they had, and they can't come back without a new invitation. When many people join A session's usual size is up to 20 people besides you. It is not a hard limit: how many people can work well together depends on their computers, the file and the network, so Studio asks you before a session grows past it instead of deciding for you. When someone knocks and would make more than 20, their card says so, and Studio explains what to expect: every change goes to everyone, so with many people each change takes longer to reach each person, and slower laptops and connections fall behind first. Then you choose: Allow anyway lets more people in for this session. Each person still waits for your Edit, View only or Deny. Keep it at 20 turns away the people waiting over the limit; they are told you are keeping the session small. To allow more before anyone knocks, choose Allow more people… under In the session. Back to 20 puts the usual size back. Your choice lasts for this session only; the next one starts at 20 again. If things slow down, fewer people, a quieter file (fewer people changing it at the same moment) or a better connection (a cable instead of Wi-Fi) helps. Codes on your own network A code works for 10 minutes. New code makes another; Stop inviting closes it. People already in stay. One-time code lets one person in per code. After 3 wrong codes in a row, Studio closes the code and makes a new one, and tells you. Sessions on your own network are free, for everyone. Invite links over the internet: not open yet Invite links aren't open yet. Sessions on your own network are free. Today the Over the internet part of the Live panel says so in a plain sentence instead of offering a link. When invite links open, this is how they will work: Under Over the internet, choose how long the link works (1 hour, 8 hours, or until you end the session), then Invite by link. Copy the link and send it like a meeting link. The link's secret part (after #) never reaches getcadcraft.com: the site only passes on encrypted messages it can't read, and you still approve each person. New link makes the old one stop working at once; Stop the link ends it. Making an invite link will need you signed in, with a commercial license in force for the app. Guests will need nothing: no account, no license. Join someone else's session In CadCraft Studio's home screen, find Join a live session. Type the code the host gave you, or type the address shown under their code followed by the code. (Once invite links open, you will paste the host's link here too.) Type your name and choose Join. Studio opens the right app; it fills with the host's file once the host lets you in. Without Studio, scan the picture under the host's code with a device on the same network: the app opens in the browser. In CadCraft, if you already have the same file open in a Studio window, you can join from that window instead: choose Live, then under Join with the drawing open here type the code or the address and code, and choose Join here. Only the changes made since the file was opened travel. If the host's drawing is a different one, it replaces the one in that window; your file on disk stays as it is. Guest, hotel and school Wi-Fi often keep devices apart, so a code can't find the host there. Use a network that lets devices see each other, such as the office network. Invite links, for when you are not on the same network, are not open yet. Who changed what: Recent edits The Live panel lists Recent edits: who made each change and what it was (the step's name, such as Line or Move), newest first, with how long ago. Show more lists more of them. With something selected in CadCraft or MarkupCraft, the panel also says who changed it last, and Only what is selected keeps just the edits that touched it. The list is made in your own window from the session's edits while the session runs. It is gone when the session ends: it is never saved in the file or sent anywhere. Everyone in the session can see who made each change. If you joined after people had started, or were away for a while, the edits from before are not in your list, and the panel says so. Assistants in a session If someone turned on assistants in their app's settings, their AI assistant can ask to work in the session too. The host's own assistant joins at once; anyone else's waits for the host, with a card saying whose assistant it is: Allow or Deny. An assistant edits like its person, with its own undo, and leaves the session when its person leaves. When the session ends The host chooses End the session, or closes the file. Everyone else keeps an unsaved copy of the file as it was: save it if you want to keep it. Leave does the same for you alone.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"pen-and-touch","scope":"basics","title":"Draw with a pen on a tablet","summary":"What the app does when you draw with a pen on a touch screen: it ignores your palm, can leave the drawing to the pen while your fingers move the view, and lets the pen's buttons and a two-finger tap undo.","section":"tasks","order":94,"keywords":["pen","stylus","tablet","touch","palm","palm rejection","finger","fingers","two fingers","tap","undo","redo","side button","barrel button","eraser","pressure","hover","phone"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn","level":2},{"id":"a-pen-is-noticed-the-first-time","text":"A pen is noticed the first time","level":2},{"id":"your-palm-is-ignored","text":"Your palm is ignored","level":2},{"id":"let-the-pen-draw-and-your-fingers-move-the-view","text":"Let the pen draw and your fingers move the view","level":2},{"id":"undo-with-a-tap","text":"Undo with a tap","level":2},{"id":"the-pen-s-buttons","text":"The pen's buttons","level":2},{"id":"these-are-the-device-s-settings","text":"These are the device's settings","level":2},{"id":"not-built-yet","text":"Not built yet","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\"\u003EWhat you will learn\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHow the app works with a pen on a tablet or a touch-screen laptop, and the settings that change it. They are in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPen and touch\u003C/span\u003E, which shows on a touch screen, or once you have used a pen.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"a-pen-is-noticed-the-first-time\"\u003EA pen is noticed the first time\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENothing changes until you touch the drawing with a pen. From then on the app remembers, on this device, that you draw with one, and the choices below start to apply. Before that, your fingers draw and select as usual.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"your-palm-is-ignored\"\u003EYour palm is ignored\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWith \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EIgnore my palm while I use a pen\u003C/span\u003E on (it starts on), a touch on the drawing is ignored while the pen touches the screen, for a moment after you lift it or while it hovers just above the screen, and whenever the contact is as big as a palm. Rest your hand on the screen and draw.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf a quick finger right after the pen does nothing, wait a moment: that moment is the palm rule at work.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"let-the-pen-draw-and-your-fingers-move-the-view\"\u003ELet the pen draw and your fingers move the view\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUnder \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFingers on the drawing, once you use a pen here\u003C/span\u003E:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDraw and select\u003C/span\u003E (the start): a finger does what the pen does.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOnly move the view\u003C/span\u003E: one finger on the drawing does nothing, so a stray touch never draws or picks. Two fingers still move and zoom the view as usual.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe toolbars, panels and dialogs work with your fingers either way.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"undo-with-a-tap\"\u003EUndo with a tap\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWith \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETap with two fingers to undo, three to redo\u003C/span\u003E on (it starts on), tap the drawing with two fingers together to undo your last step, and with three to redo it. It works with or without a pen. Moving the fingers is a pan or a zoom, never a tap.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-pen-s-buttons\"\u003EThe pen&#039;s buttons\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPen side button\u003C/span\u003E: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWhat the app does (a right click)\u003C/span\u003E at the start, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUndo\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERedo\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPen eraser end\u003C/span\u003E (on pens that have one): \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWhat the app does\u003C/span\u003E at the start, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUndo\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERedo\u003C/span\u003E. In ModelCraft, what the app does is to erase while the eraser end touches.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"these-are-the-device-s-settings\"\u003EThese are the device&#039;s settings\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe pen and touch choices are kept for this device and apply in every app on it, so you set them once. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReset the layout\u003C/span\u003E and resetting an app&#039;s settings leave them as they are.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"not-built-yet\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPen pressure and tilt don&#039;t change line weights yet, and no app has a handwriting or sketch-to-shape tool. The app reads the pen&#039;s pressure, tilt and hover, so these can come later per app.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"What you will learn How the app works with a pen on a tablet or a touch-screen laptop, and the settings that change it. They are in Settings › General › Pen and touch, which shows on a touch screen, or once you have used a pen. A pen is noticed the first time Nothing changes until you touch the drawing with a pen. From then on the app remembers, on this device, that you draw with one, and the choices below start to apply. Before that, your fingers draw and select as usual. Your palm is ignored With Ignore my palm while I use a pen on (it starts on), a touch on the drawing is ignored while the pen touches the screen, for a moment after you lift it or while it hovers just above the screen, and whenever the contact is as big as a palm. Rest your hand on the screen and draw. If a quick finger right after the pen does nothing, wait a moment: that moment is the palm rule at work. Let the pen draw and your fingers move the view Under Fingers on the drawing, once you use a pen here: Draw and select (the start): a finger does what the pen does. Only move the view: one finger on the drawing does nothing, so a stray touch never draws or picks. Two fingers still move and zoom the view as usual. The toolbars, panels and dialogs work with your fingers either way. Undo with a tap With Tap with two fingers to undo, three to redo on (it starts on), tap the drawing with two fingers together to undo your last step, and with three to redo it. It works with or without a pen. Moving the fingers is a pan or a zoom, never a tap. The pen's buttons Pen side button: What the app does (a right click) at the start, or Undo or Redo. Pen eraser end (on pens that have one): What the app does at the start, or Undo or Redo. In ModelCraft, what the app does is to erase while the eraser end touches. These are the device's settings The pen and touch choices are kept for this device and apply in every app on it, so you set them once. Reset the layout and resetting an app's settings leave them as they are. Not built yet Pen pressure and tilt don't change line weights yet, and no app has a handwriting or sketch-to-shape tool. The app reads the pen's pressure, tilt and hover, so these can come later per app.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"input-devices","scope":"basics","title":"Use a trackpad, 3D mouse or controller","summary":"Set what a trackpad, your mouse's side buttons, a pen, finger taps, a 3D mouse, a game controller or a MIDI controller does, for every app or for one app.","section":"tasks","order":95,"keywords":["trackpad","touchpad","two fingers","pinch","scroll","mouse","side button","back button","forward button","tilt wheel","pen","stylus","eraser","tap","3D mouse","space mouse","6 axis","game controller","gamepad","joystick","MIDI","knob","fader","pad","input device","customize","assign"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn","level":2},{"id":"every-app-or-only-this-app","text":"Every app, or only this app","level":2},{"id":"trackpad","text":"Trackpad","level":2},{"id":"mouse-buttons","text":"Mouse buttons","level":2},{"id":"pen-and-finger-taps","text":"Pen and finger taps","level":2},{"id":"3d-mouse","text":"3D mouse","level":2},{"id":"game-controller","text":"Game controller","level":2},{"id":"midi-controller","text":"MIDI controller","level":2},{"id":"share-your-settings","text":"Share your settings","level":2},{"id":"not-built-yet","text":"Not built yet","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\"\u003EWhat you will learn\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHow to choose what each of your input devices does in the app. The settings are in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGlobal\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EInput devices\u003C/span\u003E, one card per device. Until you change something, every device works as it did before.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"every-app-or-only-this-app\"\u003EEvery app, or only this app\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAt the top, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EThese settings are for\u003C/span\u003E:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEvery app\u003C/span\u003E (the start): what you set applies in every app that has no settings of its own.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOnly\u003C/span\u003E this app: the app takes a copy of the shared settings and keeps its own from then on. Change them here without touching the other apps.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEvery app\u003C/span\u003E again to drop this app&#039;s own settings and go back to the shared ones. The settings are kept on this device, in every app window and in the desktop app&#039;s apps.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"trackpad\"\u003ETrackpad\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETwo-finger scroll\u003C/span\u003E: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWhat the app does\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPan the view\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EZoom\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOrbit (3D views)\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPinch\u003C/span\u003E: zoom, as the app does, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENothing\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESpeed\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReverse the direction\u003C/span\u003E apply when you have chosen pan, zoom or orbit.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe app tells a trackpad from a mouse wheel by the way it scrolls, so a mouse wheel keeps doing what it does.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"mouse-buttons\"\u003EMouse buttons\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBack side button\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EForward side button\u003C/span\u003E: run any command, such as Undo and Redo, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFit everything in view\u003C/span\u003E. A side button set to a command no longer takes the browser back a page.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWheel tilted sideways\u003C/span\u003E: what the app does, pan the view, or nothing.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe wheel&#039;s zoom direction and which buttons pan or orbit are in the app&#039;s own tab, under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMenus, keys and mouse\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"pen-and-finger-taps\"\u003EPen and finger taps\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESide button\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEraser end\u003C/span\u003E: what the app does, or any command.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETwo-finger tap\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EThree-finger tap\u003C/span\u003E (they start as Undo and Redo): any command, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENothing\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPalm rejection and what your fingers do once you use a pen stay under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPen and touch\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"3d-mouse\"\u003E3D mouse\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EConnect a 3D mouse\u003C/span\u003E and pick it in the browser&#039;s list; the browser remembers it for this site. Then push, lift, tilt and twist the cap to move the view under the pointer:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEach movement (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPush left and right\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPush forward and back\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELift and press\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETilt forward and back\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETilt left and right\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETwist\u003C/span\u003E) can pan, zoom, orbit or do nothing, and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReverse\u003C/span\u003E turns it round.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESpeed\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDead zone\u003C/span\u003E (how far the cap moves before anything happens) and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOnly the strongest movement at a time\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEach button runs a command or fits the view.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA 3D mouse needs Chrome or Edge in the browser.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"game-controller\"\u003EGame controller\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPlug in a controller and press any button so the browser shows it to the app. The sticks pan, orbit or zoom; the triggers zoom in and out; every button can run a command. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESpeed\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDead zone\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReverse up and down on the sticks\u003C/span\u003E are there too.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"midi-controller\"\u003EMIDI controller\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EConnect MIDI\u003C/span\u003E. Then, for each control: pick what it should do under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd a control for\u003C/span\u003E (a knob or fader pans, zooms or orbits; a pad or key runs a command), choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELearn it\u003C/span\u003E, and turn the knob or press the pad. Each learned control is listed, with a button to remove it. MIDI needs Chrome or Edge in the browser.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"share-your-settings\"\u003EShare your settings\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport\u003C/span\u003E saves the settings shown as a file; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EImport\u003C/span\u003E reads one back (settings this version doesn&#039;t know are left out, and you&#039;re told how many). \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBack to the defaults\u003C/span\u003E undoes every change for the settings shown.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"not-built-yet\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA wheel dial (such as the ones on Windows) and the desktop app&#039;s own drivers for 3D mice are not supported yet. A 3D mouse, controller or knob moves the view only in apps that have handed their views over; their buttons work everywhere.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"What you will learn How to choose what each of your input devices does in the app. The settings are in Settings › Global › Input devices, one card per device. Until you change something, every device works as it did before. Every app, or only this app At the top, These settings are for: Every app (the start): what you set applies in every app that has no settings of its own. Only this app: the app takes a copy of the shared settings and keeps its own from then on. Change them here without touching the other apps. Choose Every app again to drop this app's own settings and go back to the shared ones. The settings are kept on this device, in every app window and in the desktop app's apps. Trackpad Two-finger scroll: What the app does, Pan the view, Zoom or Orbit (3D views). Pinch: zoom, as the app does, or Nothing. Speed and Reverse the direction apply when you have chosen pan, zoom or orbit. The app tells a trackpad from a mouse wheel by the way it scrolls, so a mouse wheel keeps doing what it does. Mouse buttons Back side button and Forward side button: run any command, such as Undo and Redo, or Fit everything in view. A side button set to a command no longer takes the browser back a page. Wheel tilted sideways: what the app does, pan the view, or nothing. The wheel's zoom direction and which buttons pan or orbit are in the app's own tab, under Menus, keys and mouse. Pen and finger taps Side button and Eraser end: what the app does, or any command. Two-finger tap and Three-finger tap (they start as Undo and Redo): any command, or Nothing. Palm rejection and what your fingers do once you use a pen stay under Pen and touch. 3D mouse Choose Connect a 3D mouse and pick it in the browser's list; the browser remembers it for this site. Then push, lift, tilt and twist the cap to move the view under the pointer: Each movement (Push left and right, Push forward and back, Lift and press, Tilt forward and back, Tilt left and right, Twist) can pan, zoom, orbit or do nothing, and Reverse turns it round. Speed, Dead zone (how far the cap moves before anything happens) and Only the strongest movement at a time. Each button runs a command or fits the view. A 3D mouse needs Chrome or Edge in the browser. Game controller Plug in a controller and press any button so the browser shows it to the app. The sticks pan, orbit or zoom; the triggers zoom in and out; every button can run a command. Speed, Dead zone and Reverse up and down on the sticks are there too. MIDI controller Choose Connect MIDI. Then, for each control: pick what it should do under Add a control for (a knob or fader pans, zooms or orbits; a pad or key runs a command), choose Learn it, and turn the knob or press the pad. Each learned control is listed, with a button to remove it. MIDI needs Chrome or Edge in the browser. Share your settings Export saves the settings shown as a file; Import reads one back (settings this version doesn't know are left out, and you're told how many). Back to the defaults undoes every change for the settings shown. Not built yet A wheel dial (such as the ones on Windows) and the desktop app's own drivers for 3D mice are not supported yet. A 3D mouse, controller or knob moves the view only in apps that have handed their views over; their buttons work everywhere.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"full-screen-crosshair","scope":"basics","title":"Full-screen crosshair","summary":"Two thin lines through the pointer, edge to edge across the drawing or view, to check by eye that things line up. One switch turns it on in every app.","section":"reference","order":30,"keywords":["full screen crosshair","full-screen cursor","crosshair","cursor","cursorsize","cursorsize 100","edge to edge","line up","alignment","align","guide lines","cross hairs"],"headings":[{"id":"turn-it-on","text":"Turn it on","level":2},{"id":"what-it-does","text":"What it does","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EThe full-screen crosshair draws two thin lines through the pointer: one across the whole width of the drawing or view under it, one down its whole height. Move the pointer to an edge or a corner and you can see at once whether other things sit on the same line. It is the same as AutoCAD&#039;s \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ECURSORSIZE\u003C/code\u003E 100.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"turn-it-on\"\u003ETurn it on\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOpen \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E (the gear in the app bar, or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command ,\"\u003E⌘,\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+,\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), go to \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E and tick \u003Cstrong\u003ECrosshair from edge to edge in every app\u003C/strong\u003E under \u003Cstrong\u003EFull-screen crosshair\u003C/strong\u003E. It is one switch for all eight apps on this computer: turn it on in one app and the others have it too, also in tabs that are already open.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-it-does\"\u003EWhat it does\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe lines show while the pointer is over a drawing or a view, also when no tool is running.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EToolbars, cards and hints that sit over the view stay on top of the lines; a dialog never gets them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWith a finger nothing changes: the lines follow a mouse, a trackpad or a pen.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn CadCraft the crosshair is drawn by the drawing itself: it turns with the coordinate system (UCS), and with the view split it stays in the pane you are working in. CadCraft also has it as a cursor size of its own: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECadCraft\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cstrong\u003ECursor\u003C/strong\u003E › \u003Cstrong\u003EFull screen\u003C/strong\u003E, or type \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ECURSORSIZE\u003C/code\u003E and 100.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"The full-screen crosshair draws two thin lines through the pointer: one across the whole width of the drawing or view under it, one down its whole height. Move the pointer to an edge or a corner and you can see at once whether other things sit on the same line. It is the same as AutoCAD's CURSORSIZE 100. Turn it on Open Settings (the gear in the app bar, or Ctrl+,), go to General and tick Crosshair from edge to edge in every app under Full-screen crosshair. It is one switch for all eight apps on this computer: turn it on in one app and the others have it too, also in tabs that are already open. What it does The lines show while the pointer is over a drawing or a view, also when no tool is running. Toolbars, cards and hints that sit over the view stay on top of the lines; a dialog never gets them. With a finger nothing changes: the lines follow a mouse, a trackpad or a pen. In CadCraft the crosshair is drawn by the drawing itself: it turns with the coordinate system (UCS), and with the view split it stays in the pane you are working in. CadCraft also has it as a cursor size of its own: Settings › CadCraft › Cursor › Full screen, or type CURSORSIZE and 100.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"unit-lists","scope":"basics","title":"Units, typed values and the unit lists","summary":"Type a length in any unit with its mark, choose which units the unit lists show, and star the unit new work starts in. A file always keeps its own unit.","section":"reference","order":60,"keywords":["units","unit list","metric","imperial","feet","inches","feet and inches","meters","millimeters","US survey foot","survey feet","international foot","yards","miles","nautical mile","chain","rod","link","furlong","fathom","point","pica","acre","hectare","square","roofing square","acre-foot","board foot","gallon","gon","grad","radian","degrees minutes seconds","slope","percent","H:V","rise run","default unit","star","INSUNITS"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn","level":2},{"id":"type-a-length-in-any-unit","text":"Type a length in any unit","level":2},{"id":"choose-the-units-the-lists-show","text":"Choose the units the lists show","level":2},{"id":"where-the-lists-and-the-star-are-used","text":"Where the lists and the star are used","level":2},{"id":"a-file-keeps-its-own-unit","text":"A file keeps its own unit","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\"\u003EWhat you will learn\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHow to type a value in any unit, and how to choose which units the unit lists in the apps show and which one new work starts in. Every app shares one table of units, with the exact sizes their legal definitions give.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"type-a-length-in-any-unit\"\u003EType a length in any unit\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn a box that takes a length, type the number and the unit&#039;s mark after it. The app turns it into the unit the box is in.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EType\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMeans\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E12&#039;6&quot;\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E12&#039; 6 1/2&quot;\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E12&#039;-6&quot;\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E3 ft 6 in\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Efeet and inches\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E450 mm\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E3.2 m\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E1.5 km\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E2 dm\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E400 nm\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Emetric lengths (\u003Ccode\u003Enm\u003C/code\u003E is the nanometer)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E3 yd\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E2.5 mi\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E250 mil\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eyards, miles, thousandths of an inch\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E10 US ft\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E1 US mi\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ethe US survey foot and mile\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E2 rd\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E100 li\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E3 ch\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E1 fur\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Erods, links, chains, furlongs\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E1 nmi\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E1 NM\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E6 ftm\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Enautical miles, fathoms\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E12 pt\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E3 pc\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Epoints and picas (1/72 and 1/6 of an inch)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Enm\u003C/code\u003E is always the nanometer. For the nautical mile type \u003Ccode\u003Enmi\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003ENM\u003C/code\u003E. \u003Ccode\u003ENm\u003C/code\u003E is refused, because it could be either.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Eft\u003C/code\u003E is the international foot (0.3048 m). The US survey foot (1200/3937 m) is a different unit: type \u003Ccode\u003EUS ft\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eusft\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003Esurvey ft\u003C/code\u003E. Rods, links, chains, furlongs and acres are the international-foot ones.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESums work as before where the app takes them, with any of these marks: \u003Ccode\u003E2*3 yd\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E12&#039;6&quot; + 3 ch\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn CadCraft a mark is read when the drawing has units (its $INSUNITS). A drawing with no units reads one only when its length format is Engineering or Architectural, where one drawing unit is an inch.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn CivilCraft a project in feet is in US survey feet, as it always was, so \u003Ccode\u003E10 US ft\u003C/code\u003E is 10 and \u003Ccode\u003E10 ft\u003C/code\u003E is 10 project feet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"choose-the-units-the-lists-show\"\u003EChoose the units the lists show\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOpen \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E (the gear on the app bar, or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command ,\"\u003E⌘,\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+,\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E, and find \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUnits\u003C/span\u003E. There is one list for each kind: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELength\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArea\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EVolume\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAngle\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESlope\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEach unit has a switch. A unit that is off is left out of the unit lists in the apps.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the star beside a unit to make it the default for new work. Click it again to take the star away. With no star, each app keeps its own default (the file&#039;s unit, or the one for your region).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReset the unit lists\u003C/span\u003E puts the starting lists back, with no stars.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe choices are the same in every app in this browser, and they are saved as you make them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"where-the-lists-and-the-star-are-used\"\u003EWhere the lists and the star are used\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EApp\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat follows the switches\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat the star sets\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECadCraft\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe units ($INSUNITS) of a new drawing, when it is a unit a drawing can carry\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECivilCraft\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe project&#039;s units list\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELength: a new project in meters (a metric star) or feet. Area and volume: the unit areas and volumes are reported in\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EModelCraft\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe units list beside the value box\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe unit of a new model\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBimCraft\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe units list on the plan toolbar\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe display units, until you pick them on the toolbar (your earlier pick is kept)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMarkupCraft\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe measuring and position units in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E, and the unit in the calibrate dialog\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe measuring unit, until MarkupCraft&#039;s own settings have been saved\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETakeoffCraft\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe unit lists of an item\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELength and area: the unit of a new item\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe angle and slope lists are saved, but no app&#039;s box follows them yet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"a-file-keeps-its-own-unit\"\u003EA file keeps its own unit\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe settings never change a file. A drawing keeps its units, a CivilCraft project its feet or meters, a model its unit. The unit a file uses is always in its list, even when it is turned off in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"What you will learn How to type a value in any unit, and how to choose which units the unit lists in the apps show and which one new work starts in. Every app shares one table of units, with the exact sizes their legal definitions give. Type a length in any unit In a box that takes a length, type the number and the unit's mark after it. The app turns it into the unit the box is in. Type Means 12'6\", 12' 6 1/2\", 12'-6\", 3 ft 6 in feet and inches 450 mm, 3.2 m, 1.5 km, 2 dm, 400 nm metric lengths (nm is the nanometer) 3 yd, 2.5 mi, 250 mil yards, miles, thousandths of an inch 10 US ft, 1 US mi the US survey foot and mile 2 rd, 100 li, 3 ch, 1 fur rods, links, chains, furlongs 1 nmi or 1 NM, 6 ftm nautical miles, fathoms 12 pt, 3 pc points and picas (1/72 and 1/6 of an inch) nm is always the nanometer. For the nautical mile type nmi or NM. Nm is refused, because it could be either. ft is the international foot (0.3048 m). The US survey foot (1200/3937 m) is a different unit: type US ft, usft or survey ft. Rods, links, chains, furlongs and acres are the international-foot ones. Sums work as before where the app takes them, with any of these marks: 2*3 yd, 12'6\" + 3 ch. In CadCraft a mark is read when the drawing has units (its $INSUNITS). A drawing with no units reads one only when its length format is Engineering or Architectural, where one drawing unit is an inch. In CivilCraft a project in feet is in US survey feet, as it always was, so 10 US ft is 10 and 10 ft is 10 project feet. Choose the units the lists show Open Settings (the gear on the app bar, or Ctrl+,), choose General, and find Units. There is one list for each kind: Length, Area, Volume, Angle and Slope. Each unit has a switch. A unit that is off is left out of the unit lists in the apps. Click the star beside a unit to make it the default for new work. Click it again to take the star away. With no star, each app keeps its own default (the file's unit, or the one for your region). Reset the unit lists puts the starting lists back, with no stars. The choices are the same in every app in this browser, and they are saved as you make them. Where the lists and the star are used App What follows the switches What the star sets CadCraft The units ($INSUNITS) of a new drawing, when it is a unit a drawing can carry CivilCraft The project's units list Length: a new project in meters (a metric star) or feet. Area and volume: the unit areas and volumes are reported in ModelCraft The units list beside the value box The unit of a new model BimCraft The units list on the plan toolbar The display units, until you pick them on the toolbar (your earlier pick is kept) MarkupCraft The measuring and position units in Settings, and the unit in the calibrate dialog The measuring unit, until MarkupCraft's own settings have been saved TakeoffCraft The unit lists of an item Length and area: the unit of a new item The angle and slope lists are saved, but no app's box follows them yet. A file keeps its own unit The settings never change a file. A drawing keeps its units, a CivilCraft project its feet or meters, a model its unit. The unit a file uses is always in its list, even when it is turned off in Settings.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"lost-toolbar","scope":"basics","title":"Where did my toolbar go?","summary":"The bars, menus or panels went missing. Here is what hides them on purpose, the keys that bring them back, and one button that puts the whole layout back.","section":"trouble","order":5,"keywords":["toolbar gone","toolbar missing","lost toolbar","lost menu","menu disappeared","ribbon disappeared","bars hidden","show bars","full screen","panels gone","reset layout","layout","ribbon","collapsed","locked toolbar"],"headings":[{"id":"the-quickest-fix-reset-the-layout","text":"The quickest fix: reset the layout","level":2},{"id":"the-bars-are-hidden-full-screen","text":"The bars are hidden (full screen)","level":2},{"id":"the-toolbar-looks-different","text":"The toolbar looks different","level":2},{"id":"the-toolbar-s-own-menu","text":"The toolbar's own menu","level":2},{"id":"everything-is-too-big-or-too-small","text":"Everything is too big or too small","level":2},{"id":"the-panels-are-gone","text":"The panels are gone","level":2},{"id":"on-a-phone","text":"On a phone","level":2},{"id":"still-missing","text":"Still missing?","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003ENothing is deleted when a bar or a panel goes away: the app only hides or moves it. Each of these ways is on purpose, and each has a way back.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-quickest-fix-reset-the-layout\"\u003EThe quickest fix: reset the layout\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E with the gear button in the app bar, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command ,\"\u003E⌘,\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+,\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E tab.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUnder \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELayout\u003C/span\u003E, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReset the layout\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe toolbar goes back to its first order and buttons, the panels go back to their sides, the screen mode goes back to the normal view and the menu style goes back to the one the app starts with. Your theme, language, sizes and the app&#039;s own settings stay as they are, and so do any menu styles you made yourself (choose them again under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMenus, keys and mouse\u003C/span\u003E).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn CadCraft Studio, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E in Studio&#039;s own window also has \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReset the layout in every app\u003C/span\u003E. Each app&#039;s windows follow at once, and an app you open later starts from its first layout.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-bars-are-hidden-full-screen\"\u003EThe bars are hidden (full screen)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe screen-mode button in the app bar, and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control Shift F\"\u003E⌃⇧F\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+F\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, go from the normal view to full screen, then to full screen with the bars hidden, then back.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhile the bars are hidden, a small \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShow bars\u003C/span\u003E tab stays at the top middle of the window. Choose it, or point at the top edge of the window, and the bars slide down; they hide again a moment after you move away. Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control Shift F\"\u003E⌃⇧F\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+F\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to leave this mode, or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to leave full screen.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf the app opens with its bars hidden every time, look at \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScreen\u003C/span\u003E: the app does what is chosen there when it opens.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"input:touch\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003ETouch\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETap the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShow bars\u003C/span\u003E tab, or tap the top edge of the screen.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-toolbar-looks-different\"\u003EThe toolbar looks different\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETools in another order, or tools you can&#039;t find:\u003C/strong\u003E someone arranged the toolbar. Choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOriginal order\u003C/span\u003E under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EToolbar\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOnly icons, or only words:\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EToolbar buttons\u003C/span\u003E in the same place picks icons and labels, icons only or labels only. Hold the pointer over an icon to see its name.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETools won&#039;t move:\u003C/strong\u003E the toolbar is locked. Right-click the toolbar (or press and hold it) and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUnlock the toolbar\u003C/span\u003E, or clear \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELock the toolbar\u003C/span\u003E under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EToolbar\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA ribbon that shows only its tab names:\u003C/strong\u003E it is collapsed. Choose a tab name to open its tools for one command, or double-click a tab name (or choose the pin at the end of the tab row) to keep the ribbon open.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMenus or a ribbon instead of the toolbar:\u003C/strong\u003E a menu style is on. Pick the app&#039;s own style under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › the app&#039;s tab › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMenus, keys and mouse\u003C/span\u003E. Every command is in every style; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command K\"\u003E⌘K\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+K\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E finds any of them by name.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-toolbar-s-own-menu\"\u003EThe toolbar&#039;s own menu\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ERight-click the toolbar, or press and hold it on a touch screen, for a short menu:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArrange the toolbar\u003C/span\u003E: drag tools within their group and groups by their name, or move the focused one with the arrow keys. Choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDone arranging\u003C/span\u003E in the same menu, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, when it looks right.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELock the toolbar\u003C/span\u003E / \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUnlock the toolbar\u003C/span\u003E: a locked toolbar can&#039;t be arranged or reset by accident.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReset the toolbar\u003C/span\u003E: tools back in their first order.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWith a ribbon menu style: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECollapse the ribbon\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPin the ribbon open\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn a keyboard, the menu key (or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift F10\"\u003E⇧ F10\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift+F10\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) opens it for the tool that has the focus.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"everything-is-too-big-or-too-small\"\u003EEverything is too big or too small\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EInterface size\u003C/span\u003E makes the bars, toolbars, panels and dialogs of this app bigger or smaller, from 80 % to 140 %. The drawing keeps its own zoom, so a smaller interface leaves more room for it, for example with two apps side by side on one monitor. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPanel text size\u003C/span\u003E beside it changes only the text in the side panels, on top of the interface size. Each app keeps its own size; CadCraft Studio&#039;s own window has one in its \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E too.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-panels-are-gone\"\u003EThe panels are gone\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPanels can float over the drawing, dock on another side, or move to a window of their own (often on a second monitor, or behind this window).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELook for a second window called after the app with &quot;panels&quot;, and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPut the panels back\u003C/span\u003E in it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOr choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReset layout\u003C/span\u003E under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPanels\u003C/span\u003E, which brings every panel home.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"on-a-phone\"\u003EOn a phone\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn a phone the panels are sheets: open one from the bar at the bottom of the screen. The \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETools\u003C/span\u003E button in that bar hides and shows the toolbar, and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMove\u003C/span\u003E puts it at the top or the bottom of the screen.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"still-missing\"\u003EStill missing?\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFind any tool by name with \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command K\"\u003E⌘K\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+K\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, even while its bar is hidden. If something is still not where it should be after resetting the layout, tell us on the support page with the app&#039;s name and what you were doing.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Nothing is deleted when a bar or a panel goes away: the app only hides or moves it. Each of these ways is on purpose, and each has a way back. The quickest fix: reset the layout Open Settings with the gear button in the app bar, or press Ctrl+,. Choose the General tab. Under Layout, choose Reset the layout. The toolbar goes back to its first order and buttons, the panels go back to their sides, the screen mode goes back to the normal view and the menu style goes back to the one the app starts with. Your theme, language, sizes and the app's own settings stay as they are, and so do any menu styles you made yourself (choose them again under Menus, keys and mouse). In CadCraft Studio, Settings in Studio's own window also has Reset the layout in every app. Each app's windows follow at once, and an app you open later starts from its first layout. The bars are hidden (full screen) The screen-mode button in the app bar, and Ctrl+Shift+F, go from the normal view to full screen, then to full screen with the bars hidden, then back. While the bars are hidden, a small Show bars tab stays at the top middle of the window. Choose it, or point at the top edge of the window, and the bars slide down; they hide again a moment after you move away. Press Ctrl+Shift+F to leave this mode, or Esc to leave full screen. If the app opens with its bars hidden every time, look at Settings › General › Screen: the app does what is chosen there when it opens. Tap the Show bars tab, or tap the top edge of the screen. The toolbar looks different Tools in another order, or tools you can't find: someone arranged the toolbar. Choose Original order under Settings › General › Toolbar. Only icons, or only words: Toolbar buttons in the same place picks icons and labels, icons only or labels only. Hold the pointer over an icon to see its name. Tools won't move: the toolbar is locked. Right-click the toolbar (or press and hold it) and choose Unlock the toolbar, or clear Lock the toolbar under Settings › General › Toolbar. A ribbon that shows only its tab names: it is collapsed. Choose a tab name to open its tools for one command, or double-click a tab name (or choose the pin at the end of the tab row) to keep the ribbon open. Menus or a ribbon instead of the toolbar: a menu style is on. Pick the app's own style under Settings › the app's tab › Menus, keys and mouse. Every command is in every style; Ctrl+K finds any of them by name. The toolbar's own menu Right-click the toolbar, or press and hold it on a touch screen, for a short menu: Arrange the toolbar: drag tools within their group and groups by their name, or move the focused one with the arrow keys. Choose Done arranging in the same menu, or press Esc, when it looks right. Lock the toolbar / Unlock the toolbar: a locked toolbar can't be arranged or reset by accident. Reset the toolbar: tools back in their first order. With a ribbon menu style: Collapse the ribbon or Pin the ribbon open. On a keyboard, the menu key (or Shift+F10) opens it for the tool that has the focus. Everything is too big or too small Settings › General › Interface size makes the bars, toolbars, panels and dialogs of this app bigger or smaller, from 80 % to 140 %. The drawing keeps its own zoom, so a smaller interface leaves more room for it, for example with two apps side by side on one monitor. Panel text size beside it changes only the text in the side panels, on top of the interface size. Each app keeps its own size; CadCraft Studio's own window has one in its Settings too. The panels are gone Panels can float over the drawing, dock on another side, or move to a window of their own (often on a second monitor, or behind this window). Look for a second window called after the app with \"panels\", and choose Put the panels back in it. Or choose Reset layout under Settings › General › Panels, which brings every panel home. On a phone On a phone the panels are sheets: open one from the bar at the bottom of the screen. The Tools button in that bar hides and shows the toolbar, and Move puts it at the top or the bottom of the screen. Still missing? Find any tool by name with Ctrl+K, even while its bar is hidden. If something is still not where it should be after resetting the layout, tell us on the support page with the app's name and what you were doing.","lang":"en"},{"slug":"recover","scope":"basics","title":"Recover unsaved work","summary":"How the apps keep a recovery copy while you work, and how to get your work back after a crash or a closed tab.","section":"trouble","order":55,"keywords":["autosave","recover","recovery","crash","lost work","closed tab","unsaved","restore","backup"],"headings":[{"id":"when-the-copy-is-kept","text":"When the copy is kept","level":2},{"id":"get-your-work-back","text":"Get your work back","level":2},{"id":"things-to-know","text":"Things to know","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EWhile you work, each app keeps a recovery copy of what you have not saved yet. It is not a save: your file on your computer only changes when you save it. The copy is there so a crash, a closed tab or a computer that turns off does not cost you your work.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"when-the-copy-is-kept\"\u003EWhen the copy is kept\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA few seconds after you stop editing, and at least every 30 seconds while you keep going.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWhen you switch to another tab or window.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe copy stays in this browser, on this computer. Nothing is uploaded.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"get-your-work-back\"\u003EGet your work back\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen the same app again, in the same browser.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIf it closed before your work was saved, it asks \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERecover your work?\u003C/span\u003E and lists what it kept, with the time of the last copy.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERecover\u003C/span\u003E to open it, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDiscard\u003C/span\u003E to throw it away.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESave it to a file as soon as it opens.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENot now\u003C/span\u003E keeps the copies for 30 days, and the app asks again the next time it opens. A reload counts too: reload a tab with unsaved work and the app offers it back.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"things-to-know\"\u003EThings to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWhen you save, or close a file on purpose, its recovery copy is removed.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAnother tab of the same app that is still open never offers your copies. It only offers work from a tab that has gone.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPrivate windows in some browsers, and a browser with no space left, cannot keep a copy. The app tells you when that happens, so save to a file more often.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClearing your browser&#039;s site data removes the copies.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ENot every app keeps a recovery copy yet. Each app&#039;s own help says whether it does.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"While you work, each app keeps a recovery copy of what you have not saved yet. It is not a save: your file on your computer only changes when you save it. The copy is there so a crash, a closed tab or a computer that turns off does not cost you your work. When the copy is kept A few seconds after you stop editing, and at least every 30 seconds while you keep going. When you switch to another tab or window. The copy stays in this browser, on this computer. Nothing is uploaded. Get your work back Open the same app again, in the same browser. If it closed before your work was saved, it asks Recover your work? and lists what it kept, with the time of the last copy. Choose Recover to open it, or Discard to throw it away. Save it to a file as soon as it opens. Not now keeps the copies for 30 days, and the app asks again the next time it opens. A reload counts too: reload a tab with unsaved work and the app offers it back. Things to know When you save, or close a file on purpose, its recovery copy is removed. Another tab of the same app that is still open never offers your copies. It only offers work from a tab that has gone. Private windows in some browsers, and a browser with no space left, cannot keep a copy. The app tells you when that happens, so save to a file more often. Clearing your browser's site data removes the copies. Not every app keeps a recovery copy yet. Each app's own help says whether it does.","lang":"en"}],"locale":"it","chain":["it","en"]};
